<script id="question-data" type="application/json">[{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":1,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with crushing chest pain with horizontal ST depression and tall R waves in V1-V3. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Posterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction","Very-high-risk non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome","Takotsubo syndrome with dynamic LV outflow obstruction","Coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia","De Winter anterior occlusion myocardial infarction"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Posterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Posterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction. The key discriminator is diagnostic ST elevation in V7-V9. Immediate priorities include: activate immediate reperfusion and record posterior leads without delaying angiography.","learningPoint":"Posterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction: diagnostic ST elevation in V7-V9. Avoid labelling the tracing as isolated subendocardial ischaemia and delaying reperfusion.","id":"cardiovascular-001","globalNumber":1},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":2,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has posterior st-elevation myocardial infarction: crushing chest pain with horizontal ST depression and tall R waves in V1-V3. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["admit for urgent coronary angiography and avoid exercise stress testing","activate immediate reperfusion and record posterior leads without delaying angiography","give nitrates and calcium-channel blockade while treating arrhythmia and excluding fixed occlusion","arrange immediate invasive coronary assessment and haemodynamic support","obtain urgent coronary angiography and favour conservative management if stable with preserved flow"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"activate immediate reperfusion and record posterior leads without delaying angiography","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to activate immediate reperfusion and record posterior leads without delaying angiography. The decisive feature is diagnostic ST elevation in V7-V9. A major error is labelling the tracing as isolated subendocardial ischaemia and delaying reperfusion.","learningPoint":"Posterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction: diagnostic ST elevation in V7-V9. Avoid labelling the tracing as isolated subendocardial ischaemia and delaying reperfusion.","id":"cardiovascular-002","globalNumber":2},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":3,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for posterior st-elevation myocardial infarction, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["simultaneous allergic features and objective myocardial ischaemia","the De Winter pattern indicating acute proximal LAD occlusion","diagnostic ST elevation in V7-V9","a pain-free Wellens pattern suggesting critical proximal LAD stenosis","ST elevation in V4R with RV dilatation"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"diagnostic ST elevation in V7-V9","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is diagnostic ST elevation in V7-V9. In context, this supports Posterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction and should trigger: activate immediate reperfusion and record posterior leads without delaying angiography.","learningPoint":"Posterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction: diagnostic ST elevation in V7-V9. Avoid labelling the tracing as isolated subendocardial ischaemia and delaying reperfusion.","id":"cardiovascular-003","globalNumber":3},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":4,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of posterior st-elevation myocardial infarction, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["waiting for serial troponins before activating definitive care","giving nitrates or catecholamine inotropes despite dynamic outflow obstruction","performing provocative stress testing because the patient is pain free","labelling the tracing as isolated subendocardial ischaemia and delaying reperfusion","administering nitrates or aggressive diuresis despite preload dependence"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"labelling the tracing as isolated subendocardial ischaemia and delaying reperfusion","explanation":"The hazardous action is labelling the tracing as isolated subendocardial ischaemia and delaying reperfusion. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to ventricular arrhythmia or acute mitral regurgitation.","learningPoint":"Posterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction: diagnostic ST elevation in V7-V9. Avoid labelling the tracing as isolated subendocardial ischaemia and delaying reperfusion.","id":"cardiovascular-004","globalNumber":4},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":5,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with posterior st-elevation myocardial infarction initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["ventricular fibrillation, myocardial infarction and sudden death","anterior myocardial infarction and sudden cardiac death","propagation of dissection, recurrent infarction or ventricular arrhythmia","malignant ventricular arrhythmia and cardiogenic shock","ventricular arrhythmia or acute mitral regurgitation"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"ventricular arrhythmia or acute mitral regurgitation","explanation":"The important complication is ventricular arrhythmia or acute mitral regurgitation. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of posterior st-elevation myocardial infarction.","learningPoint":"Posterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction: diagnostic ST elevation in V7-V9. Avoid labelling the tracing as isolated subendocardial ischaemia and delaying reperfusion.","id":"cardiovascular-005","globalNumber":5},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":6,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with inferior ST elevation with hypotension, elevated JVP and clear lung fields. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Right ventricular myocardial infarction","Post-infarction ventricular septal rupture","Posterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction","Spontaneous coronary artery dissection","Complete heart block complicating inferior myocardial infarction"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Right ventricular myocardial infarction","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Right ventricular myocardial infarction. The key discriminator is ST elevation in V4R with RV dilatation. Immediate priorities include: give a cautious crystalloid bolus, treat bradycardia and expedite reperfusion.","learningPoint":"Right ventricular myocardial infarction: ST elevation in V4R with RV dilatation. Avoid administering nitrates or aggressive diuresis despite preload dependence.","id":"cardiovascular-006","globalNumber":6},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":7,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has right ventricular myocardial infarction: inferior ST elevation with hypotension, elevated JVP and clear lung fields. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["activate the primary PCI pathway as an occlusion myocardial infarction without waiting for ST elevation","give a cautious crystalloid bolus, treat bradycardia and expedite reperfusion","expedite revascularisation, use norepinephrine and add carefully titrated inotropy when required","provide ventilatory support and afterload reduction while arranging urgent surgical repair","activate immediate reperfusion and record posterior leads without delaying angiography"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give a cautious crystalloid bolus, treat bradycardia and expedite reperfusion","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to give a cautious crystalloid bolus, treat bradycardia and expedite reperfusion. The decisive feature is ST elevation in V4R with RV dilatation. A major error is administering nitrates or aggressive diuresis despite preload dependence.","learningPoint":"Right ventricular myocardial infarction: ST elevation in V4R with RV dilatation. Avoid administering nitrates or aggressive diuresis despite preload dependence.","id":"cardiovascular-007","globalNumber":7},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":8,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for right ventricular myocardial infarction, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["global subendocardial ischaemia with aVR elevation in a clinically unstable patient","angiographic or clinically documented reversible coronary spasm without fixed culprit obstruction","ST elevation in V4R with RV dilatation","the De Winter pattern indicating acute proximal LAD occlusion","independent atrial and ventricular activity with a slow escape rhythm"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"ST elevation in V4R with RV dilatation","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is ST elevation in V4R with RV dilatation. In context, this supports Right ventricular myocardial infarction and should trigger: give a cautious crystalloid bolus, treat bradycardia and expedite reperfusion.","learningPoint":"Right ventricular myocardial infarction: ST elevation in V4R with RV dilatation. Avoid administering nitrates or aggressive diuresis despite preload dependence.","id":"cardiovascular-008","globalNumber":8},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":9,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of right ventricular myocardial infarction, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["assuming a normal-sized heart or subtle murmur excludes acute severe regurgitation","giving fibrinolysis for presumed atherosclerotic STEMI","withholding intramuscular adrenaline in life-threatening anaphylaxis solely because of ischaemia","administering nitrates or aggressive diuresis despite preload dependence","labelling the tracing as isolated subendocardial ischaemia and delaying reperfusion"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"administering nitrates or aggressive diuresis despite preload dependence","explanation":"The hazardous action is administering nitrates or aggressive diuresis despite preload dependence. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to complete heart block and refractory right ventricular shock.","learningPoint":"Right ventricular myocardial infarction: ST elevation in V4R with RV dilatation. Avoid administering nitrates or aggressive diuresis despite preload dependence.","id":"cardiovascular-009","globalNumber":9},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":10,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with right ventricular myocardial infarction initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["recurrent rupture, exsanguination and cardiac arrest","propagation of dissection, recurrent infarction or ventricular arrhythmia","rapid biventricular failure, multiorgan shock and death","refractory hypoxaemia and cardiogenic shock","complete heart block and refractory right ventricular shock"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"complete heart block and refractory right ventricular shock","explanation":"The important complication is complete heart block and refractory right ventricular shock. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of right ventricular myocardial infarction.","learningPoint":"Right ventricular myocardial infarction: ST elevation in V4R with RV dilatation. Avoid administering nitrates or aggressive diuresis despite preload dependence.","id":"cardiovascular-010","globalNumber":10},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":11,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with a young postpartum patient with acute coronary syndrome and few atherosclerotic risk factors. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Spontaneous coronary artery dissection","Takotsubo syndrome with dynamic LV outflow obstruction","High-risk pulmonary embolism","Cardiac tamponade","Aortic intramural haematoma"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Spontaneous coronary artery dissection","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Spontaneous coronary artery dissection. The key discriminator is long smooth coronary narrowing or an intramural haematoma. Immediate priorities include: obtain urgent coronary angiography and favour conservative management if stable with preserved flow.","learningPoint":"Spontaneous coronary artery dissection: long smooth coronary narrowing or an intramural haematoma. Avoid giving fibrinolysis for presumed atherosclerotic STEMI.","id":"cardiovascular-011","globalNumber":11},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":12,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has spontaneous coronary artery dissection: a young postpartum patient with acute coronary syndrome and few atherosclerotic risk factors. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["treat anaphylaxis promptly while managing coronary ischaemia with early cardiology input","obtain urgent coronary angiography and favour conservative management if stable with preserved flow","manage as an acute aortic syndrome with anti-impulse therapy and urgent specialist input","admit for urgent coronary angiography and avoid exercise stress testing","activate the primary PCI pathway as an occlusion myocardial infarction without waiting for ST elevation"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"obtain urgent coronary angiography and favour conservative management if stable with preserved flow","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to obtain urgent coronary angiography and favour conservative management if stable with preserved flow. The decisive feature is long smooth coronary narrowing or an intramural haematoma. A major error is giving fibrinolysis for presumed atherosclerotic STEMI.","learningPoint":"Spontaneous coronary artery dissection: long smooth coronary narrowing or an intramural haematoma. Avoid giving fibrinolysis for presumed atherosclerotic STEMI.","id":"cardiovascular-012","globalNumber":12},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":13,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for spontaneous coronary artery dissection, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["an intimal flap involving the ascending aorta","hyperattenuating crescentic thickening of the aortic wall on non-contrast CT","long smooth coronary narrowing or an intramural haematoma","a pain-free Wellens pattern suggesting critical proximal LAD stenosis","angiographic or clinically documented reversible coronary spasm without fixed culprit obstruction"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"long smooth coronary narrowing or an intramural haematoma","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is long smooth coronary narrowing or an intramural haematoma. In context, this supports Spontaneous coronary artery dissection and should trigger: obtain urgent coronary angiography and favour conservative management if stable with preserved flow.","learningPoint":"Spontaneous coronary artery dissection: long smooth coronary narrowing or an intramural haematoma. Avoid giving fibrinolysis for presumed atherosclerotic STEMI.","id":"cardiovascular-013","globalNumber":13},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":14,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of spontaneous coronary artery dissection, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["withholding intramuscular adrenaline in life-threatening anaphylaxis solely because of ischaemia","administering nitrates or aggressive diuresis despite preload dependence","giving anticoagulation or thrombolysis before excluding dissection","giving fibrinolysis for presumed atherosclerotic STEMI","slowing the heart markedly or delaying surgery because the murmur is quiet"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"giving fibrinolysis for presumed atherosclerotic STEMI","explanation":"The hazardous action is giving fibrinolysis for presumed atherosclerotic STEMI. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to propagation of dissection, recurrent infarction or ventricular arrhythmia.","learningPoint":"Spontaneous coronary artery dissection: long smooth coronary narrowing or an intramural haematoma. Avoid giving fibrinolysis for presumed atherosclerotic STEMI.","id":"cardiovascular-014","globalNumber":14},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":15,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with spontaneous coronary artery dissection initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["tamponade, severe aortic regurgitation or coronary malperfusion","pulseless electrical activity arrest","extensive myocardial infarction, malignant arrhythmia and refractory shock","large anterior infarction, ventricular arrhythmia and cardiogenic shock","propagation of dissection, recurrent infarction or ventricular arrhythmia"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"propagation of dissection, recurrent infarction or ventricular arrhythmia","explanation":"The important complication is propagation of dissection, recurrent infarction or ventricular arrhythmia. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of spontaneous coronary artery dissection.","learningPoint":"Spontaneous coronary artery dissection: long smooth coronary narrowing or an intramural haematoma. Avoid giving fibrinolysis for presumed atherosclerotic STEMI.","id":"cardiovascular-015","globalNumber":15},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":16,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with abrupt tearing chest pain with pulse asymmetry, a new aortic regurgitation murmur and transient neurology. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Stanford type A aortic dissection","Acute severe aortic regurgitation","Boerhaave syndrome","Acute pericarditis with high-risk features","Aortic intramural haematoma"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Stanford type A aortic dissection","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Stanford type A aortic dissection. The key discriminator is an intimal flap involving the ascending aorta. Immediate priorities include: commence anti-impulse therapy with an intravenous beta blocker and arrange immediate cardiothoracic surgery.","learningPoint":"Stanford type A aortic dissection: an intimal flap involving the ascending aorta. Avoid giving anticoagulation or thrombolysis before excluding dissection.","id":"cardiovascular-016","globalNumber":16},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":17,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has stanford type a aortic dissection: abrupt tearing chest pain with pulse asymmetry, a new aortic regurgitation murmur and transient neurology. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["obtain urgent coronary angiography and favour conservative management if stable with preserved flow","commence anti-impulse therapy with an intravenous beta blocker and arrange immediate cardiothoracic surgery","support perfusion with afterload reduction when feasible and arrange emergency surgery","admit for monitored evaluation, anti-inflammatory treatment and assessment for specific cause or tamponade","manage as an acute aortic syndrome with anti-impulse therapy and urgent specialist input"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"commence anti-impulse therapy with an intravenous beta blocker and arrange immediate cardiothoracic surgery","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to commence anti-impulse therapy with an intravenous beta blocker and arrange immediate cardiothoracic surgery. The decisive feature is an intimal flap involving the ascending aorta. A major error is giving anticoagulation or thrombolysis before excluding dissection.","learningPoint":"Stanford type A aortic dissection: an intimal flap involving the ascending aorta. Avoid giving anticoagulation or thrombolysis before excluding dissection.","id":"cardiovascular-017","globalNumber":17},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":18,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for stanford type a aortic dissection, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["long smooth coronary narrowing or an intramural haematoma","right atrial systolic and right ventricular diastolic collapse on echocardiography","an intimal flap involving the ascending aorta","a dilated hypokinetic right ventricle with septal flattening","premature mitral valve closure and severe regurgitation with a non-dilated left ventricle"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"an intimal flap involving the ascending aorta","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is an intimal flap involving the ascending aorta. In context, this supports Stanford type A aortic dissection and should trigger: commence anti-impulse therapy with an intravenous beta blocker and arrange immediate cardiothoracic surgery.","learningPoint":"Stanford type A aortic dissection: an intimal flap involving the ascending aorta. Avoid giving anticoagulation or thrombolysis before excluding dissection.","id":"cardiovascular-018","globalNumber":18},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":19,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of stanford type a aortic dissection, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["slowing the heart markedly or delaying surgery because the murmur is quiet","discharging a patient with a large effusion or evolving haemodynamic compromise","giving fibrinolysis for presumed atherosclerotic STEMI","giving anticoagulation or thrombolysis before excluding dissection","dismissing the diagnosis because an intimal flap is absent"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"giving anticoagulation or thrombolysis before excluding dissection","explanation":"The hazardous action is giving anticoagulation or thrombolysis before excluding dissection. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to tamponade, severe aortic regurgitation or coronary malperfusion.","learningPoint":"Stanford type A aortic dissection: an intimal flap involving the ascending aorta. Avoid giving anticoagulation or thrombolysis before excluding dissection.","id":"cardiovascular-019","globalNumber":19},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":20,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with stanford type a aortic dissection initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["propagation of dissection, recurrent infarction or ventricular arrhythmia","refractory pulmonary oedema, coronary hypoperfusion and cardiogenic shock","obstructive cardiac arrest and acute right ventricular failure","tamponade, recurrent pericarditis and constrictive physiology","tamponade, severe aortic regurgitation or coronary malperfusion"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"tamponade, severe aortic regurgitation or coronary malperfusion","explanation":"The important complication is tamponade, severe aortic regurgitation or coronary malperfusion. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of stanford type a aortic dissection.","learningPoint":"Stanford type A aortic dissection: an intimal flap involving the ascending aorta. Avoid giving anticoagulation or thrombolysis before excluding dissection.","id":"cardiovascular-020","globalNumber":20},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":21,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with hypotension, raised JVP, pulsus paradoxus and electrical alternans after malignancy or pericarditis. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Cardiac tamponade","Left ventricular free-wall rupture","High-risk pulmonary embolism","Complete heart block complicating inferior myocardial infarction","Post-infarction cardiogenic shock"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Cardiac tamponade","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Cardiac tamponade. The key discriminator is right atrial systolic and right ventricular diastolic collapse on echocardiography. Immediate priorities include: perform urgent ultrasound-guided pericardial drainage while maintaining preload.","learningPoint":"Cardiac tamponade: right atrial systolic and right ventricular diastolic collapse on echocardiography. Avoid inducing positive-pressure ventilation without haemodynamic preparation.","id":"cardiovascular-021","globalNumber":21},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":22,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has cardiac tamponade: hypotension, raised JVP, pulsus paradoxus and electrical alternans after malignancy or pericarditis. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["give reperfusion therapy with systemic thrombolysis or urgent embolectomy according to contraindications","perform urgent ultrasound-guided pericardial drainage while maintaining preload","stabilise with afterload reduction and mechanical support while arranging emergency surgical repair","perform immediate resuscitation with urgent cardiothoracic surgical control and temporising pericardial drainage when appropriate","expedite revascularisation, use norepinephrine and add carefully titrated inotropy when required"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"perform urgent ultrasound-guided pericardial drainage while maintaining preload","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to perform urgent ultrasound-guided pericardial drainage while maintaining preload. The decisive feature is right atrial systolic and right ventricular diastolic collapse on echocardiography. A major error is inducing positive-pressure ventilation without haemodynamic preparation.","learningPoint":"Cardiac tamponade: right atrial systolic and right ventricular diastolic collapse on echocardiography. Avoid inducing positive-pressure ventilation without haemodynamic preparation.","id":"cardiovascular-022","globalNumber":22},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":23,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for cardiac tamponade, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["new haemopericardium with tamponade physiology after myocardial infarction","severe eccentric acute mitral regurgitation on echocardiography","right atrial systolic and right ventricular diastolic collapse on echocardiography","a dilated hypokinetic right ventricle with septal flattening","low output with pulmonary congestion and a mechanical or ischaemic cause"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"right atrial systolic and right ventricular diastolic collapse on echocardiography","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is right atrial systolic and right ventricular diastolic collapse on echocardiography. In context, this supports Cardiac tamponade and should trigger: perform urgent ultrasound-guided pericardial drainage while maintaining preload.","learningPoint":"Cardiac tamponade: right atrial systolic and right ventricular diastolic collapse on echocardiography. Avoid inducing positive-pressure ventilation without haemodynamic preparation.","id":"cardiovascular-023","globalNumber":23},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":24,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of cardiac tamponade, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["giving repeated large fluid boluses that worsen RV distension","giving an unselected large crystalloid bolus","giving thrombolysis or anticoagulation during unexplained post-infarction tamponade","inducing positive-pressure ventilation without haemodynamic preparation","giving anticoagulation or thrombolysis before excluding dissection"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"inducing positive-pressure ventilation without haemodynamic preparation","explanation":"The hazardous action is inducing positive-pressure ventilation without haemodynamic preparation. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to pulseless electrical activity arrest.","learningPoint":"Cardiac tamponade: right atrial systolic and right ventricular diastolic collapse on echocardiography. Avoid inducing positive-pressure ventilation without haemodynamic preparation.","id":"cardiovascular-024","globalNumber":24},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":25,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with cardiac tamponade initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["multiorgan failure, malignant arrhythmia and death","obstructive cardiac arrest and acute right ventricular failure","recurrent rupture, exsanguination and cardiac arrest","progression to classic dissection, rupture or malperfusion","pulseless electrical activity arrest"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"pulseless electrical activity arrest","explanation":"The important complication is pulseless electrical activity arrest. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of cardiac tamponade.","learningPoint":"Cardiac tamponade: right atrial systolic and right ventricular diastolic collapse on echocardiography. Avoid inducing positive-pressure ventilation without haemodynamic preparation.","id":"cardiovascular-025","globalNumber":25},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":26,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with sudden dyspnoea, syncope, shock and severe right ventricular strain without another cause. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["High-risk pulmonary embolism","Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy with shock","Pulmonary hypertension crisis","Critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock","Cardiac tamponade"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"High-risk pulmonary embolism","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with High-risk pulmonary embolism. The key discriminator is a dilated hypokinetic right ventricle with septal flattening. Immediate priorities include: give reperfusion therapy with systemic thrombolysis or urgent embolectomy according to contraindications.","learningPoint":"High-risk pulmonary embolism: a dilated hypokinetic right ventricle with septal flattening. Avoid giving repeated large fluid boluses that worsen RV distension.","id":"cardiovascular-026","globalNumber":26},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":27,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has high-risk pulmonary embolism: sudden dyspnoea, syncope, shock and severe right ventricular strain without another cause. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["perform urgent ultrasound-guided pericardial drainage while maintaining preload","give reperfusion therapy with systemic thrombolysis or urgent embolectomy according to contraindications","restore preload, use phenylephrine and reduce contractility with expert-guided beta blockade","maintain preload and coronary perfusion, use cautious vasopressor support and arrange urgent valve intervention","stabilise haemodynamics and arrange urgent cardiothoracic surgical assessment"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give reperfusion therapy with systemic thrombolysis or urgent embolectomy according to contraindications","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to give reperfusion therapy with systemic thrombolysis or urgent embolectomy according to contraindications. The decisive feature is a dilated hypokinetic right ventricle with septal flattening. A major error is giving repeated large fluid boluses that worsen RV distension.","learningPoint":"High-risk pulmonary embolism: a dilated hypokinetic right ventricle with septal flattening. Avoid giving repeated large fluid boluses that worsen RV distension.","id":"cardiovascular-027","globalNumber":27},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":28,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for high-risk pulmonary embolism, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["right atrial systolic and right ventricular diastolic collapse on echocardiography","apical or mid-ventricular ballooning with basal hyperkinesis and dynamic outflow obstruction","a dilated hypokinetic right ventricle with septal flattening","severely restricted aortic valve opening with a high gradient or low-flow severe stenosis","dynamic systolic anterior motion and left ventricular outflow obstruction"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a dilated hypokinetic right ventricle with septal flattening","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a dilated hypokinetic right ventricle with septal flattening. In context, this supports High-risk pulmonary embolism and should trigger: give reperfusion therapy with systemic thrombolysis or urgent embolectomy according to contraindications.","learningPoint":"High-risk pulmonary embolism: a dilated hypokinetic right ventricle with septal flattening. Avoid giving repeated large fluid boluses that worsen RV distension.","id":"cardiovascular-028","globalNumber":28},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":29,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of high-risk pulmonary embolism, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["inducing positive-pressure ventilation without haemodynamic preparation","giving nitrates, diuretics or inotropes that worsen obstruction","treating recurrent positional collapse as vasovagal syncope without echocardiography","giving repeated large fluid boluses that worsen RV distension","giving aggressive nitrates, diuretics or vasodilators despite fixed outflow obstruction"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"giving repeated large fluid boluses that worsen RV distension","explanation":"The hazardous action is giving repeated large fluid boluses that worsen RV distension. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to obstructive cardiac arrest and acute right ventricular failure.","learningPoint":"High-risk pulmonary embolism: a dilated hypokinetic right ventricle with septal flattening. Avoid giving repeated large fluid boluses that worsen RV distension.","id":"cardiovascular-029","globalNumber":29},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":30,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with high-risk pulmonary embolism initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["ventricular arrhythmia and sudden cardiac death","refractory pulmonary oedema, myocardial ischaemia and cardiac arrest","ventricular fibrillation and sudden cardiac death","pulseless electrical activity arrest","obstructive cardiac arrest and acute right ventricular failure"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"obstructive cardiac arrest and acute right ventricular failure","explanation":"The important complication is obstructive cardiac arrest and acute right ventricular failure. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of high-risk pulmonary embolism.","learningPoint":"High-risk pulmonary embolism: a dilated hypokinetic right ventricle with septal flattening. Avoid giving repeated large fluid boluses that worsen RV distension.","id":"cardiovascular-030","globalNumber":30},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":31,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with recurrent ischaemic pain with dynamic ST change, pulmonary oedema or haemodynamic instability. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Very-high-risk non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome","Left main coronary artery occlusion","Kounis syndrome","Spontaneous coronary artery dissection","Wellens syndrome"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Very-high-risk non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Very-high-risk non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome. The key discriminator is ongoing ischaemia despite medical therapy or cardiogenic shock. Immediate priorities include: arrange immediate invasive coronary angiography and revascularisation.","learningPoint":"Very-high-risk non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome: ongoing ischaemia despite medical therapy or cardiogenic shock. Avoid waiting for serial troponins before activating definitive care.","id":"cardiovascular-031","globalNumber":31},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":32,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has very-high-risk non-st-elevation acute coronary syndrome: recurrent ischaemic pain with dynamic ST change, pulmonary oedema or haemodynamic instability. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["obtain urgent coronary angiography and favour conservative management if stable with preserved flow","arrange immediate invasive coronary angiography and revascularisation","admit for urgent coronary angiography and avoid exercise stress testing","give nitrates and calcium-channel blockade while treating arrhythmia and excluding fixed occlusion","activate the primary PCI pathway as an occlusion myocardial infarction without waiting for ST elevation"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"arrange immediate invasive coronary angiography and revascularisation","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to arrange immediate invasive coronary angiography and revascularisation. The decisive feature is ongoing ischaemia despite medical therapy or cardiogenic shock. A major error is waiting for serial troponins before activating definitive care.","learningPoint":"Very-high-risk non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome: ongoing ischaemia despite medical therapy or cardiogenic shock. Avoid waiting for serial troponins before activating definitive care.","id":"cardiovascular-032","globalNumber":32},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":33,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for very-high-risk non-st-elevation acute coronary syndrome, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["ST elevation in V4R with RV dilatation","the De Winter pattern indicating acute proximal LAD occlusion","ongoing ischaemia despite medical therapy or cardiogenic shock","long smooth coronary narrowing or an intramural haematoma","angiographic or clinically documented reversible coronary spasm without fixed culprit obstruction"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"ongoing ischaemia despite medical therapy or cardiogenic shock","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is ongoing ischaemia despite medical therapy or cardiogenic shock. In context, this supports Very-high-risk non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome and should trigger: arrange immediate invasive coronary angiography and revascularisation.","learningPoint":"Very-high-risk non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome: ongoing ischaemia despite medical therapy or cardiogenic shock. Avoid waiting for serial troponins before activating definitive care.","id":"cardiovascular-033","globalNumber":33},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":34,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of very-high-risk non-st-elevation acute coronary syndrome, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["labelling the tracing as isolated subendocardial ischaemia and delaying reperfusion","administering nitrates or aggressive diuresis despite preload dependence","giving nitrates or catecholamine inotropes despite dynamic outflow obstruction","waiting for serial troponins before activating definitive care","assuming the ECG pattern alone proves left main disease while ignoring alternative shock states"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"waiting for serial troponins before activating definitive care","explanation":"The hazardous action is waiting for serial troponins before activating definitive care. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to malignant ventricular arrhythmia and cardiogenic shock.","learningPoint":"Very-high-risk non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome: ongoing ischaemia despite medical therapy or cardiogenic shock. Avoid waiting for serial troponins before activating definitive care.","id":"cardiovascular-034","globalNumber":34},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":35,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with very-high-risk non-st-elevation acute coronary syndrome initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["ventricular arrhythmia or acute mitral regurgitation","anterior myocardial infarction and sudden cardiac death","propagation of dissection, recurrent infarction or ventricular arrhythmia","complete heart block and refractory right ventricular shock","malignant ventricular arrhythmia and cardiogenic shock"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"malignant ventricular arrhythmia and cardiogenic shock","explanation":"The important complication is malignant ventricular arrhythmia and cardiogenic shock. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of very-high-risk non-st-elevation acute coronary syndrome.","learningPoint":"Very-high-risk non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome: ongoing ischaemia despite medical therapy or cardiogenic shock. Avoid waiting for serial troponins before activating definitive care.","id":"cardiovascular-035","globalNumber":35},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":36,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with fever-associated syncope with a coved ST elevation pattern in V1-V2 and no structural heart disease. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Brugada syndrome","Congenital long-QT syndrome","Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy with shock","Cardiac sarcoidosis with ventricular arrhythmia","Torsades de pointes"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Brugada syndrome","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Brugada syndrome. The key discriminator is a spontaneous type 1 Brugada ECG pattern. Immediate priorities include: aggressively treat fever, correct electrolytes and obtain urgent electrophysiology advice.","learningPoint":"Brugada syndrome: a spontaneous type 1 Brugada ECG pattern. Avoid administering flecainide or another potent sodium-channel blocker.","id":"cardiovascular-036","globalNumber":36},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":37,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has brugada syndrome: fever-associated syncope with a coved ST elevation pattern in V1-V2 and no structural heart disease. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["stabilise haemodynamics and arrange urgent cardiothoracic surgical assessment","aggressively treat fever, correct electrolytes and obtain urgent electrophysiology advice","restore preload, use phenylephrine and reduce contractility with expert-guided beta blockade","correct electrolytes, stop QT-prolonging drugs and obtain urgent specialist management with beta blockade","administer digoxin-specific antibody fragments and correct life threats"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"aggressively treat fever, correct electrolytes and obtain urgent electrophysiology advice","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to aggressively treat fever, correct electrolytes and obtain urgent electrophysiology advice. The decisive feature is a spontaneous type 1 Brugada ECG pattern. A major error is administering flecainide or another potent sodium-channel blocker.","learningPoint":"Brugada syndrome: a spontaneous type 1 Brugada ECG pattern. Avoid administering flecainide or another potent sodium-channel blocker.","id":"cardiovascular-037","globalNumber":37},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":38,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for brugada syndrome, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["angiographic or clinically documented reversible coronary spasm without fixed culprit obstruction","QRS complexes twisting around the baseline with a prolonged QT interval","a spontaneous type 1 Brugada ECG pattern","a reproducibly prolonged QTc with characteristic trigger and no reversible cause","dynamic systolic anterior motion and left ventricular outflow obstruction"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a spontaneous type 1 Brugada ECG pattern","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a spontaneous type 1 Brugada ECG pattern. In context, this supports Brugada syndrome and should trigger: aggressively treat fever, correct electrolytes and obtain urgent electrophysiology advice.","learningPoint":"Brugada syndrome: a spontaneous type 1 Brugada ECG pattern. Avoid administering flecainide or another potent sodium-channel blocker.","id":"cardiovascular-038","globalNumber":38},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":39,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of brugada syndrome, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["giving nitrates, diuretics or inotropes that worsen obstruction","reassuring the patient because the resting ECG is normal","giving additional QT-prolonging antiarrhythmics for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia","administering flecainide or another potent sodium-channel blocker","dismissing a very short QT interval as a normal variant without context"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"administering flecainide or another potent sodium-channel blocker","explanation":"The hazardous action is administering flecainide or another potent sodium-channel blocker. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to ventricular fibrillation and sudden cardiac death.","learningPoint":"Brugada syndrome: a spontaneous type 1 Brugada ECG pattern. Avoid administering flecainide or another potent sodium-channel blocker.","id":"cardiovascular-039","globalNumber":39},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":40,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with brugada syndrome initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["asystole, ventricular escape failure or shock","obstructive cardiac arrest and acute right ventricular failure","ventricular fibrillation, myocardial infarction and sudden death","torsades de pointes, ventricular fibrillation and sudden death","ventricular fibrillation and sudden cardiac death"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"ventricular fibrillation and sudden cardiac death","explanation":"The important complication is ventricular fibrillation and sudden cardiac death. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of brugada syndrome.","learningPoint":"Brugada syndrome: a spontaneous type 1 Brugada ECG pattern. Avoid administering flecainide or another potent sodium-channel blocker.","id":"cardiovascular-040","globalNumber":40},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":41,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with recurrent polymorphic ventricular tachycardia in a patient with marked QT prolongation. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Torsades de pointes","Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy","Coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia","Brugada syndrome","Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Torsades de pointes","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Torsades de pointes. The key discriminator is QRS complexes twisting around the baseline with a prolonged QT interval. Immediate priorities include: give intravenous magnesium and perform immediate defibrillation if unstable.","learningPoint":"Torsades de pointes: QRS complexes twisting around the baseline with a prolonged QT interval. Avoid giving amiodarone or another QT-prolonging drug.","id":"cardiovascular-041","globalNumber":41},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":42,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has torsades de pointes: recurrent polymorphic ventricular tachycardia in a patient with marked QT prolongation. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["use procainamide or synchronised cardioversion according to stability","give intravenous magnesium and perform immediate defibrillation if unstable","treat unstable arrhythmia, restrict exercise and obtain urgent cardiology or electrophysiology assessment","stop exertion, treat instability electrically and obtain urgent electrophysiology advice with beta blockade","combine defibrillation as required with deep sedation, sympathetic blockade and antiarrhythmic therapy"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give intravenous magnesium and perform immediate defibrillation if unstable","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to give intravenous magnesium and perform immediate defibrillation if unstable. The decisive feature is QRS complexes twisting around the baseline with a prolonged QT interval. A major error is giving amiodarone or another QT-prolonging drug.","learningPoint":"Torsades de pointes: QRS complexes twisting around the baseline with a prolonged QT interval. Avoid giving amiodarone or another QT-prolonging drug.","id":"cardiovascular-042","globalNumber":42},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":43,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for torsades de pointes, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["epsilon waves or ventricular arrhythmia with left bundle branch block morphology plus RV structural disease","atrioventricular dissociation, capture beats or fusion beats","QRS complexes twisting around the baseline with a prolonged QT interval","bidirectional or polymorphic ventricular ectopy induced by adrenergic stress","a reproducibly prolonged QTc with characteristic trigger and no reversible cause"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"QRS complexes twisting around the baseline with a prolonged QT interval","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is QRS complexes twisting around the baseline with a prolonged QT interval. In context, this supports Torsades de pointes and should trigger: give intravenous magnesium and perform immediate defibrillation if unstable.","learningPoint":"Torsades de pointes: QRS complexes twisting around the baseline with a prolonged QT interval. Avoid giving amiodarone or another QT-prolonging drug.","id":"cardiovascular-043","globalNumber":43},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":44,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of torsades de pointes, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["implanting a simple pacemaker without considering defibrillator risk or active inflammation","giving adenosine, verapamil, diltiazem or digoxin","diagnosing benign athlete changes without evaluating ventricular arrhythmia","giving amiodarone or another QT-prolonging drug","reassuring the patient because the resting ECG is normal"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"giving amiodarone or another QT-prolonging drug","explanation":"The hazardous action is giving amiodarone or another QT-prolonging drug. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to degeneration to ventricular fibrillation.","learningPoint":"Torsades de pointes: QRS complexes twisting around the baseline with a prolonged QT interval. Avoid giving amiodarone or another QT-prolonging drug.","id":"cardiovascular-044","globalNumber":44},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":45,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with torsades de pointes initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["ventricular fibrillation and sudden cardiac death","ventricular fibrillation, progressive heart block and heart failure","ventricular fibrillation through rapid accessory-pathway conduction","recurrent ventricular tachycardia, right-heart failure and sudden death","degeneration to ventricular fibrillation"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"degeneration to ventricular fibrillation","explanation":"The important complication is degeneration to ventricular fibrillation. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of torsades de pointes.","learningPoint":"Torsades de pointes: QRS complexes twisting around the baseline with a prolonged QT interval. Avoid giving amiodarone or another QT-prolonging drug.","id":"cardiovascular-045","globalNumber":45},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":46,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with an irregular very rapid broad-complex tachycardia with beat-to-beat variation in QRS morphology. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Pre-excited atrial fibrillation","Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy","Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia","Sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia","Severe digoxin toxicity"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Pre-excited atrial fibrillation","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Pre-excited atrial fibrillation. The key discriminator is an irregular wide tachycardia with very short RR intervals and variable pre-excitation. Immediate priorities include: use procainamide or synchronised cardioversion according to stability.","learningPoint":"Pre-excited atrial fibrillation: an irregular wide tachycardia with very short RR intervals and variable pre-excitation. Avoid giving adenosine, verapamil, diltiazem or digoxin.","id":"cardiovascular-046","globalNumber":46},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":47,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has pre-excited atrial fibrillation: an irregular very rapid broad-complex tachycardia with beat-to-beat variation in QRS morphology. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["stop exertion, treat instability electrically and obtain urgent electrophysiology advice with beta blockade","use procainamide or synchronised cardioversion according to stability","combine defibrillation as required with deep sedation, sympathetic blockade and antiarrhythmic therapy","give intravenous magnesium and perform immediate defibrillation if unstable","treat unstable arrhythmia, restrict exercise and obtain urgent cardiology or electrophysiology assessment"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"use procainamide or synchronised cardioversion according to stability","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to use procainamide or synchronised cardioversion according to stability. The decisive feature is an irregular wide tachycardia with very short RR intervals and variable pre-excitation. A major error is giving adenosine, verapamil, diltiazem or digoxin.","learningPoint":"Pre-excited atrial fibrillation: an irregular wide tachycardia with v