ia. In context, this supports Acute eosinophilic pneumonia and should trigger: give systemic corticosteroids after excluding major infectious mimics.","learningPoint":"Acute eosinophilic pneumonia: marked eosinophilia in bronchoalveolar lavage despite absent early blood eosinophilia. Avoid excluding the diagnosis because the peripheral eosinophil count is initially normal.","id":"respiratory-058","globalNumber":158},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":59,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of acute eosinophilic pneumonia, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["diagnosing EVALI without excluding infection or other causes of diffuse lung injury","labeling the illness as routine community-acquired pneumonia despite rapid non-response","continuing high negative-pressure suction despite worsening hypoxaemia","excluding the diagnosis because the peripheral eosinophil count is initially normal","treating only bronchospasm while missing vasculitic organ injury"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"excluding the diagnosis because the peripheral eosinophil count is initially normal","explanation":"The hazardous action is excluding the diagnosis because the peripheral eosinophil count is initially normal. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to fulminant ARDS.","learningPoint":"Acute eosinophilic pneumonia: marked eosinophilia in bronchoalveolar lavage despite absent early blood eosinophilia. Avoid excluding the diagnosis because the peripheral eosinophil count is initially normal.","id":"respiratory-059","globalNumber":159},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":60,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with acute eosinophilic pneumonia initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["respiratory failure, pneumothorax and recurrent injury with resumed vaping","bilateral 