 ventilation and carefully titrated oxygen while treating the precipitant. The decisive feature is chronic compensated respiratory acidosis with acute CO2 rise. A major error is giving uncontrolled high-flow oxygen without ventilatory support.","learningPoint":"Acute-on-chronic obesity hypoventilation: chronic compensated respiratory acidosis with acute CO2 rise. Avoid giving uncontrolled high-flow oxygen without ventilatory support.","id":"respiratory-082","globalNumber":182},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":83,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for acute-on-chronic obesity hypoventilation, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["lobar collapse with absent air bronchograms and an obstructing secretion burden","respiratory acidosis with clinical work of breathing and no immediate contraindication to non-invasive ventilation","chronic compensated respiratory acidosis with acute CO2 rise","a normalising or rising PaCO2 despite ongoing severe distress","expiratory flow failing to return to zero before the next breath"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"chronic compensated respiratory acidosis with acute CO2 rise","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is chronic compensated respiratory acidosis with acute CO2 rise. In context, this supports Acute-on-chronic obesity hypoventilation and should trigger: use non-invasive ventilation and carefully titrated oxygen while treating the precipitant.","learningPoint":"Acute-on-chronic obesity hypoventilation: chronic compensated respiratory acidosis with acute CO2 rise. Avoid giving uncontrolled high-flow oxygen without ventilatory support.","id":"respiratory-083","globalNumber":183},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":84,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of acute-on-chronic obesity hypoventilation, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive