a, incentive spirometry and early transfusion consultation. The decisive feature is a new infiltrate plus respiratory symptoms in sickle cell disease. A major error is giving excessive crystalloid that worsens pulmonary oedema.","learningPoint":"Acute chest syndrome: a new infiltrate plus respiratory symptoms in sickle cell disease. Avoid giving excessive crystalloid that worsens pulmonary oedema.","id":"respiratory-087","globalNumber":187},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":88,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for acute chest syndrome, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["a halo sign or cavitating nodules in a high-risk host","severe pneumonia with compatible urinary antigen or molecular testing and extrapulmonary features","a new infiltrate plus respiratory symptoms in sickle cell disease","delayed infectious features rather than immediate chemical injury","airway compromise from blood rather than measured blood volume alone"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a new infiltrate plus respiratory symptoms in sickle cell disease","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a new infiltrate plus respiratory symptoms in sickle cell disease. In context, this supports Acute chest syndrome and should trigger: give oxygen, antibiotics, analgesia, incentive spirometry and early transfusion consultation.","learningPoint":"Acute chest syndrome: a new infiltrate plus respiratory symptoms in sickle cell disease. Avoid giving excessive crystalloid that worsens pulmonary oedema.","id":"respiratory-088","globalNumber":188},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":89,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of acute chest syndrome, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["waiting for culture confirmation despite rapidly progressive invasive disease","withholding antibiotics despite established infection and sepsis","waiting for definitive organism confirmation in a severely hypoxaemic patient","giving excessive crystalloid that worsens pulmonary oedema","treating only bronchospasm while missing vasculitic organ injury"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"giving excessive crystalloid that worsens pulmonary oedema","explanation":"The hazardous action is giving excessive crystalloid that worsens pulmonary oedema. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to rapidly progressive respiratory failure and multiorgan dysfunction.","learningPoint":"Acute chest syndrome: a new infiltrate plus respiratory symptoms in sickle cell disease. Avoid giving excessive crystalloid that worsens pulmonary oedema.","id":"respiratory-089","globalNumber":189},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":90,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with acute chest syndrome initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["empyema, ARDS and multiorgan failure","respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis and renal injury","massive haemoptysis, dissemination and respiratory failure","right-heart failure and refractory hypoxaemia","rapidly progressive respiratory failure and multiorgan dysfunction"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"rapidly progressive respiratory failure and multiorgan dysfunction","explanation":"The important complication is rapidly progressive respiratory failure and multiorgan dysfunction. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of acute chest syndrome.","learningPoint":"Acute chest syndrome: a new infiltrate plus respiratory symptoms in sickle cell disease. Avoid giving excessive crystalloid that worsens pulmonary oedema.","id":"respiratory-090","globalNumber":190},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":91,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with acute frothy pulmonary oedema immediately after relief of laryngospas