":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with major blunt chest trauma with pneumomediastinum and a persistent air leak despite chest drainage. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Tracheobronchial disruption","Tension hydrothorax","Tracheostomy tube obstruction","Re-expansion pulmonary oedema","Adult foreign-body aspiration"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Tracheobronchial disruption","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Tracheobronchial disruption. The key discriminator is persistent pneumothorax with massive air leak or the fallen-lung sign. Immediate priorities include: secure the airway beyond the injury when possible and arrange urgent bronchoscopy and surgery.","learningPoint":"Tracheobronchial disruption: persistent pneumothorax with massive air leak or the fallen-lung sign. Avoid inserting repeated chest tubes without investigating airway disruption.","id":"respiratory-096","globalNumber":196},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":97,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has tracheobronchial disruption: major blunt chest trauma with pneumomediastinum and a persistent air leak despite chest drainage. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["maintain spontaneous ventilation when possible and obtain urgent airway and interventional-bronchoscopy planning","secure the airway beyond the injury when possible and arrange urgent bronchoscopy and surgery","stop excessive suction, support oxygenation and ventilation, and manage haemodynamics","drain the pneumothorax, continue antimicrobial therapy and obtain early thoracic advice","perform immediate finger thoracostomy followed by definitive chest drainage"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"secure the airway beyond the injury when possible and arrange urgent bronchoscopy and surgery","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to secure the airway beyond the injury when possible and arrange urge