 marked effective osmolality with little acidosis. Avoid rapidly lowering osmolality or giving early high-dose insulin before fluids.","id":"endocrine-007","globalNumber":207},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":8,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["hypotension refractory to fluid in a steroid-dependent or adrenal patient","very low urine osmolality despite hyperosmolar plasma, improving with desmopressin","marked effective osmolality with little acidosis","high measured osmolality with clinical dehydration and a free-water deficit","severe decompensated hypothyroidism with hypothermia and CO2 retention"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"marked effective osmolality with little acidosis","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is marked effective osmolality with little acidosis. In context, this supports Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state and should trigger: replace fluid gradually, correct electrolytes and start low-dose insulin after initial volume restoration.","learningPoint":"Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state: marked effective osmolality with little acidosis. Avoid rapidly lowering osmolality or giving early high-dose insulin before fluids.","id":"endocrine-008","globalNumber":208},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":9,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["stopping insulin when glucose normalises before ketonaemia clears","delaying steroids until cortisol results return","dismissing ketoacidosis because the glucose is not markedly elevated","rapidly lowering osmolality or giving early high-dose insulin before fluids","rapidly lowering chronic hypernatraemia before intravascular volume is restored"],"answerIndex