id storm","Adrenal crisis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Euglycaemic ketoacidosis from an SGLT2 inhibitor","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Euglycaemic ketoacidosis from an SGLT2 inhibitor. The key discriminator is significant ketonaemia with glucose below the usual DKA range. Immediate priorities include: stop the SGLT2 inhibitor and give insulin with dextrose plus fluids and electrolytes.","learningPoint":"Euglycaemic ketoacidosis from an SGLT2 inhibitor: significant ketonaemia with glucose below the usual DKA range. Avoid dismissing ketoacidosis because the glucose is not markedly elevated.","id":"endocrine-011","globalNumber":211},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":12,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has euglycaemic ketoacidosis from an sglt2 inhibitor: nausea, tachypnoea and high-anion-gap acidosis with ketones despite near-normal glucose. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["restore circulation with isotonic fluid, then replace free water gradually with close sodium monitoring","stop the SGLT2 inhibitor and give insulin with dextrose plus fluids and electrolytes","give beta blockade when appropriate, thionamide, iodine after thionamide, corticosteroid and supportive care","give isotonic fluid, commence fixed-rate insulin after checking potassium and replace electrolytes","replace fluid gradually, correct electrolytes and start low-dose insulin after initial volume restoration"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"stop the SGLT2 inhibitor and give insulin with dextrose plus fluids and electrolytes","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to stop the SGLT2 inhibitor and give insulin with dextrose plus fluids and electrolytes. The decisive feature is significant ketonaemia with glucose below the usual DKA range. A major error is dismissing ketoacidosis because the glucose is not markedly elevated.","learningPoint":"Euglycaemic ketoacidosis from an SGLT2 inhibitor: significa