king new hormone synthesis.","id":"endocrine-017","globalNumber":217},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":18,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for thyroid storm, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["labile hypertension with catecholamine-mediated cardiac dysfunction","hypotension refractory to fluid in a steroid-dependent or adrenal patient","severe multisystem decompensation rather than thyroid hormone level alone","hypokalaemic paralysis with suppressed TSH and no sensory or sphincter involvement","suppressed TSH and elevated thyroid hormones after a large iodine load"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"severe multisystem decompensation rather than thyroid hormone level alone","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is severe multisystem decompensation rather than thyroid hormone level alone. In context, this supports Thyroid storm and should trigger: give beta blockade when appropriate, thionamide, iodine after thionamide, corticosteroid and supportive care.","learningPoint":"Thyroid storm: severe multisystem decompensation rather than thyroid hormone level alone. Avoid administering iodine before blocking new hormone synthesis.","id":"endocrine-018","globalNumber":218},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":19,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of thyroid storm, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["delaying steroids until cortisol results return","assuming additional iodine exposure is harmless in uncontrolled thyrotoxicosis","giving isolated beta blockade before alpha blockade","administering iodine before blocking new hormone synthesis","aggressively replacing potassium without recognising intracellular shift and rebound hyperkalaemia"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"administering iodine before blocking new hormone synthesis","explanation":"The hazardous actio