":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with a neonate with temperature instability, poor feeding, lethargy or apnoea. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Neonatal sepsis","Malrotation with midgut volvulus","Duct-dependent congenital heart disease","Febrile infant younger than 28 days","Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Neonatal sepsis","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Neonatal sepsis. The key discriminator is nonspecific systemic deterioration in the first month of life. Immediate priorities include: obtain rapid cultures and give immediate age-appropriate intravenous antibiotics with supportive care.","learningPoint":"Neonatal sepsis: nonspecific systemic deterioration in the first month of life. Avoid delaying treatment because fever is absent.","id":"paediatrics-001","globalNumber":481},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":2,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has neonatal sepsis: a neonate with temperature instability, poor feeding, lethargy or apnoea. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["correct chloride and potassium depletion before definitive pyloromyotomy","obtain rapid cultures and give immediate age-appropriate intravenous antibiotics with supportive care","start prostaglandin E1 and obtain urgent cardiac support while managing apnoea risk","resuscitate and obtain immediate paediatric surgical management without delaying for prolonged imaging","give stress-dose hydrocortisone, isotonic fluid and urgent electrolyte correction"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"obtain rapid cultures and give immediate age-appropriate intravenous antibiotics with supportive care","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to obtain rapid cultures and give immediate age-appropriate intravenous antibiotics with supportive care. The decisive feature is nonspecific systemic deterioration in the first month of life. A major error is delay