he hazardous action is delaying treatment because fever is absent. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to shock, meningitis and disseminated infection.","learningPoint":"Neonatal sepsis: nonspecific systemic deterioration in the first month of life. Avoid delaying treatment because fever is absent.","id":"paediatrics-004","globalNumber":484},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":5,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with neonatal sepsis initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["apnoea, arrhythmia and aspiration","occult bacteraemia, meningitis and rapid deterioration","ductal closure, profound hypoxaemia and cardiac arrest","refractory shock, arrhythmia and death","shock, meningitis and disseminated infection"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"shock, meningitis and disseminated infection","explanation":"The important complication is shock, meningitis and disseminated infection. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of neonatal sepsis.","learningPoint":"Neonatal sepsis: nonspecific systemic deterioration in the first month of life. Avoid delaying treatment because fever is absent.","id":"paediatrics-005","globalNumber":485},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":6,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with a cyanotic or shocked neonate with differential saturations and poor response to oxygen. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Duct-dependent congenital heart disease","Neonatal sepsis","Multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children","Paediatric myocarditis","Kawasaki disease shock syndrome"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Duct-dependent congenital heart disease","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Duct-dependent congenital heart disease. The key discriminator is rapid improvement in perfusion or oxygenation after ductal reopening. Immediate priorities include: start prostaglandin E1 and obtain urgent cardiac support while managing apnoea risk.","learningPoint":"Duct-dependent congenital heart disease: rapid improvement in perfusion or oxygenation after ductal reopening. Avoid giving repeated fluid boluses while delaying prostaglandin.","id":"paediatrics-006","globalNumber":486},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":7,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has duct-dependent congenital heart disease: a cyanotic or shocked neonate with differential saturations and poor response to oxygen. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["give IVIG, aspirin and shock support with paediatric cardiology input","start prostaglandin E1 and obtain urgent cardiac support while managing apnoea risk","minimise myocardial workload, support perfusion cautiously and obtain early intensive-care and cardiology input","support shock, obtain early intensive-care and immunomodulatory specialist management","obtain rapid cultures and give immediate age-appropriate intravenous antibiotics with supportive care"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"start prostaglandin E1 and obtain urgent cardiac support while managing apnoea risk","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to start prostaglandin E1 and obtain urgent cardiac support while managing apnoea risk. The decisive feature is rapid improvement in perfusion or oxygenation after ductal reopening. A major error is giving repeated fluid boluses while delaying prostaglandin.","learningPoint":"Duct-dependent congenital heart disease: rapid improvement in perfusion or oxygenation after ductal reopening. Avoid giving repeated fluid boluses while delaying prostaglandin.","id":"paediatrics-007","globalNumber":487},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":8,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for duct-dependent congenital heart disease, which finding is the most discriminating and s