ism","giving repeated bicarbonate while delaying dialysis in refractory severe toxicity","using ethanol or supportive care alone when fomepizole and dialysis are indicated","waiting for transaminase elevation before starting antidote","assuming a normal liver profile excludes serious valproate toxicity"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"waiting for transaminase elevation before starting antidote","explanation":"The hazardous action is waiting for transaminase elevation before starting antidote. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to acute liver failure, lactic acidosis and hypoglycaemia.","learningPoint":"Paracetamol poisoning: a four-hour or later concentration above the treatment line. Avoid waiting for transaminase elevation before starting antidote.","id":"toxicology-004","globalNumber":584},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":5,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with paracetamol poisoning initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["refractory shock, arrhythmia and multiorgan failure","permanent blindness, basal ganglia injury and death","recurrent arrest and severe neurologic injury","renal failure, tetany and cerebral oedema","acute liver failure, lactic acidosis and hypoglycaemia"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"acute liver failure, lactic acidosis and hypoglycaemia","explanation":"The important complication is acute liver failure, lactic acidosis and hypoglycaemia. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of paracetamol poisoning.","learningPoint":"Paracetamol poisoning: a four-hour or later concentration above the treatment line. Avoid waiting for transaminase elevation before starting antidote.","id":"toxicology-005","globalNumber":585},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":6,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with tinnitus, tachypnoea, mixed respiratory alkalosis and metabolic 