rgic syndrome after a large black spider bite. Avoid removing the pressure bandage before resuscitation and antivenom are ready.","id":"environmental-emergency-medicine-045","globalNumber":675},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":46,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with transient arrest, neurologic deficits, tympanic rupture or branching skin marks after a strike. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Lightning injury","Exertional heat stroke","High-voltage electrical injury","Arterial gas embolism","Severe accidental hypothermia"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Lightning injury","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Lightning injury. The key discriminator is keraunoparalysis or Lichtenberg figures with a compatible event. Immediate priorities include: use reverse triage, prioritising apparently pulseless victims, and provide standard resuscitation.","learningPoint":"Lightning injury: keraunoparalysis or Lichtenberg figures with a compatible event. Avoid assuming fixed dilated pupils indicate irreversible death.","id":"environmental-emergency-medicine-046","globalNumber":676},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":47,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has lightning injury: transient arrest, neurologic deficits, tympanic rupture or branching skin marks after a strike. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["perform trauma assessment, ECG monitoring and aggressive management of rhabdomyolysis and compartment risk","use reverse triage, prioritising apparently pulseless victims, and provide standard resuscitation","give 100 percent oxygen and arrange emergency recompression","rapidly rewarm in circulating warm water, give analgesia and obtain early specialist thrombolysis or iloprost assessment","perform immediate cold-water immersion when feasible and continue until core temperature approaches 39 degrees"],"answerInd