ategoryNumber":24,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of resuscitative hysterotomy, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["waiting for radiography or relying on a small cannula that may kink","using a blind landmark approach when ultrasound is immediately available","performing the procedure after prolonged unwitnessed blunt-trauma arrest with no signs of life","delaying the procedure for fetal viability assessment, imaging or theatre transfer","accepting electrical spikes without confirming mechanical capture"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"delaying the procedure for fetal viability assessment, imaging or theatre transfer","explanation":"The hazardous action is delaying the procedure for fetal viability assessment, imaging or theatre transfer. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to maternal death from persistent aortocaval compression.","learningPoint":"Resuscitative hysterotomy: uterine decompression improves maternal venous return and CPR efficacy. Avoid delaying the procedure for fetal viability assessment, imaging or theatre transfer.","id":"emergency-medicine-procedures-024","globalNumber":794},{"category":"Emergency medicine procedures","categoryNumber":25,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with resuscitative hysterotomy initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["coronary or myocardial injury, dysrhythmia and recurrent tamponade","staff injury, uncontrolled haemorrhage and futile resource use","unresolved tension pneumothorax, bleeding and lung injury","extravasation, compartment syndrome and osteomyelitis","maternal death from persistent aortocaval compression"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"maternal death from persistent aortocaval compression","explanation":"The important complication is maternal death from persistent aortocaval compression. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of resuscitative hysterotomy.","learningPoint":"Resuscitative hysterotomy: uterine decompression improves maternal venous return and CPR efficacy. Avoid delaying the procedure for fetal viability assessment, imaging or theatre transfer.","id":"emergency-medicine-procedures-025","globalNumber":795},{"category":"Emergency medicine procedures","categoryNumber":26,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with acute visual loss, proptosis and a tense orbit after trauma or retrobulbar haemorrhage. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Lateral canthotomy and cantholysis","Ultrasound-guided suprapubic catheterisation","Finger thoracostomy","Emergency escharotomy","Emergency cricothyrotomy"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Lateral canthotomy and cantholysis","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Lateral canthotomy and cantholysis. The key discriminator is reduced visual acuity, afferent pupillary defect and high intraocular pressure. Immediate priorities include: perform immediate lateral canthotomy with inferior cantholysis when orbital compartment syndrome is present.","learningPoint":"Lateral canthotomy and cantholysis: reduced visual acuity, afferent pupillary defect and high intraocular pressure. Avoid waiting for CT or ophthalmology arrival before decompression.","id":"emergency-medicine-procedures-026","globalNumber":796},{"category":"Emergency medicine procedures","categoryNumber":27,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has lateral canthotomy and cantholysis: acute visual loss, proptosis and a tense orbit after trauma or retrobulbar haemorrhage. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["perform a scalpel-finger-bougie cricothyrotomy without further laryngoscopy attempts","perform immediate lateral canthotomy with inferior cantholysis when orbital compartment syndrome is present","insert under sterile ultrasound guidance and confirm urine drainage before securing","incise through the full eschar along safe anatomical lines and reassess perfusion or ventilation","open the pleural space immediately with blunt finger dissection, then place a definitive drain"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"perform immediate lateral canthotomy with inferior cantholysis when orbital compartment syndrome is present","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to perform immediate lateral canthotomy with inferior cantholysis when orbital compartment syndrome is present. The decisive feature is reduced visual acuity, afferent pupillary defect and high intraocular pressure. A major error is waiting for CT or ophthalmology arrival before decompression.","learningPoint":"Lateral canthotomy and cantholysis: reduced visual acuity, afferent pupillary defect and high intraocular pressure. Avoid waiting for CT or ophthalmology arrival before decompression.","id":"emergency-medicine-procedures-027","globalNumber":797},{"category":"Emergency medicine procedures","categoryNumber":28,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for lateral canthotomy and cantholysis, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["rising compartment pressure or ventilatory restriction from a non-compliant burn eschar","immediate release of air and improved ventilation or circulation","reduced visual acuity, afferent pupillary defect and high intraocular pressure","inability to oxygenate despite optimised upper-airway techniques","a clearly visualised full bladder with a safe percutaneous path above the pubic symphysis"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"reduced visual acuity, afferent pupillary defect and high intraocular pressure","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is reduced visual acuity, afferent pupillary defect and high intraocular pressure. In context, this supports Lateral canthotomy and cantholysis and should trigger: perform immediate lateral canthotomy with inferior cantholysis when orbital compartment syndrome is present.","learningPoint":"Lateral canthotomy and cantholysis: reduced visual acuity, afferent pupillary defect and high intraocular pressure. Avoid waiting for CT or ophthalmology arrival before decompression.","id":"emergency-medicine-procedures-028","globalNumber":798},{"category":"Emergency medicine procedures","categoryNumber":29,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of lateral canthotomy and cantholysis, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["blind insertion into an inadequately filled bladder or through bowel-containing lower abdomen","waiting for absent pulses or performing an incomplete superficial incision","persisting with repeated intubation attempts after CICO is declared","waiting for CT or ophthalmology arrival before decompression","waiting for radiography or relying on a small cannula that may kink"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"waiting for CT or ophthalmology arrival before decompression","explanation":"The hazardous action is waiting for CT or ophthalmology arrival before decompression. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to irreversible optic nerve ischaemia and blindness.","learningPoint":"Lateral canthotomy and cantholysis: reduced visual acuity, afferent pupillary defect and high intraocular pressure. Avoid waiting for CT or ophthalmology arrival before decompression.","id":"emergency-medicine-procedures-029","globalNumber":799},{"category":"Emergency medicine procedures","categoryNumber":30,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with lateral canthotomy and cantholysis initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["limb ischaemia, respiratory failure and compartment syndrome","hypoxic brain injury, false passage and haemorrhage","unresolved tension pneumothorax, bleeding and lung injury","bowel injury, haemorrhage and urinary peritonitis","irreversible optic nerve ischaemia and blindness"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"irreversible optic nerve ischaemia and blindness","explanation":"The important complication is irreversible optic nerve ischaemia and blindness. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of lateral canthotomy and cantholysis.","learningPoint":"Lateral canthotomy and cantholysis: reduced visual acuity, afferent pupillary defect and high intraocular pressure. Avoid waiting for CT or ophthalmology arrival before decompression.","id":"emergency-medicine-procedures-030","globalNumber":800},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":1,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with blunt trauma with shock, pelvic instability and no intrathoracic source. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Exsanguinating pelvic ring injury","Traumatic adrenal haemorrhage with adrenal crisis","Morel-Lavallée closed degloving injury","Traumatic tension pneumothorax","Renal pedicle avulsion"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Exsanguinating pelvic ring injury","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Exsanguinating pelvic ring injury. The key discriminator is haemodynamic instability with an open-book or vertical-shear pelvic pattern. Immediate priorities include: apply a pelvic binder over the greater trochanters, activate massive transfusion and arrange embolisation or packing.","learningPoint":"Exsanguinating pelvic ring injury: haemodynamic instability with an open-book or vertical-shear pelvic pattern. Avoid repeatedly log-rolling or placing the binder over the iliac crests.","id":"trauma-001","globalNumber":801},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":2,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has exsanguinating pelvic ring injury: blunt trauma with shock, pelvic instability and no intrathoracic source. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["perform immediate surgical decompression or resuscitative thoracotomy according to setting and expertise","apply a pelvic binder over the greater trochanters, activate massive transfusion and arrange embolisation or packing","control bleeding with direct pressure or tourniquet, resuscitate and preserve the part cool and dry","give stress-dose corticosteroid while continuing trauma resuscitation","continue damage-control resuscitation and obtain immediate specialist operative control"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"apply a pelvic binder over the greater trochanters, activate massive transfusion and arrange embolisation or packing","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to apply a pelvic binder over the greater trochanters, activate massive transfusion and arrange embolisation or packing. The decisive feature is haemodynamic instability with an open-book or vertical-shear pelvic pattern. A major error is repeatedly log-rolling or placing the binder over the iliac crests.","learningPoint":"Exsanguinating pelvic ring injury: haemodynamic instability with an open-book or vertical-shear pelvic pattern. Avoid repeatedly log-rolling or placing the binder over the iliac crests.","id":"trauma-002","globalNumber":802},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":3,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for exsanguinating pelvic ring injury, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["retroperitoneal gas or contrast leak after a handlebar or seatbelt mechanism","systemic toxicity after reperfusion of crushed muscle","haemodynamic instability with an open-book or vertical-shear pelvic pattern","pericardial fluid with right-sided collapse in a compatible mechanism","bilateral adrenal haemorrhage with haemodynamic features not explained by bleeding alone"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"haemodynamic instability with an open-book or vertical-shear pelvic pattern","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is haemodynamic instability with an open-book or vertical-shear pelvic pattern. In context, this supports Exsanguinating pelvic ring injury and should trigger: apply a pelvic binder over the greater trochanters, activate massive transfusion and arrange embolisation or packing.","learningPoint":"Exsanguinating pelvic ring injury: haemodynamic instability with an open-book or vertical-shear pelvic pattern. Avoid repeatedly log-rolling or placing the binder over the iliac crests.","id":"trauma-003","globalNumber":803},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":4,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of exsanguinating pelvic ring injury, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["waiting for endocrine testing before treating suspected adrenal failure","reassuring from minimal haematuria despite a high-risk mechanism","clamping a functioning drain in an unstable bleeding patient without a surgical plan","repeatedly log-rolling or placing the binder over the iliac crests","forcing a urethral catheter through resistance after pelvic trauma"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"repeatedly log-rolling or placing the binder over the iliac crests","explanation":"The hazardous action is repeatedly log-rolling or placing the binder over the iliac crests. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to uncontrolled retroperitoneal haemorrhage and coagulopathy.","learningPoint":"Exsanguinating pelvic ring injury: haemodynamic instability with an open-book or vertical-shear pelvic pattern. Avoid repeatedly log-rolling or placing the binder over the iliac crests.","id":"trauma-004","globalNumber":804},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":5,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with exsanguinating pelvic ring injury initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be s