":"A patient has blunt traumatic aortic injury: high-energy deceleration with chest pain, pulse discrepancy or mediastinal abnormality. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["correct reversible traumatic causes immediately with blood, haemorrhage control and bilateral thoracostomies","control impulse with beta blockade when safe and obtain urgent CT angiography and vascular repair planning","monitor continuously, treat dysrhythmia and shock, and obtain echocardiography when unstable","start immediate CPR and defibrillate as early as possible","activate massive transfusion and obtain immediate operative or resuscitative thoracotomy control"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"control impulse with beta blockade when safe and obtain urgent CT angiography and vascular repair planning","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to control impulse with beta blockade when safe and obtain urgent CT angiography and vascular repair planning. The decisive feature is focal injury at the aortic isthmus with contour abnormality. A major error is aggressive hypertension or delaying imaging because the chest radiograph is normal.","learningPoint":"Blunt traumatic aortic injury: focal injury at the aortic isthmus with contour abnormality. Avoid aggressive hypertension or delaying imaging because the chest radiograph is normal.","id":"trauma-007","globalNumber":807},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":8,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for blunt traumatic aortic injury, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["a potentially reversible mechanical or haemorrhagic mechanism","instantaneous ventricular fibrillation after impact during the vulnerable phase of repolarisation without structural injury","focal injury at the aortic isthmus with contour abnormality","cardiac displacement or torsion through a pericardial tear","pericardial fluid with right-sided collapse in a compatible mechanism"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"focal injury at the aortic isthmus with contour abnormality","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is focal injury at the aortic isthmus with contour abnormality. In context, this supports Blunt traumatic aortic injury and should trigger: control impulse with beta blockade when safe and obtain urgent CT angiography and vascular repair planning.","learningPoint":"Blunt traumatic aortic injury: focal injury at the aortic isthmus with contour abnormality. Avoid aggressive hypertension or delaying imaging because the chest radiograph is normal.","id":"trauma-008","globalNumber":808},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":9,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of blunt traumatic aortic injury, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["using a normal initial troponin or chest radiograph alone to exclude clinically important injury","attributing shock solely to tension pneumothorax despite persistent collapse after decompression","sending an unstable patient for CT before surgical haemorrhage control","aggressive hypertension or delaying imaging because the chest radiograph is normal","assuming myocardial injury is only cardiac contusion and ignoring territorial ischaemia"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"aggressive hypertension or delaying imaging because the chest radiograph is normal","explanation":"The hazardous action is aggressive hypertension or delaying imaging because the chest radiograph is normal. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to free rupture, stroke and distal malperfusion.","learningPoint":"Blunt traumatic aortic injury: focal injury at the aortic isthmus with contour abnormality. Avoid aggressive hypertension or delaying imaging because the chest radiograph is normal.","id":"trauma-009","globalNumber":809},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":10,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with blunt traumatic aortic injury initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["tamponade, exsanguination and cardiac arrest","myocardial infarction, ventricular arrhythmia and cardiogenic shock","ventricular arrhythmia, cardiac failure and delayed rupture","great-vessel torsion, obstructive shock and death","free rupture, stroke and distal malperfusion"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"free rupture, stroke and distal malperfusion","explanation":"The important complication is free rupture, stroke and distal malperfusion. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of blunt traumatic aortic injury.","learningPoint":"Blunt traumatic aortic injury: focal injury at the aortic isthmus with contour abnormality. Avoid aggressive hypertension or delaying imaging because the chest radiograph is normal.","id":"trauma-010","globalNumber":810},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":11,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with shocked trauma patient with unilateral absent breath sounds and rapidly worsening ventilation. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Traumatic tension pneumothorax","Severe hepatic venous injury","Traumatic cardiac arrest","Massive haemothorax","Traumatic cardiac tamponade"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Traumatic tension pneumothorax","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Traumatic tension pneumothorax. The key discriminator is obstructive shock that improves after pleural decompression. Immediate priorities include: perform immediate finger thoracostomy followed by chest drainage.","learningPoint":"Traumatic tension pneumothorax: obstructive shock that improves after pleural decompression. Avoid waiting for ultrasound or radiography when clinical tension is evident.","id":"trauma-011","globalNumber":811},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":12,"type":"ma