ation":"The most discriminating finding is crescentic blood crossing suture lines with underlying brain injury. In context, this supports Acute subdural haematoma and should trigger: reverse anticoagulation, optimise cerebral perfusion and seek urgent neurosurgical evacuation when indicated.","learningPoint":"Acute subdural haematoma: crescentic blood crossing suture lines with underlying brain injury. Avoid delaying reversal of anticoagulation until surgery is confirmed.","id":"trauma-048","globalNumber":848},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":49,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of acute subdural haematoma, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["attempting blind nasotracheal intubation with suspected skull-base injury","focusing on the dramatic facial appearance while missing underlying chest trauma","routine prolonged hyperventilation or allowing even a single hypotensive episode","delaying reversal of anticoagulation until surgery is confirmed","observing a deteriorating patient because the initial GCS was normal"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"delaying reversal of anticoagulation until surgery is confirmed","explanation":"The hazardous action is delaying reversal of anticoagulation until surgery is confirmed. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to cerebral oedema, seizure and herniation.","learningPoint":"Acute subdural haematoma: crescentic blood crossing suture lines with underlying brain injury. Avoid delaying reversal of anticoagulation until surgery is confirmed.","id":"trauma-049","globalNumber":849},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":50,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with acute subdural haematoma initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["brainstem injury, respiratory arrest and death","cerebral oedema, retinal injury and respirato