y despite no bleeding source. In context, this supports Neurogenic shock from cervical spinal cord injury and should trigger: maintain spinal alignment, support perfusion with norepinephrine and exclude haemorrhage.","learningPoint":"Neurogenic shock from cervical spinal cord injury: vasodilation and bradycardia after cord injury despite no bleeding source. Avoid assuming all hypotension is neurogenic before excluding occult haemorrhage.","id":"trauma-053","globalNumber":853},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":54,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of neurogenic shock from cervical spinal cord injury, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["repeated angiographic embolisation for predominantly venous exsanguination","confusing areflexia with distributive circulatory shock","applying traction or repeated airway manipulation without recognising instability","assuming all hypotension is neurogenic before excluding occult haemorrhage","following prolonged medical ALS while delaying HOTT interventions"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"assuming all hypotension is neurogenic before excluding occult haemorrhage","explanation":"The hazardous action is assuming all hypotension is neurogenic before excluding occult haemorrhage. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to spinal cord ischaemia, hypothermia and organ hypoperfusion.","learningPoint":"Neurogenic shock from cervical spinal cord injury: vasodilation and bradycardia after cord injury despite no bleeding source. Avoid assuming all hypotension is neurogenic before excluding occult haemorrhage.","id":"trauma-054","globalNumber":854},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":55,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with neurogenic shock from cervical spinal cord injury initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["pressure in