":4,"answer":"pressure injury, venous thrombosis and delayed recognition of evolving cord injury","explanation":"The important complication is pressure injury, venous thrombosis and delayed recognition of evolving cord injury. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of spinal shock.","learningPoint":"Spinal shock: transient areflexia including absent bulbocavernosus reflex. Avoid confusing areflexia with distributive circulatory shock.","id":"trauma-060","globalNumber":860},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":61,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with a large sucking chest wound with respiratory distress after penetrating trauma. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Open pneumothorax","Atlanto-occipital dissociation","Impending herniation after severe traumatic brain injury","Flail chest with pulmonary contusion","Occult traumatic pneumothorax before positive-pressure ventilation"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Open pneumothorax","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Open pneumothorax. The key discriminator is an open chest-wall defect impairing ventilation. Immediate priorities include: apply a vented occlusive dressing and place a chest tube away from the wound.","learningPoint":"Open pneumothorax: an open chest-wall defect impairing ventilation. Avoid sealing all four sides without a vent or drain and creating tension.","id":"trauma-061","globalNumber":861},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":62,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has open pneumothorax: a large sucking chest wound with respiratory distress after penetrating trauma. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["give multimodal analgesia, oxygen or ventilatory support and manage associated contusion","apply a vented occlusive dressing and place a chest tube away from the wound","secure oxygenation, prevent hypotension, give hyperosmolar t