 pneumothorax, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["mistaking the acute cavity for infection and delaying trauma management","attempting blind nasotracheal intubation with suspected skull-base injury","applying traction or repeated airway manipulation without recognising instability","sealing all four sides without a vent or drain and creating tension","excessive crystalloid or obligatory intubation based only on the flail segment"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"sealing all four sides without a vent or drain and creating tension","explanation":"The hazardous action is sealing all four sides without a vent or drain and creating tension. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to tension pneumothorax and severe hypoxaemia.","learningPoint":"Open pneumothorax: an open chest-wall defect impairing ventilation. Avoid sealing all four sides without a vent or drain and creating tension.","id":"trauma-064","globalNumber":864},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":65,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with open pneumothorax initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["delayed respiratory failure, pneumonia and ARDS","cerebral oedema, retinal injury and respiratory failure","transtentorial herniation and secondary brain injury","massive haemoptysis, persistent air leak and infection","tension pneumothorax and severe hypoxaemia"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"tension pneumothorax and severe hypoxaemia","explanation":"The important complication is tension pneumothorax and severe hypoxaemia. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of open pneumothorax.","learningPoint":"Open pneumothorax: an open chest-wall defect impairing ventilation. Avoid sealing all four sides without a vent or drain and creating tension.","id":"trauma-065","globalNumber":865},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":