 is to provide supportive respiratory care and stabilise fractures early. The decisive feature is triad of respiratory, neurologic and petechial findings after marrow trauma. A major error is attributing hypoxaemia solely to pulmonary embolism without considering timing and rash.","learningPoint":"Fat embolism syndrome: triad of respiratory, neurologic and petechial findings after marrow trauma. Avoid attributing hypoxaemia solely to pulmonary embolism without considering timing and rash.","id":"orthopaedic-097","globalNumber":1147},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":98,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for fat embolism syndrome, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["severe pain and sensory change despite preserved pulses","a femoral shaft fracture capable of substantial concealed blood loss","triad of respiratory, neurologic and petechial findings after marrow trauma","absent pulse or poor perfusion after an extension-type fracture","talar displacement compromising soft tissue and blood supply"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"triad of respiratory, neurologic and petechial findings after marrow trauma","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is triad of respiratory, neurologic and petechial findings after marrow trauma. In context, this supports Fat embolism syndrome and should trigger: provide supportive respiratory care and stabilise fractures early.","learningPoint":"Fat embolism syndrome: triad of respiratory, neurologic and petechial findings after marrow trauma. Avoid attributing hypoxaemia solely to pulmonary embolism without considering timing and rash.","id":"orthopaedic-098","globalNumber":1148},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":99,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of fat embolism syndrome, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["waiting for