tal growth restriction","liver failure, disseminated coagulopathy and fetal death","thrombosis, renal failure and respiratory compromise","pulmonary oedema, arrhythmia and thromboembolism","hepatic rupture, DIC and placental abruption"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"hepatic rupture, DIC and placental abruption","explanation":"The important complication is hepatic rupture, DIC and placental abruption. Ongoing reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of hellp syndrome.","learningPoint":"HELLP syndrome: microangiopathic haemolysis with hepatic injury and low platelets. Avoid assuming normal blood pressure excludes the syndrome.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-030","globalNumber":1280},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":31,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with painful vaginal bleeding, uterine tenderness and fetal distress. Which diagnosis best accounts for the complete pattern?","options":["Placental abruption","Eclampsia","Uterine rupture","Pregnancy-associated spontaneous coronary artery dissection","Placenta accreta spectrum haemorrhage"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Placental abruption","explanation":"The vignette is most consistent with Placental abruption. The key discriminator is painful bleeding with a tense hypertonic uterus. Immediate priorities include: resuscitate, correct coagulopathy and expedite delivery according to maternal and fetal status.","learningPoint":"Placental abruption: painful bleeding with a tense hypertonic uterus. Avoid performing digital vaginal examination before placenta previa is excluded.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-031","globalNumber":1281},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":32,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has placental abruption: painful vaginal bleeding, uterine tenderness and fetal distress. Which is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["activate massive transfusion and obtain immediate senior obstetric and surgical haemorrhage control","resuscitate, correct coagulopathy and expedite delivery according to maternal and fetal status","treat acute heart failure with pregnancy-compatible therapy and obtain urgent cardiology and obstetric input","activate immediate laparotomy and massive transfusion","provide immediate advanced cardiopulmonary support, massive transfusion and expedite delivery if undelivered"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"resuscitate, correct coagulopathy and expedite delivery according to maternal and fetal status","explanation":"The best immediate approach is to resuscitate, correct coagulopathy and expedite delivery according to maternal and fetal status. The decisive feature is painful bleeding with a tense hypertonic uterus. A major error is performing digital vaginal examination before placenta previa is excluded.","learningPoint":"Placental abruption: painful bleeding with a tense hypertonic uterus. Avoid performing digital vaginal examination before placenta previa is excluded.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-032","globalNumber":1282},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":33,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient being assessed for placental abruption, which finding is the most discriminating and should carry the greatest diagnostic weight?","options":["abrupt fetal compromise with cessation of contractions or palpable fetal parts","reduced ejection fraction without another cause near the end of pregnancy","painful bleeding with a tense hypertonic uterus","abnormally adherent or invasive placenta with absent normal cleavage plane","empty endometrial cavity with gestational tissue in the caesarean scar and thin anterior myometrium"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"painful bleeding with a tense hypertonic uterus","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is painful bleeding with a tense hypertonic uterus. In context, this supports Placental abruption and should trigger: resuscitate, correct coagulopathy and expedite delivery according to maternal and fetal status.","learningPoint":"Placental abruption: painful bleeding with a tense hypertonic uterus. Avoid performing digital vaginal examination before placenta previa is excluded.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-033","globalNumber":1283},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":34,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of placental abruption, which action is specifically hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["performing blind curettage without recognising scar implantation","attributing orthopnoea and oedema to normal pregnancy","delaying for confirmatory imaging","performing digital vaginal examination before placenta previa is excluded","forcefully removing a firmly adherent placenta"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"performing digital vaginal examination before placenta previa is excluded","explanation":"The hazardous action is performing digital vaginal examination before placenta previa is excluded. It can worsen physiology, delay definitive treatment and contribute to DIC, fetal death and haemorrhagic shock.","learningPoint":"Placental abruption: painful bleeding with a tense hypertonic uterus. Avoid performing digital vaginal examination before placenta previa is excluded.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-034","globalNumber":1284},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":35,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with placental abruption initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated and monitored for?","options":["maternal and fetal death from prolonged low-flow state","pulmonary oedema, arrhythmia and thromboembolism","maternal exsanguination and fetal death","maternal infarction, arrhythmia and cardiogenic shock","DIC, fetal death and haemorrhagic shock"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"DIC, fetal death and haemorrhagic shock","explanati