2,"answer":"the De Winter pattern indicating acute proximal LAD occlusion","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is the De Winter pattern indicating acute proximal LAD occlusion. In context, this supports De Winter anterior occlusion myocardial infarction and should prompt clinicians to activate the primary PCI pathway as an occlusion myocardial infarction without waiting for ST elevation.","learningPoint":"De Winter anterior occlusion myocardial infarction: the De Winter pattern indicating acute proximal LAD occlusion. Avoid treating the tracing as low-risk non-ST-elevation ACS and delaying reperfusion.","id":"cardiovascular-103","globalNumber":1353,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":104,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of de winter anterior occlusion myocardial infarction, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["giving fibrinolysis for presumed atherosclerotic STEMI","assuming the ECG pattern alone proves left main disease while ignoring alternative shock states","withholding intramuscular adrenaline in life-threatening anaphylaxis solely because of ischaemia","treating the tracing as low-risk non-ST-elevation ACS and delaying reperfusion","waiting for serial troponins before activating definitive care"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"treating the tracing as low-risk non-ST-elevation ACS and delaying reperfusion","explanation":"The hazardous action is treating the tracing as low-risk non-ST-elevation ACS and delaying reperfusion. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is large anterior infarction, ventricular arrhythmia and cardiogenic shock.","learningPoint":"De Winter anterior occlusion myocardial infarction: the De Winter pattern indicating acute proximal LAD occlusion. Avoid treating the tracing as low-risk non-ST-elevation ACS and delaying reperfusion.","id":"cardiovascular-104","globalNumber":