ction and mechanical support while arranging emergency surgical repair. The decisive feature is a new left-to-right shunt with step-up in right-sided oxygen saturation or septal defect on echocardiography. A major error is attributing the murmur and shock to uncomplicated infarct-related heart failure.","learningPoint":"Post-infarction ventricular septal rupture: a new left-to-right shunt with step-up in right-sided oxygen saturation or septal defect on echocardiography. Avoid attributing the murmur and shock to uncomplicated infarct-related heart failure.","id":"cardiovascular-117","globalNumber":1367,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":118,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for post-infarction ventricular septal rupture in this presentation?","options":["severe eccentric acute mitral regurgitation on echocardiography","independent atrial and ventricular activity with a slow escape rhythm","a new left-to-right shunt with step-up in right-sided oxygen saturation or septal defect on echocardiography","low output with pulmonary congestion and a mechanical or ischaemic cause","ST elevation in V4R with RV dilatation"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a new left-to-right shunt with step-up in right-sided oxygen saturation or septal defect on echocardiography","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a new left-to-right shunt with step-up in right-sided oxygen saturation or septal defect on echocardiography. In context, this supports Post-infarction ventricular septal rupture and should prompt clinicians to stabilise with afterload reduction and mechanical support while arranging emergency surgical repair.","learningPoint":"Post-infarction ventricular septal rupture: a new left-to-right shunt with step-up in right-sided oxygen saturation or septal defect on echocardiography. Avoid attributing the murmur and shock to uncomplicated infarct-related heart failure.","id":"cardiovascular-118","globalNumber":1368,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":119,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in post-infarction ventricular septal rupture?","options":["inducing positive-pressure ventilation without haemodynamic preparation","giving an unselected large crystalloid bolus","relying on repeated atropine despite persistent instability","attributing the murmur and shock to uncomplicated infarct-related heart failure","treating only with diuresis and delaying surgical referral"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"attributing the murmur and shock to uncomplicated infarct-related heart failure","explanation":"The hazardous action is attributing the murmur and shock to uncomplicated infarct-related heart failure. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is rapid biventricular failure, multiorgan shock and death.","learningPoint":"Post-infarction ventricular septal rupture: a new left-to-right shunt with step-up in right-sided oxygen saturation or septal defect on echocardiography. Avoid attributing the murmur and shock to uncomplicated infarct-related heart failure.","id":"cardiovascular-119","globalNumber":1369,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":120,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in post-infarction ventricular septal rupture, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["refractory pulmonary oedema and cardiogenic shock","pulseless electrical activity arrest","recurrent rupture, exsanguination and cardiac arrest","asystole, ventricular escape failure or shock","rapid biventricular failure, multiorgan shock and death"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"rapid biventricular failure, multiorgan shock and death","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is rapid biventricular failure, multiorgan shock and death. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of post-infarction ventricular septal rupture.","learningPoint":"Post-infarction ventricular septal rupture: a new left-to-right shunt with step-up in right-sided oxygen saturation or septal defect on echocardiography. Avoid attributing the murmur and shock to uncomplicated infarct-related heart failure.","id":"cardiovascular-120","globalNumber":1370,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":121,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with sudden collapse with pulseless electrical activity and pericardial effusion after a recent transmural infarction. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Left ventricular free-wall rupture","Takotsubo syndrome with dynamic LV outflow obstruction","Post-infarction cardiogenic shock","Post-infarction ventricular septal rupture","Cardiac tamponade"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Left ventricular free-wall rupture","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Left ventricular free-wall rupture. The key discriminator is new haemopericardium with tamponade physiology after myocardial infarction. Immediate priorities include: perform immediate resuscitation with urgent cardiothoracic surgical control and temporising pericardial drainage when appropriate.","learningPoint":"Left ventricular free-wall rupture: new haemopericardium with tamponade physiology after myocardial infarction. Avoid giving thrombolysis or anticoagulation during unexplained post-infarction tamponade.","id":"cardiovascular-121","globalNumber":1371,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":122,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has left ventricular free-wall rupture: sudden collapse with pulseless electrical activity and pericardial effusion after a recent transmural infarction. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["stabilise with afterload reduction and mechanical support while arranging emergency surgery","perform immediate resuscitation with urgent cardiothoracic surgical control and temporising pericardial drainage when appropriate","perform urgent ultrasound-guided pericardial drainage while maintaining preload","expedite revascularisation, use norepinephrine and add carefully titrated inotropy when required","provide shock support, avoid harmful catecholamine escalation and obtain early mechanical-circulatory-support advice"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"perform immediate resuscitation with urgent cardiothoracic surgical control and temporising pericardial drainage when appropriate","explanation":"The priority is to perform immediate resuscitation with urgent cardiothoracic surgical control and temporising pericardial drainage when appropriate. The decisive feature is new haemopericardium with tamponade physiology after myocardial infarction. A major error is giving thrombolysis or anticoagulation during unexplained post-infarction tamponade.","learningPoint":"Left ventricular free-wall rupture: new haemopericardium with tamponade physiology after myocardial infarction. Avoid giving thrombolysis or anticoagulation during unexplained post-infarction tamponade.","id":"cardiovascular-122","globalNumber":1372,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":123,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected left ventricular free-wall rupture, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["low output with pulmonary congestion and a mechanical or ischaemic cause","apical or mid-ventricular ballooning with basal hyperkinesis and dynamic outflow obstruction","new haemopericardium with tamponade physiology after myocardial infarction","severe global ventricular dysfunction with myocardial injury despite unobstructed coronary arteries","right atrial systolic and right ventricular diastolic collapse on echocardiography"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"new haemopericardium with tamponade physiology after myocardial infarction","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is new haemopericardium with tamponade physiology after myocardial infarction. In context, this supports Left ventricular free-wall rupture and should prompt clinicians to perform immediate resuscitation with urgent cardiothoracic surgical control and temporising pericardial drainage when appropriate.","learningPoint":"Left ventricular free-wall rupture: new haemopericardium with tamponade physiology after myocardial infarction. Avoid giving thrombolysis or anticoagulation during unexplained post-infarction tamponade.","id":"cardiovascular-123","globalNumber":1373,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":124,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of left ventricular free-wall rupture, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["inducing positive-pressure ventilation without haemodynamic preparation","giving nitrates, diuretics or inotropes that worsen obstruction","giving an unselected large crystalloid bolus","giving thrombolysis or anticoagulation during unexplained post-infarction tamponade","giving nitrates or catecholamine inotropes despite dynamic outflow obstruction"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"giving thrombolysis or anticoagulation during unexplained post-infarction tamponade","explanation":"The hazardous action is giving thrombolysis or anticoagulation during unexplained post-infarction tamponade. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is recurrent rupture, exsanguination and cardiac arrest.","learningPoint":"Left ventricular free-wall rupture: new haemopericardium with tamponade physiology after myocardial infarction. Avoid giving thrombolysis or anticoagulation during unexplained post-infarction tamponade.","id":"cardiovascular-124","globalNumber":1374,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":125,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with left ventricular free-wall rupture initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["complete heart block and refractory right ventricular shock","multiorgan failure, malignant arrhythmia and death","ventricular arrhythmia, apical thrombus and cardiogenic shock","pulseless electrical activity arrest","recurrent rupture, exsanguination and cardiac arrest"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"recurrent rupture, exsanguination and cardiac arrest","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is recurrent rupture, exsanguination and cardiac arrest. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of left ventricular free-wall rupture.","learningPoint":"Left ventricular free-wall rupture: new haemopericardium with tamponade physiology after myocardial infarction. Avoid giving thrombolysis or anticoagulation during unexplained post-infarction tamponade.","id":"cardiovascular-125","globalNumber":1375,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":126,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with a previously well patient with viral prodrome, rapidly progressive heart failure, arrhythmia and cardiogenic shock?","options":["Fulminant myocarditis","Critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock","High-risk pulmonary embolism","Cardiac tamponade","Takotsubo syndrome with dynamic LV outflow obstruction"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Fulminant myocarditis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Fulminant myocarditis. The key discriminator is severe global ventricular dysfunction with myocardial injury despite unobstructed coronary arteries. Immediate priorities include: provide shock support, avoid harmful catecholamine escalation and obtain early mechanical-circulatory-support advice.","learningPoint":"Fulminant myocarditis: severe global ventricular dysfunction with myocardial injury despite unobstructed coronary arteries. Avoid assuming all ST elevation with shock is coronary occlusion without considering myocarditis.","id":"cardiovascular-126","globalNumber":1376,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":127,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with fulminant myocarditis presenting with a previously well patient with viral prodrome, rapidly progressive heart failure, arrhythmia and cardiogenic shock, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["give reperfusion therapy with systemic thrombolysis or urgent embolectomy according to contraindications","provide shock support, avoid harmful catecholamine escalation and obtain early mechanical-circulatory-support advice","expedite revascularisation, use norepinephrine and add carefully titrated inotropy when required","support preload, avoid inotropes that worsen obstruction and use specialist-guided vasopressor or mechanical support","maintain preload and coronary perfusion, use cautious vasopressor support and arrange urgent valve intervention"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"provide shock support, avoid harmful catecholamine escalation and obtain early mechanical-circulatory-support advice","explanation":"The priority is to provide shock support, avoid harmful catecholamine escalation and obtain early mechanical-circulatory-support advice. The decisive feature is severe global ventricular dysfunction with myocardial injury despite unobstructed coronary arteries. A major error is assuming all ST elevation with shock is coronary occlusion without considering myocarditis.","learningPoint":"Fulminant myocarditis: severe global ventricular dysfunction with myocardial injury despite unobstructed coronary arteries. Avoid assuming all ST elevation with shock is coronary occlusion without considering myocarditis.","id":"cardiovascular-127","globalNumber":1377,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":128,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for fulminant myocarditis?","options":["new haemopericardium with tamponade physiology after myocardial infarction","severely restricted aortic valve opening with a high gradient or low-flow severe stenosis","severe global ventricular dysfunction with myocardial injury despite unobstructed coronary arteries","dynamic systolic anterior motion and left ventricular outflow obstruction","right atrial systolic and right ventricular diastolic collapse on echocardiography"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"severe global ventricular dysfunction with myocardial injury despite unobstructed coronary arteries","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is severe global ventricular dysfunction with myocardial injury despite unobstructed coronary arteries. In context, this supports Fulminant myocarditis and should prompt clinicians to provide shock support, avoid harmful catecholamine escalation and obtain early mechanical-circulatory-support advice.","learningPoint":"Fulminant myocarditis: severe global ventricular dysfunction with myocardial injury despite unobstructed coronary arteries. Avoid assuming all ST elevation with shock is coronary occlusion without considering myocarditis.","id":"cardiovascular-128","globalNumber":1378,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":129,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in fulminant myocarditis?","options":["giving nitrates, diuretics or inotropes that worsen obstruction","giving thrombolysis or anticoagulation during unexplained post-infarction tamponade","giving nitrates or catecholamine inotropes despite dynamic outflow obstruction","assuming all ST elevation with shock is coronary occlusion without considering myocarditis","inducing positive-pressure ventilation without haemodynamic preparation"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"assuming all ST elevation with shock is coronary occlusion without considering myocarditis","explanation":"The hazardous action is assuming all ST elevation with shock is coronary occlusion without considering myocarditis. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is refractory cardiogenic shock, ventricular arrhythmia and complete heart block.","learningPoint":"Fulminant myocarditis: severe global ventricular dysfunction with myocardial injury despite unobstructed coronary arteries. Avoid assuming all ST elevation with shock is coronary occlusion without considering myocarditis.","id":"cardiovascular-129","globalNumber":1379,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":130,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of fulminant myocarditis, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["ventricular arrhythmia and sudden cardiac death","recurrent rupture, exsanguination and cardiac arrest","refractory pulmonary oedema, myocardial ischaemia and cardiac arrest","ventricular arrhythmia, apical thrombus and cardiogenic shock","refractory cardiogenic shock, ventricular arrhythmia and complete heart block"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"refractory cardiogenic shock, ventricular arrhythmia and complete heart block","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is refractory cardiogenic shock, ventricular arrhythmia and complete heart block. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of fulminant myocarditis.","learningPoint":"Fulminant myocarditis: severe global ventricular dysfunction with myocardial injury despite unobstructed coronary arteries. Avoid assuming all ST elevation with shock is coronary occlusion without considering myocarditis.","id":"cardiovascular-130","globalNumber":1380,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":131,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by acute chest pain and shock after emotional or physiological stress with apical ballooning and no culprit coronary lesion. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Takotsubo syndrome with dynamic LV outflow obstruction","Left ventricular free-wall rupture","Critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock","Coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia","Kounis syndrome"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Takotsubo syndrome with dynamic LV outflow obstruction","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Takotsubo syndrome with dynamic LV outflow obstruction. The key discriminator is apical or mid-ventricular ballooning with basal hyperkinesis and dynamic outflow obstruction. Immediate priorities include: support preload, avoid inotropes that worsen obstruction and use specialist-guided vasopressor or mechanical support.","learningPoint":"Takotsubo syndrome with dynamic LV outflow obstruction: apical or mid-ventricular ballooning with basal hyperkinesis and dynamic outflow obstruction. Avoid giving nitrates or catecholamine inotropes despite dynamic outflow obstruction.","id":"cardiovascular-131","globalNumber":1381,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":132,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is takotsubo syndrome with dynamic lv outflow obstruction. The patient has acute chest pain and shock after emotional or physiological stress with apical ballooning and no culprit coronary lesion. What is the best immediate management?","options":["activate immediate reperfusion and record posterior leads without delaying angiography","support preload, avoid inotropes that worsen obstruction and use specialist-guided vasopressor or mechanical support","admit for urgent coronary angiography and avoid exercise stress testing","restore preload, use phenylephrine and reduce contractility with expert-guided beta blockade","give nitrates and calcium-channel blockade while treating arrhythmia and excluding fixed occlusion"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"support preload, avoid inotropes that worsen obstruction and use specialist-guided vasopressor or mechanical support","explanation":"The priority is to support preload, avoid inotropes that worsen obstruction and use specialist-guided vasopressor or mechanical support. The decisive feature is apical or mid-ventricular ballooning with basal hyperkinesis and dynamic outflow obstruction. A major error is giving nitrates or catecholamine inotropes despite dynamic outflow obstruction.","learningPoint":"Takotsubo syndrome with dynamic LV outflow obstruction: apical or mid-ventricular ballooning with basal hyperkinesis and dynamic outflow obstruction. Avoid giving nitrates or catecholamine inotropes despite dynamic outflow obstruction.","id":"cardiovascular-132","globalNumber":1382,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":133,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have takotsubo syndrome with dynamic lv outflow obstruction. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["a dilated hypokinetic right ventricle with septal flattening","the De Winter pattern indicating acute proximal LAD occlusion","apical or mid-ventricular ballooning with basal hyperkinesis and dynamic outflow obstruction","angiographic or clinically documented reversible coronary spasm without fixed culprit obstruction","low output with pulmonary congestion and a mechanical or ischaemic cause"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"apical or mid-ventricular ballooning with basal hyperkinesis and dynamic outflow obstruction","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is apical or mid-ventricular ballooning with basal hyperkinesis and dynamic outflow obstruction. In context, this supports Takotsubo syndrome with dynamic LV outflow obstruction and should prompt clinicians to support preload, avoid inotropes that worsen obstruction and use specialist-guided vasopressor or mechanical support.","learningPoint":"Takotsubo syndrome with dynamic LV outflow obstruction: apical or mid-ventricular ballooning with basal hyperkinesis and dynamic outflow obstruction. Avoid giving nitrates or catecholamine inotropes despite dynamic outflow obstruction.","id":"cardiovascular-133","globalNumber":1383,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":134,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with takotsubo syndrome with dynamic lv outflow obstruction is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["withholding intramuscular adrenaline in life-threatening anaphylaxis solely because of ischaemia","inducing positive-pressure ventilation without haemodynamic preparation","using non-selective beta blockade in isolated vasospastic disease","giving nitrates or catecholamine inotropes despite dynamic outflow obstruction","assuming all ST elevation with shock is coronary occlusion without considering myocarditis"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"giving nitrates or catecholamine inotropes despite dynamic outflow obstruction","explanation":"The hazardous action is giving nitrates or catecholamine inotropes despite dynamic outflow obstruction. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is ventricular arrhythmia, apical thrombus and cardiogenic shock.","learningPoint":"Takotsubo syndrome with dynamic LV outflow obstruction: apical or mid-ventricular ballooning with basal hyperkinesis and dynamic outflow obstruction. Avoid giving nitrates or catecholamine inotropes despite dynamic outflow obstruction.","id":"cardiovascular-134","globalNumber":1384,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":135,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with takotsubo syndrome with dynamic lv outflow obstruction?","options":["coronary thrombosis, vasospasm, arrhythmia and shock","refractory pulmonary oedema, myocardial ischaemia and cardiac arrest","multiorgan failure, malignant arrhythmia and death","ventricular arrhythmia and sudden cardiac death","ventricular arrhythmia, apical thrombus and cardiogenic shock"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"ventricular arrhythmia, apical thrombus and cardiogenic shock","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is ventricular arrhythmia, apical thrombus and cardiogenic shock. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of takotsubo syndrome with dynamic lv outflow obstruction.","learningPoint":"Takotsubo syndrome with dynamic LV outflow obstruction: apical or mid-ventricular ballooning with basal hyperkinesis and dynamic outflow obstruction. Avoid giving nitrates or catecholamine inotropes despite dynamic outflow obstruction.","id":"cardiovascular-135","globalNumber":1385,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":136,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has exercise- or emotion-triggered syncope in a young person with a normal resting ECG and structurally normal heart. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia","Congenital long-QT syndrome","Coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia","Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy","Torsades de pointes"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia. The key discriminator is bidirectional or polymorphic ventricular ectopy induced by adrenergic stress. Immediate priorities include: stop exertion, treat instability electrically and obtain urgent electrophysiology advice with beta blockade.","learningPoint":"Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia: bidirectional or polymorphic ventricular ectopy induced by adrenergic stress. Avoid reassuring the patient because the resting ECG is normal.","id":"cardiovascular-136","globalNumber":1386,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":137,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia when the presentation is exercise- or emotion-triggered syncope in a young person with a normal resting ECG and structurally normal heart?","options":["give intravenous magnesium and perform immediate defibrillation if unstable","stop exertion, treat instability electrically and obtain urgent electrophysiology advice with beta blockade","treat unstable arrhythmia and obtain urgent electrophysiology and inflammatory-disease assessment","use procainamide or synchronised cardioversion according to stability","treat unstable arrhythmia, restrict exercise and obtain urgent cardiology or electrophysiology assessment"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"stop exertion, treat instability electrically and obtain urgent electrophysiology advice with beta blockade","explanation":"The priority is to stop exertion, treat instability electrically and obtain urgent electrophysiology advice with beta blockade. The decisive feature is bidirectional or polymorphic ventricular ectopy induced by adrenergic stress. A major error is reassuring the patient because the resting ECG is normal.","learningPoint":"Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia: bidirectional or polymorphic ventricular ectopy induced by adrenergic stress. Avoid reassuring the patient because the resting ECG is normal.","id":"cardiovascular-137","globalNumber":1387,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":138,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia in this presentation?","options":["dynamic systolic anterior motion and left ventricular outflow obstruction","patchy myocardial inflammation or scar with conduction disease and no better cause","bidirectional or polymorphic ventricular ectopy induced by adrenergic stress","QRS complexes twisting around the baseline with a prolonged QT interval","epsilon waves or ventricular arrhythmia with left bundle branch block morphology plus RV structural disease"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"bidirectional or polymorphic ventricular ectopy induced by adrenergic stress","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is bidirectional or polymorphic ventricular ectopy induced by adrenergic stress. In context, this supports Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia and should prompt clinicians to stop exertion, treat instability electrically and obtain urgent electrophysiology advice with beta blockade.","learningPoint":"Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia: bidirectional or polymorphic ventricular ectopy induced by adrenergic stress. Avoid reassuring the patient because the resting ECG is normal.","id":"cardiovascular-138","globalNumber":1388,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":139,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia?","options":["using non-selective beta blockade in isolated vasospastic disease","giving adenosine, verapamil, diltiazem or digoxin","giving amiodarone or another QT-prolonging drug","reassuring the patient because the resting ECG is normal","diagnosing benign athlete changes without evaluating ventricular arrhythmia"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"reassuring the patient because the resting ECG is normal","explanation":"The hazardous action is reassuring the patient because the resting ECG is normal. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is ventricular fibrillation and sudden cardiac death.","learningPoint":"Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia: bidirectional or polymorphic ventricular ectopy induced by adrenergic stress. Avoid reassuring the patient because the resting ECG is normal.","id":"cardiovascular-139","globalNumber":1389,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":140,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["ventricular fibrillation through rapid accessory-pathway conduction","recurrent ventricular tachycardia, right-heart failure and sudden death","degeneration to ventricular fibrillation","ventricular fibrillation, progressive heart block and heart failure","ventricular fibrillation and sudden cardiac death"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"ventricular fibrillation and sudden cardiac death","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is ventricular fibrillation and sudden cardiac death. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia.","learningPoint":"Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia: bidirectional or polymorphic ventricular ectopy induced by adrenergic stress. Avoid reassuring the patient because the resting ECG is normal.","id":"cardiovascular-140","globalNumber":1390,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":141,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with exertional syncope or ventricular tachycardia with right-precordial T-wave inversion and a family history of sudden death. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy","Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia","Torsades de pointes","Electrical storm","Sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy. The key discriminator is epsilon waves or ventricular arrhythmia with left bundle branch block morphology plus RV structural disease. Immediate priorities include: treat unstable arrhythmia, restrict exercise and obtain urgent cardiology or electrophysiology assessment.","learningPoint":"Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy: epsilon waves or ventricular arrhythmia with left bundle branch block morphology plus RV structural disease. Avoid diagnosing benign athlete changes without evaluating ventricular arrhythmia.","id":"cardiovascular-141","globalNumber":1391,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":142,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy: exertional syncope or ventricular tachycardia with right-precordial T-wave inversion and a family history of sudden death. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["give intravenous magnesium and perform immediate defibrillation if unstable","treat unstable arrhythmia, restrict exercise and obtain urgent cardiology or electrophysiology assessment","treat unstable arrhythmia and arrange urgent inherited-arrhythmia evaluation","administer digoxin-specific antibody fragments and correct life threats","stop exertion, treat instability electrically and obtain urgent electrophysiology advice with beta blockade"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"treat unstable arrhythmia, restrict exercise and obtain urgent cardiology or electrophysiology assessment","explanation":"The priority is to treat unstable arrhythmia, restrict exercise and obtain urgent cardiology or electrophysiology assessment. The decisive feature is epsilon waves or ventricular arrhythmia with left bundle branch block morphology plus RV structural disease. A major error is diagnosing benign athlete changes without evaluating ventricular arrhythmia.","learningPoint":"Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy: epsilon waves or ventricular arrhythmia with left bundle branch block morphology plus RV structural disease. Avoid diagnosing benign athlete changes without evaluating ventricular arrhythmia.","id":"cardiovascular-142","globalNumber":1392,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":143,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["QRS complexes twisting around the baseline with a prolonged QT interval","bidirectional or polymorphic ventricular ectopy induced by adrenergic stress","epsilon waves or ventricular arrhythmia with left bundle branch block morphology plus RV structural disease","atrioventricular dissociation, capture beats or fusion beats","angiographic or clinically documented reversible coronary spasm without fixed culprit obstruction"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"epsilon waves or ventricular arrhythmia with left bundle branch block morphology plus RV structural disease","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is epsilon waves or ventricular arrhythmia with left bundle branch block morphology plus RV structural disease. In context, this supports Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy and should prompt clinicians to treat unstable arrhythmia, restrict exercise and obtain urgent cardiology or electrophysiology assessment.","learningPoint":"Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy: epsilon waves or ventricular arrhythmia with left bundle branch block morphology plus RV structural disease. Avoid diagnosing benign athlete changes without evaluating ventricular arrhythmia.","id":"cardiovascular-143","globalNumber":1393,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":144,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["reassuring the patient because the resting ECG is normal","delivering repeated shocks without controlling pain and adrenergic drive","assuming supraventricular tachycardia and giving verapamil","diagnosing benign athlete changes without evaluating ventricular arrhythmia","giving amiodarone or another QT-prolonging drug"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"diagnosing benign athlete changes without evaluating ventricular arrhythmia","explanation":"The hazardous action is diagnosing benign athlete changes without evaluating ventricular arrhythmia. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is recurrent ventricular tachycardia, right-heart failure and sudden death.","learningPoint":"Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy: epsilon waves or ventricular arrhythmia with left bundle branch block morphology plus RV structural disease. Avoid diagnosing benign athlete changes without evaluating ventricular arrhythmia.","id":"cardiovascular-144","globalNumber":1394,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":145,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["ventricular arrhythmia, high-grade block and cardiac arrest","ventricular fibrillation and sudden cardiac death","degeneration to ventricular fibrillation","ventricular fibrillation through rapid accessory-pathway conduction","recurrent ventricular tachycardia, right-heart failure and sudden death"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"recurrent ventricular tachycardia, right-heart failure and sudden death","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is recurrent ventricular tachycardia, right-heart failure and sudden death. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy.","learningPoint":"Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy: epsilon waves or ventricular arrhythmia with left bundle branch block morphology plus RV structural disease. Avoid diagnosing benign athlete changes without evaluating ventricular arrhythmia.","id":"cardiovascular-145","globalNumber":1395,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":146,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with recurrent syncope after exertion, sudden auditory stimulus or emotional stress with a prolonged corrected QT interval?","options":["Congenital long-QT syndrome","Complete heart block complicating inferior myocardial infarction","Cardiac myxoma with intermittent mitral obstruction","Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy with shock","Brugada syndrome"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Congenital long-QT syndrome","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Congenital long-QT syndrome. The key discriminator is a reproducibly prolonged QTc with characteristic trigger and no reversible cause. Immediate priorities include: correct electrolytes, stop QT-prolonging drugs and obtain urgent specialist management with beta blockade.","learningPoint":"Congenital long-QT syndrome: a reproducibly prolonged QTc with characteristic trigger and no reversible cause. Avoid giving additional QT-prolonging antiarrhythmics for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia.","id":"cardiovascular-146","globalNumber":1396,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":147,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with congenital long-qt syndrome presenting with recurrent syncope after exertion, sudden auditory stimulus or emotional stress with a prolonged corrected QT interval, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["restore preload, use phenylephrine and reduce contractility with expert-guided beta blockade","correct electrolytes, stop QT-prolonging drugs and obtain urgent specialist management with beta blockade","give nitrates and calcium-channel blockade while treating arrhythmia and excluding fixed occlusion","treat unstable arrhythmia, restrict exercise and obtain urgent cardiology or electrophysiology assessment","aggressively treat fever, correct electrolytes and obtain urgent electrophysiology advice"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"correct electrolytes, stop QT-prolonging drugs and obtain urgent specialist management with beta blockade","explanation":"The priority is to correct electrolytes, stop QT-prolonging drugs and obtain urgent specialist management with beta blockade. The decisive feature is a reproducibly prolonged QTc with characteristic trigger and no reversible cause. A major error is giving additional QT-prolonging antiarrhythmics for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia.","learningPoint":"Congenital long-QT syndrome: a reproducibly prolonged QTc with characteristic trigger and no reversible cause. Avoid giving additional QT-prolonging antiarrhythmics for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia.","id":"cardiovascular-147","globalNumber":1397,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":148,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for congenital long-qt syndrome?","options":["a spontaneous type 1 Brugada ECG pattern","persistently shortened QTc with absent secondary cause","a reproducibly prolonged QTc with characteristic trigger and no reversible cause","a mobile left atrial mass intermittently obstructing mitral inflow","dynamic systolic anterior motion and left ventricular outflow obstruction"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a reproducibly prolonged QTc with characteristic trigger and no reversible cause","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a reproducibly prolonged QTc with characteristic trigger and no reversible cause. In context, this supports Congenital long-QT syndrome and should prompt clinicians to correct electrolytes, stop QT-prolonging drugs and obtain urgent specialist management with beta blockade.","learningPoint":"Congenital long-QT syndrome: a reproducibly prolonged QTc with characteristic trigger and no reversible cause. Avoid giving additional QT-prolonging antiarrhythmics for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia.","id":"cardiovascular-148","globalNumber":1398,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":149,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in congenital long-qt syndrome?","options":["treating recurrent positional collapse as vasovagal syncope without echocardiography","dismissing a very short QT interval as a normal variant without context","administering flecainide or another potent sodium-channel blocker","giving additional QT-prolonging antiarrhythmics for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia","giving nitrates, diuretics or inotropes that worsen obstruction"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"giving additional QT-prolonging antiarrhythmics for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia","explanation":"The hazardous action is giving additional QT-prolonging antiarrhythmics for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is torsades de pointes, ventricular fibrillation and sudden death.","learningPoint":"Congenital long-QT syndrome: a reproducibly prolonged QTc with characteristic trigger and no reversible cause. Avoid giving additional QT-prolonging antiarrhythmics for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia.","id":"cardiovascular-149","globalNumber":1399,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":150,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of congenital long-qt syndrome, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["ventricular arrhythmia and sudden cardiac death","ventricular fibrillation, progressive heart block and heart failure","ventricular fibrillation, myocardial infarction and sudden death","ventricular fibrillation and sudden cardiac death","torsades de pointes, ventricular fibrillation and sudden death"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"torsades de pointes, ventricular fibrillation and sudden death","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is torsades de pointes, ventricular fibrillation and sudden death. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of congenital long-qt syndrome.","learningPoint":"Congenital long-QT syndrome: a reproducibly prolonged QTc with characteristic trigger and no reversible cause. Avoid giving additional QT-prolonging antiarrhythmics for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia.","id":"cardiovascular-150","globalNumber":1400,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":151,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by unexplained atrial or ventricular arrhythmia with an abnormally short QT interval and family history of sudden death. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Short-QT syndrome","Coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia","Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy with shock","Congenital long-QT syndrome","Torsades de pointes"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Short-QT syndrome","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Short-QT syndrome. The key discriminator is persistently shortened QTc with absent secondary cause. Immediate priorities include: treat unstable arrhythmia and arrange urgent inherited-arrhythmia evaluation.","learningPoint":"Short-QT syndrome: persistently shortened QTc with absent secondary cause. Avoid dismissing a very short QT interval as a normal variant without context.","id":"cardiovascular-151","globalNumber":1401,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":152,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is short-qt syndrome. The patient has unexplained atrial or ventricular arrhythmia with an abnormally short QT interval and family history of sudden death. What is the best immediate management?","options":["aggressively treat fever, correct electrolytes and obtain urgent electrophysiology advice","treat unstable arrhythmia and arrange urgent inherited-arrhythmia evaluation","give nitrates and calcium-channel blockade while treating arrhythmia and excluding fixed occlusion","correct electrolytes, stop QT-prolonging drugs and obtain urgent specialist management with beta blockade","restore preload, use phenylephrine and reduce contractility with expert-guided beta blockade"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"treat unstable arrhythmia and arrange urgent inherited-arrhythmia evaluation","explanation":"The priority is to treat unstable arrhythmia and arrange urgent inherited-arrhythmia evaluation. The decisive feature is persistently shortened QTc with absent secondary cause. A major error is dismissing a very short QT interval as a normal variant without context.","learningPoint":"Short-QT syndrome: persistently shortened QTc with absent secondary cause. Avoid dismissing a very short QT interval as a normal variant without context.","id":"cardiovascular-152","globalNumber":1402,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":153,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have short-qt syndrome. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["a spontaneous type 1 Brugada ECG pattern","dynamic systolic anterior motion and left ventricular outflow obstruction","persistently shortened QTc with absent secondary cause","epsilon waves or ventricular arrhythmia with left bundle branch block morphology plus RV structural disease","angiographic or clinically documented reversible coronary spasm without fixed culprit obstruction"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"persistently shortened QTc with absent secondary cause","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is persistently shortened QTc with absent secondary cause. In context, this supports Short-QT syndrome and should prompt clinicians to treat unstable arrhythmia and arrange urgent inherited-arrhythmia evaluation.","learningPoint":"Short-QT syndrome: persistently shortened QTc with absent secondary cause. Avoid dismissing a very short QT interval as a normal variant without context.","id":"cardiovascular-153","globalNumber":1403,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":154,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with short-qt syndrome is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["administering flecainide or another potent sodium-channel blocker","giving additional QT-prolonging antiarrhythmics for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia","implanting a simple pacemaker without considering defibrillator risk or active inflammation","dismissing a very short QT interval as a normal variant without context","reassuring the patient because the resting ECG is normal"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"dismissing a very short QT interval as a normal variant without context","explanation":"The hazardous action is dismissing a very short QT interval as a normal variant without context. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is ventricular fibrillation and sudden cardiac death.","learningPoint":"Short-QT syndrome: persistently shortened QTc with absent secondary cause. Avoid dismissing a very short QT interval as a normal variant without context.","id":"cardiovascular-154","globalNumber":1404,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":155,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with short-qt syndrome?","options":["torsades de pointes, ventricular fibrillation and sudden death","ventricular fibrillation, progressive heart block and heart failure","recurrent ventricular tachycardia, right-heart failure and sudden death","degeneration to ventricular fibrillation","ventricular fibrillation and sudden cardiac death"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"ventricular fibrillation and sudden cardiac death","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is ventricular fibrillation and sudden cardiac death. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of short-qt syndrome.","learningPoint":"Short-QT syndrome: persistently shortened QTc with absent secondary cause. Avoid dismissing a very short QT interval as a normal variant without context.","id":"cardiovascular-155","globalNumber":1405,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":156,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has syncope, angina or pulmonary oedema with a late-peaking systolic murmur and profound hypotension. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock","High-risk pulmonary embolism","Complete heart block complicating inferior myocardial infarction","Cardiac myxoma with intermittent mitral obstruction","Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy with shock"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock. The key discriminator is severely restricted aortic valve opening with a high gradient or low-flow severe stenosis. Immediate priorities include: maintain preload and coronary perfusion, use cautious vasopressor support and arrange urgent valve intervention.","learningPoint":"Critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock: severely restricted aortic valve opening with a high gradient or low-flow severe stenosis. Avoid giving aggressive nitrates, diuretics or vasodilators despite fixed outflow obstruction.","id":"cardiovascular-156","globalNumber":1406,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":157,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock when the presentation is syncope, angina or pulmonary oedema with a late-peaking systolic murmur and profound hypotension?","options":["expedite revascularisation, use norepinephrine and add carefully titrated inotropy when required","maintain preload and coronary perfusion, use cautious vasopressor support and arrange urgent valve intervention","give reperfusion therapy with systemic thrombolysis or urgent embolectomy according to contraindications","restore preload, use phenylephrine and reduce contractility with expert-guided beta blockade","stabilise haemodynamics and arrange urgent cardiothoracic surgical assessment"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"maintain preload and coronary perfusion, use cautious vasopressor support and arrange urgent valve intervention","explanation":"The priority is to maintain preload and coronary perfusion, use cautious vasopressor support and arrange urgent valve intervention. The decisive feature is severely restricted aortic valve opening with a high gradient or low-flow severe stenosis. A major error is giving aggressive nitrates, diuretics or vasodilators despite fixed outflow obstruction.","learningPoint":"Critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock: severely restricted aortic valve opening with a high gradient or low-flow severe stenosis. Avoid giving aggressive nitrates, diuretics or vasodilators despite fixed outflow obstruction.","id":"cardiovascular-157","globalNumber":1407,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":158,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock in this presentation?","options":["a mobile left atrial mass intermittently obstructing mitral inflow","bidirectional ventricular tachycardia or multiple simultaneous dysrhythmias","severely restricted aortic valve opening with a high gradient or low-flow severe stenosis","dynamic systolic anterior motion and left ventricular outflow obstruction","a dilated hypokinetic right ventricle with septal flattening"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"severely restricted aortic valve opening with a high gradient or low-flow severe stenosis","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is severely restricted aortic valve opening with a high gradient or low-flow severe stenosis. In context, this supports Critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock and should prompt clinicians to maintain preload and coronary perfusion, use cautious vasopressor support and arrange urgent valve intervention.","learningPoint":"Critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock: severely restricted aortic valve opening with a high gradient or low-flow severe stenosis. Avoid giving aggressive nitrates, diuretics or vasodilators despite fixed outflow obstruction.","id":"cardiovascular-158","globalNumber":1408,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":159,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock?","options":["treating recurrent positional collapse as vasovagal syncope without echocardiography","anticoagulating a patient with cerebral septic emboli without specialist review","giving nitrates, diuretics or inotropes that worsen obstruction","giving aggressive nitrates, diuretics or vasodilators despite fixed outflow obstruction","giving repeated large fluid boluses that worsen RV distension"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"giving aggressive nitrates, diuretics or vasodilators despite fixed outflow obstruction","explanation":"The hazardous action is giving aggressive nitrates, diuretics or vasodilators despite fixed outflow obstruction. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is refractory pulmonary oedema, myocardial ischaemia and cardiac arrest.","learningPoint":"Critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock: severely restricted aortic valve opening with a high gradient or low-flow severe stenosis. Avoid giving aggressive nitrates, diuretics or vasodilators despite fixed outflow obstruction.","id":"cardiovascular-159","globalNumber":1409,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":160,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["obstructive cardiac arrest and acute right ventricular failure","pulseless electrical activity arrest","sudden inflow obstruction, systemic embolism and death","ventricular arrhythmia and sudden cardiac death","refractory pulmonary oedema, myocardial ischaemia and cardiac arrest"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"refractory pulmonary oedema, myocardial ischaemia and cardiac arrest","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is refractory pulmonary oedema, myocardial ischaemia and cardiac arrest. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock.","learningPoint":"Critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock: severely restricted aortic valve opening with a high gradient or low-flow severe stenosis. Avoid giving aggressive nitrates, diuretics or vasodilators despite fixed outflow obstruction.","id":"cardiovascular-160","globalNumber":1410,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":161,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with sudden pulmonary oedema and shock with a soft early diastolic murmur after endocarditis or aortic injury. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Acute severe aortic regurgitation","Critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock","Infective endocarditis with acute valvular failure","Stanford type A aortic dissection","Mechanical prosthetic valve dysfunction from pannus"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Acute severe aortic regurgitation","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Acute severe aortic regurgitation. The key discriminator is premature mitral valve closure and severe regurgitation with a non-dilated left ventricle. Immediate priorities include: support perfusion with afterload reduction when feasible and arrange emergency surgery.","learningPoint":"Acute severe aortic regurgitation: premature mitral valve closure and severe regurgitation with a non-dilated left ventricle. Avoid slowing the heart markedly or delaying surgery because the murmur is quiet.","id":"cardiovascular-161","globalNumber":1411,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":162,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has acute severe aortic regurgitation: sudden pulmonary oedema and shock with a soft early diastolic murmur after endocarditis or aortic injury. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["maintain preload and coronary perfusion, use cautious vasopressor support and arrange urgent valve intervention","support perfusion with afterload reduction when feasible and arrange emergency surgery","perform urgent ultrasound-guided pericardial drainage while maintaining preload","obtain urgent multimodal valve imaging and cardiothoracic surgical review","obtain blood cultures, start appropriate intravenous antibiotics and seek urgent surgical review"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"support perfusion with afterload reduction when feasible and arrange emergency surgery","explanation":"The priority is to support perfusion with afterload reduction when feasible and arrange emergency surgery. The decisive feature is premature mitral valve closure and severe regurgitation with a non-dilated left ventricle. A major error is slowing the heart markedly or delaying surgery because the murmur is quiet.","learningPoint":"Acute severe aortic regurgitation: premature mitral valve closure and severe regurgitation with a non-dilated left ventricle. Avoid slowing the heart markedly or delaying surgery because the murmur is quiet.","id":"cardiovascular-162","globalNumber":1412,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":163,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected acute severe aortic regurgitation, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["severe eccentric acute mitral regurgitation on echocardiography","a flail mitral leaflet with severe eccentric regurgitation on echocardiography","premature mitral valve closure and severe regurgitation with a non-dilated left ventricle","restricted leaflet motion with an abrupt rise in transvalvular gradient","a mobile left atrial mass intermittently obstructing mitral inflow"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"premature mitral valve closure and severe regurgitation with a non-dilated left ventricle","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is premature mitral valve closure and severe regurgitation with a non-dilated left ventricle. In context, this supports Acute severe aortic regurgitation and should prompt clinicians to support perfusion with afterload reduction when feasible and arrange emergency surgery.","learningPoint":"Acute severe aortic regurgitation: premature mitral valve closure and severe regurgitation with a non-dilated left ventricle. Avoid slowing the heart markedly or delaying surgery because the murmur is quiet.","id":"cardiovascular-163","globalNumber":1413,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":164,"type":"pitfall","diffic