rvention should be prioritised?","options":["expedite revascularisation, use norepinephrine and add carefully titrated inotropy when required","provide ventilatory support and afterload reduction while arranging urgent surgical repair","support perfusion with afterload reduction when feasible and arrange emergency surgery","stabilise with afterload reduction and mechanical support while arranging emergency surgery","perform urgent ultrasound-guided pericardial drainage while maintaining preload"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"provide ventilatory support and afterload reduction while arranging urgent surgical repair","explanation":"The priority is to provide ventilatory support and afterload reduction while arranging urgent surgical repair. The decisive feature is a flail mitral leaflet with severe eccentric regurgitation on echocardiography. A major error is assuming a normal-sized heart or subtle murmur excludes acute severe regurgitation.","learningPoint":"Acute chordal rupture causing severe mitral regurgitation: a flail mitral leaflet with severe eccentric regurgitation on echocardiography. Avoid assuming a normal-sized heart or subtle murmur excludes acute severe regurgitation.","id":"cardiovascular-167","globalNumber":1417,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":168,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for acute chordal rupture causing severe mitral regurgitation?","options":["premature mitral valve closure and severe regurgitation with a non-dilated left ventricle","a new left-to-right shunt with step-up in right-sided oxygen saturation or septal defect on echocardiography","a flail mitral leaflet with severe eccentric regurgitation on echocardiography","low output with pulmonary congestion and a mechanical or ischaemic cause","independent atrial and ventricular activity with a slow escape rhythm"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a flail mitral leaflet with severe eccentric regurgitation on echocardiography","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a flail mitral leaflet with severe eccentric regurgitation on echocardiography. In context, this supports Acute chordal rupture causing severe mitral regurgitation and should prompt clinicians to provide ventilatory support and afterload reduction while arranging urgent surgical repair.","learningPoint":"Acute chordal rupture causing severe mitral regurgitation: a flail mitral leaflet with severe eccentric regurgitation on echocardiography. Avoid assuming a normal-sized heart or subtle murmur excludes acute severe regurgitation.","id":"cardiovascular-168","globalNumber":1418,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":169,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in acute chordal rupture causing severe mitral regurgitation?","options":["treating only with diuresis and delaying surgical referral","treating recurrent positional collapse as vasovagal syncope without echocardiography","administering nitrates or aggressive diuresis despite preload dependence","assuming a normal-sized heart or subtle murmur excludes acute severe regurgitation","assuming every obstructed prosthetic valve should receive thrombolysis"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"assuming a normal-sized heart or subtle murmur excludes acute severe regurgitation","explanation":"The hazardous action is assuming a normal-sized heart or subtle murmur excludes acute severe regurgitation. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is refractory hypoxaemia and cardiogenic shock.","learningPoint":"Acute chordal rupture causing severe mitral regurgitation: a flail mitral leaflet with severe eccentric regurgitation on echocardiography. Avoid assuming a normal-sized heart or subtle murmur excludes acute severe regurgitation.","id":"cardiovascular-169","globalNumber":1419,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":170,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of acute chordal rupture causing severe mitral regurgitation, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["refractory pulmonary oedema, coronary hypoperfusion and cardiogenic shock","refractory pulmonary oedema and cardiogenic shock","systemic embolisation, acute heart failure or valve obstruction","acute valve obstruction, pulmonary oedema and cardiogenic shock","refractory hypoxaemia and cardiogenic shock"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"refractory hypoxaemia and cardiogenic shock","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is refractory hypoxaemia and cardiogenic shock. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of acute chordal rupture causing severe mitral regurgitation.","learningPoint":"Acute chordal rupture causing severe mitral regurgitation: a flail mitral leaflet with severe eccentric regurgitation on echocardiography. Avoid assuming a normal-sized heart or subtle murmur excludes acute severe regurgitation.","id":"cardiovascular-170","globalNumber":1420,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":171,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by rest pain with transient focal ST elevation that resolves and recurs, sometimes with ventricular arrhythmia. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia","Left main coronary artery occlusion","Brugada syndrome","Cardiac sarcoidosis with ventricular arrhythmia","Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia. The key discriminator is angiographic or clinically documented reversible coronary spasm without fixed culprit obstruction. Immediate priorities include: give nitrates and calcium-channel blockade while treating arrhythmia and excluding fixed occlusion.","learningPoint":"Coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia: angiographic or clinically documented reversible coronary spasm without fixed culprit obstruction. Avoid using non-selective beta blockade in isolated vasospastic disease.","id":"cardiovascular-171","globalNumber":1421,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":172,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia. The patient has rest pain with transient focal ST elevation that resolves and recurs, sometimes with ventricular arrhythmia. What is the best immediate management?","options":["arrange immediate invasive coronary assessment and haemodynamic support","give nitrates and calcium-channel blockade while treating arrhythmia and excluding fixed occlusion","arrange immediate invasive coronary angiography and revascularisation","treat unstable arrhythmia, restrict exercise and obtain urgent cardiology or electrophysiology assessment","stop exertion, treat instability electrically and obtain urgent electrophysiology advice with beta blockade"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give nitrates and calcium-channel blockade while treating arrhythmia and excluding fixed occlusion","explanation":"The priority is to give nitrates and calcium-channel blockade while treating arrhythmia and excluding fixed occlusion. The decisive feature is angiographic or clinically documented reversible coronary spasm without fixed culprit obstruction. A major error is using non-selective beta blockade in isolated vasospastic disease.","learningPoint":"Coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia: angiographic or clinically documented reversible coronary spasm without fixed culprit obstruction. Avoid using non-selective beta blockade in isolated vasospastic disease.","id":"cardiovascular-172","globalNumber":1422,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":173,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["ongoing ischaemia despite medical therapy or cardiogenic shock","global subendocardial ischaemia with aVR elevation in a clinically unstable patient","angiographic or clinically documented reversible coronary spasm without fixed culprit obstruction","a spontaneous type 1 Brugada ECG pattern","simultaneous allergic features and objective myocardial ischaemia"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"angiographic or clinically documented reversible coronary spasm without fixed culprit obstruction","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is angiographic or clinically documented reversible coronary spasm without fixed culprit obstruction. In context, this supports Coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia and should prompt clinicians to give nitrates and calcium-channel blockade while treating arrhythmia and excluding fixed occlusion.","learningPoint":"Coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia: angiographic or clinically documented reversible coronary spasm without fixed culprit obstruction. Avoid using non-selective beta blockade in isolated vasospastic disease.","id":"cardiovascular-173","globalNumber":1423,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":174,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["dismissing a very short QT interval as a normal variant without context","diagnosing benign athlete changes without evaluating ventricular arrhythmia","labelling the tracing as isolated subendocardial ischaemia and delaying reperfusion","using non-selective beta blockade in isolated vasospastic disease","reassuring the patient because the resting ECG is normal"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"using non-selective beta blockade in isolated vasospastic disease","explanation":"The hazardous action is using non-selective beta blockade in isolated vasospastic disease. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is ventricular fibrillation, myocardial infarction and sudden death.","learningPoint":"Coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia: angiographic or clinically documented reversible coronary spasm without fixed culprit obstruction. Avoid using non-selective beta blockade in isolated vasospastic disease.","id":"cardiovascular-174","globalNumber":1424,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":175,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia?","options":["malignant ventricular arrhythmia and cardiogenic shock","coronary thrombosis, vasospasm, arrhythmia and shock","ventricular fibrillation, progressive heart block and heart failure","anterior myocardial infarction and sudden cardiac death","ventricular fibrillation, myocardial infarction and sudden death"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"ventricular fibrillation, myocardial infarction and sudden death","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is ventricular fibrillation, myocardial infarction and sudden death. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia.","learningPoint":"Coronary vasospasm with malignant arrhythmia: angiographic or clinically documented reversible coronary spasm without fixed culprit obstruction. Avoid using non-selective beta blockade in isolated vasospastic disease.","id":"cardiovascular-175","globalNumber":1425,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":176,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has acute coronary syndrome occurring during anaphylaxis with urticaria, wheeze or hypotension. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Kounis syndrome","De Winter anterior occlusion myocardial infarction","Spontaneous coronary artery dissection","Left main coronary artery occlusion","Very-high-risk non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Kounis syndrome","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Kounis syndrome. The key discriminator is simultaneous allergic features and objective myocardial ischaemia. Immediate priorities include: treat anaphylaxis promptly while managing coronary ischaemia with early cardiology input.","learningPoint":"Kounis syndrome: simultaneous allergic features and objective myocardial ischaemia. Avoid withholding intramuscular adrenaline in life-threatening anaphylaxis solely because of ischaemia.","id":"cardiovascular-176","globalNumber":1426,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":177,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for kounis syndrome when the presentation is acute coronary syndrome occurring during anaphylaxis with urticaria, wheeze or hypotension?","options":["arrange immediate invasive coronary assessment and haemodynamic support","treat anaphylaxis promptly while managing coronary ischaemia with early cardiology input","activate the primary PCI pathway as an occlusion myocardial infarction without waiting for ST elevation","admit for urgent coronary angiography and avoid exercise stress testing","give nitrates and calcium-channel blockade while treating arrhythmia and excluding fixed occlusion"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"treat anaphylaxis promptly while managing coronary ischaemia with early cardiology input","explanation":"The priority is to treat anaphylaxis promptly while managing coronary ischaemia with early cardiology input. The decisive feature is simultaneous allergic features and objective myo