isolated subendocardial ischaemia and delaying reperfusion","withholding intramuscular adrenaline in life-threatening anaphylaxis solely because of ischaemia","giving nitrates or catecholamine inotropes despite dynamic outflow obstruction"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"withholding intramuscular adrenaline in life-threatening anaphylaxis solely because of ischaemia","explanation":"The hazardous action is withholding intramuscular adrenaline in life-threatening anaphylaxis solely because of ischaemia. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is coronary thrombosis, vasospasm, arrhythmia and shock.","learningPoint":"Kounis syndrome: simultaneous allergic features and objective myocardial ischaemia. Avoid withholding intramuscular adrenaline in life-threatening anaphylaxis solely because of ischaemia.","id":"cardiovascular-179","globalNumber":1429,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":180,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in kounis syndrome, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["ventricular arrhythmia, apical thrombus and cardiogenic shock","propagation of dissection, recurrent infarction or ventricular arrhythmia","ventricular fibrillation, myocardial infarction and sudden death","malignant ventricular arrhythmia and cardiogenic shock","coronary thrombosis, vasospasm, arrhythmia and shock"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"coronary thrombosis, vasospasm, arrhythmia and shock","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is coronary thrombosis, vasospasm, arrhythmia and shock. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of kounis syndrome.","learningPoint":"Kounis syndrome: simultaneous allergic features and objective myocardial ischaemia. Avoid withholding intramuscular adrenaline in life-threatening anaphylaxis solely because of ischaemia.","id":"cardiovascular-180","globalNumber":1430,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":181,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with episodic syncope, positional dyspnoea or embolic phenomena with a tumour-plop sound. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Cardiac myxoma with intermittent mitral obstruction","Critical aortic stenosis with cardiogenic shock","Severe digoxin toxicity","Mechanical prosthetic valve dysfunction from pannus","Prosthetic valve thrombosis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Cardiac myxoma with intermittent mitral obstruction","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Cardiac myxoma with intermittent mitral obstruction. The key discriminator is a mobile left atrial mass intermittently obstructing mitral inflow. Immediate priorities include: stabilise haemodynamics and arrange urgent cardiothoracic surgical assessment.","learningPoint":"Cardiac myxoma with intermittent mitral obstruction: a mobile left atrial mass intermittently obstructing mitral inflow. Avoid treating recurrent positional collapse as vasovagal syncope without echocardiography.","id":"cardiovascular-181","globalNumber":1431,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":182,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has cardiac myxoma with intermittent mitral obstruction: episodic syncope, positional dyspnoea or embolic phenomena with a tumour-plop sound. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["support perfusion with afterload reduction when feasible and arrange emergency surgery","stabilise haemodynamics and arrange urgent cardiothoracic surgical assessment","administer digoxin-specific antibody fragments and correct life threats","obtain urgent echocardiography and cardiothoracic advice for anticoagulation, thrombolysis or surgery","maintain preload and coronary perfusion, use cautious vasopressor support and arrange urgent valve intervention"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"stabilise haemodynamics and arrange urgent cardiothoracic surgical assessment","explanation":"The priority is to stabilise haemodynamics and arrange urgent cardiothoracic surgical assessment. The decisive feature is a mobile left atrial mass intermittently obstructing mitral inflow. A major error is treating recurrent positional collapse as vasovagal syncope without echocardiography.","learningPoint":"Cardiac myxoma with intermittent mitral obstruction: a mobile left atrial mass intermittently obstructing mitral inflow. Avoid treating recurrent positional collapse as vasovagal syncope without echocardiography.","id":"cardiovascular-182","globalNumber":1432,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":183,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected cardiac myxoma with intermittent mitral obstruction, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["premature mitral valve closure and severe regurgitation with a non-dilated left ventricle","restricted leaflet motion with an abrupt rise in transvalvular gradient","a mobile left atrial mass intermittently obstructing mitral inflow","severely restricted aortic valve opening with a high gradient or low-flow severe stenosis","a reproducibly prolonged QTc with characteristic trigger and no reversible cause"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a mobile left atrial mass intermittently obstructing mitral inflow","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a mobile left atrial mass intermittently obstructing mitral inflow. In context, this supports Cardiac myxoma with intermittent mitral obstruction and should prompt clinicians to stabilise haemodynamics and arrange urgent cardiothoracic surgical assessment.","learningPoint":"Cardiac myxoma with intermittent mitral obstruction: a mobile left atrial mass intermittently obstructing mitral inflow. Avoid treating recurrent positional collapse as vasovagal syncope without echocardiography.","id":"cardiovascular-1