Index":4,"answer":"sudden inflow obstruction, systemic embolism and death","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is sudden inflow obstruction, systemic embolism and death. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of cardiac myxoma with intermittent mitral obstruction.","learningPoint":"Cardiac myxoma with intermittent mitral obstruction: a mobile left atrial mass intermittently obstructing mitral inflow. Avoid treating recurrent positional collapse as vasovagal syncope without echocardiography.","id":"cardiovascular-185","globalNumber":1435,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":186,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with pleuritic positional chest pain and diffuse ST elevation accompanied by fever, large effusion or immunosuppression?","options":["Acute pericarditis with high-risk features","Cardiac tamponade","Boerhaave syndrome","Acute severe aortic regurgitation","Aortic intramural haematoma"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Acute pericarditis with high-risk features","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Acute pericarditis with high-risk features. The key discriminator is diffuse PR depression and ST elevation without reciprocal territorial change, plus a high-risk feature. Immediate priorities include: admit for monitored evaluation, anti-inflammatory treatment and assessment for specific cause or tamponade.","learningPoint":"Acute pericarditis with high-risk features: diffuse PR depression and ST elevation without reciprocal territorial change, plus a high-risk feature. Avoid discharging a patient with a large effusion or evolving haemodynamic compromise.","id":"cardiovascular-186","globalNumber":1436,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":187,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with acute pericarditis with high-risk features presenting with pleuritic positional chest pain and diffuse ST elevation accompanied by fever, large effusion or immunosuppression, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["obtain urgent coronary angiography and favour conservative management if stable with preserved flow","admit for monitored evaluation, anti-inflammatory treatment and assessment for specific cause or tamponade","give reperfusion therapy with systemic thrombolysis or urgent embolectomy according to contraindications","support perfusion with afterload reduction when feasible and arrange emergency surgery","commence anti-impulse therapy with an intravenous beta blocker and arrange immediate cardiothoracic surgery"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"admit for monitored evaluation, anti-inflammatory treatment and assessment for specific cause or tamponade","explanation":"The priority is to admit for monitored evaluation, anti-inflammatory treatment and assessment for specific cause or tamponade. The decisive feature is diffuse PR depression and ST elevation without reciprocal territorial change, plus a high-risk feature. A major error is discharging a patient with a large effusion or evolving haemodynamic compromise.","learningPoint":"Acute pericarditis with high-risk features: diffuse PR depression and ST elevation without reciprocal territorial change, plus a high-risk feature. Avoid discharging a patient with a large effusion or evolving haemodynamic compromise.","id":"cardiovascular-187","globalNumber":1437,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Cardiovascular","categoryNumber":188,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for acute pericarditis with high-risk features?","options":["long smooth coronary narrowing or an intramural haematoma","a dilated hypokinetic right ventricle with septal flattening","diffuse PR depression and ST elevation without reciprocal territorial change, plus a high-risk feature","right atrial systolic and right v