"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in mechanical prosthetic valve dysfunction from pannus, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["systemic embolisation, acute heart failure or valve obstruction","sudden inflow obstruction, systemic embolism and death","refractory pulmonary oedema and cardiogenic shock","refractory hypoxaemia and cardiogenic shock","acute valve obstruction, pulmonary oedema and cardiogenic shock"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"acute valve obstruction, pulmonary oedema and cardiogenic shock","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is acute valve obstruction, pulmonary oedema and cardiogenic shock. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of mechanical prosthetic valve dysfunction from pannus.","learningPoint":"Mechanical prosthetic valve dysfunction from pannus: fixed prosthetic obstruction with dense subvalvular tissue rather than mobile thrombus. Avoid assuming every obstructed prosthetic valve should receive thrombolysis.","id":"cardiovascular-200","globalNumber":1450,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":101,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with worsening dyspnoea with acidotic hypercapnia, fatigue and reduced air entry in a patient with COPD. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Acute hypercapnic respiratory failure from COPD","Dynamic hyperinflation in ventilated obstructive lung disease","Acute-on-chronic obesity hypoventilation","Acute respiratory distress syndrome","Near-fatal asthma"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Acute hypercapnic respiratory failure from COPD","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Acute hypercapnic respiratory failure from COPD. The key discriminator is respiratory acidosis with clinical work of breathing and no immediate contraindication to non-invasive ventilation. Immediate priorities include: give controlled oxygen, bronchodilators, corticosteroids and early non-invasive ventilation.","learningPoint":"Acute hypercapnic respiratory failure from COPD: respiratory acidosis with clinical work of breathing and no immediate contraindication to non-invasive ventilation. Avoid delivering uncontrolled oxygen or delaying ventilatory support until coma.","id":"respiratory-101","globalNumber":1451,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":102,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has acute hypercapnic respiratory failure from copd: worsening dyspnoea with acidotic hypercapnia, fatigue and reduced air entry in a patient with COPD. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["use non-invasive ventilation and carefully titrated oxygen while treating the precipitant","give controlled oxygen, bronchodilators, corticosteroids and early non-invasive ventilation","disconnect the circuit briefly, allow exhalation and reset a low rate with prolonged expiratory time","continue maximal bronchodilation and steroids, give intravenous magnesium and prepare controlled intubation","arrange urgent bronchoscopic removal"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give controlled oxygen, bronchodilators, corticosteroids and early non-invasive ventilation","explanation":"The priority is to give controlled oxygen, bronchodilators, corticosteroids and early non-invasive ventilation. The decisive feature is respiratory acidosis with clinical work of breathing and no immediate contraindication to non-invasive ventilation. A major error is delivering uncontrolled oxygen or delaying ventilatory support until coma.","learningPoint":"Acute hypercapnic respiratory failure from COPD: respiratory acidosis with clinical work of breathing and no immediate contraindication to non-invasive ventilation. Avoid delivering uncontrolled oxygen or delaying ventilatory support until coma.","id":"respiratory-102","globalNumber":1452,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":103,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected acute hypercapnic respiratory failure from copd, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["a normalising or rising PaCO2 despite ongoing severe distress","chronic compensated respiratory acidosis with acute CO2 rise","respiratory acidosis with clinical work of breathing and no immediate contraindication to non-invasive ventilation","reduced PaO2 to FiO2 ratio with bilateral non-cardiogenic infiltrates","expiratory flow failing to return to zero before the next breath"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"respiratory acidosis with clinical work of breathing and no immediate contraindication to non-invasive ventilation","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is respiratory acidosis with clinical work of breathing and no immediate contraindication to non-invasive ventilation. In context, this supports Acute hypercapnic respiratory failure from COPD and should prompt clinicians to give controlled oxygen, bronchodilators, corticosteroids and early non-invasive ventilation.","learningPoint":"Acute hypercapnic respiratory failure from COPD: respiratory acidosis with clinical work of breathing and no immediate contraindication to non-invasive ventilation. Avoid delivering uncontrolled oxygen or delaying ventilatory support until coma.","id":"respiratory-103","globalNumber":1453,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":104,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of acute hypercapnic respiratory failure from copd, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["misdiagnosing fixed stridor as refractory asthma","using a high respiratory rate after intubation and causing dynamic hyperinflation","giving uncontrolled high-flow oxygen without ventilatory support","delivering uncontrolled oxygen or delaying ventilatory support until coma","increasing respiratory rate or tidal volume to correct hypercapnia"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"delivering uncontrolled oxygen or delaying ventilatory support until coma","explanation":"The hazardous action is delivering uncontrolled oxygen or delaying ventilatory support until coma. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is worsening acidosis, aspiration and respiratory arrest.","learningPoint":"Acute hypercapnic respiratory failure from COPD: respiratory acidosis with clinical work of breathing and no immediate contraindication to non-invasive ventilation. Avoid delivering uncontrolled oxygen or delaying ventilatory support until coma.","id":"respiratory-104","globalNumber":1454,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":105,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with acute hypercapnic respiratory failure from copd initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["carbon dioxide narcosis and need for intubation","sudden respiratory arrest and aspiration","peri-intubation hypotension, barotrauma and cardiac arrest","tension physiology, barotrauma and pulseless electrical activity","worsening acidosis, aspiration and respiratory arrest"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"worsening acidosis, aspiration and respiratory arrest","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is worsening acidosis, aspiration and respiratory arrest. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of acute hypercapnic respiratory failure from copd.","learningPoint":"Acute hypercapnic respiratory failure from COPD: respiratory acidosis with clinical work of breathing and no immediate contraindication to non-invasive ventilation. Avoid delivering uncontrolled oxygen or delaying ventilatory support until coma.","id":"respiratory-105","globalNumber":1455,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":106,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with pneumonia followed by persistent fever, pleuritic pain and a loculated pleural collection?","options":["Empyema with sepsis","Severe Legionella pneumonia","Severe community-acquired pneumonia with septic shock","Aspiration pneumonia","Tension pneumothorax"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Empyema with sepsis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Empyema with sepsis. The key discriminator is frank pus or low pleural pH with loculation. Immediate priorities include: give antibiotics and arrange prompt image-guided drainage with surgical input when complex.","learningPoint":"Empyema with sepsis: frank pus or low pleural pH with loculation. Avoid repeated thoracentesis without definitive drainage of a loculated infected space.","id":"respiratory-106","globalNumber":1456,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":107,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with empyema with sepsis presenting with pneumonia followed by persistent fever, pleuritic pain and a loculated pleural collection, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["give prompt appropriate antibiotics, source-focused resuscitation and norepinephrine when fluid unresponsive","give antibiotics and arrange prompt image-guided drainage with surgical input when complex","give prolonged appropriate antibiotics and evaluate for obstruction or drainage need","start high-dose trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and add corticosteroids when significantly hypoxaemic","start prompt mould-active antifungal therapy and reverse immunosuppression where possible"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give antibiotics and arrange prompt image-guided drainage with surgical input when complex","explanation":"The priority is to give antibiotics and arrange prompt image-guided drainage with surgical input when complex. The decisive feature is frank pus or low pleural pH with loculation. A major error is repeated thoracentesis without definitive drainage of a loculated infected space.","learningPoint":"Empyema with sepsis: frank pus or low pleural pH with loculation. Avoid repeated thoracentesis without definitive drainage of a loculated infected space.","id":"respiratory-107","globalNumber":1457,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":108,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for empyema with sepsis?","options":["severe pneumonia with compatible urinary antigen or molecular testing and extrapulmonary features","continuous bubbling throughout inspiration and expiration","frank pus or low pleural pH with loculation","new pneumothorax with cystic lung change and persistent air leak","mediastinal air compressing the heart or great vessels with haemodynamic compromise"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"frank pus or low pleural pH with loculation","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is frank pus or low pleural pH with loculation. In context, this supports Empyema with sepsis and should prompt clinicians to give antibiotics and arrange prompt image-guided drainage with surgical input when complex.","learningPoint":"Empyema with sepsis: frank pus or low pleural pH with loculation. Avoid repeated thoracentesis without definitive drainage of a loculated infected space.","id":"respiratory-108","globalNumber":1458,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":109,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in empyema with sepsis?","options":["delaying antiviral treatment while awaiting definitive virology in a critically ill patient","assuming all pneumomediastinum is benign despite shock","inserting repeated chest tubes without investigating airway disruption","repeated thoracentesis without definitive drainage of a loculated infected space","waiting for definitive organism confirmation in a severely hypoxaemic patient"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"repeated thoracentesis without definitive drainage of a loculated infected space","explanation":"The hazardous action is repeated thoracentesis without definitive drainage of a loculated infected space. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is fibrothorax, bronchopleural fistula and septic shock.","learningPoint":"Empyema with sepsis: frank pus or low pleural pH with loculation. Avoid repeated thoracentesis without definitive drainage of a loculated infected space.","id":"respiratory-109","globalNumber":1459,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":110,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of empyema with sepsis, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["lung abscess, empyema and respiratory failure","pneumothorax and progressive respiratory failure","massive haemoptysis, dissemination and respiratory failure","cardiovascular collapse and airway compression","fibrothorax, bronchopleural fistula and septic shock"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"fibrothorax, bronchopleural fistula and septic shock","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is fibrothorax, bronchopleural fistula and septic shock. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of empyema with sepsis.","learningPoint":"Empyema with sepsis: frank pus or low pleural pH with loculation. Avoid repeated thoracentesis without definitive drainage of a loculated infected space.","id":"respiratory-110","globalNumber":1460,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":111,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by subacute fever, weight loss and foul sputum with a cavitating lesion and air-fluid level. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Lung abscess","Aspiration pneumonia","Adult foreign-body aspiration","Severe community-acquired pneumonia with septic shock","Empyema with sepsis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Lung abscess","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Lung abscess. The key discriminator is a thick-walled cavity in dependent lung after aspiration risk. Immediate priorities include: give prolonged appropriate antibiotics and evaluate for obstruction or drainage need.","learningPoint":"Lung abscess: a thick-walled cavity in dependent lung after aspiration risk. Avoid assuming every cavitary lesion is uncomplicated bacterial pneumonia.","id":"respiratory-111","globalNumber":1461,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":112,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is lung abscess. The patient has subacute fever, weight loss and foul sputum with a cavitating lesion and air-fluid level. What is the best immediate management?","options":["give antibiotics covering likely oral and enteric flora according to local guidance","give prolonged appropriate antibiotics and evaluate for obstruction or drainage need","give prompt appropriate antibiotics, source-focused resuscitation and norepinephrine when fluid unresponsive","start high-dose trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and add corticosteroids when significantly hypoxaemic","start antiviral therapy promptly, provide lung-protective support and treat suspected bacterial coinfection"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give prolonged appropriate antibiotics and evaluate for obstruction or drainage need","explanation":"The priority is to give prolonged appropriate antibiotics and evaluate for obstruction or drainage need. The decisive feature is a thick-walled cavity in dependent lung after aspiration risk. A major error is assuming every cavitary lesion is uncomplicated bacterial pneumonia.","learningPoint":"Lung abscess: a thick-walled cavity in dependent lung after aspiration risk. Avoid assuming every cavitary lesion is uncomplicated bacterial pneumonia.","