t can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is massive haemoptysis, empyema and sepsis.","learningPoint":"Lung abscess: a thick-walled cavity in dependent lung after aspiration risk. Avoid assuming every cavitary lesion is uncomplicated bacterial pneumonia.","id":"respiratory-114","globalNumber":1464,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":115,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with lung abscess?","options":["rapidly progressive respiratory failure and multiorgan dysfunction","ARDS and secondary bacterial pneumonia","lung abscess, empyema and respiratory failure","pneumothorax and progressive respiratory failure","massive haemoptysis, empyema and sepsis"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"massive haemoptysis, empyema and sepsis","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is massive haemoptysis, empyema and sepsis. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of lung abscess.","learningPoint":"Lung abscess: a thick-walled cavity in dependent lung after aspiration risk. Avoid assuming every cavitary lesion is uncomplicated bacterial pneumonia.","id":"respiratory-115","globalNumber":1465,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":116,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has rapidly progressive hypoxaemia with bilateral infiltrates during influenza circulation and systemic illness. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Severe influenza viral pneumonia","Empyema with sepsis","Acute chest syndrome","Severe community-acquired pneumonia with septic shock","Severe Legionella pneumonia"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Severe influenza viral pneumonia","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Severe influenza viral pneumonia. The key discriminator is positive influenza testing with diffuse viral pneumonitis and no better bacterial explanation. Immediate priorities include: start antiviral therapy promptly, provide lung-protective support and treat suspected bacterial coinfection.","learningPoint":"Severe influenza viral pneumonia: positive influenza testing with diffuse viral pneumonitis and no better bacterial explanation. Avoid delaying antiviral treatment while awaiting definitive virology in a critically ill patient.","id":"respiratory-116","globalNumber":1466,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":117,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for severe influenza viral pneumonia when the presentation is rapidly progressive hypoxaemia with bilateral infiltrates during influenza circulation and systemic illness?","options":["give prompt antimicrobial therapy active against Legionella and support organ failure","start antiviral therapy promptly, provide lung-protective support and treat suspected bacterial coinfection","give prolonged appropriate antibiotics and evaluate for obstruction or drainage need","give oxygen, antibiotics, analgesia, incentive spirometry and early transfusion consultation","give antibiotics covering likely oral and enteric flora according to local guidance"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"start antiviral therapy promptly, provide lung-protective support and treat suspected bacterial coinfection","explanation":"The priority is to start antiviral therapy promptly, provide lung-protective support and treat suspected bacterial coinfection. The decisive feature is positive influenza testing with diffuse viral pneumonitis and no better bacterial explanation. A major error is delaying antiviral treatment while awaiting definitive virology in a critically ill patient.","learningPoint":"Severe influenza viral pneumonia: positive influenza testing with diffuse viral pneumonitis and no better bacterial explanation. Avoid delaying antiviral treatment while awaiting definitive virology in a critically ill patient.","id":"respiratory-117","globalNumber":1467,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":118,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for severe influenza viral pneumonia in this presentation?","options":["a new infiltrate plus respiratory symptoms in sickle cell disease","a halo sign or cavitating nodules in a high-risk host","positive influenza testing with diffuse viral pneumonitis and no better bacterial explanation","a widened alveolar-arterial gradient with bilateral ground-glass change","delayed infectious features rather than immediate chemical injury"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"positive influenza testing with diffuse viral pneumonitis and no better bacterial explanation","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is positive influenza testing with diffuse viral pneumonitis and no better bacterial explanation. In context, this supports Severe influenza viral pneumonia and should prompt clinicians to start antiviral therapy promptly, provide lung-protective support and treat suspected bacterial coinfection.","learningPoint":"Severe influenza viral pneumonia: positive influenza testing with diffuse viral pneumonitis and no better bacterial explanation. Avoid delaying antiviral treatment while awaiting definitive virology in a critically ill patient.","id":"respiratory-118","globalNumber":1468,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":119,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in severe influenza viral pneumonia?","options":["waiting for culture confirmation despite rapidly progressive invasive disease","using beta-lactam monotherapy that lacks intracellular coverage","delaying antibiotics until after CT or prolonged diagnostic testing","delaying antiviral treatment while awaiting definitive virology in a critically ill patient","repeated thoracentesis without definitive drainage of a loculated infected space"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"delaying antiviral treatment while awaiting definitive virology in a critically ill patient","explanation":"The hazardous action is delaying antiviral treatment while awaiting definitive virology in a critically ill patient. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is ARDS, myocarditis and secondary bacterial pneumonia.","learningPoint":"Severe influenza viral pneumonia: positive influenza testing with diffuse viral pneumonitis and no better bacterial explanation. Avoid delaying antiviral treatment while awaiting definitive virology in a critically ill patient.","id":"respiratory-119","globalNumber":1469,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":120,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in severe influenza viral pneumonia, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis and renal injury","rapidly progressive respiratory failure and multiorgan dysfunction","massive haemoptysis, empyema and sepsis","empyema, ARDS and multiorgan failure","ARDS, myocarditis and secondary bacterial pneumonia"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"ARDS, myocarditis and secondary bacterial pneumonia","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is ARDS, myocarditis and secondary bacterial pneumonia. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of severe influenza viral pneumonia.","learningPoint":"Severe influenza viral pneumonia: positive influenza testing with diffuse viral pneumonitis and no better bacterial explanation. Avoid delaying antiviral treatment while awaiting definitive virology in a critically ill patient.","id":"respiratory-120","globalNumber":1470,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":121,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with acute hypoxaemic respiratory illness with bilateral ground-glass opacities after recent vaping exposure. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["E-cigarette or vaping-associated lung injury","Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis crisis","Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage","Re-expansion pulmonary oedema","Acute interstitial pneumonia"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"E-cigarette or vaping-associated lung injury","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with E-cigarette or vaping-associated lung injury. The key discriminator is compatible imaging and inflammatory illness after exclusion of infection and alternative causes. Immediate priorities include: stop exposure, support oxygenation and consider corticosteroids after infection assessment.","learningPoint":"E-cigarette or vaping-associated lung injury: compatible imaging and inflammatory illness after exclusion of infection and alternative causes. Avoid diagnosing EVALI without excluding infection or other causes of diffuse lung injury.","id":"respiratory-121","globalNumber":1471,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":122,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has e-cigarette or vaping-associated lung injury: acute hypoxaemic respiratory illness with bilateral ground-glass opacities after recent vaping exposure. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["give systemic corticosteroids and obtain urgent specialist immunosuppressive management","stop exposure, support oxygenation and consider corticosteroids after infection assessment","support oxygenation and arrange specialist whole-lung lavage or targeted therapy","provide lung-protective critical care and early specialist diagnostic evaluation","stop excessive suction, support oxygenation and ventilation, and manage haemodynamics"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"stop exposure, support oxygenation and consider corticosteroids after infection assessment","explanati