.","id":"respiratory-123","globalNumber":1473,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":124,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of e-cigarette or vaping-associated lung injury, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["continuing high negative-pressure suction despite worsening hypoxaemia","treating recurrent diffuse opacities only with antibiotics without establishing the diagnosis","treating only with diuretics while failing to address airway and positive pressure needs","diagnosing EVALI without excluding infection or other causes of diffuse lung injury","continuing anticoagulation despite active pulmonary bleeding"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"diagnosing EVALI without excluding infection or other causes of diffuse lung injury","explanation":"The hazardous action is diagnosing EVALI without excluding infection or other causes of diffuse lung injury. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is respiratory failure, pneumothorax and recurrent injury with resumed vaping.","learningPoint":"E-cigarette or vaping-associated lung injury: compatible imaging and inflammatory illness after exclusion of infection and alternative causes. Avoid diagnosing EVALI without excluding infection or other causes of diffuse lung injury.","id":"respiratory-124","globalNumber":1474,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":125,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with e-cigarette or vaping-associated lung injury initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["refractory ARDS and multiorgan failure","severe hypoxaemia and secondary lung injury","bilateral oedema, shock and respiratory failure","severe hypoxaemia and opportunistic infection","respiratory failure, pneumothorax and recurrent injury with resumed vaping"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"respiratory failure, pneumothorax and recurrent injury with resumed vaping","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is respiratory failure, pneumothorax and recurrent injury with resumed vaping. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of e-cigarette or vaping-associated lung injury.","learningPoint":"E-cigarette or vaping-associated lung injury: compatible imaging and inflammatory illness after exclusion of infection and alternative causes. Avoid diagnosing EVALI without excluding infection or other causes of diffuse lung injury.","id":"respiratory-125","globalNumber":1475,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":126,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with massive unilateral pleural effusion causing mediastinal shift, hypotension and severe dyspnoea?","options":["Tension hydrothorax","Tracheobronchial disruption","Tension pneumothorax","Pneumomediastinum with tension physiology","Re-expansion pulmonary oedema"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Tension hydrothorax","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Tension hydrothorax. The key discriminator is obstructive physiology from a large pleural collection rather than intrapleural air. Immediate priorities include: perform urgent controlled pleural drainage while preparing definitive management.","learningPoint":"Tension hydrothorax: obstructive physiology from a large pleural collection rather than intrapleural air. Avoid rapidly draining a very large chronic effusion without monitoring for re-expansion injury.","id":"respiratory-126","globalNumber":1476,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":127,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with tension hydrothorax presenting with massive unilateral pleural effusion causing mediastinal shift, hypotension and severe dyspnoea, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["stop excessive suction, support oxygenation and ventilation, and manage haemodynamics","perform urgent controlled pleural drainage while preparing definitive management","reduce airway pressures, ensure drainage and obtain thoracic specialist input for isolation or repair","drain the pneumothorax, continue antimicrobial therapy and obtain early thoracic advice","give antibiotics and arrange prompt image-guided drainage with surgical input when complex"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"perform urgent controlled pleural drainage while preparing definitive management","explanation":"The priority is to perform urgent controlled pleural drainage while preparing definitive management. The decisive feature is obstructive physiology from a large pleural collection rather than intrapleural air. A major error is rapidly draining a very large chronic effusion without monitoring for re-expansion injury.","learningPoint":"Tension hydrothorax: obstructive physiology from a large pleural collection rather than intrapleural air. Avoid rapidly draining a very large chronic effusion without monitoring for re-expansion injury.","id":"respiratory-127","globalNumber":1477,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":128,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for tension hydrothorax?","options":["persistent pneumothorax with massive air leak or the fallen-lung sign","mediastinal air compressing the heart or great vessels with haemodynamic compromise","obstructive physiology from a large pleural collection rather than intrapleural air","pulmonary oedema confined predominantly to the recently re-expanded lung","new pneumothorax with cystic lung change and persistent air leak"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"obstructive physiology from a large pleural collection rather than intrapleural air","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is obstructive physiology from a large pleural collection rather than intrapleural air. In context, this supports Tension hydrothorax and should prompt clinicians to perform urgent controlled pleural drainage while preparing definitive management.","learningPoint":"Tension hydrothorax: obstructive physiology from a large pleural collection rather than intrapleural air. Avoid rapidly draining a very large chronic effusion without monitoring for re-expansion injury.","id":"respiratory-128","globalNumber":1478,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":129,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in tension hydrothorax?","options":["repeated thoracentesis without definitive drainage of a loculated infected space","using non-invasive ventilation without assessing for pneumothorax in sudden deterioration","assuming all pneumomediastinum is benign despite shock","rapidly draining a very large chronic effusion without monitoring for re-expansion injury","increasing PEEP in an attempt to recruit the affected lung"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"rapidly draining a very large chronic effusion without monitoring for re-expansion injury","explanation":"The hazardous action is rapidly draining a very large chronic effusion without monitoring for re-expansion injury. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is obstructive shock and re-expansion pulmonary oedema.","learningPoint":"Tension hydrothorax: obstructive physiology from a large pleural collection rather than intrapleural air. Avoid rapidly draining a very large chronic effusion without monitoring for re-expansion injury.","id":"respiratory-129","globalNumber":1479,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":130,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of tension hydrothorax, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["fibrothorax, bronchopleural fistula and septic shock","bilateral oedema, shock and respiratory failure","cardiac arrest from obstructive shock","cardiovascular collapse and airway compression","obstructive shock and re-expansion pulmonary oedema"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"obstructive shock and re-expansion pulmonary oedema","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is obstructive shock and re-expansion pulmonary oedema. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of tension hydrothorax.","learningPoint":"Tension hydrothorax: obstructive physiology from a large pleural collection rather than intrapleural air. Avoid rapidly draining a very large chronic effusion without monitoring for re-expansion injury.","id":"respiratory-130","globalNumber":1480,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":131,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by new severe hypoxaemia and unilateral alveolar oedema after rapid drainage of a large chronic pneumothorax or effusion. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Re-expansion pulmonary oedema","Tension pneumothorax","Acute respiratory distress syndrome","Tension hydrothorax","Severe Pneumocystis-related pneumothorax"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Re-expansion pulmonary oedema","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Re-expansion pulmonary oedema. The key discriminator is pulmonary oedema confined predominantly to the recently re-expanded lung. Immediate priorities include: stop excessive suction, support oxygenation and ventilation, and manage haemodynamics.","learningPoint":"Re-expansion pulmonary oedema: pulmonary oedema confined predominantly to the recently re-expanded lung. Avoid continuing high negative-pressure suction despite worsening hypoxaemia.","id":"respiratory-131","globalNumber":1481,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":132,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is re-expansion pulmonary oedema. The patient has new severe hypoxaemia and unilateral alveolar oedema after rapid drainage of a large chronic pneumothorax or effusion. What is the best immediate management?","options":["reduce airway pressures, ensure drainage and obtain thoracic specialist input for isolation or repair","stop excessive suction, support oxygenation and ventilation, and manage haemodynamics","give antibiotics and arrange prompt image-guided drainage with surgical input when complex","treat the underlying leak and obtain urgent thoracic decompression advice","stop exposure, support oxygenation and consider corticosteroids after infection assessment"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"stop excessive suction, support oxygenation and ventilation, and manage haemodynamics","explanation":"The priority is to stop excessive suction, support oxygenation and ventilation, and manage haemodynamics. The decisive feature is pulmonary oedema confined predominantly to the recently re-expanded lung. A major error is continuing high negative-pressure suction despite worsening hypoxaemia.","learningPoint":"Re-expansion pulmonary oedema: pulmonary oedema confined predominantly to the recently re-expanded lung. Avoid continuing high negative-pressure suction despite worsening hypoxaemia.","id":"respiratory-132","globalNumber":1482,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":133,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have re-expansion pulmonary oedema. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["rapid onset after forceful inspiration against an obstructed airway","eosinophilic inflammation with multisystem vasculitis rather than isolated asthma","pulmonary oedema confined predominantly to the recently re-expanded lung","obstructive shock with absent lung sliding and no alternative cause","progressively bloodier bronchoalveolar lavage aliquots"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"pulmonary oedema confined predominantly to the recently re-expanded lung","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is pulmonary oedema confined predominantly to the recently re-expanded lung. In context, this supports Re-expansion pulmonary oedema and should prompt clinicians to stop excessive suction, support oxygenation and ventilation, and manage haemodynamics.","learningPoint":"Re-expansion pulmonary oedema: pulmonary oedema confined predominantly to the recently re-expanded lung. Avoid continuing high negative-pressure suction despite worsening hypoxaemia.","id":"respiratory-133","globalNumber":1483,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":134,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with re-expansion pulmonary oedema is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["treating only with diuretics while failing to address airway and positive pressure needs","excluding the diagnosis because the peripheral eosinophil count is initially normal","using non-invasive ventilation without assessing for pneumothorax in sudden deterioration","continuing high negative-pressure suction despite worsening hypoxaemia","treating only bronchospasm while missing vasculitic organ injury"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"continuing high negative-pressure suction despite worsening hypoxaemia","explanation":"The hazardous action is continuing high negative-pressure suction despite worsening hypoxaemia. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is bilateral oedema, shock and respiratory failure.","learningPoint":"Re-expansion pulmonary oedema: pulmonary oedema confined predominantly to the recently re-expanded lung. Avoid continuing high negative-pressure suction despite worsening hypoxaemia.","id":"respiratory-134","globalNumber":1484,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":135,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with re-expansion pulmonary oedema?","options":["fulminant ARDS","obstructive shock and re-expansion pulmonary oedema","ventilator-induced lung injury and refractory hypoxaemia","alveolar haemorrhage, myocarditis and multiorgan failure","bilateral oedema, shock and respiratory failure"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"bilateral oedema, shock and respiratory failure","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is bilateral oedema, shock and respiratory failure. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of re-expansion pulmonary oedema.","learningPoint":"Re-expansion pulmonary oedema: pulmonary oedema confined predominantly to the recently re-expanded lung. Avoid continuing high negative-pressure suction despite worsening hypoxaemia.","id":"respiratory-135","globalNumber":1485,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":136,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has progressive stridor, monophonic wheeze or positional dyspnoea with a mediastinal or endobronchial mass. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Central airway obstruction","Tracheal stenosis with critical airflow limitation","Tracheobronchial disruption","Tracheostomy tube obstruction","Adult foreign-body aspiration"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Central airway obstruction","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Central airway obstruction. The key discriminator is fixed flattening of inspiratory and expiratory flow or direct imaging of tracheal obstruction. Immediate priorities include: maintain spontaneous ventilation when possible and obtain urgent airway and interventional-bronchoscopy planning.","learningPoint":"Central airway obstruction: fixed flattening of inspiratory and expiratory flow or direct imaging of tracheal obstruction. Avoid paralysing and intubating without a rescue plan when distal airway access may be impossible.","id":"respiratory-136","globalNumber":1486,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":137,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for central airway obstruction when the presentation is progressive stridor, monophonic wheeze or positional dyspnoea with a mediastinal or endobronchial mass?","options":["optimise humidification and suction, recruit cautiously and arrange bronchoscopy when persistent","maintain spontaneous ventilation when possible and obtain urgent airway and interventional-bronchoscopy planning","arrange urgent bronchoscopic removal","remove inner cannula, suction and replace or remove the obstructed tube while oxygenating both airway routes","avoid traumatic repeated intubation and obtain urgent ENT or interventional-airway management"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"maintain spontaneous ventilation when possible and obtain urgent airway and interventional-bronchoscopy planning","explanation":"The priority is to maintain spontaneous ventilation when possible and obtain urgent airway and interventional-bronchoscopy planning. The decisive feature is fixed flattening of inspiratory and expiratory flow or direct imaging of tracheal obstruction. A major error is paralysing and intubating without a rescue plan when distal airway access may be impossible.","learningPoint":"Central airway obstruction: fixed flattening of inspiratory and expiratory flow or direct imaging of tracheal obstruction. Avoid paralysing and intubating without a rescue plan when distal airway access may be impossible.","id":"respiratory-137","globalNumber":1487,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":138,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for central airway obstruction in this presentation?","options":["inability to pass a suction catheter through the tube","failure to pass suction catheter with air tracking into neck tissues","fixed flattening of inspiratory and expiratory flow or direct imaging of tracheal obstruction","fixed upper-airway obstruction at a prior cuff or tracheostomy site","abrupt onset with localised wheeze or unilateral hyperinflation"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"fixed flattening of inspiratory and expiratory flow or direct imaging of tracheal obstruction","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is fixed flattening of inspiratory and expiratory flow or direct imaging of tracheal obstruction. In context, this supports Central airway obstruction and should prompt clinicians to maintain spontaneous ventilation when possible and obtain urgent airway and interventional-bronchoscopy planning.","learningPoint":"Central airway obstruction: fixed flattening of inspiratory and expiratory flow or direct imaging of tracheal obstruction. Avoid paralysing and intubating without a rescue plan when distal airway access may be impossible.","id":"respiratory-138","globalNumber":1488,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":139,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in central airway obstruction?","options":["misdiagnosing fixed stridor as refractory asthma","repeatedly ventilating through a blocked tube without checking patency","blindly forcing a tube through an immature tracheostomy tract","paralysing and intubating without a rescue plan when distal airway access may be impossible","repeatedly treating presumed asthma without airway imaging or bronchoscopy"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"paralysing and intubating without a rescue plan when distal airway access may be impossible","explanation":"The hazardous action is paralysing and intubating without a rescue plan when distal airway access may be impossible. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is complete airway obstruction and cardiovascular collapse.","learningPoint":"Central airway obstruction: fixed flattening of inspiratory and expiratory flow or direct imaging of tracheal obstruction. Avoid paralysing and intubating without a rescue plan when distal airway access may be impossible.","id":"respiratory-139","globalNumber":1489,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":140,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in central airway obstruction, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["carbon dioxide narcosis and need for intubation","complete airway obstruction and failed intubation","complete obstruction, atelectasis or post-obstructive pneumonia","post-obstructive pneumonia and refractory hypoxaemia","complete airway obstruction and cardiovascular collapse"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"complete airway obstruction and cardiovascular collapse","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is complete airway obstruction and cardiovascular collapse. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of central airway obstruction.","learningPoint":"Central airway obstruction: fixed flattening of inspiratory and expiratory flow or direct imaging of tracheal obstruction. Avoid paralysing and intubating without a rescue plan when distal airway access may be impossible.","id":"respiratory-140","globalNumber":1490,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":141,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with abrupt hypoxaemia and increased airway pressure with unilateral volume loss after intubation or surgery. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Mucus-plug lobar collapse","Negative-pressure pulmonary oedema","Central airway obstruction","Lung abscess","Adult foreign-body aspiration"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Mucus-plug lobar collapse","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Mucus-plug lobar collapse. The key discriminator is lobar collapse with absent air bronchograms and an obstructing secretion burden. Immediate priorities include: optimise humidification and suction, recruit cautiously and arrange bronchoscopy when persistent.","learningPoint":"Mucus-plug lobar collapse: lobar collapse with absent air bronchograms and an obstructing secretion burden. Avoid treating unilateral collapse as pneumothorax without confirming the cause.","id":"respiratory-141","globalNumber":1491,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":142,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has mucus-plug lobar collapse: abrupt hypoxaemia and increased airway pressure with unilateral volume loss after intubation or surgery. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["maintain airway patency and provide CPAP or PEEP with supportive ventilation","optimise humidification and suction, recruit cautiously and arrange bronchoscopy when persistent","arrange urgent bronchoscopic removal","provide supportive oxygenation and ventilation while reassessing for secondary infection","use low tidal volume ventilation based on predicted body weight, limit plateau pressure and prone when severe"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"optimise humidification and suction, recruit cautiously and arrange bronchoscopy when persistent","explanation":"The priority is to optimise humidification and suction, recruit cautiously and arrange bronchoscopy when persistent. The decisive feature is lobar collapse with absent air bronchograms and an obstructing secretion burden. A major error is treating unilateral collapse as pneumothorax without confirming the cause.","learningPoint":"Mucus-plug lobar collapse: lobar collapse with absent air bronchograms and an obstructing secretion burden. Avoid treating unilateral collapse as pneumothorax without confirming the cause.","id":"respiratory-142","globalNumber":1492,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":143,"type":"discriminator","diffi