":"Tracheal stenosis with critical airflow limitation","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Tracheal stenosis with critical airflow limitation. The key discriminator is fixed upper-airway obstruction at a prior cuff or tracheostomy site. Immediate priorities include: avoid traumatic repeated intubation and obtain urgent ENT or interventional-airway management.","learningPoint":"Tracheal stenosis with critical airflow limitation: fixed upper-airway obstruction at a prior cuff or tracheostomy site. Avoid misdiagnosing fixed stridor as refractory asthma.","id":"respiratory-146","globalNumber":1496,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":147,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with tracheal stenosis with critical airflow limitation presenting with progressive exertional dyspnoea and biphasic stridor weeks after prolonged intubation, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["maintain spontaneous ventilation when possible and obtain urgent airway and interventional-bronchoscopy planning","avoid traumatic repeated intubation and obtain urgent ENT or interventional-airway management","arrange urgent bronchoscopic removal","optimise humidification and suction, recruit cautiously and arrange bronchoscopy when persistent","give controlled oxygen, bronchodilators, corticosteroids and early non-invasive ventilation"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"avoid traumatic repeated intubation and obtain urgent ENT or interventional-airway management","explanation":"The priority is to avoid traumatic repeated intubation and obtain urgent ENT or interventional-airway management. The decisive feature is fixed upper-airway obstruction at a prior cuff or tracheostomy site. A major error is misdiagnosing fixed stridor as refractory asthma.","learningPoint":"Tracheal stenosis with critical airflow limitation: fixed upper-airway obstruction at a prior cuff or tracheostomy site. Avoid misdiagnosing fixed stridor 