oscopy"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"misdiagnosing fixed stridor as refractory asthma","explanation":"The hazardous action is misdiagnosing fixed stridor as refractory asthma. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is complete airway obstruction and failed intubation.","learningPoint":"Tracheal stenosis with critical airflow limitation: fixed upper-airway obstruction at a prior cuff or tracheostomy site. Avoid misdiagnosing fixed stridor as refractory asthma.","id":"respiratory-149","globalNumber":1499,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":150,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of tracheal stenosis with critical airflow limitation, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["complete airway obstruction and toxic gas complications","complete obstruction, atelectasis or post-obstructive pneumonia","peri-intubation hypotension, barotrauma and cardiac arrest","complete airway obstruction and cardiovascular collapse","complete airway obstruction and failed intubation"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"complete airway obstruction and failed intubation","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is complete airway obstruction and failed intubation. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of tracheal stenosis with critical airflow limitation.","learningPoint":"Tracheal stenosis with critical airflow limitation: fixed upper-airway obstruction at a prior cuff or tracheostomy site. Avoid misdiagnosing fixed stridor as refractory asthma.","id":"respiratory-150","globalNumber":1500,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":151,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by neutropenia or major immunosuppression with fever, pleuritic pain, haemoptysis and nodular lung lesions. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis","Severe influenza viral pneumonia","Acute eosinophilic granulomatosis pulmonary crisis","Acute chest syndrome","Pulmonary tumour embolism or lymphangitic crisis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis. The key discriminator is a halo sign or cavitating nodules in a high-risk host. Immediate priorities include: start prompt mould-active antifungal therapy and reverse immunosuppression where possible.","learningPoint":"Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis: a halo sign or cavitating nodules in a high-risk host. Avoid waiting for culture confirmation despite rapidly progressive invasive disease.","id":"respiratory-151","globalNumber":1501,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":152,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is invasive pulmonary aspergillosis. The patient has neutropenia or major immunosuppression with fever, pleuritic pain, haemoptysis and nodular lung lesions. What is the best immediate management?","options":["optimise oxygenation and acid-base status, support systemic pressure and use pulmonary vasodilator therapy with expert help","start prompt mould-active antifungal therapy and reverse immunosuppression where possible","give prolonged appropriate antibiotics and evaluate for obstruction or drainage need","give oxygen, antibiotics, analgesia, incentive spirometry and early transfusion consultation","give antibiotics covering likely oral and enteric flora according to local guidance"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"start prompt mould-active antifungal therapy and reverse immunosuppression where possible","explanation":"The priority is to start prompt mould-active antifungal therapy and reverse immunosuppression where possible. The decisive feature is a halo sign or cavitating nodules in a high-risk host. A major error is waiting for culture confirmation despite rapidly progressive invasive disease.","learningPoint":"Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis: a halo sign or cavitating nodules in a high-risk host. Avoid waiting for culture confirmation despite rapidly progressive invasive disease.","id":"respiratory-152","globalNumber":1502,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":153,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have invasive pulmonary aspergillosis. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["a new infiltrate plus respiratory symptoms in sickle cell disease","acute right ventricular pressure overload with septal shift","a halo sign or cavitating nodules in a high-risk host","delayed infectious features rather than immediate chemical injury","a thick-walled cavity in dependent lung after aspiration risk"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a halo sign or cavitating nodules in a high-risk host","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a halo sign or cavitating nodules in a high-risk host. In context, this supports Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis and should prompt clinicians to start prompt mould-active antifungal therapy and reverse immunosuppression where possible.","learningPoint":"Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis: a halo sign or cavitating nodules in a high-risk host. Avoid waiting for culture confirmation despite rapidly progressive invasive disease.","id":"respiratory-153","globalNumber":1503,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":154,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with invasive pulmonary aspergillosis is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["continuing anticoagulation despite active pulmonary bleeding","giving excessive crystalloid that worsens pulmonary oedema","assuming every cavitary lesion is uncomplicated bacterial pneumonia","waiting for culture confirmation despite rapidly progressive invasive disease","unplanned intubation or excessive PEEP without circulatory preparation"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"waiting for culture confirmation despite rapidly progressive invasive disease","explanation":"The hazardous action is waiting for culture confirmation despite rapidly progressive invasive disease. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is massive haemoptysis, dissemination and respiratory failure.","learningPoint":"Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis: a halo sign or cavitating nodules in a high-risk host. Avoid waiting for culture confirmation despite rapidly progressive invasive disease.","id":"respiratory-154","globalNumber":1504,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":155,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with invasive pulmonary aspergillosis?","options":["alveolar haemorrhage, myocarditis and multiorgan failure","rapidly progressive respiratory failure and multiorgan dysfunction","empyema, ARDS and multiorgan failure","fibrothorax, bronchopleural fistula and septic shock","massive haemoptysis, dissemination and respiratory failure"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"massive haemoptysis, dissemination and respiratory failure","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is massive haemoptysis, dissemination and respiratory failure. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of invasive pulmonary aspergillosis.","learningPoint":"Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis: a halo sign or cavitating nodules in a high-risk host. Avoid waiting for culture confirmation despite rapidly progressive invasive disease.","id":"respiratory-155","globalNumber":1505,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":156,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has rapidly progressive idiopathic hypoxaemic respiratory failure with diffuse bilateral opacities and no clear trigger. Which underlying diagnosis should be prio