1510,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":161,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with subcutaneous emphysema, chest pain and shock with mediastinal air after barotrauma or oesophageal injury. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Pneumomediastinum with tension physiology","Bronchopleural fistula","Severe Pneumocystis-related pneumothorax","Re-expansion pulmonary oedema","Tension hydrothorax"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Pneumomediastinum with tension physiology","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Pneumomediastinum with tension physiology. The key discriminator is mediastinal air compressing the heart or great vessels with haemodynamic compromise. Immediate priorities include: treat the underlying leak and obtain urgent thoracic decompression advice.","learningPoint":"Pneumomediastinum with tension physiology: mediastinal air compressing the heart or great vessels with haemodynamic compromise. Avoid assuming all pneumomediastinum is benign despite shock.","id":"respiratory-161","globalNumber":1511,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":162,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has pneumomediastinum with tension physiology: subcutaneous emphysema, chest pain and shock with mediastinal air after barotrauma or oesophageal injury. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["perform urgent controlled pleural drainage while preparing definitive management","treat the underlying leak and obtain urgent thoracic decompression advice","give antibiotics and arrange prompt image-guided drainage with surgical input when complex","drain the pneumothorax, continue antimicrobial therapy and obtain early thoracic advice","stop excessive suction, support oxygenation and ventilation, and manage haemodynamics"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"treat the underlying leak and obtain urgent thoracic decompression advice","explanation":"The priority is to treat the underlying leak and obtain urgent thoracic decompression advice. The decisive feature is mediastinal air compressing the heart or great vessels with haemodynamic compromise. A major error is assuming all pneumomediastinum is benign despite shock.","learningPoint":"Pneumomediastinum with tension physiology: mediastinal air compressing the heart or great vessels with haemodynamic compromise. Avoid assuming all pneumomediastinum is benign despite shock.","id":"respiratory-162","globalNumber":1512,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":163,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected pneumomediastinum with tension physiology, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["obstructive physiology from a large pleural collection rather than intrapleural air","frank pus or low pleural pH with loculation","mediastinal air compressing the heart or great vessels with haemodynamic compromise","pulmonary oedema confined predominantly to the recently re-expanded lung","persistent pneumothorax with massive air leak or the fallen-lung sign"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"mediastinal air compressing the heart or great vessels with haemodynamic compromise","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is mediastinal air compressing the heart or great vessels with haemodynamic compromise. In context, this supports Pneumomediastinum with tension physiology and should prompt clinicians to treat the underlying leak and obtain urgent thoracic decompression advice.","learningPoint":"Pneumomediastinum with tension physiology: mediastinal air compressing the heart or great vessels with haemodynamic compromise. Avoid assuming all pneumomediastinum is benign despite shock.","id":"respiratory-163","globalNumber":1513,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":164,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency 