ng urgent oncology and respiratory input.","learningPoint":"Pulmonary tumour embolism or lymphangitic crisis: severe pulmonary vascular resistance with diffuse tumour microembolisation or lymphangitic spread. Avoid repeated thrombolysis despite absent thrombotic embolus and progressive malignant pattern.","id":"respiratory-186","globalNumber":1536,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":187,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with pulmonary tumour embolism or lymphangitic crisis presenting with rapidly progressive hypoxaemia and pulmonary hypertension in a patient with malignancy but little visible large-vessel embolus, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["start prompt mould-active antifungal therapy and reverse immunosuppression where possible","support oxygenation and right-ventricular perfusion while obtaining urgent oncology and respiratory input","support oxygenation, reverse anticoagulation when appropriate and initiate disease-specific immunotherapy with specialists","position the bleeding lung dependent, secure a large endotracheal tube and arrange bronchoscopy or embolisation","optimise oxygenation and acid-base status, support systemic pressure and use pulmonary vasodilator therapy with expert help"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"support oxygenation and right-ventricular perfusion while obtaining urgent oncology and respiratory input","explanation":"The priority is to support oxygenation and right-ventricular perfusion while obtaining urgent oncology and respiratory input. The decisive feature is severe pulmonary vascular resistance with diffuse tumour microembolisation or lymphangitic spread. A major error is repeated thrombolysis despite absent thrombotic embolus and progressive malignant pattern.","learningPoint":"Pulmonary tumour embolism or lymphangitic crisis: severe pulmonary vascular resistance with diffuse tumour microembolisation or lymphangitic spread. Avoid repeated thrombolysis despite absent thrombotic embolus and progressive malignant pattern.","id":"respiratory-187","globalNumber":1537,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":188,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for pulmonary tumour embolism or lymphangitic crisis?","options":["a halo sign or cavitating nodules in a high-risk host","a new infiltrate plus respiratory symptoms in sickle cell disease","severe pulmonary vascular resistance with diffuse tumour microembolisation or lymphangitic spread","acute right ventricular pressure overload with septal shift","airway compromise from blood rather than measured blood volume alone"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"severe pulmonary vascular resistance with diffuse tumour microembolisation or lymphangitic spread","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is severe pulmonary vascular resistance with diffuse tumour microembolisation or lymphangitic spread. In context, this supports Pulmonary tumour embolism or lymphangitic crisis and should prompt clinicians to support oxygenation and right-ventricular perfusion while obtaining urgent oncology and respiratory input.","learningPoint":"Pulmonary tumour embolism or lymphangitic crisis: severe pulmonary vascular resistance with diffuse tumour microembolisation or lymphangitic spread. Avoid repeated thrombolysis despite absent thrombotic embolus and progressive malignant pattern.","id":"respiratory-188","globalNumber":1538,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":189,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in pulmonary tumour embolism or lymphangitic crisis?","options":["unplanned intubation or excessive PEEP without circulatory preparation","waiting for culture confirmation despite rapidly progressive invasive disease","continuing anticoagulation despite active pulmonary bleeding","repeated thrombolysis despite absent thrombotic embolus and progressive malignant pattern","treating only bronchospasm while missing vasculitic organ injury"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"repeated thrombolysis despite absent thrombotic embolus and progressive malignant pattern","explanation":"The hazardous action is repeated thrombolysis despite absent thrombotic embolus and progressive malignant pattern. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is right-heart failure and refractory hypoxaemia.","learningPoint":"Pulmonary tumour embolism or lymphangitic crisis: severe pulmonary vascular resistance with diffuse tumour microembolisation or lymphangitic spread. Avoid repeated thrombolysis despite absent thrombotic embolus and progressive malignant pattern.","id":"respiratory-189","globalNumber":1539,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":190,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of pulmonary tumour embolism or lymphangitic crisis, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["right ventricular ischaemia and cardiac arrest","rapidly progressive respiratory failure and multiorgan dysfunction","alveolar haemorrhage, myocarditis and multiorgan failure","asphyxiation, contralateral lung flooding and shock","right-heart failure and refractory hypoxaemia"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"right-heart failure and refractory hypoxaemia","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is right-heart failure and refractory hypoxaemia. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of pulmonary tumour embolism or lymphangitic crisis.","learningPoint":"Pulmonary tumour embolism or lymphangitic crisis: severe pulmonary vascular resistance with diffuse tumour microembolisation or lymphangitic spread. Avoid repeated thrombolysis despite absent thrombotic embolus and progressive malignant pattern.","id":"respiratory-190","globalNumber":1540,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":191,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by multisystem pneumonia with high fever, confusion, diarrhoea, hyponatraemia and hepatic enzyme elevation. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Severe Legionella pneumonia","Empyema with sepsis","Severe community-acquired pneumonia with septic shock","Aspiration pneumonia","Severe influenza viral pneumonia"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Severe Legionella pneumonia","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Severe Legionella pneumonia. The key discriminator is severe pneumonia with compatible urinary antigen or molecular testing and extrapulmonary features. Immediate priorities include: give prompt antimicrobial therapy active against Legionella and support organ failure.","learningPoint":"Severe Legionella pneumonia: severe pneumonia with compatible urinary antigen or molecular testing and extrapulmonary features. Avoid using beta-lactam monotherapy that lacks intracellular coverage.","id":"respiratory-191","globalNumber":1541,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":192,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is severe legionella pneumonia. The patient has multisystem pneumonia with high fever, confusion, diarrhoea, hyponatraemia and hepatic enzyme elevation. What is the best immediate management?","options":["give antibiotics and arrange prompt image-guided drainage with surgical input when complex","give prompt antimicrobial therapy active against Legionella and support organ failure","start high-dose trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and add corticosteroids when significantly hypoxaemic","give prompt appropriate antibiotics, source-focused resuscitation and norepinephrine when fluid unresponsive","give prolonged appropriate antibiotics and evaluate for obstruction or drainage need"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give prompt antimicrobial therapy active against Legionella and support organ failure","explanation":"The priority is to give prompt antimicrobial therapy active against Legionella and support organ failure. The decisive feature is severe pneumonia with compatible urinary antigen or molecular testing and extrapulmonary features. A major error is using beta-lactam monotherapy that lacks intracellular coverage.","learningPoint":"Severe Legionella pneumonia: severe pneumonia with compatible urinary antigen or molecular testing and extrapulmonary features. Avoid using beta-lactam monotherapy that lacks intracellular coverage.","id":"respiratory-192","globalNumber":1542,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":193,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have severe legionella pneumonia. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["a new infiltrate plus respiratory symptoms in sickle cell disease","a halo sign or cavitating nodules in a high-risk host","severe pneumonia with compatible urinary antigen or molecular testing and extrapulmonary features","a widened alveolar-arterial gradient with bilateral ground-glass change","delayed infectious features rather than immediate chemical injury"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"severe pneumonia with compatible urinary antigen or molecular testing and extrapulmonary features","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is severe pneumonia with compatible urinary antigen or molecular testing and extrapulmonary features. In context, this supports Severe Legionella pneumonia and should prompt clinicians to give prompt antimicrobial therapy active against Legionella and support organ failure.","learningPoint":"Severe Legionella pneumonia: severe pneumonia with compatible urinary antigen or molecular testing and extrapulmonary features. Avoid using beta-lactam monotherapy that lacks intracellular coverage.","id":"respiratory-193","globalNumber":1543,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":194,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with severe legionella pneumonia is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["giving excessive crystalloid that worsens pulmonary oedema","delaying antiviral treatment while awaiting definitive virology in a critically ill patient","assuming every cavitary lesion is uncomplicated bacterial pneumonia","using beta-lactam monotherapy that lacks intracellular coverage","waiting for definitive organism confirmation in a severely hypoxaemic patient"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"using beta-lactam monotherapy that lacks intracellular coverage","explanation":"The hazardous action is using beta-lactam monotherapy that lacks intracellular coverage. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis and renal injury.","learningPoint":"Severe Legionella pneumonia: severe pneumonia with compatible urinary antigen or molecular testing and extrapulmonary features. Avoid using beta-lactam monotherapy that lacks intracellular coverage.","id":"respiratory-194","globalNumber":1544,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":195,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with severe legionella pneumonia?","options":["massive haemoptysis, dissemination and respiratory failure","massive haemoptysis, empyema and sepsis","ARDS, myocarditis and secondary bacterial pneumonia","fibrothorax, bronchopleural fistula and septic shock","respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis and renal injury"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis and renal injury","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis and renal injury. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of severe legionella pneumonia.","learningPoint":"Severe Legionella pneumonia: severe pneumonia with compatible urinary antigen or molecular testing and extrapulmonary features. Avoid using beta-lactam monotherapy that lacks intracellular coverage.","id":"respiratory-195","globalNumber":1545,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":196,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has asthma, eosinophilia, pulmonary infiltrates and systemic vasculitic features with neuropathy or cardiac involvement. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Acute eosinophilic granulomatosis pulmonary crisis","Pulmonary hypertension crisis","Re-expansion pulmonary oedema","Acute chest syndrome","Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Acute eosinophilic granulomatosis pulmonary crisis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Acute eosinophilic granulomatosis pulmonary crisis. The key discriminator is eosinophilic inflammation with multisystem vasculitis rather than isolated asthma. Immediate priorities include: give systemic corticosteroids and obtain urgent specialist immunosuppressive management.","learningPoint":"Acute eosinophilic granulomatosis pulmonary crisis: eosinophilic inflammation with multisystem vasculitis rather than isolated asthma. Avoid treating only bronchospasm while missing vasculitic organ injury.","id":"respiratory-196","globalNumber":1546,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":197,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for acute eosinophilic granulomatosis pulmonary crisis when the presentation is asthma, eosinophilia, pulmonary infiltrates and systemic vasculitic features with neuropathy or cardiac involvement?","options":["stop excessive suction, support oxygenation and ventilation, and manage haemodynamics","give systemic corticosteroids and obtain urgent specialist immunosuppressive management","support oxygenation, reverse anticoagulation when appropriate and initiate disease-specific immunotherapy with specialists","stop exposure, support oxygenation and consider corticosteroids after infection assessment","provide lung-protective critical care and early specialist diagnostic evaluation"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give systemic corticosteroids and obtain urgent specialist immunosuppressive management","explanation":"The priority is to give systemic corticosteroids and obtain urgent specialist immunosuppressive management. The decisive feature is eosinophilic inflammation with multisystem vasculitis rather than isolated asthma. A major error is treating only bronchospasm while missing vasculitic organ injury.","learningPoint":"Acute eosinophilic granulomatosis pulmonary crisis: eosinophilic inflammation with multisystem vasculitis rather than isolated asthma. Avoid treating only bronchospasm while missing vasculitic organ injury.","id":"respiratory-197","globalNumber":1547,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":198,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for acute eosinophilic granulomatosis pulmonary crisis in this presentation?","options":["lipoproteinaceous alveolar material with impaired gas exchange and no hydrostatic cause","progressively bloodier bronchoalveolar lavage aliquots","eosinophilic inflammation with multisystem vasculitis rather than isolated asthma","acute right ventricular pressure overload with septal shift","marked eosinophilia in bronchoalveolar lavage despite absent early blood eosinophilia"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"eosinophilic inflammation with multisystem vasculitis rather than isolated asthma","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is eosinophilic inflammation with multisystem vasculitis rather than isolated asthma. In context, this supports Acute eosinophilic granulomatosis pulmonary crisis and should prompt clinicians to give systemic corticosteroids and obtain urgent specialist immunosuppressive management.","learningPoint":"Acute eosinophilic granulomatosis pulmonary crisis: eosinophilic inflammation with multisystem vasculitis rather than isolated asthma. Avoid treating only bronchospasm while missing vasculitic organ injury.","id":"respiratory-198","globalNumber":1548,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":199,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in acute eosinophilic granulomatosis pulmonary crisis?","options":["unplanned intubation or excessive PEEP without circulatory preparation","diagnosing EVALI without excluding infection or other causes of diffuse lung injury","continuing anticoagulation despite active pulmonary bleeding","treating only bronchospasm while missing vasculitic organ injury","labeling the illness as routine community-acquired pneumonia despite rapid non-response"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"treating only bronchospasm while missing vasculitic organ injury","explanation":"The hazardous action is treating only bronchospasm while missing vasculitic organ injury. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is alveolar haemorrhage, myocarditis and multiorgan failure.","learningPoint":"Acute eosinophilic granulomatosis pulmonary crisis: eosinophilic inflammation with multisystem vasculitis rather than isolated asthma. Avoid treating only bronchospasm while missing vasculitic organ injury.","id":"respiratory-199","globalNumber":1549,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Respiratory","categoryNumber":200,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in acute eosinophilic granulomatosis pulmonary crisis, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["severe hypoxaemic failure and anaemia","fulminant ARDS","ventilator-induced lung injury and refractory hypoxaemia","right ventricular ischaemia and cardiac arrest","alveolar haemorrhage, myocarditis and multiorgan failure"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"alveolar haemorrhage, myocarditis and multiorgan failure","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is alveolar haemorrhage, myocarditis and multiorgan failure. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of acute eosinophilic granulomatosis pulmonary crisis.","learningPoint":"Acute eosinophilic granulomatosis pulmonary crisis: eosinophilic inflammation with multisystem vasculitis rather than isolated asthma. Avoid treating only bronchospasm while missing vasculitic organ injury.","id":"respiratory-200","globalNumber":1550,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":51,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with acute flaccid weakness after a carbohydrate load or exertion in a thyrotoxic patient with marked hypokalaemia. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Thyrotoxic periodic paralysis","Thyroid storm","Iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis","Phaeochromocytoma crisis","Acute severe hypophosphataemia"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Thyrotoxic periodic paralysis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Thyrotoxic periodic paralysis. The key discriminator is hypokalaemic paralysis with suppressed TSH and no sensory or sphincter involvement. Immediate priorities include: give cautious potassium replacement, non-selective beta blockade and treat thyrotoxicosis.","learningPoint":"Thyrotoxic periodic paralysis: hypokalaemic paralysis with suppressed TSH and no sensory or sphincter involvement. Avoid aggressively replacing potassium without recognising intracellular shift and rebound hyperkalaemia.","id":"endocrine-051","globalNumber":1551,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":52,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has thyrotoxic periodic paralysis: acute flaccid weakness after a carbohydrate load or exertion in a thyrotoxic patient with marked hypokalaemia. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["control with titratable alpha blockade and specialist critical care support","give cautious potassium replacement, non-selective beta blockade and treat thyrotoxicosis","replace phosphate carefully, correct associated electrolyte deficits and treat the precipitant","control adrenergic symptoms and start specialist-directed antithyroid therapy based on mechanism","give beta blockade when appropriate, thionamide, iodine after thionamide, corticosteroid and supportive care"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give cautious potassium replacement, non-selective beta blockade and treat thyrotoxicosis","explanation":"The priority is to give cautious potassium replacement, non-selective beta blockade and treat thyrotoxicosis. The decisive feature is hypokalaemic paralysis with suppressed TSH and no sensory or sphincter involvement. A major error is aggressively replacing potassium without recognising intracellular shift and rebound hyperkalaemia.","learningPoint":"Thyrotoxic periodic paralysis: hypokalaemic paralysis with suppressed TSH and no sensory or sphincter involvement. Avoid aggressively replacing potassium without recognising intracellular shift and rebound hyperkalaemia.","id":"endocrine-052","globalNumber":1552,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":53,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected thyrotoxic periodic paralysis, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["carpopedal spasm or QT prolongation with low ionised calcium","profound phosphate depletion explaining neuromuscular and haematological dysfunction","hypokalaemic paralysis with suppressed TSH and no sensory or sphincter involvement","suppressed TSH and elevated thyroid hormones after a large iodine load","labile hypertension with catecholamine-mediated cardiac dysfunction"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"hypokalaemic paralysis with suppressed TSH and no sensory or sphincter involvement","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is hypokalaemic paralysis with suppressed TSH and no sensory or sphincter involvement. In context, this supports Thyrotoxic periodic paralysis and should prompt clinicians to give cautious potassium replacement, non-selective beta blockade and treat thyrotoxicosis.","learningPoint":"Thyrotoxic periodic paralysis: hypokalaemic paralysis with suppressed TSH and no sensory or sphincter involvement. Avoid aggressively replacing potassium without recognising intracellular shift and rebound hyperkalaemia.","id":"endocrine-053","globalNumber":1553,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":54,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of thyrotoxic periodic paralysis, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["assuming additional iodine exposure is harmless in uncontrolled thyrotoxicosis","correcting calcium without identifying severe hypomagnesaemia","administering iodine before blocking new hormone synthesis","aggressively replacing potassium without recognising intracellular shift and rebound hyperkalaemia","giving isolated beta blockade before alpha blockade"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"aggressively replacing potassium without recognising intracellular shift and rebound hyperkalaemia","explanation":"The hazardous action is aggressively replacing potassium without recognising intracellular shift and rebound hyperkalaemia. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is respiratory weakness, ventricular arrhythmia and rebound hyperkalaemia.","learningPoint":"Thyrotoxic periodic paralysis: hypokalaemic paralysis with suppressed TSH and no sensory or sphincter involvement. Avoid aggressively replacing potassium without recognising intracellular shift and rebound hyperkalaemia.","id":"endocrine-054","globalNumber":1554,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":55,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with thyrotoxic periodic paralysis initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["arrhythmia, high-output failure and hepatic dysfunction","laryngospasm, seizure and ventricular arrhythmia","thyroid storm, atrial arrhythmia and heart failure","respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis and arrhythmia","respiratory weakness, ventricular arrhythmia and rebound hyperkalaemia"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"respiratory weakness, ventricular arrhythmia and rebound hyperkalaemia","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is respiratory weakness, ventricular arrhythmia and rebound hyperkalaemia. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of thyrotoxic periodic paralysis.","learningPoint":"Thyrotoxic periodic paralysis: hypokalaemic paralysis with suppressed TSH and no sensory or sphincter involvement. Avoid aggressively replacing potassium without recognising intracellular shift and rebound hyperkalaemia.","id":"endocrine-055","globalNumber":1555,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":56,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with recurrent fasting neuroglycopenic episodes that resolve with glucose in a patient not using glucose-lowering medication?","options":["Insulinoma with recurrent hypoglycaemia","Adrenal crisis","Acute bilateral adrenal haemorrhage","Myxoedema coma","Pituitary apoplexy"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Insulinoma with recurrent hypoglycaemia","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Insulinoma with recurrent hypoglycaemia. The key discriminator is inappropriately elevated insulin and C-peptide during documented hypoglycaemia. Immediate priorities include: correct hypoglycaemia, prevent recurrence with continuous carbohydrate support and obtain endocrine localisation advice.","learningPoint":"Insulinoma with recurrent hypoglycaemia: inappropriately elevated insulin and C-peptide during documented hypoglycaemia. Avoid discharging after a single glucose bolus without preventing recurrent hypoglycaemia.","id":"endocrine-056","globalNumber":1556,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":57,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with insulinoma with recurrent hypoglycaemia presenting with recurrent fasting neuroglycopenic episodes that resolve with glucose in a patient not using glucose-lowering medication, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["give dextrose for immediate correction and octreotide to suppress further insulin release","correct hypoglycaemia, prevent recurrence with continuous carbohydrate support and obtain endocrine localisation advice","give immediate stress-dose corticosteroid and haemodynamic support without awaiting confirmatory testing","give stress-dose hydrocortisone and obtain urgent neurosurgical and endocrine review","give intravenous thyroid hormone, stress-dose hydrocortisone and cautious supportive care"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"correct hypoglycaemia, prevent recurrence with continuous carbohydrate support and obtain endocrine localisation advice","explanation":"The priority is to correct hypoglycaemia, prevent recurrence with continuous carbohydrate support and obtain endocrine localisation advice. The decisive feature is inappropriately elevated insulin and C-peptide during documented hypoglycaemia. A major error is discharging after a single glucose bolus without preventing recurrent hypoglycaemia.","learningPoint":"Insulinoma with recurrent hypoglycaemia: inappropriately elevated insulin and C-peptide during documented hypoglycaemia. Avoid discharging after a single glucose bolus without preventing recurrent hypoglycaemia.","id":"endocrine-057","globalNumber":1557,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":58,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for insulinoma with recurrent hypoglycaemia?","options":["hypotension refractory to fluid in a steroid-dependent or adrenal patient","acute sellar haemorrhage with cranial nerve dysfunction","inappropriately elevated insulin and C-peptide during documented hypoglycaemia","hypoglycaemia with endogenous insulin secretion and recurrence after dextrose alone","severe decompensated hypothyroidism with hypothermia and CO2 retention"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"inappropriately elevated insulin and C-peptide during documented hypoglycaemia","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is inappropriately elevated insulin and C-peptide during documented hypoglycaemia. In context, this supports Insulinoma with recurrent hypoglycaemia and should prompt clinicians to correct hypoglycaemia, prevent recurrence with continuous carbohydrate support and obtain endocrine localisation advice.","learningPoint":"Insulinoma with recurrent hypoglycaemia: inappropriately elevated insulin and C-peptide during documented hypoglycaemia. Avoid discharging after a single glucose bolus without preventing recurrent hypoglycaemia.","id":"endocrine-058","globalNumber":1558,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":59,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in insulinoma with recurrent hypoglycaemia?","options":["treating only with repeated dextrose boluses that stimulate additional insulin release","delaying steroids until cortisol results return","waiting for MRI before treating suspected adrenal insufficiency","discharging after a single glucose bolus without preventing recurrent hypoglycaemia","aggressive external rewarming that provokes vasodilation and collapse"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"discharging after a single glucose bolus without preventing recurrent hypoglycaemia","explanation":"The hazardous action is discharging after a single glucose bolus without preventing recurrent hypoglycaemia. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is seizure, neurological injury and recurrent profound hypoglycaemia.","learningPoint":"Insulinoma with recurrent hypoglycaemia: inappropriately elevated insulin and C-peptide during documented hypoglycaemia. Avoid discharging after a single glucose bolus without preventing recurrent hypoglycaemia.","id":"endocrine-059","globalNumber":1559,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":60,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of insulinoma with recurrent hypoglycaemia, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["permanent visual loss and cardiovascular collapse","respiratory failure, hypoglycaemia and cardiovascular collapse","refractory shock and life-threatening hyperkalaemia","recurrent neuroglycopenia, aspiration and dysrhythmia","seizure, neurological injury and recurrent profound hypoglycaemia"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"seizure, neurological injury and recurrent profound hypoglycaemia","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is seizure, neurological injury and recurrent profound hypoglycaemia. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of insulinoma with recurrent hypoglycaemia.","learningPoint":"Insulinoma with recurrent hypoglycaemia: inappropriately elevated insulin and C-peptide during documented hypoglycaemia. Avoid discharging after a single glucose bolus without preventing recurrent hypoglycaemia.","id":"endocrine-060","globalNumber":1560,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":61,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by persistent or recurrent hypoglycaemia after access to oral diabetes medication, despite repeated dextrose. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Sulfonylurea-associated recurrent hypoglycaemia","Myxoedema coma","Adrenal crisis","Acute bilateral adrenal haemorrhage","Insulinoma with recurrent hypoglycaemia"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Sulfonylurea-associated recurrent hypoglycaemia","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Sulfonylurea-associated recurrent hypoglycaemia. The key discriminator is hypoglycaemia with endogenous insulin secretion and recurrence after dextrose alone. Immediate priorities include: give dextrose for immediate correction and octreotide to suppress further insulin release.","learningPoint":"Sulfonylurea-associated recurrent hypoglycaemia: hypoglycaemia with endogenous insulin secretion and recurrence after dextrose alone. Avoid treating only with repeated dextrose boluses that stimulate additional insulin release.","id":"endocrine-061","globalNumber":1561,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":62,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is sulfonylurea-associated recurrent hypoglycaemia. The patient has persistent or recurrent hypoglycaemia after access to oral diabetes medication, despite repeated dextrose. What is the best immediate management?","options":["give intravenous thyroid hormone, stress-dose hydrocortisone and cautious supportive care","give dextrose for immediate correction and octreotide to suppress further insulin release","give immediate intravenous hydrocortisone, isotonic fluid and dextrose as required","correct hypoglycaemia, prevent recurrence with continuous carbohydrate support and obtain endocrine localisation advice","give stress-dose hydrocortisone and obtain urgent neurosurgical and endocrine review"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give dextrose for immediate correction and octreotide to suppress further insulin release","explanation":"The priority is to give dextrose for immediate correction and octreotide to suppress further insulin release. The decisive feature is hypoglycaemia with endogenous insulin secretion and recurrence after dextrose alone. A major error is treating only with repeated dextrose boluses that stimulate additional insulin release.","learningPoint":"Sulfonylurea-associated recurrent hypoglycaemia: hypoglycaemia with endogenous insulin secretion and recurrence after dextrose alone. Avoid treating only with repeated dextrose boluses that stimulate additional insulin release.","id":"endocrine-062","globalNumber":1562,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":63,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have sulfonylurea-associated recurrent hypoglycaemia. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["inappropriately elevated insulin and C-peptide during documented hypoglycaemia","bilateral adrenal enlargement or haemorrhage with biochemical adrenal failure","hypoglycaemia with endogenous insulin secretion and recurrence after dextrose alone","acute sellar haemorrhage with cranial nerve dysfunction","severe decompensated hypothyroidism with hypothermia and CO2 retention"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"hypoglycaemia with endogenous insulin secretion and recurrence after dextrose alone","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is hypoglycaemia with endogenous insulin secretion and recurrence after dextrose alone. In context, this supports Sulfonylurea-associated recurrent hypoglycaemia and should prompt clinicians to give dextrose for immediate correction and octreotide to suppress further insulin release.","learningPoint":"Sulfonylurea-associated recurrent hypoglycaemia: hypoglycaemia with endogenous insulin secretion and recurrence after dextrose alone. Avoid treating only with repeated dextrose boluses that stimulate additional insulin release.","id":"endocrine-063","globalNumber":1563,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":64,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with sulfonylurea-associated recurrent hypoglycaemia is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["discharging after a single glucose bolus without preventing recurrent hypoglycaemia","aggressive external rewarming that provokes vasodilation and collapse","delaying steroids until cortisol results return","treating only with repeated dextrose boluses that stimulate additional insulin release","waiting for MRI before treating suspected adrenal insufficiency"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"treating only with repeated dextrose boluses that stimulate additional insulin release","explanation":"The hazardous action is treating only with repeated dextrose boluses that stimulate additional insulin release. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is recurrent neuroglycopenia, aspiration and dysrhythmia.","learningPoint":"Sulfonylurea-associated recurrent hypoglycaemia: hypoglycaemia with endogenous insulin secretion and recurrence after dextrose alone. Avoid treating only with repeated dextrose boluses that stimulate additional insulin release.","id":"endocrine-064","globalNumber":1564,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":65,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with sulfonylurea-associated recurrent hypoglycaemia?","options":["refractory shock and life-threatening hyperkalaemia","refractory shock, hypoglycaemia and death","respiratory failure, hypoglycaemia and cardiovascular collapse","permanent visual loss and cardiovascular collapse","recurrent neuroglycopenia, aspiration and dysrhythmia"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"recurrent neuroglycopenia, aspiration and dysrhythmia","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is recurrent neuroglycopenia, aspiration and dysrhythmia. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of sulfonylurea-associated recurrent hypoglycaemia.","learningPoint":"Sulfonylurea-associated recurrent hypoglycaemia: hypoglycaemia with endogenous insulin secretion and recurrence after dextrose alone. Avoid treating only with repeated dextrose boluses that stimulate additional insulin release.","id":"endocrine-065","globalNumber":1565,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":66,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has seizure or coma with a rapid fall in serum sodium and no competing explanation. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Acute symptomatic hyponatraemia with cerebral oedema","Symptomatic hypocalcaemia","Acute severe hypophosphataemia","Adrenal crisis","Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Acute symptomatic hyponatraemia with cerebral oedema","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Acute symptomatic hyponatraemia with cerebral oedema. The key discriminator is severe neurological symptoms temporally related to hypotonic hyponatraemia. Immediate priorities include: give controlled hypertonic saline boluses with frequent sodium and neurological reassessment.","learningPoint":"Acute symptomatic hyponatraemia with cerebral oedema: severe neurological symptoms temporally related to hypotonic hyponatraemia. Avoid correcting sodium too rapidly after symptoms improve.","id":"endocrine-066","globalNumber":1566,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":67,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for acute symptomatic hyponatraemia with cerebral oedema when the presentation is seizure or coma with a rapid fall in serum sodium and no competing explanation?","options":["give cautious potassium replacement, non-selective beta blockade and treat thyrotoxicosis","give controlled hypertonic saline boluses with frequent sodium and neurological reassessment","replace fluid gradually, correct electrolytes and start low-dose insulin after initial volume restoration","give intravenous thyroid hormone, stress-dose hydrocortisone and cautious supportive care","give intravenous calcium with ECG monitoring and correct magnesium"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give controlled hypertonic saline boluses with frequent sodium and neurological reassessment","explanation":"The priority is to give controlled hypertonic saline boluses with frequent sodium and neurological reassessment. The decisive feature is severe neurological symptoms temporally related to hypotonic hyponatraemia. A major error is correcting sodium too rapidly after symptoms improve.","learningPoint":"Acute symptomatic hyponatraemia with cerebral oedema: severe neurological symptoms temporally related to hypotonic hyponatraemia. Avoid correcting sodium too rapidly after symptoms improve.","id":"endocrine-067","globalNumber":1567,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":68,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for acute symptomatic hyponatraemia with cerebral oedema in this presentation?","options":["high measured osmolality with clinical dehydration and a free-water deficit","very low urine osmolality despite hyperosmolar plasma, improving with desmopressin","severe neurological symptoms temporally related to hypotonic hyponatraemia","severe decompensated hypothyroidism with hypothermia and CO2 retention","hypokalaemic paralysis with suppressed TSH and no sensory or sphincter involvement"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"severe neurological symptoms temporally related to hypotonic hyponatraemia","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is severe neurological symptoms temporally related to hypotonic hyponatraemia. In context, this supports Acute symptomatic hyponatraemia with cerebral oedema and should prompt clinicians to give controlled hypertonic saline boluses with frequent sodium and neurological reassessment.","learningPoint":"Acute symptomatic hyponatraemia with cerebral oedema: severe neurological symptoms temporally related to hypotonic hyponatraemia. Avoid correcting sodium too rapidly after symptoms improve.","id":"endocrine-068","globalNumber":1568,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":69,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in acute symptomatic hyponatraemia with cerebral oedema?","options":["giving repeated desmopressin without matching intake and sodium surveillance","rapidly lowering chronic hypernatraemia before intravascular volume is restored","aggressively replacing potassium without recognising intracellular shift and rebound hyperkalaemia","correcting sodium too rapidly after symptoms improve","giving rapid unmonitored intravenous phosphate or ignoring coexistent hypocalcaemia"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"correcting sodium too rapidly after symptoms improve","explanation":"The hazardous action is correcting sodium too rapidly after symptoms improve. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is brain herniation or osmotic demyelination from inappropriate correction.","learningPoint":"Acute symptomatic hyponatraemia with cerebral oedema: severe neurological symptoms temporally related to hypotonic hyponatraemia. Avoid correcting sodium too rapidly after symptoms improve.","id":"endocrine-069","globalNumber":1569,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":70,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in acute symptomatic hyponatraemia with cerebral oedema, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["respiratory failure, hypoglycaemia and cardiovascular collapse","severe hypernatraemia or treatment-related water intoxication","respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis and arrhythmia","cerebral oedema, seizures and vascular thrombosis","brain herniation or osmotic demyelination from inappropriate correction"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"brain herniation or osmotic demyelination from inappropriate correction","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is brain herniation or osmotic demyelination from inappropriate correction. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of acute symptomatic hyponatraemia with cerebral oedema.","learningPoint":"Acute symptomatic hyponatraemia with cerebral oedema: severe neurological symptoms temporally related to hypotonic hyponatraemia. Avoid correcting sodium too rapidly after symptoms improve.","id":"endocrine-070","globalNumber":1570,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":71,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with profound water deficit with altered consciousness, hypotension and marked hypernatraemia. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Hypernatraemic dehydration","Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state","Central diabetes insipidus after neurosurgery","Adrenal crisis","Euglycaemic ketoacidosis from an SGLT2 inhibitor"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Hypernatraemic dehydration","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Hypernatraemic dehydration. The key discriminator is high measured osmolality with clinical dehydration and a free-water deficit. Immediate priorities include: restore circulation with isotonic fluid, then replace free water gradually with close sodium monitoring.","learningPoint":"Hypernatraemic dehydration: high measured osmolality with clinical dehydration and a free-water deficit. Avoid rapidly lowering chronic hypernatraemia before intravascular volume is restored.","id":"endocrine-071","globalNumber":1571,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":72,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has hypernatraemic dehydration: profound water deficit with altered consciousness, hypotension and marked hypernatraemia. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["give immediate intravenous hydrocortisone, isotonic fluid and dextrose as required","restore circulation with isotonic fluid, then replace free water gradually with close sodium monitoring","give beta blockade when appropriate, thionamide, iodine after thionamide, corticosteroid and supportive care","replace fluid gradually, correct electrolytes and start low-dose insulin after initial volume restoration","give controlled hypertonic saline boluses with frequent sodium and neurological reassessment"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"restore circulation with isotonic fluid, then replace free water gradually with close sodium monitoring","explanation":"The priority is to restore circulation with isotonic fluid, then replace free water gradually with close sodium monitoring. The decisive feature is high measured osmolality with clinical dehydration and a free-water deficit. A major error is rapidly lowering chronic hypernatraemia before intravascular volume is restored.","learningPoint":"Hypernatraemic dehydration: high measured osmolality with clinical dehydration and a free-water deficit. Avoid rapidly lowering chronic hypernatraemia before intravascular volume is restored.","id":"endocrine-072","globalNumber":1572,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":73,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected hypernatraemic dehydration, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["severe neurological symptoms temporally related to hypotonic hyponatraemia","significant ketonaemia with glucose below the usual DKA range","high measured osmolality with clinical dehydration and a free-water deficit","marked effective osmolality with little acidosis","hypotension refractory to fluid in a steroid-dependent or adrenal patient"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"high measured osmolality with clinical dehydration and a free-water deficit","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is high measured osmolality with clinical dehydration and a free-water deficit. In context, this supports Hypernatraemic dehydration and should prompt clinicians to restore circulation with isotonic fluid, then replace free water gradually with close sodium monitoring.","learningPoint":"Hypernatraemic dehydration: high measured osmolality with clinical dehydration and a free-water deficit. Avoid rapidly lowering chronic hypernatraemia before intravascular volume is restored.","id":"endocrine-073","globalNumber":1573,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":74,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of hypernatraemic dehydration, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["dismissing ketoacidosis because the glucose is not markedly elevated","delaying steroids until cortisol results return","rapidly lowering osmolality or giving early high-dose insulin before fluids","rapidly lowering chronic hypernatraemia before intravascular volume is restored","giving repeated desmopressin without matching intake and sodium surveillance"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"rapidly lowering chronic hypernatraemia before intravascular volume is restored","explanation":"The hazardous action is rapidly lowering chronic hypernatraemia before intravascular volume is restored. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is cerebral oedema, seizures and vascular thrombosis.","learningPoint":"Hypernatraemic dehydration: high measured osmolality with clinical dehydration and a free-water deficit. Avoid rapidly lowering chronic hypernatraemia before intravascular volume is restored.","id":"endocrine-074","globalNumber":1574,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":75,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with hypernatraemic dehydration initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["arrhythmia, high-output failure and hepatic dysfunction","refractory shock and life-threatening hyperkalaemia","cerebral oedema, thrombosis and circulatory collapse","brain herniation or osmotic demyelination from inappropriate correction","cerebral oedema, seizures and vascular thrombosis"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"cerebral oedema, seizures and vascular thrombosis","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is cerebral oedema, seizures and vascular thrombosis. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of hypernatraemic dehydration.","learningPoint":"Hypernatraemic dehydration: high measured osmolality with clinical dehydration and a free-water deficit. Avoid rapidly lowering chronic hypernatraemia before intravascular volume is restored.","id":"endocrine-075","globalNumber":1575,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":76,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with abrupt polyuria, rising sodium and dilute urine after pituitary or hypothalamic intervention?","options":["Central diabetes insipidus after neurosurgery","Adrenal crisis","Myxoedema coma","Hypernatraemic dehydration","Acute bilateral adrenal haemorrhage"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Central diabetes insipidus after neurosurgery","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Central diabetes insipidus after neurosurgery. The key discriminator is very low urine osmolality despite hyperosmolar plasma, improving with desmopressin. Immediate priorities include: replace volume and free water, give carefully titrated desmopressin and monitor sodium frequently.","learningPoint":"Central diabetes insipidus after neurosurgery: very low urine osmolality despite hyperosmolar plasma, improving with desmopressin. Avoid giving repeated desmopressin without matching intake and sodium surveillance.","id":"endocrine-076","globalNumber":1576,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":77,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with central diabetes insipidus after neurosurgery presenting with abrupt polyuria, rising sodium and dilute urine after pituitary or hypothalamic intervention, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["give intravenous thyroid hormone, stress-dose hydrocortisone and cautious supportive care","replace volume and free water, give carefully titrated desmopressin and monitor sodium frequently","give immediate intravenous hydrocortisone, isotonic fluid and dextrose as required","restore circulation with isotonic fluid, then replace free water gradually with close sodium monitoring","give controlled hypertonic saline boluses with frequent sodium and neurological reassessment"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"replace volume and free water, give carefully titrated desmopressin and monitor sodium frequently","explanation":"The priority is to replace volume and free water, give carefully titrated desmopressin and monitor sodium frequently. The decisive feature is very low urine osmolality despite hyperosmolar plasma, improving with desmopressin. A major error is giving repeated desmopressin without matching intake and sodium surveillance.","learningPoint":"Central diabetes insipidus after neurosurgery: very low urine osmolality despite hyperosmolar plasma, improving with desmopressin. Avoid giving repeated desmopressin without matching intake and sodium surveillance.","id":"endocrine-077","globalNumber":1577,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":78,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for central diabetes insipidus after neurosurgery?","options":["severe decompensated hypothyroidism with hypothermia and CO2 retention","hypotension refractory to fluid in a steroid-dependent or adrenal patient","very low urine osmolality despite hyperosmolar plasma, improving with desmopressin","severe neurological symptoms temporally related to hypotonic hyponatraemia","marked effective osmolality with little acidosis"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"very low urine osmolality despite hyperosmolar plasma, improving with desmopressin","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is very low urine osmolality despite hyperosmolar plasma, improving with desmopressin. In context, this supports Central diabetes insipidus after neurosurgery and should prompt clinicians to replace volume and free water, give carefully titrated desmopressin and monitor sodium frequently.","learningPoint":"Central diabetes insipidus after neurosurgery: very low urine osmolality despite hyperosmolar plasma, improving with desmopressin. Avoid giving repeated desmopressin without matching intake and sodium surveillance.","id":"endocrine-078","globalNumber":1578,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":79,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in central diabetes insipidus after neurosurgery?","options":["delaying steroids until cortisol results return","rapidly lowering osmolality or giving early high-dose insulin before fluids","rapidly lowering chronic hypernatraemia before intravascular volume is restored","giving repeated desmopressin without matching intake and sodium surveillance","aggressive external rewarming that provokes vasodilation and collapse"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"giving repeated desmopressin without matching intake and sodium surveillance","explanation":"The hazardous action is giving repeated desmopressin without matching intake and sodium surveillance. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is severe hypernatraemia or treatment-related water intoxication.","learningPoint":"Central diabetes insipidus after neurosurgery: very low urine osmolality despite hyperosmolar plasma, improving with desmopressin. Avoid giving repeated desmopressin without matching intake and sodium surveillance.","id":"endocrine-079","globalNumber":1579,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":80,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of central diabetes insipidus after neurosurgery, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["respiratory failure, hypoglycaemia and cardiovascular collapse","refractory shock and life-threatening hyperkalaemia","pulmonary oedema, stroke and circulatory collapse","cerebral oedema, seizures and vascular thrombosis","severe hypernatraemia or treatment-related water intoxication"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"severe hypernatraemia or treatment-related water intoxication","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is severe hypernatraemia or treatment-related water intoxication. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of central diabetes insipidus after neurosurgery.","learningPoint":"Central diabetes insipidus after neurosurgery: very low urine osmolality despite hyperosmolar plasma, improving with desmopressin. Avoid giving repeated desmopressin without matching intake and sodium surveillance.","id":"endocrine-080","globalNumber":1580,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":81,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by shock, abdominal or flank pain, hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia during sepsis, anticoagulation or major stress. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Acute bilateral adrenal haemorrhage","Myxoedema coma","Adrenal crisis","Phaeochromocytoma crisis","Pituitary apoplexy"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Acute bilateral adrenal haemorrhage","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Acute bilateral adrenal haemorrhage. The key discriminator is bilateral adrenal enlargement or haemorrhage with biochemical adrenal failure. Immediate priorities include: give immediate stress-dose corticosteroid and haemodynamic support without awaiting confirmatory testing.","learningPoint":"Acute bilateral adrenal haemorrhage: bilateral adrenal enlargement or haemorrhage with biochemical adrenal failure. Avoid delaying steroids until cortisol or imaging results return.","id":"endocrine-081","globalNumber":1581,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":82,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is acute bilateral adrenal haemorrhage. The patient has shock, abdominal or flank pain, hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia during sepsis, anticoagulation or major stress. What is the best immediate management?","options":["give immediate intravenous hydrocortisone, isotonic fluid and dextrose as required","give immediate stress-dose corticosteroid and haemodynamic support without awaiting confirmatory testing","give stress-dose hydrocortisone and obtain urgent neurosurgical and endocrine review","give intravenous thyroid hormone, stress-dose hydrocortisone and cautious supportive care","control with titratable alpha blockade and specialist critical care support"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give immediate stress-dose corticosteroid and haemodynamic support without awaiting confirmatory testing","explanation":"The priority is to give immediate stress-dose corticosteroid and haemodynamic support without awaiting confirmatory testing. The decisive feature is bilateral adrenal enlargement or haemorrhage with biochemical adrenal failure. A major error is delaying steroids until cortisol or imaging results return.","learningPoint":"Acute bilateral adrenal haemorrhage: bilateral adrenal enlargement or haemorrhage with biochemical adrenal failure. Avoid delaying steroids until cortisol or imaging results return.","id":"endocrine-082","globalNumber":1582,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":83,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5"