orsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is refractory shock, hypoglycaemia and death.","learningPoint":"Acute bilateral adrenal haemorrhage: bilateral adrenal enlargement or haemorrhage with biochemical adrenal failure. Avoid delaying steroids until cortisol or imaging results return.","id":"endocrine-084","globalNumber":1584,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":85,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with acute bilateral adrenal haemorrhage?","options":["respiratory failure, hypoglycaemia and cardiovascular collapse","refractory shock and life-threatening hyperkalaemia","permanent visual loss and cardiovascular collapse","recurrent neuroglycopenia, aspiration and dysrhythmia","refractory shock, hypoglycaemia and death"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"refractory shock, hypoglycaemia and death","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is refractory shock, hypoglycaemia and death. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of acute bilateral adrenal haemorrhage.","learningPoint":"Acute bilateral adrenal haemorrhage: bilateral adrenal enlargement or haemorrhage with biochemical adrenal failure. Avoid delaying steroids until cortisol or imaging results return.","id":"endocrine-085","globalNumber":1585,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":86,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has prolonged symptomatic hypocalcaemia after parathyroidectomy with low phosphate and high bone uptake. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Hungry bone syndrome","Acute severe hypophosphataemia","Hypercalcaemic crisis","Acute symptomatic hyponatraemia with cerebral oedema","Thyrotoxic periodic paralysis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Hungry bone syndrome","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Hungry bone syndrome. The key discriminator is p