x":3,"answer":"treating a single calcium value without anticipating prolonged replacement needs","explanation":"The hazardous action is treating a single calcium value without anticipating prolonged replacement needs. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is tetany, seizures and ventricular arrhythmia.","learningPoint":"Hungry bone syndrome: postoperative hypocalcaemia with low phosphate and magnesium rather than recurrent hyperparathyroidism. Avoid treating a single calcium value without anticipating prolonged replacement needs.","id":"endocrine-089","globalNumber":1589,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":90,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in hungry bone syndrome, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis and arrhythmia","dysrhythmia, pancreatitis and acute kidney injury","laryngospasm, seizure and ventricular arrhythmia","respiratory weakness, ventricular arrhythmia and rebound hyperkalaemia","tetany, seizures and ventricular arrhythmia"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"tetany, seizures and ventricular arrhythmia","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is tetany, seizures and ventricular arrhythmia. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of hungry bone syndrome.","learningPoint":"Hungry bone syndrome: postoperative hypocalcaemia with low phosphate and magnesium rather than recurrent hyperparathyroidism. Avoid treating a single calcium value without anticipating prolonged replacement needs.","id":"endocrine-090","globalNumber":1590,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":91,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with respiratory weakness, haemolysis or rhabdomyolysis in a malnourished or refeeding patient with very low phosphate. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Acu