onitored intravenous phosphate or ignoring coexistent hypocalcaemia.","learningPoint":"Acute severe hypophosphataemia: profound phosphate depletion explaining neuromuscular and haematological dysfunction. Avoid giving rapid unmonitored intravenous phosphate or ignoring coexistent hypocalcaemia.","id":"endocrine-092","globalNumber":1592,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":93,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected acute severe hypophosphataemia, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["hypokalaemic paralysis with suppressed TSH and no sensory or sphincter involvement","carpopedal spasm or QT prolongation with low ionised calcium","profound phosphate depletion explaining neuromuscular and haematological dysfunction","markedly elevated corrected or ionised calcium with neurocognitive change","severe multisystem decompensation rather than thyroid hormone level alone"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"profound phosphate depletion explaining neuromuscular and haematological dysfunction","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is profound phosphate depletion explaining neuromuscular and haematological dysfunction. In context, this supports Acute severe hypophosphataemia and should prompt clinicians to replace phosphate carefully, correct associated electrolyte deficits and treat the precipitant.","learningPoint":"Acute severe hypophosphataemia: profound phosphate depletion explaining neuromuscular and haematological dysfunction. Avoid giving rapid unmonitored intravenous phosphate or ignoring coexistent hypocalcaemia.","id":"endocrine-093","globalNumber":1593,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":94,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of acute severe hypophosphataemia, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["correcting sodium too rapidly after symptoms improve","treating a single calciu