and start specialist-directed antithyroid therapy based on mechanism","explanation":"The priority is to control adrenergic symptoms and start specialist-directed antithyroid therapy based on mechanism. The decisive feature is suppressed TSH and elevated thyroid hormones after a large iodine load. A major error is assuming additional iodine exposure is harmless in uncontrolled thyrotoxicosis.","learningPoint":"Iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis: suppressed TSH and elevated thyroid hormones after a large iodine load. Avoid assuming additional iodine exposure is harmless in uncontrolled thyrotoxicosis.","id":"endocrine-097","globalNumber":1597,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":98,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis?","options":["labile hypertension with catecholamine-mediated cardiac dysfunction","severe multisystem decompensation rather than thyroid hormone level alone","suppressed TSH and elevated thyroid hormones after a large iodine load","hypokalaemic paralysis with suppressed TSH and no sensory or sphincter involvement","hypotension refractory to fluid in a steroid-dependent or adrenal patient"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"suppressed TSH and elevated thyroid hormones after a large iodine load","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is suppressed TSH and elevated thyroid hormones after a large iodine load. In context, this supports Iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis and should prompt clinicians to control adrenergic symptoms and start specialist-directed antithyroid therapy based on mechanism.","learningPoint":"Iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis: suppressed TSH and elevated thyroid hormones after a large iodine load. Avoid assuming additional iodine exposure is harmless in uncontrolled thyrotoxicosis.","id":"endocrine-098","globalNumber":1598,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":99,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis?","options":["administering iodine before blocking new hormone synthesis","aggressively replacing potassium without recognising intracellular shift and rebound hyperkalaemia","delaying steroids until cortisol results return","assuming additional iodine exposure is harmless in uncontrolled thyrotoxicosis","giving isolated beta blockade before alpha blockade"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"assuming additional iodine exposure is harmless in uncontrolled thyrotoxicosis","explanation":"The hazardous action is assuming additional iodine exposure is harmless in uncontrolled thyrotoxicosis. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is thyroid storm, atrial arrhythmia and heart failure.","learningPoint":"Iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis: suppressed TSH and elevated thyroid hormones after a large iodine load. Avoid assuming additional iodine exposure is harmless in uncontrolled thyrotoxicosis.","id":"endocrine-099","globalNumber":1599,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Endocrine","categoryNumber":100,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["arrhythmia, high-output failure and hepatic dysfunction","respiratory weakness, ventricular arrhythmia and rebound hyperkalaemia","respiratory failure, hypoglycaemia and cardiovascular collapse","pulmonary oedema, stroke and circulatory collapse","thyroid storm, atrial arrhythmia and heart failure"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"thyroid storm, atrial arrhythmia and heart failure","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is thyroid storm, atrial arrhythmia and heart failure. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis.","learningPoint":"Iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis: suppressed TSH and elevated thyroid hormones after a large iodine load. Avoid assuming additional iodine exposure is harmless in uncontrolled thyrotoxicosis.","id":"endocrine-100","globalNumber":1600,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":51,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with days to weeks of renal failure with haematuria, proteinuria and red-cell casts. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis","Renal cortical necrosis","Scleroderma renal crisis","Haemolytic uraemic syndrome","Acute interstitial nephritis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis. The key discriminator is active urinary sediment with rapidly rising creatinine and serological evidence of crescentic disease. Immediate priorities include: obtain urgent nephrology input, send immune studies and begin cause-directed immunosuppression when indicated.","learningPoint":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis: active urinary sediment with rapidly rising creatinine and serological evidence of crescentic disease. Avoid waiting for outpatient biopsy while renal function rapidly deteriorates.","id":"renal-051","globalNumber":1601,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":52,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis: days to weeks of renal failure with haematuria, proteinuria and red-cell casts. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["stop the offending agent, provide supportive care and obtain nephrology input regarding corticosteroids","obtain urgent nephrology input, send immune studies and begin cause-directed immunosuppression when indicated","support organ function, treat the precipitating shock or coagulopathy and involve nephrology early","control blood pressure with titratable intravenous therapy, manage fluid overload and treat the renal cause","start an ACE inhibitor urgently and titrate despite an initial creatinine rise"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"obtain urgent nephrology input, send immune studies and begin cause-directed immunosuppression when indicated","explanation":"The priority is to obtain urgent nephrology input, send immune studies and begin cause-directed immunosuppression when indicated. The decisive feature is active urinary sediment with rapidly rising creatinine and serological evidence of crescentic disease. A major error is waiting for outpatient biopsy while renal function rapidly deteriorates.","learningPoint":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis: active urinary sediment with rapidly rising creatinine and serological evidence of crescentic disease. Avoid waiting for outpatient biopsy while renal function rapidly deteriorates.","id":"renal-052","globalNumber":1602,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":53,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["red-cell casts with diffuse alveolar haemorrhage","nephritic urine with acute hypertension and end-organ injury","active urinary sediment with rapidly rising creatinine and serological evidence of crescentic disease","schistocytes and renal-predominant thrombotic microangiopathy after Shiga-toxin exposure","sterile pyuria and renal dysfunction temporally linked to a likely trigger"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"active urinary sediment with rapidly rising creatinine and serological evidence of crescentic disease","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is active urinary sediment with rapidly rising creatinine and serological evidence of crescentic disease. In context, this supports Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis and should prompt clinicians to obtain urgent nephrology input, send immune studies and begin cause-directed immunosuppression when indicated.","learningPoint":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis: active urinary sediment with rapidly rising creatinine and serological evidence of crescentic disease. Avoid waiting for outpatient biopsy while renal function rapidly deteriorates.","id":"renal-053","globalNumber":1603,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":54,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["anticoagulating presumed pulmonary embolism without considering alveolar haemorrhage","assuming all post-shock acute tubular injury will recover rapidly","treating only with beta blockade or withholding ACE inhibition because creatinine rises","waiting for outpatient biopsy while renal function rapidly deteriorates","lowering blood pressure abruptly to normal or giving indiscriminate fluid boluses"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"waiting for outpatient biopsy while renal function rapidly deteriorates","explanation":"The hazardous action is waiting for outpatient biopsy while renal function rapidly deteriorates. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is dialysis dependence, pulmonary haemorrhage and irreversible renal failure.","learningPoint":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis: active urinary sediment with rapidly rising creatinine and serological evidence of crescentic disease. Avoid waiting for outpatient biopsy while renal function rapidly deteriorates.","id":"renal-054","globalNumber":1604,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":55,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["intracranial haemorrhage, pulmonary oedema and acute kidney injury","permanent dialysis dependence and severe hypertension","respiratory failure and rapidly progressive renal failure","renal failure, neurological injury and hypertension","dialysis dependence, pulmonary haemorrhage and irreversible renal failure"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"dialysis dependence, pulmonary haemorrhage and irreversible renal failure","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is dialysis dependence, pulmonary haemorrhage and irreversible renal failure. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis.","learningPoint":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis: active urinary sediment with rapidly rising creatinine and serological evidence of crescentic disease. Avoid waiting for outpatient biopsy while renal function rapidly deteriorates.","id":"renal-055","globalNumber":1605,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":56,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with oedema, haematuria, severe hypertension and encephalopathy after an infectious or immune trigger?","options":["Hypertensive emergency from acute glomerulonephritis","Renal cortical necrosis","Rhabdomyolysis with acute kidney injury","Scleroderma renal crisis","Pulmonary-renal syndrome"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Hypertensive emergency from acute glomerulonephritis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Hypertensive emergency from acute glomerulonephritis. The key discriminator is nephritic urine with acute hypertension and end-organ injury. Immediate priorities include: control blood pressure with titratable intravenous therapy, manage fluid overload and treat the renal cause.","learningPoint":"Hypertensive emergency from acute glomerulonephritis: nephritic urine with acute hypertension and end-organ injury. Avoid lowering blood pressure abruptly to normal or giving indiscriminate fluid boluses.","id":"renal-056","globalNumber":1606,"