 rise with electrolyte collapse in an at-risk cancer patient. Immediate priorities include: give aggressive monitored hydration, rasburicase when indicated and urgent renal support for refractory complications.","learningPoint":"Tumour lysis syndrome: rapid urate and phosphate rise with electrolyte collapse in an at-risk cancer patient. Avoid using potassium-containing fluid or delaying treatment until oliguria is established.","id":"renal-061","globalNumber":1611,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":62,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is tumour lysis syndrome. The patient has acute kidney injury, hyperkalaemia, hyperphosphataemia and hypocalcaemia after treatment of a high-burden malignancy. What is the best immediate management?","options":["stop or slow dialysis and treat cerebral oedema supportively with specialist input","give aggressive monitored hydration, rasburicase when indicated and urgent renal support for refractory complications","give early isotonic crystalloid, monitor potassium and treat the underlying cause","start an ACE inhibitor urgently and titrate despite an initial creatinine rise","replace a proportion of urine losses with isotonic fluid and monitor sodium, potassium and magnesium closely"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give aggressive monitored hydration, rasburicase when indicated and urgent renal support for refractory complications","explanation":"The priority is to give aggressive monitored hydration, rasburicase when indicated and urgent renal support for refractory complications. The decisive feature is rapid urate and phosphate rise with electrolyte collapse in an at-risk cancer patient. A major error is using potassium-containing fluid or delaying treatment until oliguria is established.","learningPoint":"Tumour lysis syndrome: rapid urate and phosphate rise with electrolyte collapse in an at-risk cancer patient. Avoid using potassium-containing fluid or delaying treatment unti