btain nephrology or haematology advice. The decisive feature is schistocytes and renal-predominant thrombotic microangiopathy after Shiga-toxin exposure. A major error is giving prophylactic platelet transfusion or antibiotics that may increase toxin release without indication.","learningPoint":"Haemolytic uraemic syndrome: schistocytes and renal-predominant thrombotic microangiopathy after Shiga-toxin exposure. Avoid giving prophylactic platelet transfusion or antibiotics that may increase toxin release without indication.","id":"renal-067","globalNumber":1617,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":68,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for haemolytic uraemic syndrome in this presentation?","options":["bilateral cortical non-enhancement with preserved medullary perfusion","active urinary sediment with rapidly rising creatinine and serological evidence of crescentic disease","schistocytes and renal-predominant thrombotic microangiopathy after Shiga-toxin exposure","sterile pyuria and renal dysfunction temporally linked to a likely trigger","malignant hypertension with schistocytes and recent diffuse skin disease"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"schistocytes and renal-predominant thrombotic microangiopathy after Shiga-toxin exposure","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is schistocytes and renal-predominant thrombotic microangiopathy after Shiga-toxin exposure. In context, this supports Haemolytic uraemic syndrome and should prompt clinicians to provide meticulous supportive care, avoid antimotility drugs and obtain nephrology or haematology advice.","learningPoint":"Haemolytic uraemic syndrome: schistocytes and renal-predominant thrombotic microangiopathy after Shiga-toxin exposure. Avoid giving prophylactic platelet transfusion or antibiotics that may increase toxin release without indication.","id":"renal-068","globalNumber":1618,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":69,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in haemolytic uraemic syndrome?","options":["treating only with beta blockade or withholding ACE inhibition because creatinine rises","assuming all post-shock acute tubular injury will recover rapidly","repeated large crystalloid boluses or nephrotoxic contrast without reassessing the diagnosis","giving prophylactic platelet transfusion or antibiotics that may increase toxin release without indication","continuing the culprit medication because the classic triad is incomplete"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"giving prophylactic platelet transfusion or antibiotics that may increase toxin release without indication","explanation":"The hazardous action is giving prophylactic platelet transfusion or antibiotics that may increase toxin release without indication. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is renal failure, neurological injury and hypertension.","learningPoint":"Haemolytic uraemic syndrome: schistocytes and renal-predominant thrombotic microangiopathy after Shiga-toxin exposure. Avoid giving prophylactic platelet transfusion or antibiotics that may increase toxin release without indication.","id":"renal-069","globalNumber":1619,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":70,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in haemolytic uraemic syndrome, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["intracranial haemorrhage, pulmonary oedema and acute kidney injury","permanent dialysis dependence and severe hypertension","progressive renal failure and chronic interstitial fibrosis","pulmonary oedema, encephalopathy and irreversible renal failure","renal failure, neurological injury and hypertension"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"renal failure, neurological injury and hypertension","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is renal failure, neurological injury and hypertension. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of haemolytic uraemic syndrome.","learningPoint":"Haemolytic uraemic syndrome: schistocytes and renal-predominant thrombotic microangiopathy after Shiga-toxin exposure. Avoid giving prophylactic platelet transfusion or antibiotics that may increase toxin release without indication.","id":"renal-070","globalNumber":1620,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":71,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with anuria or severe oliguria with hydronephrosis from pelvic malignancy, stones or retroperitoneal disease. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Bilateral obstructive uropathy causing acute kidney injury","Post-obstructive diuresis","Renal infarction","Renal cortical necrosis","Infected obstructed urinary system"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Bilateral obstructive uropathy causing acute kidney injury","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Bilateral obstructive uropathy causing acute kidney injury. The key discriminator is bilateral collecting-system obstruction or obstruction in a solitary kidney. Immediate priorities include: decompress urgently with ureteric stents or nephrostomy while treating infection and electrolyte problems.","learningPoint":"Bilateral obstructive uropathy causing acute kidney injury: bilateral collecting-system obstruction or obstruction in a solitary kidney. Avoid waiting for spontaneous passage despite anuria, infection or rising potassium.","id":"renal-071","globalNumber":1621,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":72,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has bilateral obstructive uropathy causing acute kidney injury: anuria or severe oliguria with hydronephrosis from pelvic malignancy, stones or retroperitoneal disease. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["support organ function, treat the precipitating shock or coagulopathy and involve nephrology early","decompress urgently with ureteric stents or nephrostomy while treating infection and electrolyte problems","control blood pressure with titratable intravenous therapy, manage fluid overload and treat the renal cause","give antibiotics and arrange urgent urinary decompression","obtain contrast vascular imaging and discuss anticoagulation or reperfusion urgently"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"decompress urgently with ureteric stents or nephrostomy while treating infection and electrolyte problems","explanation":"The priority is to decompress urgently with ureteric stents or nephrostomy while treating infection and electrolyte problems. The decisive feature is bilateral collecting-system obstruction or obstruction in a solitary kidney. A major error is waiting for spontaneous passage despite anuria, infection or rising potassium.","learningPoint":"Bilateral obstructive uropathy causing acute kidney injury: bilateral collecting-system obstruction or obstruction in a solitary kidney. Avoid waiting for spontaneous passage despite anuria, infection or rising potassium.","id":"renal-072","globalNumber":1622,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":73,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected bilateral obstructive uropathy causing acute kidney injury, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["haem-positive urine with few red cells and marked CK elevation","pyonephrosis or hydronephrosis with systemic infection","bilateral collecting-system obstruction or obstruction in a solitary kidney","bilateral cortical non-enhancement with preserved medullary perfusion","wedge-shaped perfusion defects with disproportionate LDH elevation"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"bilateral collecting-system obstruction or obstruction in a solitary kidney","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is bilateral collecting-system obstruction or obstruction in a solitary kidney. In context, this supports Bilateral obstructive uropathy causing acute kidney injury and should prompt clinicians to decompress urgently with ureteric stents or nephrostomy while treating infection and electrolyte problems.","learningPoint":"Bilateral obstructive uropathy causing acute kidney injury: bilateral collecting-system obstruction or obstruction in a solitary kidney. Avoid waiting for spontaneous passage despite anuria, infection or rising potassium.","id":"renal-073","globalNumber":1623,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":74,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of bilateral obstructive uropathy causing acute kidney injury, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["lowering blood pressure abruptly to normal or giving indiscriminate fluid boluses","continuing the culprit medication because the classic triad is incomplete","assuming all post-shock acute tubular injury will recover rapidly","waiting for spontaneous passage despite anuria, infection or rising potassium","dismissing the pain as renal colic when no calculus is seen"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"waiting for spontaneous passage despite anuria, infection or rising potassium","explanation":"The hazardous action is waiting for spontaneous passage despite anuria, infection or rising potassium. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is urosepsis, hyperkalaemia and irreversible renal injury.","learningPoint":"Bilateral obstructive uropathy causing acute kidney injury: bilateral collecting-system obstruction or obstruction in a solitary kidney. Avoid waiting for spontaneous passage despite anuria, infection or rising potassium.","id":"renal-074","globalNumber":1624,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":75,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with bilateral obstructive uropathy causing acute kidney injury initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["hypovolaemic shock, hypokalaemia and hypernatraemia","septic shock and renal loss","renovascular hypertension and permanent renal loss","permanent dialysis dependence and severe hypertension","urosepsis, hyperkalaemia and irreversible renal injury"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"urosepsis, hyperkalaemia and irreversible renal injury","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is urosepsis, hyperkalaemia and irreversible renal injury. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of bilateral obstructive uropathy causing acute kidney injury.","learningPoint":"Bilateral obstructive uropathy causing acute kidney injury: bilateral collecting-system obstruction or obstruction in a solitary kidney. Avoid waiting for spontaneous passage despite anuria, infection or rising potassium.","id":"renal-075","globalNumber":1625,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":76,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with very high urine output with falling volume and electrolytes after relief of prolonged urinary obstruction?","options":["Post-obstructive diuresis","Renal infarction","Renal cortical necrosis","Infected obstructed urinary system","Lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Post-obstructive diuresis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Post-obstructive diuresis. The key discriminator is polyuria exceeding expected physiologic clearance after decompression. Immediate priorities include: replace a proportion of urine losses with isotonic fluid and monitor sodium, potassium and magnesium closely.","learningPoint":"Post-obstructive diuresis: polyuria exceeding expected physiologic clearance after decompression. Avoid matching every millilitre with excessive fluid and perpetuating diuresis, or ignoring losses entirely.","id":"renal-076","globalNumber":1626,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":77,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with post-obstructive diuresis presenting with very high urine output with falling volume and electrolytes after relief of prolonged urinary obstruction, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["give antibiotics and arrange urgent urinary decompression","replace a proportion of urine losses with isotonic fluid and monitor sodium, potassium and magnesium closely","give intravenous calcium immediately, shift potassium intracellularly and arrange definitive removal","give aggressive monitored hydration, rasburicase when indicated and urgent renal support for refractory complications","replace free water carefully, stop lithium and use amiloride when appropriate"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"replace a proportion of urine losses with isotonic fluid and monitor sodium, potassium and magnesium closely","explanation":"The priority is to replace a proportion of urine losses with isotonic fluid and monitor sodium, potassium and magnesium closely. The decisive feature is polyuria exceeding expected physiologic clearance after decompression. A major error is matching every millilitre with excessive fluid and perpetuating diuresis, or ignoring losses entirely.","learningPoint":"Post-obstructive diuresis: polyuria exceeding expected physiologic clearance after decompression. Avoid matching every millilitre with excessive fluid and perpetuating diuresis, or ignoring losses entirely.","id":"renal-077","globalNumber":1627,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":78,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for post-obstructive diuresis?","options":["neurologic deterioration temporally related to rapid urea removal","bila