ectly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in hepatorenal syndrome–acute kidney injury?","options":["using potassium-containing fluid or delaying treatment until oliguria is established","continuing the culprit medication because the classic triad is incomplete","assuming all post-shock acute tubular injury will recover rapidly","repeated large crystalloid boluses or nephrotoxic contrast without reassessing the diagnosis","routine bicarbonate or mannitol use without an individual indication"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"repeated large crystalloid boluses or nephrotoxic contrast without reassessing the diagnosis","explanation":"The hazardous action is repeated large crystalloid boluses or nephrotoxic contrast without reassessing the diagnosis. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is refractory renal failure, encephalopathy and death.","learningPoint":"Hepatorenal syndrome–acute kidney injury: functional renal failure in cirrhosis after excluding shock, nephrotoxins and structural kidney disease. Avoid repeated large crystalloid boluses or nephrotoxic contrast without reassessing the diagnosis.","id":"renal-089","globalNumber":1639,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":90,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in hepatorenal syndrome–acute kidney injury, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["progressive renal failure and chronic interstitial fibrosis","hyperkalaemia, compartment syndrome and acute kidney injury","ventricular arrhythmia, seizures and dialysis-requiring renal failure","renal failure, neurological injury and hypertension","refractory renal failure, encephalopathy and death"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"refractory renal failure, encephalopathy and death","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is refractory renal failure, encephalopathy and death. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisatio