 access. Avoid placing a circumferential tourniquet that thromboses the access unless required for exsanguination.","id":"renal-093","globalNumber":1643,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":94,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of dialysis access haemorrhage, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["rapidly correcting chronic hypernatraemia","continuing high-efficiency dialysis despite evolving neurology","delaying membrane stabilisation while awaiting a repeat laboratory sample","placing a circumferential tourniquet that thromboses the access unless required for exsanguination","waiting for culture confirmation before treatment"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"placing a circumferential tourniquet that thromboses the access unless required for exsanguination","explanation":"The hazardous action is placing a circumferential tourniquet that thromboses the access unless required for exsanguination. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is exsanguination, access thrombosis and distal ischaemia.","learningPoint":"Dialysis access haemorrhage: high-pressure pulsatile bleeding from a mature dialysis access. Avoid placing a circumferential tourniquet that thromboses the access unless required for exsanguination.","id":"renal-094","globalNumber":1644,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":95,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with dialysis access haemorrhage initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["pericardial effusion and tamponade","ventricular fibrillation or asystole","sepsis, catheter loss and encapsulating peritoneal injury","seizures and cerebral oedema from overcorrection","exsanguination, access thrombosis and distal ischaemia"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"exsanguination, access thrombosis and distal ischaemia","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is exsanguination, access thrombosis and distal ischaemia. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of dialysis access haemorrhage.","learningPoint":"Dialysis access haemorrhage: high-pressure pulsatile bleeding from a mature dialysis access. Avoid placing a circumferential tourniquet that thromboses the access unless required for exsanguination.","id":"renal-095","globalNumber":1645,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":96,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with persistent anuria after obstetric catastrophe, sepsis or disseminated intravascular coagulation?","options":["Renal cortical necrosis","Rhabdomyolysis with acute kidney injury","Acute interstitial nephritis","Hypertensive emergency from acute glomerulonephritis","Scleroderma renal crisis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Renal cortical necrosis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Renal cortical necrosis. The key discriminator is bilateral cortical non-enhancement with preserved medullary perfusion. Immediate priorities include: support organ function, treat the precipitating shock or coagulopathy and involve nephrology early.","learningPoint":"Renal cortical necrosis: bilateral cortical non-enhancement with preserved medullary perfusion. Avoid assuming all post-shock acute tubular injury will recover rapidly.","id":"renal-096","globalNumber":1646,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":97,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with renal cortical necrosis presenting with persistent anuria after obstetric catastrophe, sepsis or disseminated intravascular coagulation, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["start an ACE inhibitor urgently and titrate despite an initial creatinine rise","support organ function, treat the precipitating shock or coagulopathy and involve nephrology early","obtain contrast vascular imaging and discuss anticoagulation or reperfusion urgently","decompress urgently with ureteric stents or nephrostomy while treating infection and electrolyte problems","stop the offending agent, provide supportive care and obtain nephrology input regarding corticosteroids"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"support organ function, treat the precipitating shock or coagulopathy and involve nephrology early","explanation":"The priority is to support organ function, treat the precipitating shock or coagulopathy and involve nephrology early. The decisive feature is bilateral cortical non-enhancement with preserved medullary perfusion. A major error is assuming all post-shock acute tubular injury will recover rapidly.","learningPoint":"Renal cortical necrosis: bilateral cortical non-enhancement with preserved medullary perfusion. Avoid assuming all post-shock acute tubular injury will recover rapidly.","id":"renal-097","globalNumber":1647,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":98,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for renal cortical necrosis?","options":["malignant hypertension with schistocytes and recent diffuse skin disease","wedge-shaped perfusion defects with disproportionate LDH elevation","bilateral cortical non-enhancement with preserved medullary perfusion","sterile pyuria and renal dysfunction temporally linked to a likely trigger","schistocytes and renal-predominant thrombotic microangiopathy after Shiga-toxin exposure"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"bilateral cortical non-enhancement with preserved medullary perfusion","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is bilateral cortical non-enhancement with preserved medullary perfusion. In context, this supports Renal cortical necrosis and should prompt clinicians to support organ function, treat the precipitating shock or coagulopathy and involve nephrology early.","learningPoint":"Renal cortical necrosis: bilateral cortical non-enhancement with preserved medullary perfusion. Avoid assuming all post-shock acute tubular injury will recover rapidly.","id":"renal-098","globalNumber":1648,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":99,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in renal cortical necrosis?","options":["routine bicarbonate or mannitol use without an individual indication","continuing the culprit medication because the classic triad is incomplete","treating only with beta blockade or withholding ACE inhibition because creatinine rises","assuming all post-shock acute tubular injury will recover rapidly","dismissing the pain as renal colic when no calculus is seen"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"assuming all post-shock acute tubular injury will recover rapidly","explanation":"The hazardous action is assuming all post-shock acute tubular injury will recover rapidly. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is permanent dialysis dependence and severe hypertension.","learningPoint":"Renal cortical necrosis: bilateral cortical non-enhancement with preserved medullary perfusion. Avoid assuming all post-shock acute tubular injury will recover rapidly.","id":"renal-099","globalNumber":1649,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Renal","categoryNumber":100,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of renal cortical necrosis, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["pulmonary oedema, encephalopathy and irreversible renal failure","hyperkalaemia, compartment syndrome and acute kidney injury","progressive renal failure and chronic interstitial fibrosis","urosepsis, hyperkalaemia and irreversible renal injury","permanent dialysis dependence and severe hypertension"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"permanent dialysis dependence and severe hypertension","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is permanent dialysis dependence and severe hypertension. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of renal cortical necrosis.","learningPoint":"Renal cortical necrosis: bilateral cortical non-enhancement with preserved medullary perfusion. Avoid assuming all post-shock acute tubular injury will recover rapidly.","id":"renal-100","globalNumber":1650,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Neurology","categoryNumber":51,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with sudden aphasia, gaze deviation and dense hemiparesis within a potentially treatable time window. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion stroke","Basilar artery occlusion","Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome","Anticoagulant-associated intracerebral haemorrhage","Subarachnoid haemorrhage with initially negative CT"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion stroke","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion stroke. The key discriminator is a proximal intracranial arterial occlusion on vascular imaging with a disabling deficit. Immediate priorities include: activate reperfusion pathways for thrombolysis and mechanical thrombectomy as eligible without delaying transfer.","learningPoint":"Anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion stroke: a proximal intracranial arterial occlusion on vascular imaging with a disabling deficit. Avoid obtaining only non-contrast CT and failing to perform vascular imaging in a thrombectomy candidate.","id":"neurology-051","globalNumber":1651,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Neurology","categoryNumber":52,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion stroke: sudden aphasia, gaze deviation and dense hemiparesis within a potentially treatable time window. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["reverse anticoagulation immediately, control blood pressure and obtain neurosurgical or stroke input","activate reperfusion pathways for thrombolysis and mechanical thrombectomy as eligible without delaying transfer","pursue definitive exclusion with appropriate lumbar puncture or vascular imaging based on timing and local pathway","obtain urgent stroke and neurosurgical review for decompression and CSF diversion","control blood pressure carefully, treat seizures and remove the precipitant"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"activate reperfusion pathways for thrombolysis and mechanical thrombectomy as eligible without delaying transfer","explanation":"The priority is to activate reperfusion pathways for thrombolysis and mechanical thrombectomy as eligible without delaying transfer. The decisive feature is a proximal intracranial arterial occlusion on vascular imaging with a disabling deficit. A major error is obtaining only non-contrast CT and failing to perform vascular imaging in a thrombectomy candidate.","learningPoint":"Anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion stroke: a proximal intracranial arterial occlusion on vascular imaging with a disabling deficit. Avoid obtaining only non-contrast CT and failing to perform vascular imaging in a thrombectomy candidate.","id":"neurology-052","globalNumber":1652,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Neurology","categoryNumber":53,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion stroke, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["posterior circulation deficits with a basilar filling defect","venous infarction or sinus filling defect, often with haemorrhage","a proximal intracranial arterial occlusion on vascular imaging with a disabling deficit","posterior fossa infarction causing fourth-ventricle compression or hydrocephalus","posterior vasogenic oedema on MRI"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a proximal intracranial arterial occlusion on vascular imaging with a disabling deficit","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a proximal intracranial arterial occlusion on vascular imaging with a disabling deficit. In context, this supports Anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion stroke and should prompt clinicians to activate reperfusion pathways for thrombolysis and mechanical thrombectomy as eligible without delaying transfer.","learningPoint":"Anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion stroke: a proximal intracranial arterial occlusion on vascular imaging with a disabling deficit. Avoid obtaining only non-contrast CT and failing to perform vascular imaging in a thrombectomy candidate.","id":"neurology-053","globalNumber":1653,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Neurology","categoryNumber":54,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion stroke, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["reassuring the patient solely because an early CT is reported normal outside a validated pathway","attributing reduced consciousness to intoxication without vascular imaging","withholding anticoagulation solely because the venous infarct is haemorrhagic","obtaining only non-contrast CT and failing to perform vascular imaging in a thrombectomy candidate","lowering blood pressure precipitously and causing cerebral ischaemia"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"obtaining only non-contrast CT and failing to perform vascular imaging in a thrombectomy candidate","explanation":"The hazardous action is obtaining only non-contrast CT and failing to perform vascular imaging in a thrombectomy candidate. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is large territorial infarction, malignant oedema and herniation.","learningPoint":"Anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion stroke: a proximal intracranial arterial occlusion on vascular imaging with a disabling deficit. Avoid obtaining only non-contrast CT and failing to perform vascular imaging in a thrombectomy candidate.","id":"neurology-054","globalNumber":1654,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Neurology","categoryNumber":55,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion stroke initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["intracranial hypertension, herniation and recurrent thrombosis","rebleeding, hydrocephalus and vasospasm","brainstem compression, hydrocephalus and death","haematoma expansion, herniation and death","large territorial infarction, malignant oedema and herniation"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"large territorial infarction, malignant oedema and herniation","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is large territorial infarction, malignant oedema and herniation. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion stroke.","learningPoint":"Anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion stroke: a proximal intracranial arterial occlusion on vascular imaging with a disabling deficit. Avoid obtaining only non-contrast CT and failing to perform vascular imaging in a thrombectomy candidate.","id":"neurology-055","globalNumber":1655,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Neurology","categoryNumber":56,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with acute vertigo, ataxia, vomiting and declining consciousness with a cerebellar infarct?","options":["Cerebellar infarction with posterior fossa swelling","Anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion stroke","Anticoagulant-associated intracerebral haemorrhage","Subarachnoid haemorrhage with initially negative CT","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Cerebellar infarction with posterior fossa swelling","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Cerebellar infarction with posterior fossa swelling. The key discriminator is posterior fossa infarction causing fourth-ventricle compression or hydrocephalus. Immediate priorities include: obtain urgent stroke and neurosurgical review for decompression and CSF diversion.","learningPoint":"Cerebellar infarction with posterior fossa swelling: posterior fossa infarction causing fourth-ventricle compression or hydrocephalus. Avoid treating severe gait ataxia as peripheral vertigo and discharging without imaging.","id":"neurology-056","globalNumber":1656,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Neurology","categoryNumber":57,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with cerebellar infarction with posterior fossa swelling presenting with acute vertigo, ataxia, vomiting and declining consciousness with a cerebellar infarct, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["reverse anticoagulation immediately, control blood pressure and obtain neurosurgical or stroke input","obtain urgent stroke and neurosurgical review for decompression and CSF diversion","obtain urgent CT angiography and activate reperfusion assessment","anticoagulate with heparin unless a specific contraindication exists and manage raised ICP","pursue definitive exclusion with appropriate lumbar puncture or vascular imaging based on timing and local pathway"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"obtain urgent stroke and neurosurgical review for decompression and CSF diversion","explanation":"The priority is to obtain urgent stroke and neurosurgical review for decompression and CSF diversion. The decisive feature is posterior fossa infarction causing fourth-ventricle compression or hydrocephalus. A major error is treating severe gait ataxia as peripheral vertigo and discharging without imaging.","learningPoint":"Cerebellar infarction with posterior fossa swelling: posterior fossa infarction causing fourth-ventricle compression or hydrocephalus. Avoid treating severe gait ataxia as peripheral vertigo and discharging without imaging.","id":"neurology-057","globalNumber":1657,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Neurology","categoryNumber":58,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for cerebellar infarction with posterior fossa swelling?","options":["intracerebral blood on CT with clinically significant anticoagulant effect","ventricular obstruction with acute pressure rise rather than diffuse cerebral oedema alone","posterior fossa infarction causing fourth-ventricle compression or hydrocephalus","instantaneous maximal headache with blood or xanthochromia when CT sensitivity is insufficient","venous infarction or sinus filling defect, often with haemorrhage"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"posterior fossa infarction causing fourth-ventricle compression or hydrocephalus","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is posterior fossa infarction causing fourth-ventricle compression or hydrocephalus. In context, this supports Cerebellar infarction with posterior fossa swelling and should prompt clinicians to obtain urgent stroke and neurosurgical review for decompression and CSF diversion.","learningPoint":"Cerebellar infarction with posterior fossa swelling: posterior fossa infarction causing fourth-ventricle compression or hydrocephalus. Avoid treating severe gait ataxia as peripheral vertigo and discharging without imaging.","id":"neurology-058","globalNumber":1658,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Neurology","categoryNumber":59,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in cerebellar infarction with posterior fossa swelling?","options":["reassuring the patient solely because an early CT is reported normal outside a validated pathway","waiting for coagulation results or clinical deterioration before reversal","withholding anticoagulation solely because the venous infarct is haemorrhagic","treating severe gait ataxia as peripheral vertigo and discharging without imaging","attributing reduced consciousness to intoxication without vascular imaging"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"treating severe gait ataxia as peripheral vertigo and discharging without imaging","explanation":"The hazardous action is treating severe gait ataxia as peripheral vertigo and discharging without imaging. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is brainstem compression, hydrocephalus and death.","learningPoint":"Cerebellar infarction with posterior fossa swelling: posterior fossa infarction causing fourth-ventricle compression or hydrocephalus. Avoid treating severe gait ataxia as peripheral vertigo and discharging without imaging.","id":"neurology-059","globalNumber":1659,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Neurology","categoryNumber":60,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of cerebellar infarction with posterior fossa swelling, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["intracranial hypertension, herniation and recurrent thrombosis","cerebral oedema, seizure and herniation","haematoma expansion, herniation and death","rebleeding, hydrocephalus and vasospasm","brainstem compression, hydrocephalus and death"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"brainstem compression, hydrocephalus and death","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is brainstem compression, hydrocephalus and death. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of cerebellar infarction with posterior fossa swelling.","learningPoint":"Cerebellar infarction with posterior fossa swelling: posterior fossa infarction causing fourth-ventricle compression or hydrocephalus. Avoid treating severe gait ataxia as peripheral vertigo and discharging without imaging.","id":"neurology-060","globalNumber":1660,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Neurology","categoryNumber":61,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by sudden focal deficit and reduced consciousness in a patient taking warfarin or a direct oral anticoagulant. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Anticoagulant-associated intracerebral haemorrhage","Subarachnoid haemorrhage with initially negative CT","Anterior-circulation large-vessel occlusion stroke","Cerebellar infarction with posterior fossa swelling","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Anticoagulant-associated intracerebral haemorrhage","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Anticoagulant-associated intracerebral haemorrhage. The key discriminator is intracerebral blood on CT with clinically significant anticoagulant effect. Immediate priorities include: reverse anticoagulation immediately, control blood pressure and obtain neurosurgical or stroke input.","learningPoint":"Anticoagulant-associated intracerebral haemorrhage: intracerebral blood on CT with clinically significant anticoagulant effect. Avoid waiting for coagulation results or clinical deterioration before reversal.","id":"neurology-061","globalNumber":1661,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Neurology","categoryNumber":62,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is anticoagulant-associated intracerebral haemorrhage. The patient has sudden focal deficit and reduced consciousness in a patient taking warfarin or a direct oral anticoagulant. What is the best immediate management?","options":["anticoagulate with heparin unless a specific contraindication exists and manage raised ICP","reverse anticoagulation immediately, control blood pressure and obtain neurosurgical or stroke input","control blood pressure carefully, treat seizures and remove the precipitant","obtain urgent stroke and neurosurgical review for decompression and CSF diversion","pursue definitive exclusion with appropriate lumbar puncture or vascular imaging based on tim