ion results or clinical deterioration before reversal.","id":"neurology-063","globalNumber":1663,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Neurology","categoryNumber":64,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with anticoagulant-associated intracerebral haemorrhage is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["reassuring the patient solely because an early CT is reported normal outside a validated pathway","attributing reduced consciousness to intoxication without vascular imaging","withholding anticoagulation solely because the venous infarct is haemorrhagic","waiting for coagulation results or clinical deterioration before reversal","treating severe gait ataxia as peripheral vertigo and discharging without imaging"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"waiting for coagulation results or clinical deterioration before reversal","explanation":"The hazardous action is waiting for coagulation results or clinical deterioration before reversal. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is haematoma expansion, herniation and death.","learningPoint":"Anticoagulant-associated intracerebral haemorrhage: intracerebral blood on CT with clinically significant anticoagulant effect. Avoid waiting for coagulation results or clinical deterioration before reversal.","id":"neurology-064","globalNumber":1664,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Neurology","categoryNumber":65,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with anticoagulant-associated intracerebral haemorrhage?","options":["cerebral oedema, seizure and herniation","brainstem compression, hydrocephalus and death","rebleeding, hydrocephalus and vasospasm","intracranial hypertension, herniation and recurrent thrombosis","haematoma expansion, herniation and death"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"haematoma expansion, herniation and death","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is haematoma expansion, herniation and death. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of anticoagulant-associated intracerebral haemorrhage.","learningPoint":"Anticoagulant-associated intracerebral haemorrhage: intracerebral blood on CT with clinically significant anticoagulant effect. Avoid waiting for coagulation results or clinical deterioration before reversal.","id":"neurology-065","globalNumber":1665,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Neurology","categoryNumber":66,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has persistent unexplained coma, fluctuating confusion or subtle twitching after a convulsive seizure. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Non-convulsive status epilepticus","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis","Autoimmune encephalitis","Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome","Herpes simplex encephalitis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Non-convulsive status epilepticus","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Non-convulsive status epilepticus. The key discriminator is ongoing epileptiform activity on EEG despite absent major motor activity. Immediate priorities include: give appropriate antiseizure treatment and obtain urgent continuous EEG-guided escalation.","learningPoint":"Non-convulsive status epilepticus: ongoing epileptiform activity on EEG despite absent major motor activity. Avoid attributing prolonged altered consciousness solely to a postictal state without reassessment.","id":"neurology-066","globalNumber":1666,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Neurology","categoryNumber":67,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for non-convulsive status epilepticus when the presentation is persistent unexplained coma, fluctuating confusion or subtle twitching after a convulsive seizure?","options":["control blood pressure carefully, treat seizures and remove the precipitant","give appropriate antiseizure treatment and obtain urgent continuous EEG-guided escalation","start intravenous aciclovir immediately while obtaining CSF and neuroimaging when safe","control seizures and dysautonomia, exclude infection and begin specialist-directed immunotherapy","induce anaesthesia, intubate and use continuous EEG-guided therapy"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give appropriate antiseizure treatment and obtain urgent continuous EEG-guided escalation","explanation":"The priority is to give appropriate antiseizure treatment and obtain urgent continuous EEG-guided escalation. The decisive feature is ongoing epileptiform activity on EEG despite absent major motor activity. A major error is attributing prolonged altered consciousness solely to a postictal state without reassessment.","learningPoint":"Non-convulsive status epilepticus: ongoing epileptiform activity on EEG despite absent major motor activity. Avoid attributing prolonged altered consciousness solely to a postictal state without reassessment.","id":"neurology-067","globalNumber":1667,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Neurology","categoryNumber":68,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for non-convulsive status epilepticus in this presentation?","options":["posterior vasogenic oedema on MRI","encephalopathy with characteristic antibodies or syndrome after infectious causes are addressed","ongoing epileptiform activity on EEG despite absent major motor activity","temporal-lobe inflammation with compatible CSF or PCR findings","persistent seizure activity despite first- and second-line treatment"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"ongoing epileptiform activity on EEG despite absent major motor activity","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is ongoing epileptiform activity on EEG despite absent major motor activity. In context, this supports Non-convulsive status epilepticus and should prompt clinicians to give appropriate antiseizure tr