fter symptoms transiently settle.","learningPoint":"Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction: intravascular haemolysis temporally linked to incompatible transfusion. Avoid restarting the same blood unit after symptoms transiently settle.","id":"haematology-037","globalNumber":1707,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Haematology","categoryNumber":38,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for acute haemolytic transfusion reaction?","options":["schistocytes with haemolysis and severe thrombocytopenia","viscoelastic or laboratory evidence of factor and fibrinogen depletion during active bleeding","intravascular haemolysis temporally linked to incompatible transfusion","coagulopathy plus promyelocytes with t(15;17) suspicion","consumption coagulopathy with falling fibrinogen and platelets"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"intravascular haemolysis temporally linked to incompatible transfusion","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is intravascular haemolysis temporally linked to incompatible transfusion. In context, this supports Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction and should prompt clinicians to stop the transfusion, maintain renal perfusion and initiate the transfusion-reaction protocol immediately.","learningPoint":"Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction: intravascular haemolysis temporally linked to incompatible transfusion. Avoid restarting the same blood unit after symptoms transiently settle.","id":"haematology-038","globalNumber":1708,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Haematology","categoryNumber":39,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in acute haemolytic transfusion reaction?","options":["waiting for definitive cytogenetics before starting ATRA","transfusing platelets in the absence of life-threatening bleeding","correcting laboratory numbers without treating the underlying trigger","restarting the 