 is characterised by spontaneous extensive soft-tissue or mucosal bleeding with an isolated prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Acquired haemophilia A","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Direct oral anticoagulant–associated major bleeding","Massive-transfusion coagulopathy","Haemophilia with life-threatening bleeding"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Acquired haemophilia A","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Acquired haemophilia A. The key discriminator is failure of the prolonged aPTT to correct on mixing due to a factor VIII inhibitor. Immediate priorities include: control bleeding with bypassing therapy and begin specialist immunosuppression.","learningPoint":"Acquired haemophilia A: failure of the prolonged aPTT to correct on mixing due to a factor VIII inhibitor. Avoid giving factor VIII alone repeatedly without recognising an inhibitor.","id":"haematology-041","globalNumber":1711,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Haematology","categoryNumber":42,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is acquired haemophilia a. The patient has spontaneous extensive soft-tissue or mucosal bleeding with an isolated prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time. What is the best immediate management?","options":["treat the cause and replace blood components according to bleeding and laboratory deficits","control bleeding with bypassing therapy and begin specialist immunosuppression","use balanced haemostatic resuscitation, warm the patient and target fibrinogen, platelets and calcium","stop the drug, provide source control and give the appropriate specific or non-specific reversal strategy","give the missing clotting factor immediately and obtain urgent haemophilia and surgical support"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"control bleeding with bypassing therapy and begin specialist immunosuppression","explanation":"The priority is to control bleeding with bypassing therapy and begin specialist immunosuppression. The decisive feature is failure of the prolonged aPTT to correct on mixing due to a factor VIII inhibitor. A major error is giving factor VIII alone repeatedly without recognising an inhibitor.","learningPoint":"Acquired haemophilia A: failure of the prolonged aPTT to correct on mixing due to a factor VIII inhibitor. Avoid giving factor VIII alone repeatedly without recognising an inhibitor.","id":"haematology-042","globalNumber":1712,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Haematology","categoryNumber":43,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have acquired haemophilia a. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["schistocytes with haemolysis and severe thrombocytopenia","clinically important recent anticoagulant exposure with major bleeding, regardless of routine coagulation tests","failure of the prolonged aPTT to correct on mixing due to a factor VIII inhibitor","viscoelastic or laboratory evidence of factor and fibrinogen depletion during active bleeding","high-risk bleeding in a known factor deficiency, even before imaging confirmation"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"failure of the prolonged aPTT to correct on mixing due to a factor VIII inhibitor","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is failure of the prolonged aPTT to correct on mixing due to a factor VIII inhibitor. In context, this supports Acquired haemophilia A and should prompt clinicians to control bleeding with bypassing therapy and begin specialist immunosuppression.","learningPoint":"Acquired haemophilia A: failure of the prolonged aPTT to correct on mixing due to a factor VIII inhibitor. Avoid giving factor VIII alone repeatedly without recognising an inhibitor.","id":"haematology-043","globalNumber":1713,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Haematology","categoryNumber":44,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with acquired haemophilia a is deteriorating. Which pr