ource after upper bleeding is excluded","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is active colonic contrast extravasation or a diverticular source after upper bleeding is excluded. In context, this supports Severe diverticular lower gastrointestinal haemorrhage and should prompt clinicians to resuscitate, reverse anticoagulation when appropriate and arrange urgent endoscopic or angiographic haemostasis.","learningPoint":"Severe diverticular lower gastrointestinal haemorrhage: active colonic contrast extravasation or a diverticular source after upper bleeding is excluded. Avoid assuming bright red blood always excludes an upper gastrointestinal source.","id":"gastrointestinal-053","globalNumber":1733,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":54,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of severe diverticular lower gastrointestinal haemorrhage, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["treating the patient as cirrhotic portal hypertension without identifying splenic venous obstruction","using antimotility drugs or delaying rescue therapy while awaiting complete stool normalisation","giving antidiarrhoeals, opioids or colonoscopy during acute dilatation","assuming bright red blood always excludes an upper gastrointestinal source","performing endoscopy alone and missing an arterial pseudoaneurysm"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"assuming bright red blood always excludes an upper gastrointestinal source","explanation":"The hazardous action is assuming bright red blood always excludes an upper gastrointestinal source. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is recurrent haemorrhage, myocardial ischaemia and shock.","learningPoint":"Severe diverticular lower gastrointestinal haemorrhage: active colonic contrast extravasation or a diverticular source after upper bleeding is excluded. Avoid assuming bright red blood always excludes an upper gastrointestinal source.","id":"gastrointestinal-054","globalNumber":1734,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":55,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with severe diverticular lower gastrointestinal haemorrhage initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["catastrophic rebleeding and haemorrhagic shock","rebleeding, aspiration, encephalopathy and hepatorenal failure","toxic megacolon, perforation and venous thromboembolism","transmural necrosis, perforation and stricture","recurrent haemorrhage, myocardial ischaemia and shock"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"recurrent haemorrhage, myocardial ischaemia and shock","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is recurrent haemorrhage, myocardial ischaemia and shock. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of severe diverticular lower gastrointestinal haemorrhage.","learningPoint":"Severe diverticular lower gastrointestinal haemorrhage: active colonic contrast extravasation or a diverticular source after upper bleeding is excluded. Avoid assuming bright red blood always excludes an upper gastrointestinal source.","id":"gastrointestinal-055","globalNumber":1735,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":56,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with sudden severe epigastric or chest pain, retching without vomiting and inability to pass a nasogastric tube?","options":["Gastric volvulus","Boerhaave syndrome","Oesophageal food-bolus obstruction","Severe acute pancreatitis","Acute variceal haemorrhage"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Gastric volvulus","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Gastric volvulus. The key discriminator is an abnormally rotated, distended stomach with outlet obstruction and threatened ischaemia. Immediate priorities include: decompress if safely possible and obtain emergency surgical repair.","learningPoint":"Gastric volvulus: an abnormally rotated, distended stomach with outlet obstruction and threatened ischaemia. Avoid repeated blind nasogastric attempts or delaying surgery after failed decompression.","id":"gastrointestinal-056","globalNumber":1736,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":57,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with gastric volvulus presenting with sudden severe epigastric or chest pain, retching without vomiting and inability to pass a nasogastric tube, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["resuscitate and obtain endoscopic, interventional and surgical control of gastric variceal bleeding","decompress if safely possible and obtain emergency surgical repair","activate haemorrhage resuscitation and obtain urgent angiographic embolisation or surgery","resuscitate with a restrictive transfusion strategy, give vasoactive therapy and antibiotics, then urgent endoscopy","keep nil by mouth, give broad antibiotics and obtain immediate surgical or endoscopic consultation"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"decompress if safely possible and obtain emergency surgical repair","explanation":"The priority is to decompress if safely possible and obtain emergency surgical repair. The decisive feature is an abnormally rotated, distended stomach with outlet obstruction and threatened ischaemia. A major error is repeated blind nasogastric attempts or delaying surgery after failed decompression.","learningPoint":"Gastric volvulus: an abnormally rotated, distended stomach with outlet obstruction and threatened ischaemia. Avoid repeated blind nasogastric attempts or delaying surgery after failed decompression.","id":"gastrointestinal-057","globalNumber":1737,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":58,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for gastric volvulus?","options":["persistent organ failure rather than enzyme magnitude","upper gastrointestinal bleeding in a patient with portal hypertension","an abnormally rotated, distended stomach with outlet obstruction and threatened ischaemia","transmural oesophageal perforation with pleural or mediastinal contamination","splenic vein occlusion causing left-sided portal hypertension"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"an abnormally rotated, distended stomach with outlet obstruction and threatened ischaemia","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is an abnormally rotated, distended stomach with outlet obstruction and threatened ischaemia. In context, this supports Gastric volvulus and should prompt clinicians to decompress if safely possible and obtain emergency surgical repair.","learningPoint":"Gastric volvulus: an abnormally rotated, distended stomach with outlet obstruction and threatened ischaemia. Avoid repeated blind nasogastric attempts or delaying surgery after failed decompression.","id":"gastrointestinal-058","globalNumber":1738,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":59,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in gastric volvulus?","options":["performing endoscopy alone and missing an arterial pseudoaneurysm","performing routine endoscopy or delaying because ECG and troponin are normal","treating the patient as cirrhotic portal hypertension without identifying splenic venous obstruction","repeated blind nasogastric attempts or delaying surgery after failed decompression","overtransfusion that raises portal pressure or delaying antibiotics"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"repeated blind nasogastric attempts or delaying surgery after failed decompression","explanation":"The hazardous action is repeated blind nasogastric attempts or delaying surgery after failed decompression. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is gastric necrosis, perforation and mediastinal contamination.","learningPoint":"Gastric volvulus: an abnormally rotated, distended stomach with outlet obstruction and threatened ischaemia. Avoid repeated blind nasogastric attempts or delaying surgery after failed decompression.","id":"gastrointestinal-059","globalNumber":1739,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":60,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of gastric volvulus, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["necrosis, infected collections, ARDS and abdominal compartment syndrome","catastrophic rebleeding and haemorrhagic shock","rebleeding, aspiration, encephalopathy and hepatorenal failure","mediastinitis, empyema and septic shock","gastric necrosis, perforation and mediastinal contamination"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"gastric necrosis, perforation and mediastinal contamination","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is gastric necrosis, perforation and mediastinal contamination. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of gastric volvulus.","learningPoint":"Gastric volvulus: an abnormally rotated, distended stomach with outlet obstruction and threatened ischaemia. Avoid repeated blind nasogastric attempts or delaying surgery after failed decompression.","id":"gastrointestinal-060","globalNumber":1740,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":61,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by abdominal pain, hepatomegaly, ascites and acute liver dysfunction in a prothrombotic patient. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Budd-Chiari syndrome","Portal vein thrombosis","Acute liver failure","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","Acute mesenteric ischaemia"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Budd-Chiari syndrome","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Budd-Chiari syndrome. The key discriminator is hepatic venous outflow obstruction on Doppler or cross-sectional imaging. Immediate priorities include: start anticoagulation when safe and obtain urgent hepatology or interventional management.","learningPoint":"Budd-Chiari syndrome: hepatic venous outflow obstruction on Doppler or cross-sectional imaging. Avoid treating ascites alone without addressing venous obstruction.","id":"gastrointestinal-061","globalNumber":1741,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":62,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is budd-chiari syndrome. The patient has abdominal pain, hepatomegaly, ascites and acute liver dysfunction in a prothrombotic patient. What is the best immediate management?","options":["obtain urgent CT angiography and involve vascular or surgical teams for reperfusion","start anticoagulation when safe and obtain urgent hepatology or interventional management","resuscitate and obtain endoscopic, interventional and surgical control of gastric variceal bleeding","provide critical care, treat the cause such as acetylcysteine and contact a transplant centre early","treat the cause and anticoagulate when indicated with hepatology input"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"start anticoagulation when safe and obtain urgent hepatology or interventional management","explanation":"The priority is to start anticoagulation when safe and obtain urgent hepatology or interventional management. The decisive feature is hepatic venous outflow obstruction on Doppler or cross-sectional imaging. A major error is treating ascites alone without addressing venous obstruction.","learningPoint":"Budd-Chiari syndrome: hepatic venous outflow obstruction on Doppler or cross-sectional imaging. Avoid treating ascites alone without addressing venous obstruction.","id":"gastrointestinal-062","globalNumber":1742,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":63,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have budd-chiari syndrome. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["absent portal flow or intraluminal thrombus without hepatic-vein obstruction","splenic vein occlusion causing left-sided portal hypertension","hepatic venous outflow obstruction on Doppler or cross-sectional imaging","diffuse bowel hypoperfusion and vasoconstriction on CT angiography","INR elevation plus encephalopathy in an acute hepatic illness"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"hepatic venous outflow obstruction on Doppler or cross-sectional imaging","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is hepatic venous outflow obstruction on Doppler or cross-sectional imaging. In context, this supports Budd-Chiari syndrome and should prompt clinicians to start anticoagulation when safe and obtain urgent hepatology or interventional management.","learningPoint":"Budd-Chiari syndrome: hepatic venous outflow obstruction on Doppler or cross-sectional imaging. Avoid treating ascites alone without addressing venous obstruction.","id":"gastrointestinal-063","globalNumber":1743,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":64,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with budd-chiari syndrome is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["assuming normal liver enzymes exclude significant portal thrombosis","delaying paracentesis because there is no fever or guarding","reassuring from patent major mesenteric vessels despite worsening shock and abdominal findings","treating ascites alone without addressing venous obstruction","correcting INR routinely with plasma and obscuring prognostic trends without bleeding"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"treating ascites alone without addressing venous obstruction","explanation":"The hazardous action is treating ascites alone without addressing venous obstruction. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is fulminant liver failure, portal hypertension and variceal bleeding.","learningPoint":"Budd-Chiari syndrome: hepatic venous outflow obstruction on Doppler or cross-sectional imaging. Avoid treating ascites alone without addressing venous obstruction.","id":"gastrointestinal-064","globalNumber":1744,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":65,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with budd-chiari syndrome?","options":["hepatorenal syndrome, sepsis and death","transmural bowel necrosis and multiorgan failure","cerebral oedema, hypoglycaemia and multiorgan failure","bowel ischaemia, cavernous transformation and variceal haemorrhage","fulminant liver failure, portal hypertension and variceal bleeding"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"fulminant liver failure, portal hypertension and variceal bleeding","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is fulminant liver failure, portal hypertension and variceal bleeding. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of budd-chiari syndrome.","learningPoint":"Budd-Chiari syndrome: hepatic venous outflow obstruction on Doppler or cross-sectional imaging. Avoid treating ascites alone without addressing venous obstruction.","id":"gastrointestinal-065","globalNumber":1745,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":66,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has abdominal pain or portal-hypertension features with a new portal venous filling defect. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Portal vein thrombosis","Acute mesenteric ischaemia","Budd-Chiari syndrome","Non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia","Acute liver failure"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Portal vein thrombosis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Portal vein thrombosis. The key discriminator is absent portal flow or intraluminal thrombus without hepatic-vein obstruction. Immediate priorities include: treat the cause and anticoagulate when indicated with hepatology input.","learningPoint":"Portal vein thrombosis: absent portal flow or intraluminal thrombus without hepatic-vein obstruction. Avoid assuming normal liver enzymes exclude significant portal thrombosis.","id":"gastrointestinal-066","globalNumber":1746,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":67,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for portal vein thrombosis when the presentation is abdominal pain or portal-hypertension features with a new portal venous filling defect?","options":["restore perfusion, reduce vasoconstrictor burden where possible and obtain urgent surgical or vascular review","treat the cause and anticoagulate when indicated with hepatology input","provide critical care, treat the cause such as acetylcysteine and contact a transplant centre early","start anticoagulation when safe and obtain urgent hepatology or interventional management","obtain urgent CT angiography and involve vascular or surgical teams for reperfusion"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"treat the cause and anticoagulate when indicated with hepatology input","explanation":"The priority is to treat the cause and anticoagulate when indicated with hepatology input. The decisive feature is absent portal flow or intraluminal thrombus without hepatic-vein obstruction. A major error is assuming normal liver enzymes exclude significant portal thrombosis.","learningPoint":"Portal vein thrombosis: absent portal flow or intraluminal thrombus without hepatic-vein obstruction. Avoid assuming normal liver enzymes exclude significant portal thrombosis.","id":"gastrointestinal-067","globalNumber":1747,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":68,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for portal vein thrombosis in this presentation?","options":["INR elevation plus encephalopathy in an acute hepatic illness","hepatic venous outflow obstruction on Doppler or cross-sectional imaging","absent portal flow or intraluminal thrombus without hepatic-vein obstruction","diffuse bowel hypoperfusion and vasoconstriction on CT angiography","severe pain with early minimal tenderness and vascular risk"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"absent portal flow or intraluminal thrombus without hepatic-vein obstruction","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is absent portal flow or intraluminal thrombus without hepatic-vein obstruction. In context, this supports Portal vein thrombosis and should prompt clinicians to treat the cause and anticoagulate when indicated with hepatology input.","learningPoint":"Portal vein thrombosis: absent portal flow or intraluminal thrombus without hepatic-vein obstruction. Avoid assuming normal liver enzymes exclude significant portal thrombosis.","id":"gastrointestinal-068","globalNumber":1748,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":69,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in portal vein thrombosis?","options":["treating ascites alone without addressing venous obstruction","correcting INR routinely with plasma and obscuring prognostic trends without bleeding","waiting for peritonism or a raised lactate before imaging","assuming normal liver enzymes exclude significant portal thrombosis","reassuring from patent major mesenteric vessels despite worsening shock and abdominal findings"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"assuming normal liver enzymes exclude significant portal thrombosis","explanation":"The hazardous action is assuming normal liver enzymes exclude significant portal thrombosis. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is bowel ischaemia, cavernous transformation and variceal haemorrhage.","learningPoint":"Portal vein thrombosis: absent portal flow or intraluminal thrombus without hepatic-vein obstruction. Avoid assuming normal liver enzymes exclude significant portal thrombosis.","id":"gastrointestinal-069","globalNumber":1749,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":70,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in portal vein thrombosis, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["bowel necrosis, sepsis and short-gut syndrome","transmural bowel necrosis and multiorgan failure","fulminant liver failure, portal hypertension and variceal bleeding","cerebral oedema, hypoglycaemia and multiorgan failure","bowel ischaemia, cavernous transformation and variceal haemorrhage"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"bowel ischaemia, cavernous transformation and variceal haemorrhage","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is bowel ischaemia, cavernous transformation and variceal haemorrhage. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of portal vein thrombosis.","learningPoint":"Portal vein thrombosis: absent portal flow or intraluminal thrombus without hepatic-vein obstruction. Avoid assuming normal liver enzymes exclude significant portal thrombosis.","id":"gastrointestinal-070","globalNumber":1750,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":71,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with fever, right-upper-quadrant pain and sepsis with a focal hepatic collection. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Pyogenic liver abscess","Budd-Chiari syndrome","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","Pancreatic pseudoaneurysm haemorrhage","Portal vein thrombosis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Pyogenic liver abscess","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Pyogenic liver abscess. The key discriminator is a rim-enhancing liver collection with bacteraemia or biliary risk. Immediate priorities include: give broad antimicrobial therapy and arrange image-guided drainage or surgery.","learningPoint":"Pyogenic liver abscess: a rim-enhancing liver collection with bacteraemia or biliary risk. Avoid treating prolonged fever with antibiotics alone despite a drainable collection.","id":"gastrointestinal-071","globalNumber":1751,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":72,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has pyogenic liver abscess: fever, right-upper-quadrant pain and sepsis with a focal hepatic collection. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["activate haemorrhage resuscitation and obtain urgent angiographic embolisation or surgery","give broad antimicrobial therapy and arrange image-guided drainage or surgery","provide critical care, treat the cause such as acetylcysteine and contact a transplant centre early","perform diagnostic paracentesis promptly and give third-generation cephalosporin plus albumin when indicated","protect the airway, avoid hypotonic fluid, treat ammonia-generating factors and involve a transplant centre"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give broad antimicrobial therapy and arrange image-guided drainage or surgery","explanation":"The priority is to give broad antimicrobial therapy and arrange image-guided drainage or surgery. The decisive feature is a rim-enhancing liver collection with bacteraemia or biliary risk. A major error is treating prolonged fever with antibiotics alone despite a drainable collection.","learningPoint":"Pyogenic liver abscess: a rim-enhancing liver collection with bacteraemia or biliary risk. Avoid treating prolonged fever with antibiotics alone despite a drainable collection.","id":"gastrointestinal-072","globalNumber":1752,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":73,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected pyogenic liver abscess, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["INR elevation plus encephalopathy in an acute hepatic illness","ascitic neutrophil count at least 250 cells per microlitre","a rim-enhancing liver collection with bacteraemia or biliary risk","cholestasis with an obstructed infected biliary tree","severe pain with early minimal tenderness and vascular risk"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a rim-enhancing liver collection with bacteraemia or biliary risk","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a rim-enhancing liver collection with bacteraemia or biliary risk. In context, this supports Pyogenic liver abscess and should prompt clinicians to give broad antimicrobial therapy and arrange image-guided drainage or surgery.","learningPoint":"Pyogenic liver abscess: a rim-enhancing liver collection with bacteraemia or biliary risk. Avoid treating prolonged fever with antibiotics alone despite a drainable collection.","id":"gastrointestinal-073","globalNumber":1753,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":74,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of pyogenic liver abscess, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["sedating repeatedly without securing the airway or monitoring neurologic deterioration","delaying paracentesis because there is no fever or guarding","treating with antibiotics alone despite organ dysfunction","treating prolonged fever with antibiotics alone despite a drainable collection","overtransfusion that raises portal pressure or delaying antibiotics"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"treating prolonged fever with antibiotics alone despite a drainable collection","explanation":"The hazardous action is treating prolonged fever with antibiotics alone despite a drainable collection. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is rupture, septic shock and metastatic infection.","learningPoint":"Pyogenic liver abscess: a rim-enhancing liver collection with bacteraemia or biliary risk. Avoid treating prolonged fever with antibiotics alone despite a drainable collection.","id":"gastrointestinal-074","globalNumber":1754,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":75,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with pyogenic liver abscess initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["herniation, seizure and aspiration","bowel necrosis, sepsis and short-gut syndrome","fulminant liver failure, portal hypertension and variceal bleeding","hepatorenal syndrome, sepsis and death","rupture, septic shock and metastatic infection"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"rupture, septic shock and metastatic infection","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is rupture, septic shock and metastatic infection. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of pyogenic liver abscess.","learningPoint":"Pyogenic liver abscess: a rim-enhancing liver collection with bacteraemia or biliary risk. Avoid treating prolonged fever with antibiotics alone despite a drainable collection.","id":"gastrointestinal-075","globalNumber":1755,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":76,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with upper gastrointestinal or retroperitoneal bleeding after pancreatitis with a sentinel haemorrhage?","options":["Pancreatic pseudoaneurysm haemorrhage","Gastric volvulus","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","Pyogenic liver abscess","Severe diverticular lower gastrointestinal haemorrhage"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Pancreatic pseudoaneurysm haemorrhage","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Pancreatic pseudoaneurysm haemorrhage. The key discriminator is arterial-phase contrast extravasation from a peripancreatic vessel or pseudoaneurysm. Immedi