ute severe ulcerative colitis without megacolon","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Acute severe ulcerative colitis without megacolon. The key discriminator is severe clinical and laboratory criteria with continuous colitis. Immediate priorities include: give intravenous corticosteroids, thromboprophylaxis and early gastroenterology or surgical review.","learningPoint":"Acute severe ulcerative colitis without megacolon: severe clinical and laboratory criteria with continuous colitis. Avoid using antimotility drugs or delaying rescue therapy while awaiting complete stool normalisation.","id":"gastrointestinal-081","globalNumber":1761,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":82,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is acute severe ulcerative colitis without megacolon. The patient has frequent bloody diarrhoea with systemic toxicity and inflammatory colitis but no colonic dilatation. What is the best immediate management?","options":["resuscitate, reverse anticoagulation when appropriate and arrange urgent endoscopic or angiographic haemostasis","give intravenous corticosteroids, thromboprophylaxis and early gastroenterology or surgical review","resuscitate, give antibiotics when severe and obtain surgical review for peritonitis or gangrene","restore perfusion, reduce vasoconstrictor burden where possible and obtain urgent surgical or vascular review","stop precipitating drugs, give intensive medical therapy and seek early colorectal surgical review"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give intravenous corticosteroids, thromboprophylaxis and early gastroenterology or surgical review","explanation":"The priority is to give intravenous corticosteroids, thromboprophylaxis and early gastroenterology or surgical review. The decisive feature is severe clinical and laboratory criteria with continuous colitis. A major error is using antimotility drugs or delaying rescue therapy while awaiting complete stool normalisation.","learningPoint":"Acute severe ulcerative colitis without megacolon: severe clinical and laboratory criteria with continuous colitis. Avoid using antimotility drugs or delaying rescue therapy while awaiting complete stool normalisation.","id":"gastrointestinal-082","globalNumber":1762,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":83,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have acute severe ulcerative colitis without megacolon. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["non-obstructive colonic dilatation plus systemic toxicity","segmental colitis in watershed regions after low-flow injury","severe clinical and laboratory criteria with continuous colitis","active colonic contrast extravasation or a diverticular source after upper bleeding is excluded","diffuse bowel hypoperfusion and vasoconstriction on CT angiography"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"severe clinical and laboratory criteria with continuous colitis","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is severe clinical and laboratory criteria with continuous colitis. In context, this supports Acute severe ulcerative colitis without megacolon and should prompt clinicians to give intravenous corticosteroids, thromboprophylaxis and early gastroenterology or surgical review.","learningPoint":"Acute severe ulcerative colitis without megacolon: severe clinical and laboratory criteria with continuous colitis. Avoid using antimotility drugs or delaying rescue therapy while awaiting complete stool normalisation.","id":"gastrointestinal-083","globalNumber":1763,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":84,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with acute severe ulcerative colitis without megacolon is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["reassuring from patent major mesenteric vessels despite worsening shock and abdominal findings","giving antidiarrhoeals, opioids or colonoscopy during acute dilatation","assuming all post-hypotension rectal bleeding is benign","using antimotility drugs or delaying rescue therapy while awaiting complete stool normalisation","assuming bright red blood always excludes an upper gastrointestinal source"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"using antimotility drugs or delaying rescue therapy while awaiting complete stool normalisation","explanation":"The hazardous action is using antimotility drugs or delaying rescue therapy while awaiting complete stool normalisation. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is toxic megacolon, perforation and venous thromboembolism.","learningPoint":"Acute severe ulcerative colitis without megacolon: severe clinical and laboratory criteria with continuous colitis. Avoid using antimotility drugs or delaying rescue therapy while awaiting complete stool normalisation.","id":"gastrointestinal-084","globalNumber":1764,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":85,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with acute severe ulcerative colitis without megacolon?","options":["recurrent haemorrhage, myocardial ischaemia and shock","transmural bowel necrosis and multiorgan failure","perforation, haemorrhage and septic shock","transmural necrosis, perforation and stricture","toxic megacolon, perforation and venous thromboembolism"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"toxic megacolon, perforation and venous thromboembolism","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is toxic megacolon, perforation and venous thromboembolism. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of acute severe ulcerative colitis without megacolon.","learningPoint":"Acute severe ulcerative colitis without megacolon: severe clinical and laboratory criteria with continuous colitis. Avoid using antimotility drugs or delaying rescue therapy while awaiting complete stool normalisation.","id":"gastrointestinal-085","globalNumber":1765,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":86,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has sudden inability to swallow saliva after eating, without airway compromise. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Oesophageal food-bolus obstruction","Acute variceal haemorrhage","Boerhaave syndrome","Severe acute pancreatitis","Gastric volvulus"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Oesophageal food-bolus obstruction","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Oesophageal food-bolus obstruction. The key discriminator is complete oesophageal obstruction with drooling and retained bolus. Immediate priorities include: protect the airway and arrange urgent therapeutic endoscopy.","learningPoint":"Oesophageal food-bolus obstruction: complete oesophageal obstruction with drooling and retained bolus. Avoid using repeated pharmacological attempts while delaying endoscopy in complete obstruction.","id":"gastrointestinal-086","globalNumber":1766,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":87,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for oesophageal food-bolus obstruction when the presentation is sudden inability to swallow saliva after eating, without airway compromise?","options":["give goal-directed crystalloid, analgesia and early enteral nutrition while treating the cause","protect the airway and arrange urgent therapeutic endoscopy","decompress if safely possible and obtain emergency surgical repair","keep nil by mouth, give broad antibiotics and obtain immediate surgical or endoscopic consultation","resuscitate with a restrictive transfusion strategy, give vasoactive therapy and antibiotics, then urgent endoscopy"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"protect the airway and arrange urgent therapeutic endoscopy","explanation":"The priority is to protect the airway and arrange urgent therapeutic endoscopy. The decisive feature is complete oesophageal obstruction with drooling and retained bolus. A major error is using repeated pharmacological attempts while delaying endoscopy in complete obstruction.","learningPoint":"Oesophageal food-bolus obstruction: complete oesophageal obstruction with drooling and retained bolus. Avoid using repeated pharmacological attempts while delaying endoscopy in complete obstruction.","id":"gastrointestinal-087","globalNumber":1767,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":88,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for oesophageal food-bolus obstruction in this presentation?","options":["an abnormally rotated, distended stomach with outlet obstruction and threatened ischaemia","upper gastrointestinal bleeding in a patient with portal hypertension","complete oesophageal obstruction with drooling and retained bolus","persistent organ failure rather than enzyme magnitude","transmural oesophageal perforation with pleural or mediastinal contamination"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"complete oesophageal obstruction with drooling and retained bolus","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is complete oesophageal obstruction with drooling and retained bolus. In context, this supports Oesophageal food-bolus obstruction and should prompt clinicians to protect the airway and arrange urgent therapeutic endoscopy.","learningPoint":"Oesophageal food-bolus obstruction: complete oesophageal obstruction with drooling and retained bolus. Avoid using repeated pharmacological attempts while delaying endoscopy in complete obstruction.","id":"gastrointestinal-088","globalNumber":1768,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":89,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in oesophageal food-bolus obstruction?","options":["performing routine endoscopy or delaying because ECG and troponin are normal","giving prophylactic antibiotics for sterile necrosis","repeated blind nasogastric attempts or delaying surgery after failed decompression","using repeated pharmacological attempts while delaying endoscopy in complete obstruction","overtransfusion that raises portal pressure or delaying antibiotics"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"using repeated pharmacological attempts while delaying endoscopy in complete obstruction","explanation":"The hazardous action is using repeated pharmacological attempts while delaying endoscopy in complete obstruction. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is aspiration, perforation and pressure necrosis.","learningPoint":"Oesophageal food-bolus obstruction: complete oesophageal obstruction with drooling and retained bolus. Avoid using repeated pharmacological attempts while delaying endoscopy in complete obstruction.","id":"gastrointestinal-089","globalNumber":1769,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":90,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in oesophageal food-bolus obstruction, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["necrosis, infected collections, ARDS and abdominal compartment syndrome","gastric necrosis, perforation and mediastinal contamination","mediastinitis, empyema and septic shock","rebleeding, aspiration, encephalopathy and hepatorenal failure","aspiration, perforation and pressure necrosis"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"aspiration, perforation and pressure necrosis","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is aspiration, perforation and pressure necrosis. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of oesophageal food-bolus obstruction.","learningPoint":"Oesophageal food-bolus obstruction: complete oesophageal obstruction with drooling and retained bolus. Avoid using repeated pharmacological attempts while delaying endoscopy in complete obstruction.","id":"gastrointestinal-090","globalNumber":1770,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":91,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with abdominal pain and unexplained lactate elevation during shock or high-dose vasopressor therapy without a major arterial occlusion. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia","Portal vein thrombosis","Ischaemic colitis","Acute mesenteric ischaemia","Toxic megacolon"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia. The key discriminator is diffuse bowel hypoperfusion and vasoconstriction on CT angiography. Immediate priorities include: restore perfusion, reduce vasoconstrictor burden where possible and obtain urgent surgical or vascular review.","learningPoint":"Non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia: diffuse bowel hypoperfusion and vasoconstriction on CT angiography. Avoid reassuring from patent major mesenteric vessels despite worsening shock and abdominal findings.","id":"gastrointestinal-091","globalNumber":1771,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":92,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia: abdominal pain and unexplained lactate elevation during shock or high-dose vasopressor therapy without a major arterial occlusion. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["treat the cause and anticoagulate when indicated with hepatology input","restore perfusion, reduce vasoconstrictor burden where possible and obtain urgent surgical or vascular review","obtain urgent CT angiography and involve vascular or surgical teams for reperfusion","resuscitate, give antibiotics when severe and obtain surgical review for peritonitis or gangrene","stop precipitating drugs, give intensive medical therapy and seek early colorectal surgical review"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"restore perfusion, reduce vasoconstrictor burden where possible and obtain urgent surgical or vascular review","explanation":"The priority is to restore perfusion, reduce vasoconstrictor burden where possible and obtain urgent surgical or vascular review. The decisive feature is diffuse bowel hypoperfusion and vasoconstriction on CT angiography. A major error is reassuring from patent major mesenteric vessels despite worsening shock and abdominal findings.","learningPoint":"Non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia: diffuse bowel hypoperfusion and vasoconstriction on CT angiography. Avoid reassuring from patent major mesenteric vessels despite worsening shock and abdominal findings.","id":"gastrointestinal-092","globalNumber":1772,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":93,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["severe pain with early minimal tenderness and vascular risk","non-obstructive colonic dilatation plus systemic toxicity","diffuse bowel hypoperfusion and vasoconstriction on CT angiography","segmental colitis in watershed regions after low-flow injury","absent portal flow or intraluminal thrombus without hepatic-vein obstruction"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"diffuse bowel hypoperfusion and vasoconstriction on CT angiography","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is diffuse bowel hypoperfusion and vasoconstriction on CT angiography. In context, this supports Non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia and should prompt clinicians to restore perfusion, reduce vasoconstrictor burden where possible and obtain urgent surgical or vascular review.","learningPoint":"Non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia: diffuse bowel hypoperfusion and vasoconstriction on CT angiography. Avoid reassuring from patent major mesenteric vessels despite worsening shock and abdominal findings.","id":"gastrointestinal-093","globalNumber":1773,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":94,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["waiting for peritonism or a raised lactate before imaging","assuming normal liver enzymes exclude significant portal thrombosis","assuming all post-hypotension rectal bleeding is benign","reassuring from patent major mesenteric vessels despite worsening shock and abdominal findings","giving antidiarrhoeals, opioids or colonoscopy during acute dilatation"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"reassuring from patent major mesenteric vessels despite worsening shock and abdominal findings","explanation":"The hazardous action is reassuring from patent major mesenteric vessels despite worsening shock and abdominal findings. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is transmural bowel necrosis and multiorgan failure.","learningPoint":"Non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia: diffuse bowel hypoperfusion and vasoconstriction on CT angiography. Avoid reassuring from patent major mesenteric vessels despite worsening shock and abdominal findings.","id":"gastrointestinal-094","globalNumber":1774,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":95,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["bowel necrosis, sepsis and short-gut syndrome","perforation, haemorrhage and septic shock","bowel ischaemia, cavernous transformation and variceal haemorrhage","transmural necrosis, perforation and stricture","transmural bowel necrosis and multiorgan failure"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"transmural bowel necrosis and multiorgan failure","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is transmural bowel necrosis and multiorgan failure. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia.","learningPoint":"Non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia: diffuse bowel hypoperfusion and vasoconstriction on CT angiography. Avoid reassuring from patent major mesenteric vessels despite worsening shock and abdominal findings.","id":"gastrointestinal-095","globalNumber":1775,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":96,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with upper gastrointestinal bleeding after pancreatitis with isolated gastric varices and preserved liver function?","options":["Splenic vein thrombosis with gastric variceal bleeding","Budd-Chiari syndrome","Pancreatic pseudoaneurysm haemorrhage","Acute mesenteric ischaemia","Non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Splenic vein thrombosis with gastric variceal bleeding","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Splenic vein thrombosis with gastric variceal bleeding. The key discriminator is splenic vein occlusion causing left-sided portal hypertension. Immediate priorities include: resuscitate and obtain endoscopic, interventional and surgical control of gastric variceal bleeding.","learningPoint":"Splenic vein thrombosis with gastric variceal bleeding: splenic vein occlusion causing left-sided portal hypertension. Avoid treating the patient as cirrhotic portal hypertension without identifying splenic venous obstruction.","id":"gastrointestinal-096","globalNumber":1776,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":97,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with splenic vein thrombosis with gastric variceal bleeding presenting with upper gastrointestinal bleeding after pancreatitis with isolated gastric varices and preserved liver function, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["resuscitate with a restrictive transfusion strategy, give vasoactive therapy and antibiotics, then urgent endoscopy","resuscitate and obtain endoscopic, interventional and surgical control of gastric variceal bleeding","obtain urgent CT angiography and involve vascular or surgical teams for reperfusion","activate haemorrhage resuscitation and obtain urgent angiographic embolisation or surgery","resuscitate, reverse anticoagulation when appropriate and arrange urgent endoscopic or angiographic haemostasis"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"resuscitate and obtain endoscopic, interventional and surgical control of gastric variceal bleeding","explanation":"The priority is to resuscitate and obtain endoscopic, interventional and surgical control of gastric variceal bleeding. The decisive feature is splenic vein occlusion causing left-sided portal hypertension. A major error is treating the patient as cirrhotic portal hypertension without identifying splenic venous obstruction.","learningPoint":"Splenic vein thrombosis with gastric variceal bleeding: splenic vein occlusion causing left-sided portal hypertension. Avoid treating the patient as cirrhotic portal hypertension without identifying splenic venous obstruction.","id":"gastrointestinal-097","globalNumber":1777,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":98,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for splenic vein thrombosis with gastric variceal bleeding?","options":["hepatic venous outflow obstruction on Doppler or cross-sectional imaging","upper gastrointestinal bleeding in a patient with portal hypertension","splenic vein occlusion causing left-sided portal hypertension","severe pain with early minimal tenderness and vascular risk","transmural oesophageal perforation with pleural or mediastinal contamination"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"splenic vein occlusion causing left-sided portal hypertension","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is splenic vein occlusion causing left-sided portal hypertension. In context, this supports Splenic vein thrombosis with gastric variceal bleeding and should prompt clinicians to resuscitate and obtain endoscopic, interventional and surgical control of gastric variceal bleeding.","learningPoint":"Splenic vein thrombosis with gastric variceal bleeding: splenic vein occlusion causing left-sided portal hypertension. Avoid treating the patient as cirrhotic portal hypertension without identifying splenic venous obstruction.","id":"gastrointestinal-098","globalNumber":1778,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":99,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in splenic vein thrombosis with gastric variceal bleeding?","options":["correcting INR routinely with plasma and obscuring prognostic trends without bleeding","performing routine endoscopy or delaying because ECG and troponin are normal","waiting for peritonism or a raised lactate before imaging","treating the patient as cirrhotic portal hypertension without identifying splenic venous obstruction","overtransfusion that raises portal pressure or delaying antibiotics"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"treating the patient as cirrhotic portal hypertension without identifying splenic venous obstruction","explanation":"The hazardous action is treating the patient as cirrhotic portal hypertension without identifying splenic venous obstruction. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is recurrent massive haemorrhage and splenic infarction.","learningPoint":"Splenic vein thrombosis with gastric variceal bleeding: splenic vein occlusion causing left-sided portal hypertension. Avoid treating the patient as cirrhotic portal hypertension without identifying splenic venous obstruction.","id":"gastrointestinal-099","globalNumber":1779,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Gastrointestinal","categoryNumber":100,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of splenic vein thrombosis with gastric variceal bleeding, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["bowel ischaemia, cavernous transformation and variceal haemorrhage","cerebral oedema, hypoglycaemia and multiorgan failure","fulminant liver failure, portal hypertension and variceal bleeding","catastrophic rebleeding and haemorrhagic shock","recurrent massive haemorrhage and splenic infarction"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"recurrent massive haemorrhage and splenic infarction","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is recurrent massive haemorrhage and splenic infarction. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of splenic vein thrombosis with gastric variceal bleeding.","learningPoint":"Splenic vein thrombosis with gastric variceal bleeding: splenic vein occlusion causing left-sided portal hypertension. Avoid treating the patient as cirrhotic portal hypertension without identifying splenic venous obstruction.","id":"gastrointestinal-100","globalNumber":1780,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":51,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with days of minimal sleep, pressured speech, grandiosity and dangerous impulsivity with loss of reality testing. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Acute mania with psychotic features","Severe acute behavioural disturbance","Postpartum psychosis","Severe psychotic depression","High-risk suicidal psychosis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Acute mania with psychotic features","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Acute mania with psychotic features. The key discriminator is a sustained elevated or irritable mood with increased energy and psychosis not explained by delirium or substances. Immediate priorities include: provide a low-stimulation environment, manage immediate risk and use appropriate antipsychotic or mood-stabilising treatment.","learningPoint":"Acute mania with psychotic features: a sustained elevated or irritable mood with increased energy and psychosis not explained by delirium or substances. Avoid assuming reduced sleep and agitation are purely behavioural without excluding medical or toxic causes.","id":"psychiatry-051","globalNumber":1781,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":52,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has acute mania with psychotic features: days of minimal sleep, pressured speech, grandiosity and dangerous impulsivity with loss of reality testing. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["ensure immediate safety, urgent psychiatric admission and assessment of infant risk","provide a low-stimulation environment, manage immediate risk and use appropriate antipsychotic or mood-stabilising treatment","maintain continuous safety precautions and arrange urgent specialist assessment and secure disposition","use trained team de-escalation and rapid titrated chemical sedation while treating medical causes","ensure safety, treat medical compromise and obtain urgent psychiatric treatment including consideration of ECT"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"provide a low-stimulation environment, manage immediate risk and use appropriate antipsychotic or mood-stabilising treatment","explanation":"The priority is to provide a low-stimulation environment, manage immediate risk and use appropriate antipsychotic or mood-stabilising treatment. The decisive feature is a sustained elevated or irritable mood with increased energy and psychosis not explained by delirium or substances. A major error is assuming reduced sleep and agitation are purely behavioural without excluding medical or toxic causes.","learningPoint":"Acute mania with psychotic features: a sustained elevated or irritable mood with increased energy and psychosis not explained by delirium or substances. Avoid assuming reduced sleep and agitation are purely behavioural without excluding medical or toxic causes.","id":"psychiatry-052","globalNumber":1782,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":53,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected acute mania with psychotic features, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["active intent with plan, means and impaired reality testing","mood-congruent psychosis occurring only during a severe depressive episode","a sustained elevated or irritable mood with increased energy and psychosis not explained by delirium or substances","agitation accompanied by physiologic compromise rather than behaviour alone","abrupt severe mood or psychotic symptoms soon after delivery"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a sustained elevated or irritable mood with increased energy and psychosis not explained by delirium or substances","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a sustained elevated or irritable mood with increased energy and psychosis not explained by delirium or substances. In context, this supports Acute mania with psychotic features and should prompt clinicians to provide a low-stimulation environment, manage immediate risk and use appropriate antipsychotic or mood-stabilising treatment.","learningPoint":"Acute mania with psychotic features: a sustained elevated or irritable mood with increased energy and psychosis not explained by delirium or substances. Avoid assuming reduced sleep and agitation are purely behavioural without excluding medical or toxic causes.","id":"psychiatry-053","globalNumber":1783,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":54,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of acute mania with psychotic features, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["allowing unsupervised access to the infant during active psychosis","treating psychosis alone while underestimating depressive suicide risk","relying on a verbal no-harm contract as the principal risk intervention","assuming reduced sleep and agitation are purely behavioural without excluding medical or toxic causes","prolonged prone restraint or delaying temperature and glucose assessment"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"assuming reduced sleep and agitation are purely behavioural without excluding medical or toxic causes","explanation":"The hazardous action is assuming reduced sleep and agitation are purely behavioural without excluding medical or toxic causes. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is exhaustion, accidental injury, financial or sexual harm and suicide.","learningPoint":"Acute mania with psychotic features: a sustained elevated or irritable mood with increased energy and psychosis not explained by delirium or substances. Avoid assuming reduced sleep and agitation are purely behavioural without excluding medical or toxic causes.","id":"psychiatry-054","globalNumber":1784,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":55,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with acute mania with psychotic features initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["suicide, dehydration and catatonia","suicide, infanticide and severe functional deterioration","suicide attempt, absconding and harm to others","acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, trauma and sudden collapse","exhaustion, accidental injury, financial or sexual harm and suicide"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"exhaustion, accidental injury, financial or sexual harm and suicide","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is exhaustion, accidental injury, financial or sexual harm and suicide. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of acute mania with psychotic features.","learningPoint":"Acute mania with psychotic features: a sustained elevated or irritable mood with increased energy and psychosis not explained by delirium or substances. Avoid assuming reduced sleep and agitation are purely behavioural without excluding medical or toxic causes.","id":"psychiatry-055","globalNumber":1785,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":56,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with profound depressive illness with nihilistic delusions, refusal of food and high suicide risk?","options":["Severe psychotic depression","Postpartum psychosis","Acute mania with psychotic features","Severe acute behavioural disturbance","High-risk suicidal psychosis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Severe psychotic depression","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Severe psychotic depression. The key discriminator is mood-congruent psychosis occurring only during a severe depressive episode. Immediate priorities include: ensure safety, treat medical compromise and obtain urgent psychiatric treatment including consideration of ECT.","learningPoint":"Severe psychotic depression: mood-congruent psychosis occurring only during a severe depressive episode. Avoid treating psychosis alone while underestimating depressive suicide risk.","id":"psychiatry-056","globalNumber":1786,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":57,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with severe psychotic depression presenting with profound depressive illness with nihilistic delusions, refusal of food and high suicide risk, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["ensure immediate safety, urgent psychiatric admission and assessment of infant risk","ensure safety, treat medical compromise and obtain urgent psychiatric treatment including consideration of ECT","maintain continuous safety precautions and arrange urgent specialist assessment and secure disposition","provide a low-stimulation environment, manage immediate risk and use appropriate antipsychotic or mood-stabilising treatment","use trained team de-escalation and rapid titrated chemical sedation while treating medical causes"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"ensure safety, treat medical compromise and obtain urgent psychiatric treatment including consideration of ECT","explanation":"The priority is to ensure safety, treat medical compromise and obtain urgent psychiatric treatment including consideration of ECT. The decisive feature is mood-congruent psychosis occurring only during a severe depressive episode. A major error is treating psychosis alone while underestimating depressive suicide risk.","learningPoint":"Severe psychotic depression: mood-congruent psychosis occurring only during a severe depressive episode. Avoid treating psychosis alone while underestimating depressive suicide risk.","id":"psychiatry-057","globalNumber":1787,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":58,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for severe psychotic depression?","options":["a sustained elevated or irritable mood with increased energy and psychosis not explained by delirium or substances","active intent with plan, means and impaired reality testing","mood-congruent psychosis occurring only during a severe depressive episode","agitation accompanied by physiologic compromise rather than behaviour alone","abrupt severe mood or psychotic symptoms soon after delivery"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"mood-congruent psychosis occurring only during a severe depressive episode","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is mood-congruent psychosis occurring only during a severe depressive episode. In context, this supports Severe psychotic depression and should prompt clinicians to ensure safety, treat medical compromise and obtain urgent psychiatric treatment including consideration of ECT.","learningPoint":"Severe psychotic depression: mood-congruent psychosis occurring only during a severe depressive episode. Avoid treating psychosis alone while underestimating depressive suicide risk.","id":"psychiatry-058","globalNumber":1788,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":59,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in severe psychotic depression?","options":["assuming reduced sleep and agitation are purely behavioural without excluding medical or toxic causes","prolonged prone restraint or delaying temperature and glucose assessment","allowing unsupervised access to the infant during active psychosis","treating psychosis alone while underestimating depressive suicide risk","relying on a verbal no-harm contract as the principal risk intervention"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"treating psychosis alone while underestimating depressive suicide risk","explanation":"The hazardous action is treating psychosis alone while underestimating depressive suicide risk. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is suicide, dehydration and catatonia.","learningPoint":"Severe psychotic depression: mood-congruent psychosis occurring only during a severe depressive episode. Avoid treating psychosis alone while underestimating depressive suicide risk.","id":"psychiatry-059","globalNumber":1789,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":60,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of severe psychotic depression, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["suicide, infanticide and severe functional deterioration","suicide attempt, absconding and harm to others","exhaustion, accidental injury, financial or sexual harm and suicide","acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, trauma and sudden collapse","suicide, dehydration and catatonia"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"suicide, dehydration and catatonia","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is suicide, dehydration and catatonia. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of severe psychotic depression.","learningPoint":"Severe psychotic depression: mood-congruent psychosis occurring only during a severe depressive episode. Avoid treating psychosis alone while underestimating depressive suicide risk.","id":"psychiatry-060","globalNumber":1790,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":61,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by agitation, tremor, perceptual disturbance and seizures after abrupt cessation of long-term benzodiazepines. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Benzodiazepine-withdrawal syndrome","Delirium tremens","Decision-specific incapacity from delirium","Alcohol-withdrawal seizure","Severe acute behavioural disturbance"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Benzodiazepine-withdrawal syndrome","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Benzodiazepine-withdrawal syndrome. The key discriminator is autonomic hyperactivity and seizures after a compatible reduction in sedative exposure. Immediate priorities include: give a long-acting benzodiazepine regimen with monitored supportive care.","learningPoint":"Benzodiazepine-withdrawal syndrome: autonomic hyperactivity and seizures after a compatible reduction in sedative exposure. Avoid using antipsychotic monotherapy while withholding adequate benzodiazepine replacement.","id":"psychiatry-061","globalNumber":1791,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":62,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is benzodiazepine-withdrawal syndrome. The patient has agitation, tremor