 reaction","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Acute dystonic reaction. The key discriminator is rapid onset of patterned muscle contractions with preserved consciousness. Immediate priorities include: give parenteral anticholinergic therapy and observe for recurrence or airway involvement.","learningPoint":"Acute dystonic reaction: rapid onset of patterned muscle contractions with preserved consciousness. Avoid mistaking laryngeal dystonia for anxiety and delaying treatment.","id":"psychiatry-071","globalNumber":1801,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":72,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has acute dystonic reaction: painful sustained neck, jaw, ocular or laryngeal muscle spasm after dopamine-blocking medication. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["reduce or change the culprit drug and give targeted symptomatic therapy with urgent suicide-risk management","give parenteral anticholinergic therapy and observe for recurrence or airway involvement","stop the offending drug, provide intensive supportive care and consider dantrolene or bromocriptine","stop contributing drugs, resuscitate and obtain urgent surgical and psychiatric input","stop valproate, provide supportive care and consider L-carnitine with toxicology advice"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give parenteral anticholinergic therapy and observe for recurrence or airway involvement","explanation":"The priority is to give parenteral anticholinergic therapy and observe for recurrence or airway involvement. The decisive feature is rapid onset of patterned muscle contractions with preserved consciousness. A major error is mistaking laryngeal dystonia for anxiety and delaying treatment.","learningPoint":"Acute dystonic reaction: rapid onset of patterned muscle contractions with preserved consciousness. Avoid mistaking laryngeal dystonia for anxiety and delaying treatment.","id":"psychiatry-0