xplanation":"The complication to anticipate is bowel ischaemia, perforation and sepsis. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of clozapine-associated gastrointestinal hypomotility.","learningPoint":"Clozapine-associated gastrointestinal hypomotility: severe ileus or obstruction without a mechanical cause, linked to anticholinergic burden. Avoid treating with fibre alone despite obstruction or ileus features.","id":"psychiatry-090","globalNumber":1820,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":91,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with new confusion, vomiting or reduced consciousness in a patient taking valproate with normal or mildly abnormal liver tests. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Valproate-induced hyperammonaemic encephalopathy","Acute dystonic reaction","Severe anorexia nervosa with refeeding risk","Clozapine-induced myocarditis","Malignant catatonia"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Valproate-induced hyperammonaemic encephalopathy","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Valproate-induced hyperammonaemic encephalopathy. The key discriminator is elevated ammonia with compatible encephalopathy and valproate exposure. Immediate priorities include: stop valproate, provide supportive care and consider L-carnitine with toxicology advice.","learningPoint":"Valproate-induced hyperammonaemic encephalopathy: elevated ammonia with compatible encephalopathy and valproate exposure. Avoid excluding valproate toxicity because the serum concentration is therapeutic.","id":"psychiatry-091","globalNumber":1821,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":92,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has valproate-induced hyperammonaemic encephalopathy: new confusion, vomiting or reduced consciousness in a patient taking valproate with normal or mildly abnormal liver tests. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["give benzodiazepines and obtain urgent psychiatry input for electroconvulsive therapy","stop valproate, provide supportive care and consider L-carnitine with toxicology advice","stop clozapine and obtain urgent cardiology and psychiatric management","stop serotonergic agents, give benzodiazepines and aggressively cool severe cases","stop contributing drugs, resuscitate and obtain urgent surgical and psychiatric input"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"stop valproate, provide supportive care and consider L-carnitine with toxicology advice","explanation":"The priority is to stop valproate, provide supportive care and consider L-carnitine with toxicology advice. The decisive feature is elevated ammonia with compatible encephalopathy and valproate exposure. A major error is excluding valproate toxicity because the serum concentration is therapeutic.","learningPoint":"Valproate-induced hyperammonaemic encephalopathy: elevated ammonia with compatible encephalopathy and valproate exposure. Avoid excluding valproate toxicity because the serum concentration is therapeutic.","id":"psychiatry-092","globalNumber":1822,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":93,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected valproate-induced hyperammonaemic encephalopathy, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["myocardial injury and inflammation temporally associated with early clozapine exposure","clonus and hyperreflexia, especially in the lower limbs","elevated ammonia with compatible encephalopathy and valproate exposure","catatonic signs preceding autonomic collapse without a clear drug trigger","subjective distress with repetitive movement rather than goal-directed agitation or mania"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"elevated ammonia with compatible encephalopathy and valproate exposure","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is elevated ammonia with compatible encephalopathy and valproate exposure. In context, this supports Valproate-induced hyperammonaemic encephalopathy and should prompt clinicians to stop valproate, provide supportive care and consider L-carnitine with toxicology advice.","learningPoint":"Valproate-induced hyperammonaemic encephalopathy: elevated ammonia with compatible encephalopathy and valproate exposure. Avoid excluding valproate toxicity because the serum concentration is therapeutic.","id":"psychiatry-093","globalNumber":1823,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":94,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of valproate-induced hyperammonaemic encephalopathy, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["starting high-calorie feeding without electrolyte monitoring","escalating dopamine antagonists for agitation without recognising catatonia","escalating the antipsychotic because restlessness is misread as worsening psychosis","excluding valproate toxicity because the serum concentration is therapeutic","using physical restraint alone or antipyretics for muscular hyperthermia"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"excluding valproate toxicity because the serum concentration is therapeutic","explanation":"The hazardous action is excluding valproate toxicity because the serum concentration is therapeutic. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is cerebral oedema, aspiration and coma.","learningPoint":"Valproate-induced hyperammonaemic encephalopathy: elevated ammonia with compatible encephalopathy and valproate exposure. Avoid excluding valproate toxicity because the serum concentration is therapeutic.","id":"psychiatry-094","globalNumber":1824,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":95,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with valproate-induced hyperammonaemic encephalopathy initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["dehydration, thromboembolism and cardiovascular collapse","cardiogenic shock, arrhythmia and death","airway obstruction, rhabdomyolysis and recurrent dystonia","renal failure, aspiration and dysrhythmia","cerebral oedema, aspiration and coma"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"cerebral oedema, aspiration and coma","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is cerebral oedema, aspiration and coma. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of valproate-induced hyperammonaemic encephalopathy.","learningPoint":"Valproate-induced hyperammonaemic encephalopathy: elevated ammonia with compatible encephalopathy and valproate exposure. Avoid excluding valproate toxicity because the serum concentration is therapeutic.","id":"psychiatry-095","globalNumber":1825,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":96,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with a distressed postpartum patient reports unwanted intrusive harm thoughts, retains insight and actively avoids acting on them?","options":["Perinatal obsessive intrusive thoughts without psychosis","Severe psychotic depression","Postpartum psychosis","High-risk suicidal psychosis","Acute mania with psychotic features"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Perinatal obsessive intrusive thoughts without psychosis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Perinatal obsessive intrusive thoughts without psychosis. The key discriminator is ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts with preserved reality testing and no intent, unlike delusional command beliefs. Immediate priorities include: perform careful risk assessment, provide support and arrange urgent perinatal mental-health follow-up.","learningPoint":"Perinatal obsessive intrusive thoughts without psychosis: ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts with preserved reality testing and no intent, unlike delusional command beliefs. Avoid automatically equating intrusive thoughts with intent or, conversely, failing to assess safety.","id":"psychiatry-096","globalNumber":1826,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":97,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with perinatal obsessive intrusive thoughts without psychosis presenting with a distressed postpartum patient reports unwanted intrusive harm thoughts, retains insight and actively avoids acting on them, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["ensure safety, treat medical compromise and obtain urgent psychiatric treatment including consideration of ECT","perform careful risk assessment, provide support and arrange urgent perinatal mental-health follow-up","ensure immediate safety, urgent psychiatric admission and assessment of infant risk","maintain continuous safety precautions and arrange urgent specialist assessment and secure disposition","provide a low-stimulation environment, manage immediate risk and use appropriate antipsychotic or mood-stabilising treatment"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"perform careful risk assessment, provide support and arrange urgent perinatal mental-health follow-up","explanation":"The priority is to perform careful risk assessment, provide support and arrange urgent perinatal mental-health follow-up. The decisive feature is ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts with preserved reality testing and no intent, unlike delusional command beliefs. A major error is automatically equating intrusive thoughts with intent or, conversely, failing to assess safety.","learningPoint":"Perinatal obsessive intrusive thoughts without psychosis: ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts with preserved reality testing and no intent, unlike delusional command beliefs. Avoid automatically equating intrusive thoughts with intent or, conversely, failing to assess safety.","id":"psychiatry-097","globalNumber":1827,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":98,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for perinatal obsessive intrusive thoughts without psychosis?","options":["a sustained elevated or irritable mood with increased energy and psychosis not explained by delirium or substances","mood-congruent psychosis occurring only during a severe depressive episode","ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts with preserved reality testing and no intent, unlike delusional command beliefs","abrupt severe mood or psychotic symptoms soon after delivery","active intent with plan, means and impaired reality testing"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts with preserved reality testing and no intent, unlike delusional command beliefs","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts with preserved reality testing and no intent, unlike delusional command beliefs. In context, this supports Perinatal obsessive intrusive thoughts without psychosis and should prompt clinicians to perform careful risk assessment, provide support and arrange urgent perinatal mental-health follow-up.","learningPoint":"Perinatal obsessive intrusive thoughts without psychosis: ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts with preserved reality testing and no intent, unlike delusional command beliefs. Avoid automatically equating intrusive thoughts with intent or, conversely, failing to assess safety.","id":"psychiatry-098","globalNumber":1828,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":99,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in perinatal obsessive intrusive thoughts without psychosis?","options":["treating psychosis alone while underestimating depressive suicide risk","allowing unsupervised access to the infant during active psychosis","assuming reduced sleep and agitation are purely behavioural without excluding medical or toxic causes","automatically equating intrusive thoughts with intent or, conversely, failing to assess safety","relying on a verbal no-harm contract as the principal risk intervention"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"automatically equating intrusive thoughts with intent or, conversely, failing to assess safety","explanation":"The hazardous action is automatically equating intrusive thoughts with intent or, conversely, failing to assess safety. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is severe functional impairment, depression and escalating self-harm risk.","learningPoint":"Perinatal obsessive intrusive thoughts without psychosis: ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts with preserved reality testing and no intent, unlike delusional command beliefs. Avoid automatically equating intrusive thoughts with intent or, conversely, failing to assess safety.","id":"psychiatry-099","globalNumber":1829,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Psychiatry","categoryNumber":100,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of perinatal obsessive intrusive thoughts without psychosis, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["suicide, dehydration and catatonia","suicide attempt, absconding and harm to others","suicide, infanticide and severe functional deterioration","exhaustion, accidental injury, financial or sexual harm and suicide","severe functional impairment, depression and escalating self-harm risk"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"severe functional impairment, depression and escalating self-harm risk","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is severe functional impairment, depression and escalating self-harm risk. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of perinatal obsessive intrusive thoughts without psychosis.","learningPoint":"Perinatal obsessive intrusive thoughts without psychosis: ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts with preserved reality testing and no intent, unlike delusional command beliefs. Avoid automatically equating intrusive thoughts with intent or, conversely, failing to assess safety.","id":"psychiatry-100","globalNumber":1830,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":101,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with a neonate with vesicles, seizures, hepatitis or sepsis-like deterioration, sometimes without maternal history. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Neonatal herpes simplex infection","Paediatric toxic shock syndrome","Malrotation with midgut volvulus","Duct-dependent congenital heart disease","Acute bilirubin encephalopathy"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Neonatal herpes simplex infection","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Neonatal herpes simplex infection. The key discriminator is multisystem or encephalitic illness with HSV PCR positivity or characteristic vesicles. Immediate priorities include: start intravenous aciclovir immediately and obtain full sepsis and neurological evaluation.","learningPoint":"Neonatal herpes simplex infection: multisystem or encephalitic illness with HSV PCR positivity or characteristic vesicles. Avoid waiting for vesicles or PCR confirmation before treatment in a critically ill neonate.","id":"paediatrics-101","globalNumber":1831,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":102,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has neonatal herpes simplex infection: a neonate with vesicles, seizures, hepatitis or sepsis-like deterioration, sometimes without maternal history. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["give weight-based benzodiazepine promptly then an appropriate second-line antiseizure drug","start intravenous aciclovir immediately and obtain full sepsis and neurological evaluation","start emergency intensive phototherapy and arrange exchange transfusion when indicated","give immediate ceftriaxone or cefotaxime and aggressive shock resuscitation","start prostaglandin E1 and obtain urgent cardiac support while managing apnoea risk"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"start intravenous aciclovir immediately and obtain full sepsis and neurological evaluation","explanation":"The priority is to start intravenous aciclovir immediately and obtain full sepsis and neurological evaluation. The decisive feature is multisystem or encephalitic illness with HSV PCR positivity or characteristic vesicles. A major error is waiting for vesicles or PCR confirmation before treatment in a critically ill neonate.","learningPoint":"Neonatal herpes simplex infection: multisystem or encephalitic illness with HSV PCR positivity or characteristic vesicles. Avoid waiting for vesicles or PCR confirmation before treatment in a critically ill neonate.","id":"paediatrics-102","globalNumber":1832,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":103,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected neonatal herpes simplex infection, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["adrenal insufficiency with elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone and salt-wasting physiology","at least five days of fever with characteristic mucocutaneous signs and cardiac dysfunction","multisystem or encephalitic illness with HSV PCR positivity or characteristic vesicles","non-blanching purpura with systemic toxicity","severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with neurological signs"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"multisystem or encephalitic illness with HSV PCR positivity or characteristic vesicles","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is multisystem or encephalitic illness with HSV PCR positivity or characteristic vesicles. In context, this supports Neonatal herpes simplex infection and should prompt clinicians to start intravenous aciclovir immediately and obtain full sepsis and neurological evaluation.","learningPoint":"Neonatal herpes simplex infection: multisystem or encephalitic illness with HSV PCR positivity or characteristic vesicles. Avoid waiting for vesicles or PCR confirmation before treatment in a critically ill neonate.","id":"paediatrics-103","globalNumber":1833,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":104,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of neonatal herpes simplex infection, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["delaying treatment while repeating outpatient bilirubin measurements","delaying treatment because fever is absent","underdosing benzodiazepines because of fear of respiratory depression","waiting for vesicles or PCR confirmation before treatment in a critically ill neonate","using appearance alone to avoid lumbar puncture or antibiotics"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"waiting for vesicles or PCR confirmation before treatment in a critically ill neonate","explanation":"The hazardous action is waiting for vesicles or PCR confirmation before treatment in a critically ill neonate. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is disseminated infection, encephalitis and death.","learningPoint":"Neonatal herpes simplex infection: multisystem or encephalitic illness with HSV PCR positivity or characteristic vesicles. Avoid waiting for vesicles or PCR confirmation before treatment in a critically ill neonate.","id":"paediatrics-104","globalNumber":1834,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":105,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with neonatal herpes simplex infection initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["severe valvular regurgitation and chronic rheumatic heart disease","refractory shock, DIC and limb loss","kernicterus, hearing loss and permanent motor disability","recurrent injury, occult head trauma and death","disseminated infection, encephalitis and death"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"disseminated infection, encephalitis and death","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is disseminated infection, encephalitis and death. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of neonatal herpes simplex infection.","learningPoint":"Neonatal herpes simplex infection: multisystem or encephalitic illness with HSV PCR positivity or characteristic vesicles. Avoid waiting for vesicles or PCR confirmation before treatment in a critically ill neonate.","id":"paediatrics-105","globalNumber":1835,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":106,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with a young infant with vomiting, dehydration, shock, hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia, with or without ambiguous genitalia?","options":["Salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Neonatal sepsis","Malrotation with midgut volvulus","Hypernatraemic dehydration in infancy","Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia. The key discriminator is adrenal insufficiency with elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone and salt-wasting physiology. Immediate priorities include: give stress-dose hydrocortisone, isotonic fluid and urgent electrolyte correction.","learningPoint":"Salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia: adrenal insufficiency with elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone and salt-wasting physiology. Avoid treating as gastroenteritis without checking electrolytes or giving steroids.","id":"paediatrics-106","globalNumber":1836,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":107,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia presenting with a young infant with vomiting, dehydration, shock, hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia, with or without ambiguous genitalia, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["correct chloride and potassium depletion before definitive pyloromyotomy","give stress-dose hydrocortisone, isotonic fluid and urgent electrolyte correction","resuscitate and obtain immediate paediatric surgical management without delaying for prolonged imaging","obtain rapid cultures and give immediate age-appropriate intravenous antibiotics with supportive care","restore circulation first, then lower sodium gradually with frequent monitoring"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give stress-dose hydrocortisone, isotonic fluid and urgent electrolyte correction","explanation":"The priority is to give stress-dose hydrocortisone, isotonic fluid and urgent electrolyte correction. The decisive feature is adrenal insufficiency with elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone and salt-wasting physiology. A major error is treating as gastroenteritis without checking electrolytes or giving steroids.","learningPoint":"Salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia: adrenal insufficiency with elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone and salt-wasting physiology. Avoid treating as gastroenteritis without checking electrolytes or giving steroids.","id":"paediatrics-107","globalNumber":1837,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":108,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia?","options":["nonspecific systemic deterioration in the first month of life","hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis","adrenal insufficiency with elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone and salt-wasting physiology","bilious emesis with a corkscrew or obstructed duodenum","severe water deficit with high sodium despite deceptively preserved skin turgor"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"adrenal insufficiency with elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone and salt-wasting physiology","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is adrenal insufficiency with elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone and salt-wasting physiology. In context, this supports Salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia and should prompt clinicians to give stress-dose hydrocortisone, isotonic fluid and urgent electrolyte correction.","learningPoint":"Salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia: adrenal insufficiency with elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone and salt-wasting physiology. Avoid treating as gastroenteritis without checking electrolytes or giving steroids.","id":"paediatrics-108","globalNumber":1838,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":109,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia?","options":["rushing to surgery before correcting severe metabolic derangement","rapid hypotonic correction before stabilising perfusion","delaying treatment because fever is absent","treating as gastroenteritis without checking electrolytes or giving steroids","treating as gastroenteritis and feeding the child"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"treating as gastroenteritis without checking electrolytes or giving steroids","explanation":"The hazardous action is treating as gastroenteritis without checking electrolytes or giving steroids. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is refractory shock, arrhythmia and death.","learningPoint":"Salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia: adrenal insufficiency with elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone and salt-wasting physiology. Avoid treating as gastroenteritis without checking electrolytes or giving steroids.","id":"paediatrics-109","globalNumber":1839,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":110,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["shock, meningitis and disseminated infection","apnoea, arrhythmia and aspiration","cerebral oedema, seizures and venous thrombosis","bowel necrosis and short-gut syndrome","refractory shock, arrhythmia and death"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"refractory shock, arrhythmia and death","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is refractory shock, arrhythmia and death. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia.","learningPoint":"Salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia: adrenal insufficiency with elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone and salt-wasting physiology. Avoid treating as gastroenteritis without checking electrolytes or giving steroids.","id":"paediatrics-110","globalNumber":1840,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":111,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by a jaundiced neonate develops poor feeding, abnormal tone, high-pitched cry or opisthotonus. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Acute bilirubin encephalopathy","Neonatal herpes simplex infection","Non-accidental injury","Salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Febrile infant younger than 28 days"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Acute bilirubin encephalopathy","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Acute bilirubin encephalopathy. The key discriminator is severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with neurological signs. Immediate priorities include: start emergency intensive phototherapy and arrange exchange transfusion when indicated.","learningPoint":"Acute bilirubin encephalopathy: severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with neurological signs. Avoid delaying treatment while repeating outpatient bilirubin measurements.","id":"paediatrics-111","globalNumber":1841,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":112,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is acute bilirubin encephalopathy. The patient has a jaundiced neonate develops poor feeding, abnormal tone, high-pitched cry or opisthotonus. What is the best immediate management?","options":["obtain rapid cultures and give immediate age-appropriate intravenous antibiotics with supportive care","start emergency intensive phototherapy and arrange exchange transfusion when indicated","start intravenous aciclovir immediately and obtain full sepsis and neurological evaluation","correct chloride and potassium depletion before definitive pyloromyotomy","start prostaglandin E1 and obtain urgent cardiac support while managing apnoea risk"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"start emergency intensive phototherapy and arrange exchange transfusion when indicated","explanation":"The priority is to start emergency intensive phototherapy and arrange exchange transfusion when indicated. The decisive feature is severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with neurological signs. A major error is delaying treatment while repeating outpatient bilirubin measurements.","learningPoint":"Acute bilirubin encephalopathy: severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with neurological signs. Avoid delaying treatment while repeating outpatient bilirubin measurements.","id":"paediatrics-112","globalNumber":1842,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":113,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have acute bilirubin encephalopathy. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["acute hepatic synthetic failure with INR elevation not corrected by vitamin K","hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis","severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with neurological signs","multisystem or encephalitic illness with HSV PCR positivity or characteristic vesicles","ongoing generalised motor activity without recovery"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with neurological signs","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with neurological signs. In context, this supports Acute bilirubin encephalopathy and should prompt clinicians to start emergency intensive phototherapy and arrange exchange transfusion when indicated.","learningPoint":"Acute bilirubin encephalopathy: severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with neurological signs. Avoid delaying treatment while repeating outpatient bilirubin measurements.","id":"paediatrics-113","globalNumber":1843,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":114,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with acute bilirubin encephalopathy is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["waiting for vesicles or PCR confirmation before treatment in a critically ill neonate","delaying treatment because fever is absent","routine prolonged hyperventilation without signs of herniation","delaying treatment while repeating outpatient bilirubin measurements","rushing to surgery before correcting severe metabolic derangement"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"delaying treatment while repeating outpatient bilirubin measurements","explanation":"The hazardous action is delaying treatment while repeating outpatient bilirubin measurements. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is kernicterus, hearing loss and permanent motor disability.","learningPoint":"Acute bilirubin encephalopathy: severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with neurological signs. Avoid delaying treatment while repeating outpatient bilirubin measurements.","id":"paediatrics-114","globalNumber":1844,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":115,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with acute bilirubin encephalopathy?","options":["hypoxia, hyperthermia and neuronal injury","disseminated infection, encephalitis and death","apnoea, arrhythmia and aspiration","bowel necrosis and short-gut syndrome","kernicterus, hearing loss and permanent motor disability"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"kernicterus, hearing loss and permanent motor disability","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is kernicterus, hearing loss and permanent motor disability. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of acute bilirubin encephalopathy.","learningPoint":"Acute bilirubin encephalopathy: severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with neurological signs. Avoid delaying treatment while repeating outpatient bilirubin measurements.","id":"paediatrics-115","globalNumber":1845,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":116,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has a child with viral prodrome, unexplained tachycardia, hepatomegaly, respiratory distress or shock. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Paediatric myocarditis","Infant supraventricular tachycardia","Duct-dependent congenital heart disease","Kawasaki disease shock syndrome","Multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Paediatric myocarditis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Paediatric myocarditis. The key discriminator is ventricular dysfunction and myocardial injury disproportionate to respiratory findings. Immediate priorities include: minimise myocardial workload, support perfusion cautiously and obtain early intensive-care and cardiology input.","learningPoint":"Paediatric myocarditis: ventricular dysfunction and myocardial injury disproportionate to respiratory findings. Avoid giving repeated large fluid boluses for presumed sepsis without reassessing cardiac function.","id":"paediatrics-116","globalNumber":1846,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":117,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for paediatric myocarditis when the presentation is a child with viral prodrome, unexplained tachycardia, hepatomegaly, respiratory distress or shock?","options":["start prostaglandin E1 and obtain urgent cardiac support while managing apnoea risk","minimise myocardial workload, support perfusion cautiously and obtain early intensive-care and cardiology input","give IVIG, aspirin and shock support with paediatric cardiology input","support shock, obtain early intensive-care and immunomodulatory specialist management","obtain rapid cultures and give immediate age-appropriate intravenous antibiotics with supportive care"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"minimise myocardial workload, support perfusion cautiously and obtain early intensive-care and cardiology input","explanation":"The priority is to minimise myocardial workload, support perfusion cautiously and obtain early intensive-care and cardiology input. The decisive feature is ventricular dysfunction and myocardial injury disproportionate to respiratory findings. A major error is giving repeated large fluid boluses for presumed sepsis without reassessing cardiac function.","learningPoint":"Paediatric myocarditis: ventricular dysfunction and myocardial injury disproportionate to respiratory findings. Avoid giving repeated large fluid boluses for presumed sepsis without reassessing cardiac function.","id":"paediatrics-117","globalNumber":1847,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":118,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for paediatric myocarditis in this presentation?","options":["multisystem inflammation with myocardial dysfunction and no better microbial cause","rapid improvement in perfusion or oxygenation after ductal reopening","ventricular dysfunction and myocardial injury disproportionate to respiratory findings","at least five days of fever with characteristic mucocutaneous signs and cardiac dysfunction","rapid multisystem involvement after likely allergen exposure"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"ventricular dysfunction and myocardial injury disproportionate to respiratory findings","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is ventricular dysfunction and myocardial injury disproportionate to respiratory findings. In context, this supports Paediatric myocarditis and should prompt clinicians to minimise myocardial workload, support perfusion cautiously and obtain early intensive-care and cardiology input.","learningPoint":"Paediatric myocarditis: ventricular dysfunction and myocardial injury disproportionate to respiratory findings. Avoid giving repeated large fluid boluses for presumed sepsis without reassessing cardiac function.","id":"paediatrics-118","globalNumber":1848,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":119,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in paediatric myocarditis?","options":["giving repeated fluid boluses while delaying prostaglandin","treating as simple gastroenteritis despite shock and cardiac involvement","delaying antibiotics for lumbar puncture","giving repeated large fluid boluses for presumed sepsis without reassessing cardiac function","treating as bacterial sepsis alone after cultures remain negative"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"giving repeated large fluid boluses for presumed sepsis without reassessing cardiac function","explanation":"The hazardous action is giving repeated large fluid boluses for presumed sepsis without reassessing cardiac function. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is malignant arrhythmia, cardiogenic shock and cardiac arrest.","learningPoint":"Paediatric myocarditis: ventricular dysfunction and myocardial injury disproportionate to respiratory findings. Avoid giving repeated large fluid boluses for presumed sepsis without reassessing cardiac function.","id":"paediatrics-119","globalNumber":1849,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":120,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in paediatric myocarditis, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["refractory shock, DIC and limb loss","ductal closure, profound hypoxaemia and cardiac arrest","coronary abnormalities, arrhythmia and cardiogenic shock","coronary artery aneurysm, myocarditis and shock","malignant arrhythmia, cardiogenic shock and cardiac arrest"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"malignant arrhythmia, cardiogenic shock and cardiac arrest","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is malignant arrhythmia, cardiogenic shock and cardiac arrest. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of paediatric myocarditis.","learningPoint":"Paediatric myocarditis: ventricular dysfunction and myocardial injury disproportionate to respiratory findings. Avoid giving repeated large fluid boluses for presumed sepsis without reassessing cardiac function.","id":"paediatrics-120","globalNumber":1850,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":121,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with a child develops pallor, reduced urine output and bruising after bloody diarrhoea. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Paediatric haemolytic uraemic syndrome","Paediatric tumour lysis syndrome","Paediatric hyperleukocytosis","Paediatric febrile neutropenia","Meningococcal septic shock"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Paediatric haemolytic uraemic syndrome","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Paediatric haemolytic uraemic syndrome. The key discriminator is microangiopathic haemolysis, thrombocytopenia and acute kidney injury after enteric infection. Immediate priorities include: provide careful fluid and renal support with nephrology input and avoid antimotility medication.","learningPoint":"Paediatric haemolytic uraemic syndrome: microangiopathic haemolysis, thrombocytopenia and acute kidney injury after enteric infection. Avoid giving platelet transfusion without bleeding or treating diarrhoea with antimotility drugs.","id":"paediatrics-121","globalNumber":1851,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":122,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has paediatric haemolytic uraemic syndrome: a child develops pallor, reduced urine output and bruising after bloody diarrhoea. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["prevent hypoglycaemia, manage cerebral oedema risk and contact a paediatric transplant centre early","provide careful fluid and renal support with nephrology input and avoid antimotility medication","begin urgent cytoreduction and tumour-lysis prevention with paediatric oncology","give immediate ceftriaxone or cefotaxime and aggressive shock resuscitation","give monitored hydration, urate-lowering therapy and urgent renal support for refractory abnormalities"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"provide careful fluid and renal support with nephrology input and avoid antimotility medication","explanation":"The priority is to provide careful fluid and renal support with nephrology input and avoid antimotility medication. The decisive feature is microangiopathic haemolysis, thrombocytopenia and acute kidney injury after enteric infection. A major error is giving platelet transfusion without bleeding or treating diarrhoea with antimotility drugs.","learningPoint":"Paediatric haemolytic uraemic syndrome: microangiopathic haemolysis, thrombocytopenia and acute kidney injury after enteric infection. Avoid giving platelet transfusion without bleeding or treating diarrhoea with antimotility drugs.","id":"paediatrics-122","globalNumber":1852,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":123,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected paediatric haemolytic uraemic syndrome, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["clinical leukostasis and tumour-lysis risk rather than the count alone","fever with a severely reduced absolute neutrophil count in an oncology patient","microangiopathic haemolysis, thrombocytopenia and acute kidney injury after enteric infection","rapid metabolic derangement after spontaneous or treatment-related tumour breakdown","non-blanching purpura with systemic toxicity"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"microangiopathic haemolysis, thrombocytopenia and acute kidney injury after enteric infection","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is microangiopathic haemolysis, thrombocytopenia and acute kidney injury after enteric infection. In context, this supports Paediatric haemolytic uraemic syndrome and should prompt clinicians to provide careful fluid and renal support with nephrology input and avoid antimotility medication.","learningPoint":"Paediatric haemolytic uraemic syndrome: microangiopathic haemolysis, thrombocytopenia and acute kidney injury after enteric infection. Avoid giving platelet transfusion without bleeding or treating diarrhoea with antimotility drugs.","id":"paediatrics-123","globalNumber":1853,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":124,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of paediatric haemolytic uraemic syndrome, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["waiting for hypotension or positive cultures before treatment","using potassium-containing maintenance fluids in a high-risk child","giving routine red-cell transfusion before controlling hyperviscosity","giving platelet transfusion without bleeding or treating diarrhoea with antimotility drugs","delaying antibiotics for lumbar puncture"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"giving platelet transfusion without bleeding or treating diarrhoea with antimotility drugs","explanation":"The hazardous action is giving platelet transfusion without bleeding or treating diarrhoea with antimotility drugs. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is renal failure, hypertension and neurological injury.","learningPoint":"Paediatric haemolytic uraemic syndrome: microangiopathic haemolysis, thrombocytopenia and acute kidney injury after enteric infection. Avoid giving platelet transfusion without bleeding or treating diarrhoea with antimotility drugs.","id":"paediatrics-124","globalNumber":1854,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":125,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with paediatric haemolytic uraemic syndrome initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["intracranial haemorrhage, respiratory failure and tumour lysis","DIC, adrenal haemorrhage and multiorgan failure","septic shock, fungal infection and death","cerebral oedema, bleeding and multiorgan failure","renal failure, hypertension and neurological injury"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"renal failure, hypertension and neurological injury","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is renal failure, hypertension and neurological injury. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of paediatric haemolytic uraemic syndrome.","learningPoint":"Paediatric haemolytic uraemic syndrome: microangiopathic haemolysis, thrombocytopenia and acute kidney injury after enteric infection. Avoid giving platelet transfusion without bleeding or treating diarrhoea with antimotility drugs.","id":"paediatrics-125","globalNumber":1855,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":126,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with an infant with weight loss, lethargy and poor feeding has marked hypernatraemia and circulatory compromise?","options":["Hypernatraemic dehydration in infancy","Malrotation with midgut volvulus","Salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Neonatal sepsis","Paediatric tumour lysis syndrome"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Hypernatraemic dehydration in infancy","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Hypernatraemic dehydration in infancy. The key discriminator is severe water deficit with high sodium despite deceptively preserved skin turgor. Immediate priorities include: restore circulation first, then lower sodium gradually with frequent monitoring.","learningPoint":"Hypernatraemic dehydration in infancy: severe water deficit with high sodium despite deceptively preserved skin turgor. Avoid rapid hypotonic correction before stabilising perfusion.","id":"paediatrics-126","globalNumber":1856,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":127,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with hypernatraemic dehydration in infancy presenting with an infant with weight loss, lethargy and poor feeding has marked hypernatraemia and circulatory compromise, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["obtain ultrasound and arrange pneumatic or hydrostatic reduction if stable without perforation","restore circulation first, then lower sodium gradually with frequent monitoring","give stress-dose hydrocortisone, isotonic fluid and urgent electrolyte correction","obtain rapid cultures and give immediate age-appropriate intravenous antibiotics with supportive care","prevent hypoglycaemia, manage cerebral oedema risk and contact a paediatric transplant centre early"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"restore circulation first, then lower sodium gradually with frequent monitoring","explanation":"The priority is to restore circulation first, then lower sodium gradually with frequent monitoring. The decisive feature is severe water deficit with high sodium despite deceptively preserved skin turgor. A major error is rapid hypotonic correction before stabilising perfusion.","learningPoint":"Hypernatraemic dehydration in infancy: severe water deficit with high sodium despite deceptively preserved skin turgor. Avoid rapid hypotonic correction before stabilising perfusion.","id":"paediatrics-127","globalNumber":1857,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":128,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for hypernatraemic dehydration in infancy?","options":["hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis","adrenal insufficiency with elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone and salt-wasting physiology","severe water deficit with high sodium despite deceptively preserved skin turgor","target or doughnut sign on ultrasound","nonspecific systemic deterioration in the first month of life"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"severe water deficit with high sodium despite deceptively preserved skin turgor","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is severe water deficit with high sodium despite deceptively preserved skin turgor. In context, this supports Hypernatraemic dehydration in infancy and should prompt clinicians to restore circulation first, then lower sodium gradually with frequent monitoring.","learningPoint":"Hypernatraemic dehydration in infancy: severe water deficit with high sodium despite deceptively preserved skin turgor. Avoid rapid hypotonic correction before stabilising perfusion.","id":"paediatrics-128","globalNumber":1858,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":129,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in hypernatraemic dehydration in infancy?","options":["delaying corticosteroid because infection is also suspected","treating as gastroenteritis without checking electrolytes or giving steroids","using potassium-containing maintenance fluids in a high-risk child","rapid hypotonic correction before stabilising perfusion","delaying treatment because fever is absent"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"rapid hypotonic correction before stabilising perfusion","explanation":"The hazardous action is rapid hypotonic correction before stabilising perfusion. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is cerebral oedema, seizures and venous thrombosis.","learningPoint":"Hypernatraemic dehydration in infancy: severe water deficit with high sodium despite deceptively preserved skin turgor. Avoid rapid hypotonic correction before stabilising perfusion.","id":"paediatrics-129","globalNumber":1859,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":130,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of hypernatraemic dehydration in infancy, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["arrhythmia, seizure and renal failure","shock, meningitis and disseminated infection","refractory shock, arrhythmia and death","cerebral injury, hypokalaemia and hypoglycaemia","cerebral oedema, seizures and venous thrombosis"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"cerebral oedema, seizures and venous thrombosis","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is cerebral oedema, seizures and venous thrombosis. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of hypernatraemic dehydration in infancy.","learningPoint":"Hypernatraemic dehydration in infancy: severe water deficit with high sodium despite deceptively preserved skin turgor. Avoid rapid hypotonic correction before stabilising perfusion.","id":"paediatrics-130","globalNumber":1860,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":131,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by a young infant has paroxysmal cough, cyanosis, apnoea or bradycardia with little fever. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Pertussis with apnoea","Life-threatening paediatric asthma","Paediatric foreign-body aspiration","Neonatal sepsis","Febrile infant younger than 28 days"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Pertussis with apnoea","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Pertussis with apnoea. The key discriminator is apnoeic or paroxysmal illness with lymphocytosis and compatible molecular testing. Immediate priorities include: provide cardiorespiratory support, give appropriate antibiotics and arrange monitored admission.","learningPoint":"Pertussis with apnoea: apnoeic or paroxysmal illness with lymphocytosis and compatible molecular testing. Avoid reassuring from a normal examination between coughing episodes.","id":"paediatrics-131","globalNumber":1861,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":132,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is pertussis with apnoea. The patient has a young infant has paroxysmal cough, cyanosis, apnoea or bradycardia with little fever. What is the best immediate management?","options":["obtain rapid cultures and give immediate age-appropriate intravenous antibiotics with supportive care","provide cardiorespiratory support, give appropriate antibiotics and arrange monitored admission","provide airway support, oxygen or high-flow therapy and admit for monitoring","perform a full sepsis evaluation, give empiric intravenous antibiotics and admit","maintain oxygenation and arrange urgent rigid bronchoscopy when suspicion is significant"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"provide cardiorespiratory support, give appropriate antibiotics and arrange monitored admission","explanation":"The priority is to provide cardiorespiratory support, give appropriate antibiotics and arrange monitored admission. The decisive feature is apnoeic or paroxysmal illness with lymphocytosis and compatible molecular testing. A major error is reassuring from a normal examination between coughing episodes.","learningPoint":"Pertussis with apnoea: apnoeic or paroxysmal illness with lymphocytosis and compatible molecular testing. Avoid reassuring from a normal examination between coughing episodes.","id":"paediatrics-132","globalNumber":1862,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":133,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have pertussis with apnoea. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["nonspecific systemic deterioration in the first month of life","reduced air entry with fatigue or rising PaCO2","apnoeic or paroxysmal illness with lymphocytosis and compatible molecular testing","abrupt symptoms with focal examination or air trapping despite incomplete imaging findings","age under 28 days itself confers high invasive bacterial risk"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"apnoeic or paroxysmal illness with lymphocytosis and compatible molecular testing","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is apnoeic or paroxysmal illness with lymphocytosis and compatible molecular testing. In context, this supports Pertussis with apnoea and should prompt clinicians to provide cardiorespiratory support, give appropriate antibiotics and arrange monitored admission.","learningPoint":"Pertussis with apnoea: apnoeic or paroxysmal illness with lymphocytosis and compatible molecular testing. Avoid reassuring from a normal examination between coughing episodes.","id":"paediatrics-133","globalNumber":1863,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":134,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with pertussis with apnoea is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["repeatedly treating as asthma because the chest radiograph is normal","routine bronchodilators, steroids or antibiotics without another indication","using appearance alone to avoid lumbar puncture or antibiotics","reassuring from a normal examination between coughing episodes","sedating or intubating without accounting for dynamic hyperinflation"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"reassuring from a normal examination between coughing episodes","explanation":"The hazardous action is reassuring from a normal examination between coughing episodes. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is hypoxic brain injury, pulmonary hypertension and death.","learningPoint":"Pertussis with apnoea: apnoeic or paroxysmal illness with lymphocytosis and compatible molecular testing. Avoid reassuring from a normal examination between coughing episodes.","id":"paediatrics-134","globalNumber":1864,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":135,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with pertussis with apnoea?","options":["occult bacteraemia, meningitis and rapid deterioration","shock, meningitis and disseminated infection","complete obstruction, atelectasis and post-obstructive infection","respiratory failure and exhaustion","hypoxic brain injury, pulmonary hypertension and death"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"hypoxic brain injury, pulmonary hypertension and death","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is hypoxic brain injury, pulmonary hypertension and death. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of pertussis with apnoea.","learningPoint":"Pertussis with apnoea: apnoeic or paroxysmal illness with lymphocytosis and compatible molecular testing. Avoid reassuring from a normal examination between coughing episodes.","id":"paediatrics-135","globalNumber":1865,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":136,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has sudden cough or choking followed by unilateral wheeze or reduced air entry, sometimes with normal radiographs. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Paediatric foreign-body aspiration","Bronchiolitis with apnoea","Pertussis with apnoea","Bacterial tracheitis","Severe croup"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Paediatric foreign-body aspiration","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Paediatric foreign-body aspiration. The key discriminator is abrupt symptoms with focal examination or air trapping despite incomplete imaging findings. Immediate priorities include: maintain oxygenation and arrange urgent rigid bronchoscopy when suspicion is significant.","learningPoint":"Paediatric foreign-body aspiration: abrupt symptoms with focal examination or air trapping despite incomplete imaging findings. Avoid repeatedly treating as asthma because the chest radiograph is normal.","id":"paediatrics-136","globalNumber":1866,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":137,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for paediatric foreign-body aspiration when the presentation is sudden cough or choking followed by unilateral wheeze or reduced air entry, sometimes with normal radiographs?","options":["give dexamethasone and nebulised adrenaline while minimising distress and preparing airway support","maintain oxygenation and arrange urgent rigid bronchoscopy when suspicion is significant","arrange immediate endoscopic removal and assess for tissue injury","give repeated inhaled beta agonist and ipratropium, systemic steroid, magnesium and prepare escalation","keep the child calm and arrange controlled airway management with anaesthesia and ENT"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"maintain oxygenation and arrange urgent rigid bronchoscopy when suspicion is significant","explanation":"The priority is to maintain oxygenation and arrange urgent rigid bronchoscopy when suspicion is significant. The decisive feature is abrupt symptoms with focal examination or air trapping despite incomplete imaging findings. A major error is repeatedly treating as asthma because the chest radiograph is normal.","learningPoint":"Paediatric foreign-body aspiration: abrupt symptoms with focal examination or air trapping despite incomplete imaging findings. Avoid repeatedly treating as asthma because the chest radiograph is normal.","id":"paediatrics-137","globalNumber":1867,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":138,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for paediatric foreign-body aspiration in this presentation?","options":["apnoea in a young or premature infant during viral bronchiolitis","severe odynophagia and drooling with minimal cough","abrupt symptoms with focal examination or air trapping despite incomplete imaging findings","age under 28 days itself confers high invasive bacterial risk","symptoms or airway findings indicating deep aerodigestive injury, noting oral burns may be absent"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"abrupt symptoms with focal examination or air trapping despite incomplete imaging findings","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is abrupt symptoms with focal examination or air trapping despite incomplete imaging findings. In context, this supports Paediatric foreign-body aspiration and should prompt clinicians to maintain oxygenation and arrange urgent rigid bronchoscopy when suspicion is significant.","learningPoint":"Paediatric foreign-body aspiration: abrupt symptoms with focal examination or air trapping despite incomplete imaging findings. Avoid repeatedly treating as asthma because the chest radiograph is normal.","id":"paediatrics-138","globalNumber":1868,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":139,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in paediatric foreign-body aspiration?","options":["neutralising the agent, inducing vomiting or performing blind gastric lavage","forcing supine examination or throat instrumentation in the ED","giving antihistamine or nebulised therapy before adrenaline","repeatedly treating as asthma because the chest radiograph is normal","routine bronchodilators, steroids or antibiotics without another indication"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"repeatedly treating as asthma because the chest radiograph is normal","explanation":"The hazardous action is repeatedly treating as asthma because the chest radiograph is normal. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is complete obstruction, atelectasis and post-obstructive infection.","learningPoint":"Paediatric foreign-body aspiration: abrupt symptoms with focal examination or air trapping despite incomplete imaging findings. Avoid repeatedly treating as asthma because the chest radiograph is normal.","id":"paediatrics-139","globalNumber":1869,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":140,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in paediatric foreign-body aspiration, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["shock, meningitis and disseminated infection","biphasic reaction, airway obstruction and shock","complete upper-airway obstruction","hypoxic brain injury, pulmonary hypertension and death","complete obstruction, atelectasis and post-obstructive infection"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"complete obstruction, atelectasis and post-obstructive infection","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is complete obstruction, atelectasis and post-obstructive infection. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of paediatric foreign-body aspiration.","learningPoint":"Paediatric foreign-body aspiration: abrupt symptoms with focal examination or air trapping despite incomplete imaging findings. Avoid repeatedly treating as asthma because the chest radiograph is normal.","id":"paediatrics-140","globalNumber":1870,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":141,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with a child with drooling, dysphagia or nonspecific symptoms has a round radiopaque oesophageal object. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Button-battery oesophageal impaction","Caustic ingestion with upper-airway injury","Paediatric epiglottitis","Bacterial tracheitis","Paediatric foreign-body aspiration"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Button-battery oesophageal impaction","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Button-battery oesophageal impaction. The key discriminator is the double-rim or step-off sign of a button battery in the oesophagus. Immediate priorities include: arrange immediate endoscopic removal and assess for tissue injury.","learningPoint":"Button-battery oesophageal impaction: the double-rim or step-off sign of a button battery in the oesophagus. Avoid waiting for spontaneous passage of an oesophageal button battery.","id":"paediatrics-141","globalNumber":1871,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":142,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has button-battery oesophageal impaction: a child with drooling, dysphagia or nonspecific symptoms has a round radiopaque oesophageal object. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["keep the child calm and arrange controlled airway management with anaesthesia and ENT","arrange immediate endoscopic removal and assess for tissue injury","secure a threatened airway and arrange early endoscopic and surgical assessment without inducing emesis","secure the airway and give broad intravenous antibiotics","give intramuscular adrenaline into the lateral thigh and repeat as required"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"arrange immediate endoscopic removal and assess for tissue injury","explanation":"The priority is to arrange immediate endoscopic removal and assess for tissue injury. The decisive feature is the double-rim or step-off sign of a button battery in the oesophagus. A major error is waiting for spontaneous passage of an oesopha