efinitive care; an important consequence is ARDS, cerebral hypoxic injury and cardiac arrest.","learningPoint":"Paediatric submersion injury: hypoxaemic lung injury after submersion, irrespective of aspirated water type. Avoid using prophylactic antibiotics or delaying ventilation because initial chest imaging is normal.","id":"paediatrics-179","globalNumber":1909,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":180,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in paediatric submersion injury, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["recurrent injury, occult head trauma and death","hypovolaemic shock, airway obstruction and hypothermia","oesophageal perforation, stricture and airway obstruction","testicular infarction and loss","ARDS, cerebral hypoxic injury and cardiac arrest"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"ARDS, cerebral hypoxic injury and cardiac arrest","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is ARDS, cerebral hypoxic injury and cardiac arrest. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of paediatric submersion injury.","learningPoint":"Paediatric submersion injury: hypoxaemic lung injury after submersion, irrespective of aspirated water type. Avoid using prophylactic antibiotics or delaying ventilation because initial chest imaging is normal.","id":"paediatrics-180","globalNumber":1910,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":181,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with a child has significant burns, facial soot or enclosed-space smoke exposure. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Paediatric major burn with inhalation risk","Raised intracranial pressure in a child","Paediatric submersion injury","Non-accidental injury","Paediatric testicular torsion"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Paediatric major burn with inhalation risk","explanation":"The presentation is most 