king neurosurgery","prioritise oxygenation, ventilation and temperature management with monitored observation"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give analgesia and obtain immediate surgical exploration","explanation":"The priority is to give analgesia and obtain immediate surgical exploration. The decisive feature is abrupt pain with a twisted spermatic cord; Doppler should not delay surgery when classic. A major error is waiting for ultrasound in a classic time-critical presentation.","learningPoint":"Paediatric testicular torsion: abrupt pain with a twisted spermatic cord; Doppler should not delay surgery when classic. Avoid waiting for ultrasound in a classic time-critical presentation.","id":"paediatrics-192","globalNumber":1922,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":193,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have paediatric testicular torsion. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["hypoxaemic lung injury after submersion, irrespective of aspirated water type","sentinel injuries or bruising in a non-mobile infant","abrupt pain with a twisted spermatic cord; Doppler should not delay surgery when classic","symptoms or airway findings indicating deep aerodigestive injury, noting oral burns may be absent","burn size and airway features adjusted for age, with early oedema risk"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"abrupt pain with a twisted spermatic cord; Doppler should not delay surgery when classic","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is abrupt pain with a twisted spermatic cord; Doppler should not delay surgery when classic. In context, this supports Paediatric testicular torsion and should prompt clinicians to give analgesia and obtain immediate surgical exploration.","learningPoint":"Paediatric testicular torsion: abrupt pain with a twisted spermatic cord; Doppler should not delay surgery when classic. Avoid waiting for ultrasound in a classic time-critical presentation.","id":"paediatrics-193","globalNumber":1923,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":194,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with paediatric testicular torsion is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["routine prolonged hyperventilation without signs of herniation","underestimating burn size or delaying airway control as oedema progresses","neutralising the agent, inducing vomiting or performing blind gastric lavage","waiting for ultrasound in a classic time-critical presentation","confronting caregivers alone or discharging before a safety plan"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"waiting for ultrasound in a classic time-critical presentation","explanation":"The hazardous action is waiting for ultrasound in a classic time-critical presentation. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is testicular infarction and loss.","learningPoint":"Paediatric testicular torsion: abrupt pain with a twisted spermatic cord; Doppler should not delay surgery when classic. Avoid waiting for ultrasound in a classic time-critical presentation.","id":"paediatrics-194","globalNumber":1924,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":195,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with paediatric testicular torsion?","options":["ARDS, cerebral hypoxic injury and cardiac arrest","oesophageal perforation, stricture and airway obstruction","cerebral herniation and secondary brain injury","recurrent injury, occult head trauma and death","testicular infarction and loss"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"testicular infarction and loss","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is testicular infarction and loss. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of paediatric testicular torsion.","learningPoint":"Paediatric testicular torsion: abrupt pain with a twisted spermatic cord; Doppler should not delay surgery when classic. Avoid waiting for ultrasound in a classic time-critical presentation.","id":"paediatrics-195","globalNumber":1925,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":196,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has a steroid-dependent child with vomiting, hypoglycaemia, hypotension or hyponatraemia during illness. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Paediatric adrenal crisis from steroid dependence","Paediatric acute liver failure","Paediatric diabetic ketoacidosis","Salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Paediatric tumour lysis syndrome"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Paediatric adrenal crisis from steroid dependence","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Paediatric adrenal crisis from steroid dependence. The key discriminator is shock or hypoglycaemia with inadequate stress steroid response. Immediate priorities include: give immediate stress-dose hydrocortisone, glucose and isotonic fluid.","learningPoint":"Paediatric adrenal crisis from steroid dependence: shock or hypoglycaemia with inadequate stress steroid response. Avoid delaying corticosteroid because infection is also suspected.","id":"paediatrics-196","globalNumber":1926,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":197,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for paediatric adrenal crisis from steroid dependence when the presentation is a steroid-dependent child with vomiting, hypoglycaemia, hypotension or hyponatraemia during illness?","options":["give monitored hydration, urate-lowering therapy and urgent renal support for refractory abnormalities","give immediate stress-dose hydrocortisone, glucose and isotonic fluid","prevent hypoglycaemia, manage cerebral oedema risk and contact a paediatric transplant centre early","restore circulation first, then lower sodium gradually with frequent monitoring","use cautious protocolised fluids, start insulin without a bolus and monitor neurologic status"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give immediate stress-dose hydrocortisone, glucose and isotonic fluid","explanation":"The priority is to give immediate stress-dose hydrocortisone, glucose and isotonic fluid. The decisive feature is shock or hypoglycaemia with inadequate stress steroid response. A major error is delaying corticosteroid because infection is also suspected.","learningPoint":"Paediatric adrenal crisis from steroid dependence: shock or hypoglycaemia with inadequate stress steroid response. Avoid delaying corticosteroid because infection is also suspected.","id":"paediatrics-197","globalNumber":1927,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":198,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for paediatric adrenal crisis from steroid dependence in this presentation?","options":["severe water deficit with high sodium despite deceptively preserved skin turgor","rapid metabolic derangement after spontaneous or treatment-related tumour breakdown","shock or hypoglycaemia with inadequate stress steroid response","adrenal insufficiency with elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone and salt-wasting physiology","acute hepatic synthetic failure with INR elevation not corrected by vitamin K"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"shock or hypoglycaemia with inadequate stress steroid response","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is shock or hypoglycaemia with inadequate stress steroid response. In context, this supports Paediatric adrenal crisis from steroid dependence and should prompt clinicians to give immediate stress-dose hydrocortisone, glucose and isotonic fluid.","learningPoint":"Paediatric adrenal crisis from steroid dependence: shock or hypoglycaemia with inadequate stress steroid response. Avoid delaying corticosteroid because infection is also suspected.","id":"paediatrics-198","globalNumber":1928,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":199,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in paediatric adrenal crisis from steroid dependence?","options":["repeated plasma transfusion solely to normalise INR and mask deterioration","rapid hypotonic correction before stabilising perfusion","treating as gastroenteritis without checking electrolytes or giving steroids","delaying corticosteroid because infection is also suspected","giving an insulin bolus or overly rapid fluid and osmolar correction"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"delaying corticosteroid because infection is also suspected","explanation":"The hazardous action is delaying corticosteroid because infection is also suspected. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is refractory shock, seizure and death.","learningPoint":"Paediatric adrenal crisis from steroid dependence: shock or hypoglycaemia with inadequate stress steroid response. Avoid delaying corticosteroid because infection is also suspected.","id":"paediatrics-199","globalNumber":1929,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Paediatrics","categoryNumber":200,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in paediatric adrenal crisis from steroid dependence, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["cerebral oedema, bleeding and multiorgan failure","cerebral injury, hypokalaemia and hypoglycaemia","cerebral oedema, seizures and venous thrombosis","arrhythmia, seizure and renal failure","refractory shock, seizure and death"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"refractory shock, seizure and death","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is refractory shock, seizure and death. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of paediatric adrenal crisis from steroid dependence.","learningPoint":"Paediatric adrenal crisis from steroid dependence: shock or hypoglycaemia with inadequate stress steroid response. Avoid delaying corticosteroid because infection is also suspected.","id":"paediatrics-200","globalNumber":1930,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":51,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with coma, miosis and hypoventilation after suspected long-acting opioid exposure. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Opioid poisoning with recurrent respiratory depression","Anticholinergic poisoning","Sodium valproate poisoning","Carbon monoxide poisoning","Tricyclic antidepressant cardiotoxicity"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Opioid poisoning with recurrent respiratory depression","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Opioid poisoning with recurrent respiratory depression. The key discriminator is rapid improvement in ventilation after naloxone, with recurrence as naloxone wears off. Immediate priorities include: support ventilation and titrate naloxone to adequate breathing, using an infusion when repeated doses are required.","learningPoint":"Opioid poisoning with recurrent respiratory depression: rapid improvement in ventilation after naloxone, with recurrence as naloxone wears off. Avoid seeking full arousal with large naloxone boluses and precipitating severe withdrawal.","id":"toxicology-051","globalNumber":1931,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":52,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has opioid poisoning with recurrent respiratory depression: coma, miosis and hypoventilation after suspected long-acting opioid exposure. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["provide benzodiazepines, cooling and supportive care, considering physostigmine only with expert advice and no contraindication","support ventilation and titrate naloxone to adequate breathing, using an infusion when repeated doses are required","give repeated intravenous sodium bicarbonate and supportive vasopressors","decontaminate safely, give escalating atropine to dry secretions and use oxime therapy when appropriate","give benzodiazepines, correct electrolytes and arrange early haemodialysis for severe toxicity"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"support ventilation and titrate naloxone to adequate breathing, using an infusion when repeated doses are required","explanation":"The priority is to support ventilation and titrate naloxone to adequate breathing, using an infusion when repeated doses are required. The decisive feature is rapid improvement in ventilation after naloxone, with recurrence as naloxone wears off. A major error is seeking full arousal with large naloxone boluses and precipitating severe withdrawal.","learningPoint":"Opioid poisoning with recurrent respiratory depression: rapid improvement in ventilation after naloxone, with recurrence as naloxone wears off. Avoid seeking full arousal with large naloxone boluses and precipitating severe withdrawal.","id":"toxicology-052","globalNumber":1932,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":53,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected opioid poisoning with recurrent respiratory depression, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["terminal R wave in aVR or QRS prolongation with hypotension","a cholinergic toxidrome with excessive secretions and neuromuscular weakness","rapid improvement in ventilation after naloxone, with recurrence as naloxone wears off","dry skin and reduced bowel sounds distinguishing it from sympathomimetic toxicity","severe adrenergic toxicity with a raised theophylline concentration and recurrent seizures"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"rapid improvement in ventilation after naloxone, with recurrence as naloxone wears off","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is rapid improvement in ventilation after naloxone, with recurrence as naloxone wears off. In context, this supports Opioid poisoning with recurrent respiratory depression and should prompt clinicians to support ventilation and titrate naloxone to adequate breathing, using an infusion when repeated doses are required.","learningPoint":"Opioid poisoning with recurrent respiratory depression: rapid improvement in ventilation after naloxone, with recurrence as naloxone wears off. Avoid seeking full arousal with large naloxone boluses and precipitating severe withdrawal.","id":"toxicology-053","globalNumber":1933,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":54,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of opioid poisoning with recurrent respiratory depression, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["giving physostigmine or class Ia and Ic antiarrhythmics","giving physostigmine in suspected tricyclic or conduction-toxic poisoning","relying on atropine alone in severe poisoning","seeking full arousal with large naloxone boluses and precipitating severe withdrawal","titrating atropine to pupil size rather than ventilation and secretion control"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"seeking full arousal with large naloxone boluses and precipitating severe withdrawal","explanation":"The hazardous action is seeking full arousal with large naloxone boluses and precipitating severe withdrawal. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is recurrent apnoea, aspiration and non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema.","learningPoint":"Opioid poisoning with recurrent respiratory depression: rapid improvement in ventilation after naloxone, with recurrence as naloxone wears off. Avoid seeking full arousal with large naloxone boluses and precipitating severe withdrawal.","id":"toxicology-054","globalNumber":1934,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":55,"type":"complica