 poisoning and should prompt clinicians to give high-concentration oxygen and seek hyperbaric advice for severe neurological, cardiac or pregnancy-related toxicity.","learningPoint":"Carbon monoxide poisoning: elevated carboxyhaemoglobin or compatible exposure with neurological or cardiac toxicity. Avoid reassuring from a normal pulse oximeter or low delayed carboxyhaemoglobin level.","id":"toxicology-073","globalNumber":1953,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":74,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of carbon monoxide poisoning, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["giving repeated bicarbonate while delaying dialysis in refractory severe toxicity","giving physostigmine or class Ia and Ic antiarrhythmics","relying on atropine alone in severe poisoning","reassuring from a normal pulse oximeter or low delayed carboxyhaemoglobin level","assuming a normal liver profile excludes serious valproate toxicity"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"reassuring from a normal pulse oximeter or low delayed carboxyhaemoglobin level","explanation":"The hazardous action is reassuring from a normal pulse oximeter or low delayed carboxyhaemoglobin level. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is delayed neurocognitive injury, myocardial infarction and fetal harm.","learningPoint":"Carbon monoxide poisoning: elevated carboxyhaemoglobin or compatible exposure with neurological or cardiac toxicity. Avoid reassuring from a normal pulse oximeter or low delayed carboxyhaemoglobin level.","id":"toxicology-074","globalNumber":1954,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":75,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with carbon monoxide poisoning initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["permanent blindness, basal ganglia injury and death","refra