agement","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with metformin-associated lactic acidosis presenting with shock, renal impairment and profound high-anion-gap lactic acidosis in a patient taking metformin, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["alkalinise serum and urine with bicarbonate and arrange urgent haemodialysis when severe","resuscitate, treat shock and arrange early extracorporeal removal for severe acidosis or organ failure","give fomepizole, folate and arrange haemodialysis when severe","start acetylcysteine according to the nomogram, timing and evidence of liver injury","give fomepizole, cofactors and arrange haemodialysis when severe"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"resuscitate, treat shock and arrange early extracorporeal removal for severe acidosis or organ failure","explanation":"The priority is to resuscitate, treat shock and arrange early extracorporeal removal for severe acidosis or organ failure. The decisive feature is severe lactataemia with compatible exposure and no sufficient alternative explanation. A major error is giving repeated bicarbonate while delaying dialysis in refractory severe toxicity.","learningPoint":"Metformin-associated lactic acidosis: severe lactataemia with compatible exposure and no sufficient alternative explanation. Avoid giving repeated bicarbonate while delaying dialysis in refractory severe toxicity.","id":"toxicology-077","globalNumber":1957,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":78,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for metformin-associated lactic acidosis?","options":["a four-hour or later concentration above the treatment line","visual toxicity with formate-mediated acidosis","severe lactataemia with compatible exposure and no sufficient alternative explanation","respiratory alkalosis plus high-anion-gap acidosis and clinical toxicity","very high lactate with soot exposure and rapid collapse"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"severe lactataemia with compatible exposure and no sufficient alternative explanation","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is severe lactataemia with compatible exposure and no sufficient alternative explanation. In context, this supports Metformin-associated lactic acidosis and should prompt clinicians to resuscitate, treat shock and arrange early extracorporeal removal for severe acidosis or organ failure.","learningPoint":"Metformin-associated lactic acidosis: severe lactataemia with compatible exposure and no sufficient alternative explanation. Avoid giving repeated bicarbonate while delaying dialysis in refractory severe toxicity.","id":"toxicology-078","globalNumber":1958,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":79,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in metformin-associated lactic acidosis?","options":["intubating without maintaining the patient's very high minute ventilation","waiting for retinal findings or a methanol level before blocking metabolism","delaying antidote for a cyanide concentration","giving repeated bicarbonate while delaying dialysis in refractory severe toxicity","waiting for transaminase elevation before starting antidote"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"giving repeated bicarbonate while delaying dialysis in refractory severe toxicity","explanation":"The hazardous action is giving repeated bicarbonate while delaying dialysis in refractory severe toxicity. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is refractory shock, arrhythmia and multiorgan failure.","learningPoint":"Metformin-associated lactic acidosis: severe lactataemia with compatible exposure and no sufficient alternative explanation. Avoid giving repeated bicarbonate while delaying dialysis in refractory severe toxicity.","id":"toxicology-079","globalNumber":1959,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":80,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of metformin-associated lactic acidosis, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["recurrent arrest and severe neurologic injury","abrupt acidaemia, pulmonary oedema and cerebral toxicity","permanent blindness, basal ganglia injury and death","renal failure, tetany and cerebral oedema","refractory shock, arrhythmia and multiorgan failure"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"refractory shock, arrhythmia and multiorgan failure","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is refractory shock, arrhythmia and multiorgan failure. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of metformin-associated lactic acidosis.","learningPoint":"Metformin-associated lactic acidosis: severe lactataemia with compatible exposure and no sufficient alternative explanation. Avoid giving repeated bicarbonate while delaying dialysis in refractory severe toxicity.","id":"toxicology-080","globalNumber":1960,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":81,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by coma, respiratory depression, hyperammonaemia and metabolic derangement after valproate overdose. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Sodium valproate poisoning","Methanol poisoning","Beta-blocker poisoning","Paracetamol poisoning","Opioid poisoning with recurrent respiratory depression"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Sodium valproate poisoning","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Sodium valproate poisoning. The key discriminator is elevated valproate with hyperammonaemic encephalopathy and possible cerebral oedema. Immediate priorities include: support airway and circulation, give L-carnitine when indicated and use haemodialysis for severe poisoning.","learningPoint":"Sodium valproate poisoning: elevated valproate with hyperammonaemic encephalopathy and possible cerebral oedema. Avoid assuming a normal liver profile excludes serious valproate toxicity.","id":"toxicology-081","globalNumber":1961,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":82,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is sodium valproate poisoning. The patient has coma, respiratory depression, hyperammonaemia and metabolic derangement after valproate overdose. What is the best immediate management?","options":["give fomepizole, folate and arrange haemodialysis when severe","support airway and circulation, give L-carnitine when indicated and use haemodialysis for severe poisoning","support ventilation and titrate naloxone to adequate breathing, using an infusion when repeated doses are required","provide early intensive supportive care and discuss specialised antidotal options or extracorporeal support","give glucagon, high-dose insulin euglycaemic therapy and vasopressors as required"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"support airway and circulation, give L-carnitine when indicated and use haemodialysis for severe poisoning","explanation":"The priority is to support airway and circulation, give L-carnitine when indicated and use haemodialysis for severe poisoning. The decisive feature is elevated valproate with hyperammonaemic encephalopathy and possible cerebral oedema. A major error is assuming a normal liver profile excludes serious valproate toxicity.","learningPoint":"Sodium valproate poisoning: elevated valproate with hyperammonaemic encephalopathy and possible cerebral oedema. Avoid assuming a normal liver profile excludes serious valproate toxicity.","id":"toxicology-082","globalNumber":1962,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":83,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have sodium valproate poisoning. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["elevated carboxyhaemoglobin or compatible exposure with neurological or cardiac toxicity","dry skin and reduced bowel sounds distinguishing it from sympathomimetic toxicity","elevated valproate with hyperammonaemic encephalopathy and possible cerebral oedema","severe lactataemia with compatible exposure and no sufficient alternative explanation","a biphasic toxidrome after colchicine exposure with delayed cytopenias"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"elevated valproate with hyperammonaemic encephalopathy and possible cerebral oedema","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is elevated valproate with hyperammonaemic encephalopathy and possible cerebral oedema. In context, this supports Sodium valproate poisoning and should prompt clinicians to support airway and circulation, give L-carnitine when indicated and use haemodialysis for severe poisoning.","learningPoint":"Sodium valproate poisoning: elevated valproate with hyperammonaemic encephalopathy and possible cerebral oedema. Avoid assuming a normal liver profile excludes serious valproate toxicity.","id":"toxicology-083","globalNumber":1963,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":84,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with sodium valproate poisoning is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["reassuring after transient improvement in gastrointestinal symptoms","waiting for transaminase elevation before starting antidote","waiting for retinal findings or a methanol level before blocking metabolism","assuming a normal liver profile excludes serious valproate toxicity","reassuring from a normal pulse oximeter or low delayed carboxyhaemoglobin level"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"assuming a normal liver profile excludes serious valproate toxicity","explanation":"The hazardous action is assuming a normal liver profile excludes serious valproate toxicity. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is cerebral oedema, respiratory failure and shock.","learningPoint":"Sodium valproate poisoning: elevated valproate with hyperammonaemic encephalopathy and possible cerebral oedema. Avoid assuming a normal liver profile excludes serious valproate toxicity.","id":"toxicology-084","globalNumber":1964,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":85,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with sodium valproate poisoning?","options":["hyperthermia, rhabdomyolysis and dysrhythmia","renal failure, tetany and cerebral oedema","refractory shock, arrhythmia and multiorgan failure","recurrent apnoea, aspiration and non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema","cerebral oedema, respiratory failure and shock"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"cerebral oedema, respiratory failure and shock","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is cerebral oedema, respiratory failure and shock. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of sodium valproate poisoning.","learningPoint":"Sodium valproate poisoning: elevated valproate with hyperammonaemic encephalopathy and possible cerebral oedema. Avoid assuming a normal liver profile excludes serious valproate toxicity.","id":"toxicology-085","globalNumber":1965,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":86,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has refractory vomiting, tremor, tachyarrhythmia, hypokalaemia and seizures after theophylline exposure. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Theophylline poisoning","Colchicine poisoning","Severe salicylate poisoning","Digoxin poisoning","Tricyclic antidepressant cardiotoxicity"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Theophylline poisoning","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Theophylline poisoning. The key discriminator is severe adrenergic toxicity with a raised theophylline concentration and recurrent seizures. Immediate priorities include: give benzodiazepines, correct electrolytes and arrange early haemodialysis for severe toxicity.","learningPoint":"Theophylline poisoning: severe adrenergic toxicity with a raised theophylline concentration and recurrent seizures. Avoid using beta agonists or delaying extracorporeal removal while levels rise.","id":"toxicology-086","globalNumber":1966,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":87,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for theophylline poisoning when the presentation is refractory vomiting, tremor, tachyarrhythmia, hypokalaemia and seizures after theophylline exposure?","options":["provide benzodiazepines, cooling and supportive care, considering physostigmine only with expert advice and no contraindication","give benzodiazepines, correct electrolytes and arrange early haemodialysis for severe toxicity","give repeated intravenous sodium bicarbonate and supportive vasopressors","provide early intensive supportive care and discuss specialised antidotal options or extracorporeal support","decontaminate safely, give escalating atropine to dry secretions and use oxime therapy when appropriate"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give benzodiazepines, correct electrolytes and arrange early haemodialysis for severe toxicity","explanation":"The priority is to give benzodiazepines, correct electrolytes and arrange early haemodialysis for severe toxicity. The decisive feature is severe adrenergic toxicity with a raised theophylline concentration and recurrent seizures. A major error is using beta agonists or delaying extracorporeal removal while levels rise.","learningPoint":"Theophylline poisoning: severe adrenergic toxicity with a raised theophylline concentration and recurrent seizures. Avoid using beta agonists or delaying extracorporeal removal while levels rise.","id":"toxicology-087","globalNumber":1967,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":88,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for theophylline poisoning in this presentation?","options":["respiratory alkalosis plus high-anion-gap acidosis and clinical toxicity","terminal R wave in aVR or QRS prolongation with hypotension","severe adrenergic toxicity with a raised theophylline concentration and recurrent seizures","a biphasic toxidrome after colchicine exposure with delayed cytopenias","dry skin and reduced bowel sounds distinguishing it from sympathomimetic toxicity"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"severe adrenergic toxicity with a raised theophylline concentration and recurrent seizures","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is severe adrenergic toxicity with a raised theophylline concentration and recurrent seizures. In context, this supports Theophylline poisoning and should prompt clinicians to give benzodiazepines, correct electrolytes and arrange early haemodialysis for severe toxicity.","learningPoint":"Theophylline poisoning: severe adrenergic toxicity with a raised theophylline concentration and recurrent seizures. Avoid using beta agonists or delaying extracorporeal removal while levels rise.","id":"toxicology-088","globalNumber":1968,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":89,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in theophylline poisoning?","options":["intubating without maintaining the patient's very high minute ventilation","giving physostigmine or class Ia and Ic antiarrhythmics","using isolated beta blockade in cocaine-associated vasoconstriction and hypertension","using beta agonists or delaying extracorporeal removal while levels rise","giving physostigmine in suspected tricyclic or conduction-toxic poisoning"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"using beta agonists or delaying extracorporeal removal while levels rise","explanation":"The hazardous action is using beta agonists or delaying extracorporeal removal while levels rise. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is status epilepticus, ventricular arrhythmia and death.","learningPoint":"Theophylline poisoning: severe adrenergic toxicity with a raised theophylline concentration and recurrent seizures. Avoid using beta agonists or delaying extracorporeal removal while levels rise.","id":"toxicology-089","globalNumber":1969,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":90,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in theophylline poisoning, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["hyperthermia, rhabdomyolysis and dysrhythmia","rhabdomyolysis, intracranial haemorrhage and ventricular arrhythmia","ventricular arrhythmia and high-grade atrioventricular block","ventricular arrhythmia and refractory shock","status epilepticus, ventricular arrhythmia and death"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"status epilepticus, ventricular arrhythmia and death","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is status epilepticus, ventricular arrhythmia and death. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of theophylline poisoning.","learningPoint":"Theophylline poisoning: severe adrenergic toxicity with a raised theophylline concentration and recurrent seizures. Avoid using beta agonists or delaying extracorporeal removal while levels rise.","id":"toxicology-090","globalNumber":1970,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Toxicology","categoryNumber":91,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with early severe gastrointestinal symptoms followed by shock, marrow failure and multiorgan dysfunction. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Colchicine poisoning","Ethylene glycol poisoning","Theophylline poisoning","Digoxin poisoning","Severe salicylate poisoning"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Colchicine poisoning","explanati