int":"High-voltage electrical injury: a current pathway or exposure sufficient to cause deep tissue and cardiac injury. Avoid estimating injury severity from skin burns alone.","id":"environmental-073","globalNumber":2003,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":74,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of high-voltage electrical injury, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["transporting before effective cooling when safe on-site immersion is available","routine prophylactic antibiotics or diuretics without indication","assuming fixed dilated pupils indicate irreversible death","estimating injury severity from skin burns alone","delaying hyperbaric treatment because CT is normal"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"estimating injury severity from skin burns alone","explanation":"The hazardous action is estimating injury severity from skin burns alone. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is arrhythmia, compartment syndrome and renal failure.","learningPoint":"High-voltage electrical injury: a current pathway or exposure sufficient to cause deep tissue and cardiac injury. Avoid estimating injury severity from skin burns alone.","id":"environmental-074","globalNumber":2004,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":75,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with high-voltage electrical injury initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["arrhythmia, delayed neurologic injury and rhabdomyolysis","ventricular fibrillation, coagulopathy and afterdrop","respiratory arrest and aspiration","fulminant hepatic failure, DIC and rhabdomyolysis","arrhythmia, compartment syndrome and renal failure"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"arrhythmia, compartment syndrome and renal failure","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is arrhythmia, compartment syndrome and renal failure. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of high-voltage electrical injury.","learningPoint":"High-voltage electrical injury: a current pathway or exposure sufficient to cause deep tissue and cardiac injury. Avoid estimating injury severity from skin burns alone.","id":"environmental-075","globalNumber":2005,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":76,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with rapid perioral paraesthesia, flaccid paralysis and respiratory failure after a small marine bite with preserved consciousness?","options":["Blue-ringed octopus envenomation","Australian elapid snake envenomation","Stonefish envenomation","Funnel-web spider envenomation","Box jellyfish envenomation"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Blue-ringed octopus envenomation","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Blue-ringed octopus envenomation. The key discriminator is descending paralysis with minimal local injury after blue-ringed octopus exposure. Immediate priorities include: provide immediate ventilation and supportive care until toxin effect resolves.","learningPoint":"Blue-ringed octopus envenomation: descending paralysis with minimal local injury after blue-ringed octopus exposure. Avoid assuming an awake paralysed patient is not critically ill.","id":"environmental-076","globalNumber":2006,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":77,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with blue-ringed octopus envenomation presenting with rapid perioral paraesthesia, flaccid paralysis and respiratory failure after a small marine bite with preserved consciousness, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["remove from danger, start CPR if required, apply vinegar and give antivenom for severe systemic toxicity","provide immediate ventilation and supportive care until toxin effect resolves","apply pressure immobilisation and give funnel-web antivenom with airway support","immerse the limb in non-scalding hot water, give analgesia and antivenom for severe systemic toxicity","apply pressure immobilisation, avoid movement and give antivenom when systemic envenomation is established"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"provide immediate ventilation and supportive care until toxin effect resolves","explanation":"The priority is to provide immediate ventilation and supportive care until toxin effect resolves. The decisive feature is descending paralysis with minimal local injury after blue-ringed octopus exposure. A major error is assuming an awake paralysed patient is not critically ill.","learningPoint":"Blue-ringed octopus envenomation: descending paralysis with minimal local injury after blue-ringed octopus exposure. Avoid assuming an awake paralysed patient is not critically ill.","id":"environmental-077","globalNumber":2007,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":78,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for blue-ringed octopus envenomation?","options":["venom-induced consumption coagulopathy or progressive paralysis","puncture wounds with severe disproportionate pain after marine exposure","descending paralysis with minimal local injury after blue-ringed octopus exposure","rapid cholinergic and catecholaminergic syndrome after a large black spider bite","immediate collapse with characteristic tentacle marks"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"descending paralysis with minimal local injury after blue-ringed octopus exposure","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is descending paralysis with minimal local injury after blue-ringed octopus exposure. In context, this supports Blue-ringed octopus envenomation and should prompt clinicians to provide immediate ventilation and supportive care until toxin effect resolves.","learningPoint":"Blue-ringed octopus envenomation: descending paralysis with minimal local injury after blue-ringed octopus exposure. Avoid assuming an awake paralysed patient is not critically ill.","id":"environmental-078","globalNumber":2008,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":79,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in blue-ringed octopus envenomation?","options":["applying ice or delaying analgesia and antivenom during systemic deterioration","washing the bite site, using a tourniquet or cutting the wound","rubbing the skin or applying fresh water before tentacle inactivation","assuming an awake paralysed patient is not critically ill","removing the pressure bandage before resuscitation and antivenom are ready"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"assuming an awake paralysed patient is not critically ill","explanation":"The hazardous action is assuming an awake paralysed patient is not critically ill. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is hypoxic cardiac arrest from respiratory paralysis.","learningPoint":"Blue-ringed octopus envenomation: descending paralysis with minimal local injury after blue-ringed octopus exposure. Avoid assuming an awake paralysed patient is not critically ill.","id":"environmental-079","globalNumber":2009,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":80,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of blue-ringed octopus envenomation, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["tissue necrosis, shock and pulmonary oedema","cardiac arrest and severe dermal necrosis","pulmonary oedema, paralysis and cardiac arrest","intracranial haemorrhage, paralysis and renal failure","hypoxic cardiac arrest from respiratory paralysis"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"hypoxic cardiac arrest from respiratory paralysis","explanation":"The complicatio