ia and antivenom for severe systemic toxicity","provide immediate ventilation and supportive care until toxin effect resolves","remove from danger, start CPR if required, apply vinegar and give antivenom for severe systemic toxicity","apply pressure immobilisation and give funnel-web antivenom with airway support"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"immerse the limb in non-scalding hot water, give analgesia and antivenom for severe systemic toxicity","explanation":"The priority is to immerse the limb in non-scalding hot water, give analgesia and antivenom for severe systemic toxicity. The decisive feature is puncture wounds with severe disproportionate pain after marine exposure. A major error is applying ice or delaying analgesia and antivenom during systemic deterioration.","learningPoint":"Stonefish envenomation: puncture wounds with severe disproportionate pain after marine exposure. Avoid applying ice or delaying analgesia and antivenom during systemic deterioration.","id":"environmental-082","globalNumber":2012,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":83,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have stonefish envenomation. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["venom-induced consumption coagulopathy or progressive paralysis","rapid cholinergic and catecholaminergic syndrome after a large black spider bite","puncture wounds with severe disproportionate pain after marine exposure","descending paralysis with minimal local injury after blue-ringed octopus exposure","immediate collapse with characteristic tentacle marks"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"puncture wounds with severe disproportionate pain after marine exposure","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is puncture wounds with severe disproportionate pain after marine exposure. In context, this supports Stonefish envenomation and should prompt clinicians to immerse the limb in non-scalding hot water, give analgesia and antivenom for severe systemic toxicity.","learningPoint":"Stonefish envenomation: puncture wounds with severe disproportionate pain after marine exposure. Avoid applying ice or delaying analgesia and antivenom during systemic deterioration.","id":"environmental-083","globalNumber":2013,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":84,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with stonefish envenomation is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["washing the bite site, using a tourniquet or cutting the wound","assuming an awake paralysed patient is not critically ill","rubbing the skin or applying fresh water before tentacle inactivation","applying ice or delaying analgesia and antivenom during systemic deterioration","removing the pressure bandage before resuscitation and antivenom are ready"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"applying ice or delaying analgesia and antivenom during systemic deterioration","explanation":"The hazardous action is applying ice or delaying analgesia and antivenom during systemic deterioration. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is tissue necrosis, shock and pulmonary oedema.","learningPoint":"Stonefish envenomation: puncture wounds with severe disproportionate pain after marine exposure. Avoid applying ice or delaying analgesia and antivenom during systemic deterioration.","id":"environmental-084","globalNumber":2014,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":85,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with stonefish envenomation?","options":["pulmonary oedema, paralysis and cardiac arrest","hypoxic cardiac arrest from respiratory paralysis","intracranial haemorrhage, paralysis and renal failure","cardiac arrest and severe dermal necrosis","tissue necrosis, shock and pulmonary oedema"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"tissue necrosis, shock and pulmonary oedema","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is tissue necrosis, shock and pulmonary oedema. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of stonefish envenomation.","learningPoint":"Stonefish envenomation: puncture wounds with severe disproportionate pain after marine exposure. Avoid applying ice or delaying analgesia and antivenom during systemic deterioration.","id":"environmental-085","globalNumber":2015,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":86,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has delayed severe generalized pain, diaphoresis, agitation and hypertension after a minor jellyfish sting. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Irukandji syndrome","Australian elapid snake envenomation","Box jellyfish envenomation","Blue-ringed octopus envenomation","Funnel-web spider envenomation"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Irukandji syndrome","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Irukandji syndrome. The key discriminator is catecholamine-excess syndrome developing after a relatively inconspicuous sting. Immediate priorities include: provide potent analgesia, control severe hypertension and monitor for cardiac injury.","learningPoint":"Irukandji syndrome: catecholamine-excess syndrome developing after a relatively inconspicuous sting. Avoid using pressure immobilisation or discharging before the delayed syndrome declares itself.","id":"environmental-086","globalNumber":2016,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":87,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for irukandji syndrome when the presentation is delayed severe generalized pain, diaphoresis, agitation and hypertension after a minor jellyfish sting?","options":["apply pressure immobilisation and give funnel-web antivenom with airway support","provide potent analgesia, control severe hypertension and monitor for cardiac injury","apply pressure immobilisation, avoid movement and give antivenom when systemic envenomation is established","provide immediate ventilation and supportive care until toxin effect resolves","remove from danger, start CPR if required, apply vinegar and give antivenom for severe systemic toxicity"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"provide potent analgesia, control severe hypertension and monitor for cardiac injury","explanation":"The priority is to provide potent analgesia, control severe hypertension and monitor for cardiac injury. The decisive feature is catecholamine-excess syndrome developing after a relatively inconspicuous sting. A major error is using pressure immobilisation or discharging before the delayed syndrome declares itself.","learningPoint":"Irukandji syndrome: catecholamine-excess syndrome developing after a relatively inconspicuous sting. Avoid using pressure immobilisation or discharging before the delayed syndrome declares itself.","id":"environmental-087","globalNumber":2017,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":88,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for irukandji syndrome in this presentation?","options":["puncture wounds with severe disproportionate pain after marine exposure","rapid cholinergic and catecholaminergic syndrome after a large black spider bite","catecholamine-excess syndrome developing after a relatively inconspicuous sting","venom-induced consumption coagulopathy or progressive paralysis","immediate collapse with characteristic tentacle marks"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"catecholamine-excess syndrome developing after a relatively inconspicuous sting","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is catecholamine-excess syndrome developing after a relatively inconspicuous sting. In context, this supports Irukandji syndrome and should prompt clinicians to provide potent analgesia, control severe hypertension and monitor for cardiac injury.","learningPoint":"Irukandji syndrome: catecholamine-excess syndrome developing after a relatively inconspicuous sting. Avoid using pressure immobilisation or discharging before the delayed syndrome declares itself.","id":"environmental-088","globalNumber":2018,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":89,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in irukandji syndrome?","options":["rubbing the skin or applying fresh water before tentacle inactivation","assuming an awake paralysed patient is not critically ill","applying ice or delaying analgesia and antivenom during systemic deterioration","using pressure immobilisation or discharging before the delayed syndrome declares itself","washing the bite site, using a tourniquet or cutting the wound"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"using pressure immobilisation or discharging before the delayed syndrome declares itself","explanation":"The hazardous action is using pressure immobilisation or discharging before the delayed syndrome declares itself. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is cardiomyopathy, pulmonary oedema and intracranial haemorrhage.","learningPoint":"Irukandji syndrome: catecholamine-excess syndrome developing after a relatively inconspicuous sting. Avoid using pressure immobilisation or discharging before the delayed syndrome declares itself.","id":"environmental-089","globalNumber":2019,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":90,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in irukandji syndrome, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["cardiac arrest and severe dermal necrosis","intracranial haemorrhage, paralysis and renal failure","hypoxic cardiac arrest from respiratory paralysis","pulmonary oedema, paralysis and cardiac arrest","cardiomyopathy, pulmonary oedema and intracranial haemorrhage"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"cardiomyopathy, pulmonary oedema and intracranial haemorrhage","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is cardiomyopathy, pulmonary oedema and intracranial haemorrhage. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of irukandji syndrome.","learningPoint":"Irukandji syndrome: catecholamine-excess syndrome developing after a relatively inconspicuous sting. Avoid using pressure immobilisation or discharging before the delayed syndrome declares itself.","id":"environmental-090","globalNumber":2020,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":91,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with progressive ascending weakness and bulbar or respiratory dysfunction after exposure in an endemic area. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Tick paralysis","Box jellyfish envenomation","Australian elapid snake envenomation","Exertional heat stroke","Classic heat stroke"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Tick paralysis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Tick paralysis. The key discriminator is a feeding tick with flaccid paralysis and preserved sensation. Immediate priorities include: remove the tick carefully, provide respiratory support and use antivenom when indicated.","learningPoint":"Tick paralysis: a feeding tick with flaccid paralysis and preserved sensation. Avoid assuming deterioration will stop immediately after tick removal.","id":"environmental-091","globalNumber":2021,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":92,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has tick paralysis: progressive ascending weakness and bulbar or respiratory dysfunction after exposure in an endemic area. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["perform trauma assessment, ECG monitoring and aggressive management of rhabdomyolysis and compartment risk","remove the tick carefully, provide respiratory support and use antivenom when indicated","perform immediate cold-water immersion when feasible and continue until core temperature approaches 39 degrees","apply pressure immobilisation, avoid movement and give antivenom when systemic envenomation is established","handle gently, provide active internal rewarming and consider extracorporeal rewarming in instability"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"remove the tick carefully, provide respiratory support and use antivenom when indicated","explanation":"The priority is to remove the tick carefully, provide respiratory support and use antivenom when indicated. The decisive feature is a feeding tick with flaccid paralysis and preserved sensation. A major error is assuming deterioration will stop immediately after tick removal.","learningPoint":"Tick paralysis: a feeding tick with flaccid paralysis and preserved sensation. Avoid assuming deterioration will stop immediately after tick removal.","id":"environmental-092","globalNumber":2022,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":93,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected tick paralysis, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["venom-induced consumption coagulopathy or progressive paralysis","hyperthermia plus encephalopathy without another better cause","a feeding tick with flaccid paralysis and preserved sensation","immediate collapse with characteristic tentacle marks","puncture wounds with severe disproportionate pain after marine exposure"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a feeding tick with flaccid paralysis and preserved sensation","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a feeding tick with flaccid paralysis and preserved sensation. In context, this supports Tick paralysis and should prompt clinicians to remove the tick carefully, provide respiratory support and use antivenom when indicated.","learningPoint":"Tick paralysis: a feeding tick with flaccid paralysis and preserved sensation. Avoid assuming deterioration will stop immediately after tick removal.","id":"environmental-093","globalNumber":2023,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":94,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of tick paralysis, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["rubbing the skin or applying fresh water before tentacle inactivation","transporting before effective cooling when safe on-site immersion is available","washing the bite site, using a tourniquet or cutting the wound","assuming deterioration will stop immediately after tick removal","delaying cooling for imaging or relying on antipyretics"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"assuming deterioration will stop immediately after tick removal","explanation":"The hazardous action is assuming deterioration will stop immediately after tick removal. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is respiratory arrest and aspiration.","learningPoint":"Tick paralysis: a feeding tick with flaccid paralysis and preserved sensation. Avoid assuming deterioration will stop immediately after tick removal.","id":"environmental-094","globalNumber":2024,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":95,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with tick paralysis initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["arrhythmia, delayed neurologic injury and rhabdomyolysis","arrhythmia, compartment syndrome and renal failure","intracranial haemorrhage, paralysis and renal failure","ventricular fibrillation, coagulopathy and afterdrop","respiratory arrest and aspiration"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"respiratory arrest and aspiration","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is respiratory arrest and aspiration. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of tick paralysis.","learningPoint":"Tick paralysis: a feeding tick with flaccid paralysis and preserved sensation. Avoid assuming deterioration will stop immediately after tick removal.","id":"environmental-095","globalNumber":2025,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":96,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with a buried patient is extricated cold, hypoxic and pulseless after prolonged snow burial?","options":["Avalanche burial with severe hypothermia","Decompression sickness","Classic heat stroke","Drowning-associated respiratory failure","Severe accidental hypothermia"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Avalanche burial with severe hypothermia","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Avalanche burial with severe hypothermia. The key discriminator is core hypothermia with airway obstruction and burial-duration factors guiding resuscitation. Immediate priorities include: handle gently, provide prolonged resuscitation and arrange extracorporeal rewarming when appropriate.","learningPoint":"Avalanche burial with severe hypothermia: core hypothermia with airway obstruction and burial-duration factors guiding resuscitation. Avoid terminating resuscitation solely because of prolonged arrest before considering temperature and burial conditions.","id":"environmental-096","globalNumber":2026,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":97,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with avalanche burial with severe hypothermia presenting with a buried patient is extricated cold, hypoxic and pulseless after prolonged snow burial, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["handle gently, provide active internal rewarming and consider extracorporeal rewarming in instability","handle gently, provide prolonged resuscitation and arrange extracorporeal rewarming when appropriate","prioritise ventilation and oxygenation, treat hypothermia and observe based on symptoms","descend, give oxygen and reduce exertion, using nifedipine or pulmonary vasodilator rescue when descent is impossible","descend immediately, give oxygen and dexamethasone, and use a portable hyperbaric chamber if descent is delayed"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"handle gently, provide prolonged resuscitation and arrange extracorporeal rewarming when appropriate","explanation":"The priority is to handle gently, provide prolonged resuscitation and arrange extracorporeal rewarming when appropriate. The decisive feature is core hypothermia with airway obstruction and burial-duration factors guiding resuscitation. A major error is terminating resuscitation solely because of prolonged arrest before considering temperature and burial conditions.","learningPoint":"Avalanche burial with severe hypothermia: core hypothermia with airway obstruction and burial-duration factors guiding resuscitation. Avoid terminating resuscitation solely because of prolonged arrest before considering temperature and burial conditions.","id":"environmental-097","globalNumber":2027,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":98,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for avalanche burial with severe hypothermia?","options":["respiratory impairment temporally related to submersion","hyperthermia plus encephalopathy without another better cause","core hypothermia with airway obstruction and burial-duration factors guiding resuscitation","non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema at altitude with marked exertional intolerance","cold injury with intact tissue freezing point and delayed neuropathic pain"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"core hypothermia with airway obstruction and burial-duration factors guiding resuscitation","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is core hypothermia with airway obstruction and burial-duration factors guiding resuscitation. In context, this supports Avalanche burial with severe hypothermia and should prompt clinicians to handle gently, provide prolonged resuscitation and arrange extracorporeal rewarming when appropriate.","learningPoint":"Avalanche burial with severe hypothermia: core hypothermia with airway obstruction and burial-duration factors guiding resuscitation. Avoid terminating resuscitation solely because of prolonged arrest before considering temperature and burial conditions.","id":"environmental-098","globalNumber":2028,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":99,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in avalanche burial with severe hypothermia?","options":["routine prophylactic antibiotics or diuretics without indication","recompressing by returning the diver to water","allowing continued ascent or relying on acetazolamide alone in established cerebral oedema","terminating resuscitation solely because of prolonged arrest before considering temperature and burial conditions","declaring death before adequate rewarming in the absence of lethal injury"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"terminating resuscitation solely because of prolonged arrest before considering temperature and burial conditions","explanation":"The hazardous action is terminating resuscitation solely because of prolonged arrest before considering temperature and burial conditions. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is recurrent ventricular fibrillation, rewarming shock and hypoxic brain injury.","learningPoint":"Avalanche burial with severe hypothermia: core hypothermia with airway obstruction and burial-duration factors guiding resuscitation. Avoid terminating resuscitation solely because of prolonged arrest before considering temperature and burial conditions.","id":"environmental-099","globalNumber":2029,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Environmental","categoryNumber":100,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of avalanche burial with severe hypothermia, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["coma, herniation and death","spinal cord injury and persistent vestibular dysfunction","ARDS, cerebral hypoxia and aspiration pneumonia","ventricular fibrillation, coagulopathy and afterdrop","recurrent ventricular fibrillation, rewarming shock and hypoxic brain injury"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"recurrent ventricular fibrillation, rewarming shock and hypoxic brain injury","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is recurrent ventricular fibrillation, rewarming shock and hypoxic brain injury. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of avalanche burial with severe hypothermia.","learningPoint":"Avalanche burial with severe hypothermia: core hypothermia with airway obstruction and burial-duration factors guiding resuscitation. Avoid terminating resuscitation solely because of prolonged arrest before considering temperature and burial conditions.","id":"environmental-100","globalNumber":2030,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":21,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with a recently postoperative cardiac-surgery patient develops ventricular fibrillation or pulseless electrical activity in a monitored unit. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery","Traumatic cardiac arrest","Hypothermic cardiac arrest","Cardiac arrest in severe asthma","Cardiac arrest from massive pulmonary embolism"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery. The key discriminator is a witnessed arrest where tamponade, bleeding or pacing failure may require immediate chest reopening. Immediate priorities include: defibrillate rapidly, correct reversible surgical causes and prepare emergency resternotomy according to protocol.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery: a witnessed arrest where tamponade, bleeding or pacing failure may require immediate chest reopening. Avoid performing prolonged conventional CPR while delaying indicated resternotomy.","id":"als-021","globalNumber":2031,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":22,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery: a recently postoperative cardiac-surgery patient develops ventricular fibrillation or pulseless electrical activity in a monitored unit. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["use standard resuscitation plus toxin-specific antidotes, sodium bicarbonate or lipid therapy as indicated and consider ECPR","defibrillate rapidly, correct reversible surgical causes and prepare emergency resternotomy according to protocol","disconnect the circuit, decompress trapped gas and continue ALS while treating bronchospasm and pneumothorax","continue high-quality CPR and give thrombolysis or pursue embolectomy or extracorporeal support when appropriate","prioritise blood, haemorrhage control, bilateral thoracostomies, pelvic stabilisation and selected resuscitative thoracotomy"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"defibrillate rapidly, correct reversible surgical causes and prepare emergency resternotomy according to protocol","explanation":"The priority is to defibrillate rapidly, correct reversible surgical causes and prepare emergency resternotomy according to protocol. The decisive feature is a witnessed arrest where tamponade, bleeding or pacing failure may require immediate chest reopening. A major error is performing prolonged conventional CPR while delaying indicated resternotomy.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery: a witnessed arrest where tamponade, bleeding or pacing failure may require immediate chest reopening. Avoid performing prolonged conventional CPR while delaying indicated resternotomy.","id":"als-022","globalNumber":2032,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":23,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["a witnessed monitored arrest with immediate defibrillation and potential PCI, tamponade drainage or mechanical support","a high-probability pulmonary embolic arrest without another reversible cause","a witnessed arrest where tamponade, bleeding or pacing failure may require immediate chest reopening","gravid uterine compression of the aorta and vena cava impairs CPR and venous return","reversible mechanical causes dominate and conventional medical arrest algorithms are insufficient"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a witnessed arrest where tamponade, bleeding or pacing failure may require immediate chest reopening","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a witnessed arrest where tamponade, bleeding or pacing failure may require immediate chest reopening. In context, this supports Cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery and should prompt clinicians to defibrillate rapidly, correct reversible surgical causes and prepare emergency resternotomy according to protocol.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery: a witnessed arrest where tamponade, bleeding or pacing failure may require immediate chest reopening. Avoid performing prolonged conventional CPR while delaying indicated resternotomy.","id":"als-023","globalNumber":2033,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":24,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["increasing ventilation rate during arrest to correct carbon dioxide","using a steep lateral tilt that degrades chest-compression quality","prolonged closed-chest CPR while delaying correction of hypovolaemia, tension or tamponade","performing prolonged conventional CPR while delaying indicated resternotomy","stopping resuscitation soon after thrombolysis without allowing prolonged circulation time"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"performing prolonged conventional CPR while delaying indicated resternotomy","explanation":"The hazardous action is performing prolonged conventional CPR while delaying indicated resternotomy. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is graft disruption, cerebral hypoperfusion and irreversible arrest.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery: a witnessed arrest where tamponade, bleeding or pacing failure may require immediate chest reopening. Avoid performing prolonged conventional CPR while delaying indicated resternotomy.","id":"als-024","globalNumber":2034,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":25,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["recurrent arrest and severe right-ventricular failure","refractory ventricular fibrillation and mechanical circulatory collapse","preventable death from unresolved obstructive or haemorrhagic shock","barotrauma, tension pneumothorax and recurrent arrest","graft disruption, cerebral hypoperfusion and irreversible arrest"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"graft disruption, cerebral hypoperfusion and irreversible arrest","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is graft disruption, cerebral hypoperfusion and irreversible arrest. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery: a witnessed arrest where tamponade, bleeding or pacing failure may require immediate chest reopening. Avoid performing prolonged conventional CPR while delaying indicated resternotomy.","id":"als-025","globalNumber":2035,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":26,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with cardiac arrest occurs after sudden dyspnoea and shock with pulseless electrical activity and right-heart strain?","options":["Cardiac arrest from massive pulmonary embolism","Traumatic cardiac arrest","Cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery","Toxicological cardiac arrest","Cardiac arrest in severe asthma"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Cardiac arrest from massive pulmonary embolism","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Cardiac arrest from massive pulmonary embolism. The key discriminator is a high-probability pulmonary embolic arrest without another reversible cause. Immediate priorities include: continue high-quality CPR and give thrombolysis or pursue embolectomy or extracorporeal support when appropriate.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest from massive pulmonary embolism: a high-probability pulmonary embolic arrest without another reversible cause. Avoid stopping resuscitation soon after thrombolysis without allowing prolonged circulation time.","id":"als-026","globalNumber":2036,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":27,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with cardiac arrest from massive pulmonary embolism presenting with cardiac arrest occurs after sudden dyspnoea and shock with pulseless electrical activity and right-heart strain, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["perform high-quality standard ALS with manual left uterine displacement and prepare resuscitative hysterotomy by four minutes if no ROSC","continue high-quality CPR and give thrombolysis or pursue embolectomy or extracorporeal support when appropriate","defibrillate rapidly, correct reversible surgical causes and prepare emergency resternotomy according to protocol","prioritise blood, haemorrhage control, bilateral thoracostomies, pelvic stabilisation and selected resuscitative thoracotomy","disconnect the circuit, decompress trapped gas and continue ALS while treating bronchospasm and pneumothorax"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"continue high-quality CPR and give thrombolysis or pursue embolectomy or extracorporeal support when appropriate","explanation":"The priority is to continue high-quality CPR and give thrombolysis or pursue embolectomy or extracorporeal support when appropriate. The decisive feature is a high-probability pulmonary embolic arrest without another reversible cause. A major error is stopping resuscitation soon after thrombolysis without allowing prolonged circulation time.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest from massive pulmonary embolism: a high-probability pulmonary embolic arrest without another reversible cause. Avoid stopping resuscitation soon after thrombolysis without allowing prolonged circulation time.","id":"als-027","globalNumber":2037,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":28,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for cardiac arrest from massive pulmonary embolism?","options":["a witnessed arrest where tamponade, bleeding or pacing failure may require immediate chest reopening","severe hypothermia can mimic death and permits neurologically intact survival after prolonged arrest","a high-probability pulmonary embolic arrest without another reversible cause","severe poisoning may require prolonged support until redistribution or elimination occurs","reversible mechanical causes dominate and conventional medical arrest algorithms are insufficient"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a high-probability pulmonary embolic arrest without another reversible cause","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a high-probability pulmonary embolic arrest without another reversible cause. In context, this supports Cardiac arrest from massive pulmonary embolism and should prompt clinicians to continue high-quality CPR and give thrombolysis or pursue embolectomy or extracorporeal support when appropriate.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest from massive pulmonary embolism: a high-probability pulmonary embolic arrest without another reversible cause. Avoid stopping resuscitation soon after thrombolysis without allowing prolonged circulation time.","id":"als-028","globalNumber":2038,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":29,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in cardiac arrest from massive pulmonary embolism?","options":["stopping resuscitation early despite a reversible toxic mechanism","using a steep lateral tilt that degrades chest-compression quality","prolonged closed-chest CPR while delaying correction of hypovolaemia, tension or tamponade","stopping resuscitation soon after thrombolysis without allowing prolonged circulation time","increasing ventilation rate during arrest to correct carbon dioxide"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"stopping resuscitation soon after thrombolysis without allowing prolonged circulation time","explanation":"The hazardous action is stopping resuscitation soon after thrombolysis without allowing prolonged circulation time. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is recurrent arrest and severe right-ventricular failure.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest from massive pulmonary embolism: a high-probability pulmonary embolic arrest without another reversible cause. Avoid stopping resuscitation soon after thrombolysis without allowing prolonged circulation time.","id":"als-029","globalNumber":2039,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":30,"type":"complication","difficulty":