 arrest in severe asthma: dynamic hyperinflation causing obstructive shock, especially when expiratory flow does not return to zero. Avoid increasing ventilation rate during arrest to correct carbon dioxide.","id":"als-031","globalNumber":2041,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":32,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is cardiac arrest in severe asthma. The patient has a ventilated patient with life-threatening asthma develops pulseless electrical activity and very high airway pressure. What is the best immediate management?","options":["continue prolonged resuscitation, prevent further heat loss and arrange extracorporeal rewarming when available","disconnect the circuit, decompress trapped gas and continue ALS while treating bronchospasm and pneumothorax","prioritise blood, haemorrhage control, bilateral thoracostomies, pelvic stabilisation and selected resuscitative thoracotomy","perform high-quality standard ALS with manual left uterine displacement and prepare resuscitative hysterotomy by four minutes if no ROSC","continue high-quality CPR and give thrombolysis or pursue embolectomy or extracorporeal support when appropriate"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"disconnect the circuit, decompress trapped gas and continue ALS while treating bronchospasm and pneumothorax","explanation":"The priority is to disconnect the circuit, decompress trapped gas and continue ALS while treating bronchospasm and pneumothorax. The decisive feature is dynamic hyperinflation causing obstructive shock, especially when expiratory flow does not return to zero. A major error is increasing ventilation rate during arrest to correct carbon dioxide.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest in severe asthma: dynamic hyperinflation causing obstructive shock, especially when expiratory flow does not return to zero. Avoid increasing ventilation rate during arrest to correct carbon dioxide.","id":"als-032","globalNumber":2042,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":33,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have cardiac arrest in severe asthma. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["reversible mechanical causes dominate and conventional medical arrest algorithms are insufficient","gravid uterine compression of the aorta and vena cava impairs CPR and venous return","dynamic hyperinflation causing obstructive shock, especially when expiratory flow does not return to zero","a high-probability pulmonary embolic arrest without another reversible cause","severe hypothermia can mimic death and permits neurologically intact survival after prolonged arrest"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"dynamic hyperinflation causing obstructive shock, especially when expiratory flow does not return to zero","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is dynamic hyperinflation causing obstructive shock, especially when expiratory flow does not return to zero. In context, this supports Cardiac arrest in severe asthma and should prompt clinicians to disconnect the circuit, decompress trapped gas and continue ALS while treating bronchospasm and pneumothorax.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest in severe asthma: dynamic hyperinflation causing obstructive shock, especially when expiratory flow does not return to zero. Avoid increasing ventilation rate during arrest to correct carbon dioxide.","id":"als-033","globalNumber":2043,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":34,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with cardiac arrest in severe asthma is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["prolonged closed-chest CPR while delaying correction of hypovolaemia, tension or tamponade","stopping resuscitation soon after thrombolysis without allowing prolonged circulation time","using a steep lateral tilt that degrades chest-compression quality","increasing ventilation rate during arrest to correct carbon dioxide","terminating resuscitation before adequate rewarming in the absence of lethal injury"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"increasing ventilation rate during arrest to correct carbon dioxide","explanation":"The hazardous action is increasing ventilation rate during arrest to correct carbon dioxide. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is barotrauma, tension pneumothorax and recurrent arrest.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest in severe asthma: dynamic hyperinflation causing obstructive shock, especially when expiratory flow does not return to zero. Avoid increasing ventilation rate during arrest to correct carbon dioxide.","id":"als-034","globalNumber":2044,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":35,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with cardiac arrest in severe asthma?","options":["preventable death from unresolved obstructive or haemorrhagic shock","irreversible maternal hypoxia and fetal compromise","recurrent ventricular fibrillation and rewarming shock","recurrent arrest and severe right-ventricular failure","barotrauma, tension pneumothorax and recurrent arrest"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"barotrauma, tension pneumothorax and recurrent arrest","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is barotrauma, tension pneumothorax and recurrent arrest. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of cardiac arrest in severe asthma.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest in severe asthma: dynamic hyperinflation causing obstructive shock, especially when expiratory flow does not return to zero. Avoid increasing ventilation rate during arrest to correct carbon dioxide.","id":"als-035","globalNumber":2045,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":36,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has a patient arrests during coronary angiography with a shockable rhythm or mechanical complication immediately visible. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Cardiac arrest in the cardiac catheterisation laboratory","Cardiac arrest from massive pulmonary embolism","Cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery","Toxicological cardiac arrest","Traumatic cardiac arrest"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Cardiac arrest in the cardiac catheterisation laboratory","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Cardiac arrest in the cardiac catheterisation laboratory. The key discriminator is a witnessed monitored arrest with immediate defibrillation and potential PCI, tamponade drainage or mechanical support. Immediate priorities include: deliver immediate stacked shocks when appropriate and correct the catheter-laboratory cause without interrupting coronary treatment.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest in the cardiac catheterisation laboratory: a witnessed monitored arrest with immediate defibrillation and potential PCI, tamponade drainage or mechanical support. Avoid moving the patient away from definitive coronary or mechanical treatment for routine prolonged resuscitation.","id":"als-036","globalNumber":2046,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":37,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for cardiac arrest in the cardiac catheterisation laboratory when the presentation is a patient arrests during coronary angiography with a shockable rhythm or mechanical complication immediately visible?","options":["use standard resuscitation plus toxin-specific antidotes, sodium bicarbonate or lipid therapy as indicated and consider ECPR","deliver immediate stacked shocks when appropriate and correct the catheter-laboratory cause without interrupting coronary treatment","defibrillate rapidly, correct reversible surgical causes and prepare emergency resternotomy according to protocol","continue high-quality CPR and give thrombolysis or pursue embolectomy or extracorporeal support when appropriate","prioritise blood, haemorrhage control, bilateral thoracostomies, pelvic stabilisation and selected resuscitative thoracotomy"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"deliver immediate stacked shocks when appropriate and correct the catheter-laboratory cause without interrupting coronary treatment","explanation":"The priority is to deliver immediate stacked shocks when appropriate and correct the catheter-laboratory cause without interrupting coronary treatment. The decisive feature is a witnessed monitored arrest with immediate defibrillation and potential PCI, tamponade drainage or mechanical support. A major error is moving the patient away from definitive coronary or mechanical treatment for routine prolonged resuscitation.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest in the cardiac catheterisation laboratory: a witnessed monitored arrest with immediate defibrillation and potential PCI, tamponade drainage or mechanical support. Avoid moving the patient away from definitive coronary or mechanical treatment for routine prolonged resuscitation.","id":"als-037","globalNumber":2047,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":38,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for cardiac arrest in the cardiac catheterisation laboratory in this presentation?","options":["severe poisoning may require prolonged support until redistribution or elimination occurs","a witnessed arrest where tamponade, bleeding or pacing failure may require immediate chest reopening","a witnessed monitored arrest with immediate defibrillation and potential PCI, tamponade drainage or mechanical support","reversible mechanical causes dominate and conventional medical arrest algorithms are insufficient","a high-probability pulmonary embolic arrest without another reversible cause"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a witnessed monitored arrest with immediate defibrillation and potential PCI, tamponade drainage or mechanical support","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a witnessed monitored arrest with immediate defibrillation and potential PCI, tamponade drainage or mechanical support. In context, this supports Cardiac arrest in the cardiac catheterisation laboratory and should prompt clinicians to deliver immediate stacked shocks when appropriate and correct the catheter-laboratory cause without interrupting coronary treatment.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest in the cardiac catheterisation laboratory: a witnessed monitored arrest with immediate defibrillation and potential PCI, tamponade drainage or mechanical support. Avoid moving the patient away from definitive coronary or mechanical treatment for routine prolonged resuscitation.","id":"als-038","globalNumber":2048,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":39,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in cardiac arrest in the cardiac catheterisation laboratory?","options":["stopping resuscitation soon after thrombolysis without allowing prolonged circulation time","performing prolonged conventional CPR while delaying indicated resternotomy","stopping resuscitation early despite a reversible toxic mechanism","moving the patient away from definitive coronary or mechanical treatment for routine prolonged resuscitation","prolonged closed-chest CPR while delaying correction of hypovolaemia, tension or tamponade"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"moving the patient away from definitive coronary or mechanical treatment for routine prolonged resuscitation","explanation":"The hazardous action is moving the patient away from definitive coronary or mechanical treatment for routine prolonged resuscitation. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is refractory ventricular fibrillation and mechanical circulatory collapse.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest in the cardiac catheterisation laboratory: a witnessed monitored arrest with immediate defibrillation and potential PCI, tamponade drainage or mechanical support. Avoid moving the patient away from definitive coronary or mechanical treatment for routine prolonged resuscitation.","id":"als-039","globalNumber":2049,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"ALS","categoryNumber":40,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in cardiac arrest in the cardiac catheterisation laboratory, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["preventable death from unresolved obstructive or haemorrhagic shock","recurrent dysrhythmia, refractory shock and delayed neurologic injury","graft disruption, cerebral hypoperfusion and irreversible arrest","recurrent arrest and severe right-ventricular failure","refractory ventricular fibrillation and mechanical circulatory collapse"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"refractory ventricular fibrillation and mechanical circulatory collapse","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is refractory ventricular fibrillation and mechanical circulatory collapse. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of cardiac arrest in the cardiac catheterisation laboratory.","learningPoint":"Cardiac arrest in the cardiac catheterisation laboratory: a witnessed monitored arrest with immediate defibrillation and potential PCI, tamponade drainage or mechanical support. Avoid moving the patient away from definitive coronary or mechanical treatment for routine prolonged resuscitation.","id":"als-040","globalNumber":2050,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Invasive monitoring","categoryNumber":21,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with an external ventricular drain displays a falsely high intracranial pressure and drains excessive CSF after patient repositioning. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["External ventricular drain transducer positioned too low","Intracranial hypertension on invasive monitoring","Central venous pressure cannon A waves","Persistently wedged pulmonary artery catheter","Raised intra-abdominal pressure with low abdominal perfusion pressure"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"External ventricular drain transducer positioned too low","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with External ventricular drain transducer positioned too low. The key discriminator is the pressure transducer is below the tragus or prescribed reference level. Immediate priorities include: clamp as required, re-level and zero the system at the correct anatomical reference before interpretation.","learningPoint":"External ventricular drain transducer positioned too low: the pressure transducer is below the tragus or prescribed reference level. Avoid treating the displayed pressure or leaving the drain open without checking level after position change.","id":"invasive-monitoring-021","globalNumber":2051,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Invasive monitoring","categoryNumber":22,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has external ventricular drain transducer positioned too low: an external ventricular drain displays a falsely high intracranial pressure and drains excessive CSF after patient repositioning. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["remove excess tubing or air, secure connections and reassess the dynamic response","clamp as required, re-level and zero the system at the correct anatomical reference before interpretation","optimise head position, oxygenation and blood pressure, give hyperosmolar therapy and obtain urgent neurosurgical control","recognise atrioventricular dissociation and treat the underlying rhythm rather than the pressure number","inspect for clot, kinks or air and perform a fast-flush assessment before treating the displayed pressure"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"clamp as required, re-level and zero the system at the correct anatomical reference before interpretation","explanation":"The priority is to clamp as required, re-level and zero the system at the correct anatomical reference before interpretation. The decisive feature is the pressure transducer is below the tragus or prescribed reference level. A major error is treating the displayed pressure or leaving the drain open without checking level after position change.","learningPoint":"External ventricular drain transducer positioned too low: the pressure transducer is below the tragus or prescribed reference level. Avoid treating the displayed pressure or leaving the drain open without checking level after position change.","id":"invasive-monitoring-022","globalNumber":2052,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Invasive monitoring","categoryNumber":23,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected external ventricular drain transducer positioned too low, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["sustained intra-abdominal hypertension with organ dysfunction and reduced mean arterial pressure minus intra-abdominal pressure","loss of pulsatile waveform and failure of drainage despite correct levelling and a patent downstream system","the pressure transducer is below the tragus or prescribed reference level","rising P2 above P1 and plateau waves indicating poor intracranial compliance","the fast-flush test shows little or no oscillation after release"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"the pressure transducer is below the tragus or prescribed reference level","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is the pressure transducer is below the tragus or prescribed reference level. In context, this supports External ventricular drain transducer positioned too low and should prompt clinicians to clamp as required, re-level and zero the system at the correct anatomical reference before interpretation.","learningPoint":"External ventricular drain transducer positioned too low: the pressure 