ension or inappropriate vasopressor injury","abdominal compartment syndrome, renal failure and bowel ischaemia"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"abdominal compartment syndrome, renal failure and bowel ischaemia","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is abdominal compartment syndrome, renal failure and bowel ischaemia. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of raised intra-abdominal pressure with low abdominal perfusion pressure.","learningPoint":"Raised intra-abdominal pressure with low abdominal perfusion pressure: sustained intra-abdominal hypertension with organ dysfunction and reduced mean arterial pressure minus intra-abdominal pressure. Avoid relying on abdominal examination alone or measuring bladder pressure with incorrect technique.","id":"invasive-monitoring-040","globalNumber":2070,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Ventilators","categoryNumber":21,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with two ventilator breaths occur for one patient inspiratory effort, producing an excessive combined tidal volume. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Double-triggering patient–ventilator dyssynchrony","Reverse-triggering dyssynchrony","High peak pressure with normal plateau pressure","Ventilator auto-triggering","Auto-PEEP in obstructive ventilation"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Double-triggering patient–ventilator dyssynchrony","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Double-triggering patient–ventilator dyssynchrony. The key discriminator is a second breath is triggered before complete exhalation because neural inspiratory time exceeds ventilator inspiratory time. Immediate priorities include: treat pain or drive and adjust inspiratory time, flow or mode to match patient demand.","learningPoint":"Double-triggering patient–ventilator dyssynchrony: a second breath is triggered before complete exhalation because neural inspiratory time exceeds ventilator in