 dyssynchrony, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["a widened peak-to-plateau pressure gradient","trigger sensitivity is met by non-patient flow or pressure fluctuations","a second breath is triggered before complete exhalation because neural inspiratory time exceeds ventilator inspiratory time","expiratory flow remains above zero when the next breath begins","entrainment of patient effort by mandatory ventilator cycles rather than spontaneous triggering"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a second breath is triggered before complete exhalation because neural inspiratory time exceeds ventilator inspiratory time","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a second breath is triggered before complete exhalation because neural inspiratory time exceeds ventilator inspiratory time. In context, this supports Double-triggering patient–ventilator dyssynchrony and should prompt clinicians to treat pain or drive and adjust inspiratory time, flow or mode to match patient demand.","learningPoint":"Double-triggering patient–ventilator dyssynchrony: a second breath is triggered before complete exhalation because neural inspiratory time exceeds ventilator inspiratory time. Avoid simply increasing sedation without correcting an injurious ventilator mismatch.","id":"ventilators-023","globalNumber":2073,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Ventilators","categoryNumber":24,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of double-triggering patient–ventilator dyssynchrony, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["diagnosing reduced lung compliance and escalating PEEP without checking the airway","treating apparent tachypnoea with paralysis before checking the circuit","increasing minute ventilation to normalise PaCO2","simply increasing sedation without correcting an injurious ventilator mismatch","assuming all patient effort during controlled ventilation represents inadequate sedation"],"answ