ue efforts are not missed","explanation":"The priority is to remove circuit water or leaks and adjust trigger sensitivity while ensuring true efforts are not missed. The decisive feature is trigger sensitivity is met by non-patient flow or pressure fluctuations. A major error is treating apparent tachypnoea with paralysis before checking the circuit.","learningPoint":"Ventilator auto-triggering: trigger sensitivity is met by non-patient flow or pressure fluctuations. Avoid treating apparent tachypnoea with paralysis before checking the circuit.","id":"ventilators-027","globalNumber":2077,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Ventilators","categoryNumber":28,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for ventilator auto-triggering?","options":["failure to deliver or recover expected tidal volume with an obvious circuit or cuff leak","entrainment of patient effort by mandatory ventilator cycles rather than spontaneous triggering","trigger sensitivity is met by non-patient flow or pressure fluctuations","a second breath is triggered before complete exhalation because neural inspiratory time exceeds ventilator inspiratory time","expiratory flow remains above zero when the next breath begins"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"trigger sensitivity is met by non-patient flow or pressure fluctuations","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is trigger sensitivity is met by non-patient flow or pressure fluctuations. In context, this supports Ventilator auto-triggering and should prompt clinicians to remove circuit water or leaks and adjust trigger sensitivity while ensuring true efforts are not missed.","learningPoint":"Ventilator auto-triggering: trigger sensitivity is met by non-patient flow or pressure fluctuations. Avoid treating apparent tachypnoea with paralysis before checking the circuit.","id":"ventilators-028","globalNumber":2078,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Ventilators","categoryNumber":29,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in ventilator auto-triggering?","options":["repeatedly increasing set pressure without restoring a closed circuit","increasing minute ventilation to normalise PaCO2","assuming all patient effort during controlled ventilation represents inadequate sedation","treating apparent tachypnoea with paralysis before checking the circuit","simply increasing sedation without correcting an injurious ventilator mismatch"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"treating apparent tachypnoea with paralysis before checking the circuit","explanation":"The hazardous action is treating apparent tachypnoea with paralysis before checking the circuit. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is respiratory alkalosis, dynamic hyperinflation and delayed recognition of apnoea.","learningPoint":"Ventilator auto-triggering: trigger sensitivity is met by non-patient flow or pressure fluctuations. Avoid treating apparent tachypnoea with paralysis before checking the circuit.","id":"ventilators-029","globalNumber":2079,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Ventilators","categoryNumber":30,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of ventilator auto-triggering, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["volutrauma, breath stacking and respiratory muscle injury","mucus plugging, dynamic hyperinflation and barotrauma","breath stacking, diaphragm injury and volutrauma","barotrauma, tension physiology and cardiac arrest","respiratory alkalosis, dynamic hyperinflation and delayed recognition of apnoea"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"respiratory alkalosis, dynamic hyperinflation and delayed recognition of apnoea","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is respiratory alkalosis, dynamic hyperinflation and delayed recognition of apnoea. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of ventilator auto-triggering.","learningPoint":"Ventilator auto-triggering: trigger sensitivity is met by non-patient flow or pressure fluctuations. Avoid treating apparent tachypnoea with paralysis before checking the circuit.","id":"ventilators-030","globalNumber":2080,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Ventilators","categoryNumber":31,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by a low-pressure alarm occurs with loss of exhaled tidal volume and sudden hypoxaemia. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Ventilator circuit disconnection or major leak","Auto-PEEP in obstructive ventilation","Lung-protective ventilation for ARDS","Double-triggering patient–ventilator dyssynchrony","High peak pressure with normal plateau pressure"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Ventilator circuit disconnection or major leak","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Ventilator circuit disconnection or major leak. The key discriminator is failure to deliver or recover expected tidal volume with an obvious circuit or cuff leak. Immediate priorities include: disconnect from the ventilator and hand-ventilate while systematically checking tube, cuff and circuit.","learningPoint":"Ventilator circuit disconnection or major leak: failure to deliver or recover expected tidal volume with an obvious circuit or cuff leak. Avoid repeatedly increasing set pressure without restoring a closed circuit.","id":"ventilators-031","globalNumber":2081,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Ventilators","categoryNumber":32,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is ventilator circuit disconnection or major leak. The patient has a low-pressure alarm occurs with loss of exhaled tidal volume and sudden hypoxaemia. What is the best immediate management?","options":["search for increased airway resistance such as bronchospasm, secretions, biting or a kinked tube","disconnect from the ventilator and hand-ventilate while systematically checking tube, cuff and circuit","use about 6 mL per kg predicted body weight, limit plateau pressure and prone when severe","disconnect briefly if crashing, reduce respiratory rate and tidal volume, and prolong expiratory time","reduce excessive sedation when appropriate and adjust rate or mode to restore synchrony"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"disconnect from the ventilator and hand-ventilate while systematically checking tube, cuff and circuit","explanation":"The priority is to disconnect from the ventilator and hand-ventilate while systematically checking tube, cuff and circuit. The decisive feature is failure to deliver or recover expected tidal volume with an obvious circuit or cuff leak. A major error is repeatedly increasing set pressure without restoring a closed circuit.","learningPoint":"Ventilator circuit disconnection or major leak: failure to deliver or recover expected tidal volume with an obvious circuit or cuff leak. Avoid repeatedly increasing set pressure without restoring a closed circuit.","id":"ventilators-032","globalNumber":2082,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Ventilators","categoryNumber":33,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have ventilator circuit disconnection or major leak. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["a widened peak-to-plateau pressure gradient","expiratory flow remains above zero when the next breath begins","failure to deliver or recover expected tidal volume with an obvious circuit or cuff leak","both peak and plateau pressure rise together","trigger sensitivity is met by non-patient flow or pressure fluctuations"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"failure to deliver or recover expected tidal volume with an obvious circuit or cuff leak","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is failure to deliver or recover expected tidal volume with an obvious circuit or cuff leak. In context, this supports Ventilator circuit disco