s access","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Ultrasound-guided internal jugular central venous access. The key discriminator is real-time visualisation of the needle entering the target vein while avoiding the carotid artery. Immediate priorities include: use full sterile barrier precautions and dynamic ultrasound guidance, confirming wire position before dilation.","learningPoint":"Ultrasound-guided internal jugular central venous access: real-time visualisation of the needle entering the target vein while avoiding the carotid artery. Avoid dilating despite uncertainty about guidewire location or relying only on a static skin mark.","id":"emergency-medicine-procedures-031","globalNumber":2091,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Emergency medicine procedures","categoryNumber":32,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has ultrasound-guided internal jugular central venous access: a critically ill patient requires reliable central access with accessible internal jugular anatomy. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["insert under sterile ultrasound guidance and confirm urine drainage before securing","use full sterile barrier precautions and dynamic ultrasound guidance, confirming wire position before dilation","place a pacing wire under ECG or ultrasound guidance and confirm electrical and mechanical capture","drain under real-time ultrasound while maintaining preload and preparing definitive surgical care","insert into an appropriate landmark, confirm position and use pressure-assisted infusion with analgesia when conscious"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"use full sterile barrier precautions and dynamic ultrasound guidance, confirming wire position before dilation","explanation":"The priority is to use full sterile barrier precautions and dynamic ultrasound guidance, confirming wire position before dilation. The decisive feature is real-time visualisation of the needle entering 