s-033","globalNumber":2093,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Emergency medicine procedures","categoryNumber":34,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of ultrasound-guided internal jugular central venous access, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["accepting electrical spikes without confirming mechanical capture","using a blind landmark approach when ultrasound is immediately available","blind insertion into an inadequately filled bladder or through bowel-containing lower abdomen","dilating despite uncertainty about guidewire location or relying only on a static skin mark","persisting with repeated peripheral attempts while resuscitation is delayed"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"dilating despite uncertainty about guidewire location or relying only on a static skin mark","explanation":"The hazardous action is dilating despite uncertainty about guidewire location or relying only on a static skin mark. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is arterial injury, pneumothorax and guidewire loss.","learningPoint":"Ultrasound-guided internal jugular central venous access: real-time visualisation of the needle entering the target vein while avoiding the carotid artery. Avoid dilating despite uncertainty about guidewire location or relying only on a static skin mark.","id":"emergency-medicine-procedures-034","globalNumber":2094,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Emergency medicine procedures","categoryNumber":35,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with ultrasound-guided internal jugular central venous access initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["perforation, tamponade, dysrhythmia and loss of capture","bowel injury, haemorrhage and urinary peritonitis","coronary or myocardial injury, dysrhythmia and recurrent tamponade","extravasation, compartment syndrome and