ling. Avoid persisting with repeated peripheral attempts while resuscitation is delayed.","id":"emergency-medicine-procedures-039","globalNumber":2099,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Emergency medicine procedures","categoryNumber":40,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of intraosseous vascular access, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["arterial injury, pneumothorax and guidewire loss","perforation, tamponade, dysrhythmia and loss of capture","unresolved tension pneumothorax, bleeding and lung injury","coronary or myocardial injury, dysrhythmia and recurrent tamponade","extravasation, compartment syndrome and osteomyelitis"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"extravasation, compartment syndrome and osteomyelitis","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is extravasation, compartment syndrome and osteomyelitis. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of intraosseous vascular access.","learningPoint":"Intraosseous vascular access: correct needle placement with firm purchase and free flow without soft-tissue swelling. Avoid persisting with repeated peripheral attempts while resuscitation is delayed.","id":"emergency-medicine-procedures-040","globalNumber":2100,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Emergency medicine procedures","categoryNumber":41,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by a patient with penetrating thoracic trauma loses signs of life shortly before or after ED arrival. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Resuscitative thoracotomy","Finger thoracostomy","Intraosseous vascular access","Ultrasound-guided pericardiocentesis","Transvenous cardiac pacing"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Resuscitative thoracotomy","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Resuscitative thoracotomy. The key discriminator is a witnessed or very recent arrest with a potentially reversible intrathoracic cause and appropriate mechanism. Immediate priorities include: perform immediate thoracotomy for tamponade relief, haemorrhage control and aortic occlusion within a coordinated trauma response.","learningPoint":"Resuscitative thoracotomy: a witnessed or very recent arrest with a potentially reversible intrathoracic cause and appropriate mechanism. Avoid performing the procedure after prolonged unwitnessed blunt-trauma arrest with no signs of life.","id":"emergency-medicine-procedures-041","globalNumber":2101,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Emergency medicine procedures","categoryNumber":42,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is resuscitative thoracotomy. The patient has a patient with penetrating thoracic trauma loses signs of life shortly before or after ED arrival. What is the best immediate management?","options":["insert a large-bore chest drain in the safe triangle and quantify output while resuscitating","perform immediate thoracotomy for tamponade relief, haemorrhage control and aortic occlusion within a coordinated trauma response","open the pleural space immediately with blunt finger dissection, then place a definitive drain","drain under real-time ultrasound while maintaining preload and preparing definitive surgical care","place a pacing wire under ECG or ultrasound guidance and confirm electrical and mechanical capture"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"perform immediate thoracotomy for tamponade relief, haemorrhage control and aortic occlusion within a coordinated trauma response","explanation":"The priority is to perform immediate thoracotomy for tamponade relief, haemorrhage control and aortic occlusion within a coordinated trauma response. The decisive feature is a witnessed or very recent arrest with a potentially reversible intrathoracic cause and appropriate mechanism. A major error is performing the procedure after prolonged unwitnessed blunt-trauma arrest with no signs of life.","learningPoint":"Resuscitative th