essment is required even after initial stabilisation of resuscitative thoracotomy.","learningPoint":"Resuscitative thoracotomy: a witnessed or very recent arrest with a potentially reversible intrathoracic cause and appropriate mechanism. Avoid performing the procedure after prolonged unwitnessed blunt-trauma arrest with no signs of life.","id":"emergency-medicine-procedures-045","globalNumber":2105,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Emergency medicine procedures","categoryNumber":46,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has a trauma patient has a large haemothorax causing respiratory compromise or shock. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Tube thoracostomy for traumatic haemothorax","Ultrasound-guided pericardiocentesis","Emergency cricothyrotomy","Finger thoracostomy","Intraosseous vascular access"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Tube thoracostomy for traumatic haemothorax","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Tube thoracostomy for traumatic haemothorax. The key discriminator is pleural blood with significant volume or ongoing drainage requiring monitoring and surgical decision-making. Immediate priorities include: insert a large-bore chest drain in the safe triangle and quantify output while resuscitating.","learningPoint":"Tube thoracostomy for traumatic haemothorax: pleural blood with significant volume or ongoing drainage requiring monitoring and surgical decision-making. Avoid placing the tube too low or posteriorly without finger sweep and intrathoracic confirmation.","id":"emergency-medicine-procedures-046","globalNumber":2106,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Emergency medicine procedures","categoryNumber":47,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for tube thoracostomy for traumatic haemothorax when the presentation is a trauma patient has a large haemothorax causing respiratory compromise or shock?","options":["insert into a