ty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have occult traumatic pneumothorax before positive-pressure ventilation. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["obstructive shock that improves after pleural decompression","an open chest-wall defect impairing ventilation","pleural air on CT with a mechanism and planned positive-pressure exposure","a new air- or blood-filled lung cavity after high-energy chest trauma","more than 1500 mL immediate drainage or ongoing major output"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"pleural air on CT with a mechanism and planned positive-pressure exposure","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is pleural air on CT with a mechanism and planned positive-pressure exposure. In context, this supports Occult traumatic pneumothorax before positive-pressure ventilation and should prompt clinicians to monitor closely and drain when clinical context creates significant expansion risk.","learningPoint":"Occult traumatic pneumothorax before positive-pressure ventilation: pleural air on CT with a mechanism and planned positive-pressure exposure. Avoid assuming a normal supine chest radiograph excludes pneumothorax before prolonged ventilation.","id":"trauma-133","globalNumber":2153,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":134,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with occult traumatic pneumothorax before positive-pressure ventilation is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["excessive crystalloid or obligatory intubation based only on the flail segment","inserting a chest tube into herniated stomach or bowel","waiting for ultrasound or radiography when clinical tension is evident","assuming a normal supine chest radiograph excludes pneumothorax before prolonged ventilation","sealing all four sides without a vent or drain and creating tension"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"assuming a normal supine chest radiograph excludes pneumothorax before prolonged ventilation","explanation":"The hazardous action is assuming a normal supine chest radiograph excludes pneumothorax before prolonged ventilation. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is tension pneumothorax and cardiac arrest.","learningPoint":"Occult traumatic pneumothorax before positive-pressure ventilation: pleural air on CT with a mechanism and planned positive-pressure exposure. Avoid assuming a normal supine chest radiograph excludes pneumothorax before prolonged ventilation.","id":"trauma-134","globalNumber":2154,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":135,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with occult traumatic pneumothorax before positive-pressure ventilation?","options":["delayed respiratory failure, pneumonia and ARDS","tension pneumothorax and severe hypoxaemia","massive haemoptysis, persistent air leak and infection","pulseless electrical activity arrest","tension pneumothorax and cardiac arrest"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"tension pneumothorax and cardiac arrest","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is tension pneumothorax and cardiac arrest. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of occult traumatic pneumothorax before positive-pressure ventilation.","learningPoint":"Occult traumatic pneumothorax before positive-pressure ventilation: pleural air on CT with a mechanism and planned positive-pressure exposure. Avoid assuming a normal supine chest radiograph excludes pneumothorax before prolonged ventilation.","id":"trauma-135","globalNumber":2155,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":136,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has blunt trauma causes haemoptysis and a cavitary lung lesion within pulmonary contusion. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Pulmonary laceration with tra