auma. A major error is mistaking the acute cavity for infection and delaying trauma management.","learningPoint":"Pulmonary laceration with traumatic pneumatocele: a new air- or blood-filled lung cavity after high-energy chest trauma. Avoid mistaking the acute cavity for infection and delaying trauma management.","id":"trauma-137","globalNumber":2157,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":138,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for pulmonary laceration with traumatic pneumatocele in this presentation?","options":["multiple adjacent ribs fractured in at least two places with underlying lung injury","obstructive shock that improves after pleural decompression","a new air- or blood-filled lung cavity after high-energy chest trauma","an open chest-wall defect impairing ventilation","more than 1500 mL immediate drainage or ongoing major output"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a new air- or blood-filled lung cavity after high-energy chest trauma","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a new air- or blood-filled lung cavity after high-energy chest trauma. In context, this supports Pulmonary laceration with traumatic pneumatocele and should prompt clinicians to support ventilation, drain associated pleural collections and obtain thoracic input for ongoing bleeding or air leak.","learningPoint":"Pulmonary laceration with traumatic pneumatocele: a new air- or blood-filled lung cavity after high-energy chest trauma. Avoid mistaking the acute cavity for infection and delaying trauma management.","id":"trauma-138","globalNumber":2158,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":139,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in pulmonary laceration with traumatic pneumatocele?","options":["clamping a functioning drain in an unstable bleeding patient without a surgical plan","assuming a normal supine chest radiograph excludes pneumothorax before prolonged ventilation","excessive crystalloid or obligatory intubation based only on the flail segment","mistaking the acute cavity for infection and delaying trauma management","inserting a chest tube into herniated stomach or bowel"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"mistaking the acute cavity for infection and delaying trauma management","explanation":"The hazardous action is mistaking the acute cavity for infection and delaying trauma management. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is massive haemoptysis, persistent air leak and infection.","learningPoint":"Pulmonary laceration with traumatic pneumatocele: a new air- or blood-filled lung cavity after high-energy chest trauma. Avoid mistaking the acute cavity for infection and delaying trauma management.","id":"trauma-139","globalNumber":2159,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":140,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in pulmonary laceration with traumatic pneumatocele, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["delayed respiratory failure, pneumonia and ARDS","retained clot, continued haemorrhage and tension haemothorax","tension pneumothorax and cardiac arrest","strangulation, perforation and respiratory compromise","massive haemoptysis, persistent air leak and infection"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"massive haemoptysis, persistent air leak and infection","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is massive haemoptysis, persistent air leak and infection. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of pulmonary laceration with traumatic pneumatocele.","learningPoint":"Pulmonary laceration with traumatic pneumatocele: a new air- or blood-filled lung cavity after high-energy chest trauma. Avoid mistaking the acute cavity for infection and delaying trauma management.","id":"trauma-140","globalNumber":2160,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":141,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with a restrained occupant has an abdominal wall seat-belt sign, pain and initially subtle CT or examination findings. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Seat-belt abdominal injury syndrome","Unstable splenic injury","Traumatic diaphragmatic rupture","Retroperitoneal duodenal injury","Hollow-viscus injury after blunt trauma"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Seat-belt abdominal injury syndrome","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Seat-belt abdominal injury syndrome. The key discriminator is abdominal wall bruising with bowel or mesenteric injury risk after rapid deceleration. Immediate priorities include: perform serial examination and early surgical evaluation with repeat imaging when concern persists.","learningPoint":"Seat-belt abdominal injury syndrome: abdominal wall bruising with bowel or mesenteric injury risk after rapid deceleration. Avoid discharging after one reassuring examination despite a significant seat-belt sign.","id":"trauma-141","globalNumber":2161,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":142,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has seat-belt abdominal injury syndrome: a restrained occupant has an abdominal wall seat-belt sign, pain and initially subtle CT or examination findings. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["activate massive transfusion and proceed to operative haemorrhage control","perform serial examination and early surgical evaluation with repeat imaging when concern persists","decompress the stomach, avoid blind chest instrumentation and obtain urgent surgical repair","continue damage-control resuscitation and obtain immediate specialist operative control","obtain early specialist surgical management and define duct integrity"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"perform serial examination and early surgical evaluation with repeat imaging when concern persists","explanation":"The priority is to perform serial examination and early surgical evaluation with repeat imaging when concern persists. The decisive feature is abdominal wall bruising with bowel or mesenteric injury risk after rapid deceleration. A major error is discharging after one reassuring examination despite a significant seat-belt sign.","learningPoint":"Seat-belt abdominal injury syndrome: abdominal wall bruising with bowel or mesenteric injury risk after rapid deceleration. Avoid discharging after one reassuring examination despite a significant seat-belt sign.","id":"trauma-142","globalNumber":2162,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":143,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected seat-belt abdominal injury syndrome, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["retroperitoneal gas or contrast leak after a handlebar or seatbelt mechanism","herniated abdominal viscera through a diaphragmatic defect","abdominal wall bruising with bowel or mesenteric injury risk after rapid deceleration","retrohepatic caval or major hepatic venous bleeding with ongoing central haemorrhage","pancreatic transection or main-duct injury on dedicated imaging"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"abdominal wall bruising with bowel or mesenteric injury risk after rapid deceleration","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is abdominal wall bruising with bowel or mesenteric injury risk after rapid deceleration. In context, this supports Seat-belt abdominal injury syndrome and should prompt clinicians to perform serial examination and early surgical evaluation with repeat imaging when concern persists.","learningPoint":"Seat-belt abdominal injury syndrome: abdominal wall bruising with bowel or mesenteric injury risk after rapid deceleration. Avoid discharging after one reassuring examination despite a significant seat-belt sign.","id":"trauma-143","globalNumber":2163,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":144,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of seat-belt abdominal injury syndrome, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["discharging after an initially benign abdominal examination","repeated angiographic embolisation for predominantly venous exsanguination","relying on an initially normal CT despite worsening examination","discharging after one reassuring examination despite a significant seat-belt sign","inserting a chest tube into herniated stomach or bowel"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"discharging after one reassuring examination despite a significant seat-belt sign","explanation":"The hazardous action is discharging after one reassuring examination despite a significant seat-belt sign. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is delayed bowel perforation, sepsis and haemorrhage.","learningPoint":"Seat-belt abdominal injury syndrome: abdominal wall bruising with bowel or mesenteric injury risk after rapid deceleration. Avoid discharging after one reassuring examination despite a significant seat-belt sign.","id":"trauma-144","globalNumber":2164,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":145,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with seat-belt abdominal injury syndrome initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["strangulation, perforation and respiratory compromise","delayed perforation, sepsis and abdominal compartment syndrome","delayed perforation, pancreatitis and sepsis","massive haemorrhage, coagulopathy and abdominal compartment syndrome","delayed bowel perforation, sepsis and haemorrhage"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"delayed bowel perforation, sepsis and haemorrhage","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is delayed bowel perforation, sepsis and haemorrhage. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of seat-belt abdominal injury syndrome.","learningPoint":"Seat-belt abdominal injury syndrome: abdominal wall bruising with bowel or mesenteric injury risk after rapid deceleration. Avoid discharging after one reassuring examination despite a significant seat-belt sign.","id":"trauma-145","globalNumber":2165,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":146,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with epigastric impact is followed by persistent pain with initially modest enzymes and retroperitoneal fluid?","options":["Traumatic pancreatic injury","Unstable splenic injury","Hollow-viscus injury after blunt trauma","Traumatic diaphragmatic rupture","Seat-belt abdominal injury syndrome"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Traumatic pancreatic injury","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Traumatic pancreatic injury. The key discriminator is pancreatic transection or main-duct injury on dedicated imaging. Immediate priorities include: obtain early specialist surgical management and define duct integrity.","learningPoint":"Traumatic pancreatic injury: pancreatic transection or main-duct injury on dedicated imaging. Avoid relying on a normal early serum lipase to exclude pancreatic injury.","id":"trauma-146","globalNumber":2166,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":147,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with traumatic pancreatic injury presenting with epigastric impact is followed by persistent pain with initially modest enzymes and retroperitoneal fluid, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["decompress the stomach, avoid blind chest instrumentation and obtain urgent surgical repair","obtain early specialist surgical management and define duct integrity","continue damage-control resuscitation and obtain immediate specialist operative control","perform serial examination and early surgical evaluation with repeat imaging when concern persists","obtain surgical review and proceed to operative management when perforation is suspected"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"obtain early specialist surgical management and define duct integrity","explanation":"The priority is to obtain early specialist surgical management and define duct integrity. The decisive feature is pancreatic transection or main-duct injury on dedicated imaging. A major error is relying on a normal early serum lipase to exclude pancreatic injury.","learningPoint":"Traumatic pancreatic injury: pancreatic transection or main-duct injury on dedicated imaging. Avoid relying on a normal early serum lipase to exclude pancreatic injury.","id":"trauma-147","globalNumber":2167,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":148,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for traumatic pancreatic injury?","options":["mesenteric injury, free air or progressive peritoneal signs","retroperitoneal gas or contrast leak after a handlebar or seatbelt mechanism","pancreatic transection or main-duct injury on dedicated imaging","abdominal wall bruising with bowel or mesenteric injury risk after rapid deceleration","retrohepatic caval or major hepatic venous bleeding with ongoing central haemorrhage"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"pancreatic transection or main-duct injury on dedicated imaging","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is pancreatic transection or main-duct injury on dedicated imaging. In context, this supports Traumatic pancreatic injury and should prompt clinicians to obtain early specialist surgical management and define duct integrity.","learningPoint":"Traumatic pancreatic injury: pancreatic transection or main-duct injury on dedicated imaging. Avoid relying on a normal early serum lipase to exclude pancreatic injury.","id":"trauma-148","globalNumber":2168,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":149,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in traumatic pancreatic injury?","options":["relying on an initially normal CT despite worsening examination","sending an unstable patient for prolonged CT imaging","discharging after one reassuring examination despite a significant seat-belt sign","relying on a normal early serum lipase to exclude pancreatic injury","repeated angiographic embolisation for predominantly venous exsanguination"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"relying on a normal early serum lipase to exclude pancreatic injury","explanation":"The hazardous action is relying on a normal early serum lipase to exclude pancreatic injury. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is pancreatic fistula, pseudocyst and sepsis.","learningPoint":"Traumatic pancreatic injury: pancreatic transection or main-duct injury on dedicated imaging. Avoid relying on a normal early serum lipase to exclude pancreatic injury.","id":"trauma-149","globalNumber":2169,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":150,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of traumatic pancreatic injury, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["delayed bowel perforation, sepsis and haemorrhage","delayed perforation, sepsis and abdominal compartment syndrome","exsanguination and coagulopathy","strangulation, perforation and respiratory compromise","pancreatic fistula, pseudocyst and sepsis"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"pancreatic fistula, pseudocyst and sepsis","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is pancreatic fistula, pseudocyst and sepsis. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of traumatic pancreatic injury.","learningPoint":"Traumatic pancreatic injury: pancreatic transection or main-duct injury on dedicated imaging. Avoid relying on a normal early serum lipase to exclude pancreatic injury.","id":"trauma-150","globalNumber":2170,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":151,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by pelvic trauma causes inability to void, haematuria and intraperitoneal free fluid. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Intraperitoneal bladder rupture","Posterior urethral injury","Retroperitoneal duodenal injury","Unstable splenic injury","Renal pedicle avulsion"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Intraperitoneal bladder rupture","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Intraperitoneal bladder rupture. The key discriminator is contrast outlining bowel loops on CT cystography from a bladder-dome rupture. Immediate priorities include: drain the bladder and arrange operative repair.","learningPoint":"Intraperitoneal bladder rupture: contrast outlining bowel loops on CT cystography from a bladder-dome rupture. Avoid performing routine contrast CT without dedicated cystography and missing the rupture.","id":"trauma-151","globalNumber":2171,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":152,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is intraperitoneal bladder rupture. The patient has pelvic trauma causes inability to void, haematuria and intraperitoneal free fluid. What is the best immediate management?","options":["keep nil by mouth, give antibiotics and obtain urgent surgical assessment","drain the bladder and arrange operative repair","activate massive transfusion and proceed to operative haemorrhage control","activate haemorrhage control and obtain urgent urologic or vascular management","apply a pelvic binder over the greater trochanters, activate massive transfusion and arrange embolisation or packing"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"drain the bladder and arrange operative repair","explanation":"The priority is to drain the bladder and arrange operative repair. The decisive feature is contrast outlining bowel loops on CT cystography from a bladder-dome rupture. A major error is performing routine contrast CT without dedicated cystography and missing the rupture.","learningPoint":"Intraperitoneal bladder rupture: contrast outlining bowel loops on CT cystography from a bladder-dome rupture. Avoid performing routine contrast CT without dedicated cystography and missing the rupture.","id":"trauma-152","globalNumber":2172,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":153,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have intraperitoneal bladder rupture. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["abrupt renal arterial cutoff or absent renal enhancement after trauma","haemodynamic instability with splenic disruption and haemoperitoneum","contrast outlining bowel loops on CT cystography from a bladder-dome rupture","retroperitoneal gas or contrast leak after a handlebar or seatbelt mechanism","contrast extravasation on retrograde urethrography before catheterisation"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"contrast outlining bowel loops on CT cystography from a bladder-dome rupture","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is contrast outlining bowel loops on CT cystography from a bladder-dome rupture. In context, this supports Intraperitoneal bladder rupture and should prompt clinicians to drain the bladder and arrange operative repair.","learningPoint":"Intraperitoneal bladder rupture: contrast outlining bowel loops on CT cystography from a bladder-dome rupture. Avoid performing routine contrast CT without dedicated cystography and missing the rupture.","id":"trauma-153","globalNumber":2173,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":154,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with intraperitoneal bladder rupture is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["reassuring from minimal haematuria despite a high-risk mechanism","repeatedly log-rolling or placing the binder over the iliac crests","forcing a urethral catheter through resistance after pelvic trauma","performing routine contrast CT without dedicated cystography and missing the rupture","discharging after an initially benign abdominal examination"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"performing routine contrast CT without dedicated cystography and missing the rupture","explanation":"The hazardous action is performing routine contrast CT without dedicated cystography and missing the rupture. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is urinary peritonitis, sepsis and electrolyte disturbance.","learningPoint":"Intraperitoneal bladder rupture: contrast outlining bowel loops on CT cystography from a bladder-dome rupture. Avoid performing routine contrast CT without dedicated cystography and missing the rupture.","id":"trauma-154","globalNumber":2174,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":155,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with intraperitoneal bladder rupture?","options":["false passage, infection and long-term stricture","retroperitoneal haemorrhage, renal loss and hypertension","uncontrolled retroperitoneal haemorrhage and coagulopathy","delayed perforation, pancreatitis and sepsis","urinary peritonitis, sepsis and electrolyte disturbance"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"urinary peritonitis, sepsis and electrolyte disturbance","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is urinary peritonitis, sepsis and electrolyte disturbance. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of intraperitoneal bladder rupture.","learningPoint":"Intraperitoneal bladder rupture: contrast outlining bowel loops on CT cystography from a bladder-dome rupture. Avoid performing routine contrast 