ssive haemothorax","Retroperitoneal duodenal injury","Traumatic cardiac arrest","Unstable splenic injury"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Severe hepatic venous injury","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Severe hepatic venous injury. The key discriminator is retrohepatic caval or major hepatic venous bleeding with ongoing central haemorrhage. Immediate priorities include: continue damage-control resuscitation and obtain immediate specialist operative control.","learningPoint":"Severe hepatic venous injury: retrohepatic caval or major hepatic venous bleeding with ongoing central haemorrhage. Avoid repeated angiographic embolisation for predominantly venous exsanguination.","id":"trauma-166","globalNumber":2186,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":167,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with severe hepatic venous injury presenting with major blunt liver trauma causes persistent shock despite packing or arterial control, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["perform immediate surgical decompression or resuscitative thoracotomy according to setting and expertise","continue damage-control resuscitation and obtain immediate specialist operative control","activate massive transfusion and obtain urgent thoracic surgical control","give stress-dose corticosteroid while continuing trauma resuscitation","keep nil by mouth, give antibiotics and obtain urgent surgical assessment"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"continue damage-control resuscitation and obtain immediate specialist operative control","explanation":"The priority is to continue damage-control resuscitation and obtain immediate specialist operative control. The decisive feature is retrohepatic caval or major hepatic venous bleeding with ongoing central haemorrhage. A major error is repeated angiographic embolisation for predominantly venous exsanguination.","learningPoint":"Severe hepatic venous injury: retrohepatic caval or major hepa