ing drain in an unstable bleeding patient without a surgical plan"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"repeated angiographic embolisation for predominantly venous exsanguination","explanation":"The hazardous action is repeated angiographic embolisation for predominantly venous exsanguination. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is massive haemorrhage, coagulopathy and abdominal compartment syndrome.","learningPoint":"Severe hepatic venous injury: retrohepatic caval or major hepatic venous bleeding with ongoing central haemorrhage. Avoid repeated angiographic embolisation for predominantly venous exsanguination.","id":"trauma-169","globalNumber":2189,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":170,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of severe hepatic venous injury, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["retained clot, continued haemorrhage and tension haemothorax","pancreatic fistula, pseudocyst and sepsis","uncontrolled retroperitoneal haemorrhage and coagulopathy","pulseless electrical activity arrest","massive haemorrhage, coagulopathy and abdominal compartment syndrome"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"massive haemorrhage, coagulopathy and abdominal compartment syndrome","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is massive haemorrhage, coagulopathy and abdominal compartment syndrome. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of severe hepatic venous injury.","learningPoint":"Severe hepatic venous injury: retrohepatic caval or major hepatic venous bleeding with ongoing central haemorrhage. Avoid repeated angiographic embolisation for predominantly venous exsanguination.","id":"trauma-170","globalNumber":2190,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":171,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by bilateral adrenal injury after major trauma is foll