 fluids before release when possible, monitor ECG and treat hyperkalaemia aggressively"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"compress, image and obtain orthopaedic or plastic-surgical management for drainage or debridement","explanation":"The priority is to compress, image and obtain orthopaedic or plastic-surgical management for drainage or debridement. The decisive feature is a prefascial fluid collection separating subcutaneous tissue from fascia. A major error is mistaking the lesion for a simple bruise and repeatedly aspirating without definitive care.","learningPoint":"Morel-Lavallée closed degloving injury: a prefascial fluid collection separating subcutaneous tissue from fascia. Avoid mistaking the lesion for a simple bruise and repeatedly aspirating without definitive care.","id":"trauma-177","globalNumber":2197,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":178,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for morel-lavallée closed degloving injury in this presentation?","options":["systemic toxicity after reperfusion of crushed muscle","bilateral adrenal haemorrhage with haemodynamic features not explained by bleeding alone","a prefascial fluid collection separating subcutaneous tissue from fascia","complete or near-complete tissue separation with major vascular injury","haemodynamic instability with an open-book or vertical-shear pelvic pattern"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a prefascial fluid collection separating subcutaneous tissue from fascia","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a prefascial fluid collection separating subcutaneous tissue from fascia. In context, this supports Morel-Lavallée closed degloving injury and should prompt clinicians to compress, image and obtain orthopaedic or plastic-surgical management for drainage or debridement.","learningPoint":"Morel-Lavallée closed degloving injury: a prefascial fluid collection separating subcutaneous tissue from fascia. Avoid mistaking the lesion for a simple bruise and repeatedly aspirating without definitive care.","id":"trauma-178","globalNumber":2198,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":179,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in morel-lavallée closed degloving injury?","options":["repeatedly log-rolling or placing the binder over the iliac crests","placing the amputated part directly on ice or delaying haemorrhage control","releasing a heavily compressed limb without resuscitation readiness","mistaking the lesion for a simple bruise and repeatedly aspirating without definitive care","waiting for endocrine testing before treating suspected adrenal failure"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"mistaking the lesion for a simple bruise and repeatedly aspirating without definitive care","explanation":"The hazardous action is mistaking the lesion for a simple bruise and repeatedly aspirating without definitive care. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is skin necrosis, infection and chronic seroma.","learningPoint":"Morel-Lavallée closed degloving injury: a prefascial fluid collection separating subcutaneous tissue from fascia. Avoid mistaking the lesion for a simple bruise and repeatedly aspirating without definitive care.","id":"trauma-179","globalNumber":2199,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":180,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in morel-lavallée closed degloving injury, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["uncontrolled retroperitoneal haemorrhage and coagulopathy","refractory shock and multiorgan failure","exsanguination, infection and ischaemic loss of the limb","cardiac arrest, renal failure and compartment syndrome","skin necrosis, infection and chronic seroma"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"skin necrosis, infection and chroni