ower-body colour","pupillary asymmetry with a deteriorating neurologic examination"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"widened or abnormal craniocervical relationships on CT or MRI","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is widened or abnormal craniocervical relationships on CT or MRI. In context, this supports Atlanto-occipital dissociation and should prompt clinicians to maintain strict neutral immobilisation and obtain immediate specialist stabilisation.","learningPoint":"Atlanto-occipital dissociation: widened or abnormal craniocervical relationships on CT or MRI. Avoid applying traction or repeated airway manipulation without recognising instability.","id":"trauma-188","globalNumber":2208,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":189,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in atlanto-occipital dissociation?","options":["focusing on the dramatic facial appearance while missing underlying chest trauma","routine prolonged hyperventilation or allowing even a single hypotensive episode","attempting blind nasotracheal intubation with suspected skull-base injury","applying traction or repeated airway manipulation without recognising instability","confusing areflexia with distributive circulatory shock"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"applying traction or repeated airway manipulation without recognising instability","explanation":"The hazardous action is applying traction or repeated airway manipulation without recognising instability. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is brainstem injury, respiratory arrest and death.","learningPoint":"Atlanto-occipital dissociation: widened or abnormal craniocervical relationships on CT or MRI. Avoid applying traction or repeated airway manipulation without recognising instability.","id":"trauma-189","globalNumber":2209,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":190,"type":"complication