ure. Avoid attributing hand weakness to peripheral injury because leg function is relatively preserved.","id":"trauma-191","globalNumber":2211,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":192,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is central cord syndrome after cervical hyperextension. The patient has an older patient develops arm-predominant weakness after a hyperextension injury with cervical spondylosis. What is the best immediate management?","options":["maintain spinal alignment, support perfusion with norepinephrine and exclude haemorrhage","maintain cord perfusion and obtain urgent spinal evaluation","maintain spinal perfusion and obtain urgent surgical imaging and decompression assessment","provide spinal protection and supportive care while serially reassessing neurologic function","maintain spinal precautions and obtain urgent MRI and spinal specialist review"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"maintain cord perfusion and obtain urgent spinal evaluation","explanation":"The priority is to maintain cord perfusion and obtain urgent spinal evaluation. The decisive feature is disproportionate upper-limb motor impairment with cord signal change and no major fracture. A major error is attributing hand weakness to peripheral injury because leg function is relatively preserved.","learningPoint":"Central cord syndrome after cervical hyperextension: disproportionate upper-limb motor impairment with cord signal change and no major fracture. Avoid attributing hand weakness to peripheral injury because leg function is relatively preserved.","id":"trauma-192","globalNumber":2212,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":193,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have central cord syndrome after cervical hyperextension. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["transient areflexia including absent bulbocavernosus reflex","vasodilation and bradycardia after cord injury despite no bleeding source","disproportionate upper-limb motor impairment with cord signal change and no major fracture","objective cord dysfunction with MRI ligamentous or cord injury despite absent fracture","widened or abnormal craniocervical relationships on CT or MRI"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"disproportionate upper-limb motor impairment with cord signal change and no major fracture","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is disproportionate upper-limb motor impairment with cord signal change and no major fracture. In context, this supports Central cord syndrome after cervical hyperextension and should prompt clinicians to maintain cord perfusion and obtain urgent spinal evaluation.","learningPoint":"Central cord syndrome after cervical hyperextension: disproportionate upper-limb motor impairment with cord signal change and no major fracture. Avoid attributing hand weakness to peripheral injury because leg function is relatively preserved.","id":"trauma-193","globalNumber":2213,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":194,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with central cord syndrome after cervical hyperextension is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["confusing areflexia with distributive circulatory shock","clearing the spine solely because CT shows no fracture","assuming all hypotension is neurogenic before excluding occult haemorrhage","attributing hand weakness to peripheral injury because leg function is relatively preserved","applying traction or repeated airway manipulation without recognising instability"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"attributing hand weakness to peripheral injury because leg function is relatively preserved","explanation":"The hazardous action is attributing hand weakness to peripheral injury because leg function is relatively preserved. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is urinary retention, respiratory compromise and persistent upper-limb disability.","learningPoint":"Central cord syndrome after cervical hyperextension: disproportionate upper-limb motor impairment with cord signal change and no major fracture. Avoid attributing hand weakness to peripheral injury because leg function is relatively preserved.","id":"trauma-194","globalNumber":2214,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":195,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with central cord syndrome after cervical hyperextension?","options":["brainstem injury, respiratory arrest and death","pressure injury, venous thrombosis and delayed recognition of evolving cord injury","progressive cord oedema and permanent neurological deficit","spinal cord ischaemia, hypothermia and organ hypoperfusion","urinary retention, respiratory compromise and persistent upper-limb disability"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"urinary retention, respiratory compromise and persistent upper-limb disability","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is urinary retention, respiratory compromise and persistent upper-limb disability. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of central cord syndrome after cervical hyperextension.","learningPoint":"Central cord syndrome after cervical hyperextension: disproportionate upper-limb motor impairment with cord signal change and no major fracture. Avoid attributing hand weakness to peripheral injury because leg function is relatively preserved.","id":"trauma-195","globalNumber":2215,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":196,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has penetrating or focal spinal trauma causes ipsilateral weakness and proprioceptive loss with contralateral pain-temperature loss. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Brown-Séquard spinal cord syndrome","Neurogenic shock from cervical spinal cord injury","Central cord syndrome after cervical hyperextension","Spinal cord injury without radiographic abnormality","Atlanto-occipital dissociation"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Brown-Séquard spinal cord syndrome","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Brown-Séquard spinal cord syndrome. The key discriminator is a hemicord neurological pattern below the lesion. Immediate priorities include: maintain spinal perfusion and obtain urgent surgical imaging and decompression assessment.","learningPoint":"Brown-Séquard spinal cord syndrome: a hemicord neurological pattern below the lesion. Avoid assuming an incomplete asymmetric deficit is functional or peripheral.","id":"trauma-196","globalNumber":2216,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":197,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for brown-séquard spinal cord syndrome when the presentation is penetrating or focal spinal trauma causes ipsilateral weakness and proprioceptive loss with contralateral pain-temperature loss?","options":["provide spinal protection and supportive care while serially reassessing neurologic function","maintain spinal perfusion and obtain urgent surgical imaging and decompression assessment","maintain spinal precautions and obtain urgent MRI and spinal specialist review","maintain spinal alignment, support perfusion with norepinephrine and exclude haemorrhage","maintain strict neutral immobilisation and obtain immediate specialist stabilisation"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"maintain spinal perfusion and obtain urgent surgical imaging and decompression assessment","explanation":"The priority is to maintain spinal perfusion and obtain urgent surgical imaging and decompression assessment. The decisive feature is a hemicord neurological pattern below the lesion. A major error is assuming an incomplete asymmetric deficit is functional or peripheral.","learningPoint":"Brown-Séquard spinal cord syndrome: a hemicord neurological pattern below the lesion. Avoid assuming an incomplete asymmetric deficit is functional or peripheral.","id":"trauma-197","globalNumber":2217,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":198,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for brown-séquard spinal cord syndrome in this presentation?","options":["objective cord dysfunction with MRI ligamentous or cord injury despite absent fracture","widened or abnormal craniocervical relationships on CT or MRI","a hemicord neurological pattern below the lesion","transient areflexia including absent bulbocavernosus reflex","vasodilation and bradycardia after cord injury despite no bleeding source"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a hemicord neurological pattern below the lesion","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a hemicord neurological pattern below the lesion. In context, this supports Brown-Séquard spinal cord syndrome and should prompt clinicians to maintain spinal perfusion and obtain urgent surgical imaging and decompression assessment.","learningPoint":"Brown-Séquard spinal cord syndrome: a hemicord neurological pattern below the lesion. Avoid assuming an incomplete asymmetric deficit is functional or peripheral.","id":"trauma-198","globalNumber":2218,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":199,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in brown-séquard spinal cord syndrome?","options":["attributing hand weakness to peripheral injury because leg function is relatively preserved","applying traction or repeated airway manipulation without recognising instability","assuming all hypotension is neurogenic before excluding occult haemorrhage","assuming an incomplete asymmetric deficit is functional or peripheral","confusing areflexia with distributive circulatory shock"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"assuming an incomplete asymmetric deficit is functional or peripheral","explanation":"The hazardous action is assuming an incomplete asymmetric deficit is functional or peripheral. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is progressive cord oedema and permanent neurological deficit.","learningPoint":"Brown-Séquard spinal cord syndrome: a hemicord neurological pattern below the lesion. Avoid assuming an incomplete asymmetric deficit is functional or peripheral.","id":"trauma-199","globalNumber":2219,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Trauma","categoryNumber":200,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in brown-séquard spinal cord syndrome, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["spinal cord ischaemia, hypothermia and organ hypoperfusion","delayed neurological deterioration and permanent paralysis","brainstem injury, respiratory arrest and death","pressure injury, venous thrombosis and delayed recognition of evolving cord injury","progressive cord oedema and permanent neurological deficit"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"progressive cord oedema and permanent neurological deficit","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is progressive cord oedema and permanent neurological deficit. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of brown-séquard spinal cord syndrome.","learningPoint":"Brown-Séquard spinal cord syndrome: a hemicord neurological pattern below the lesion. Avoid assuming an incomplete asymmetric deficit is functional or peripheral.","id":"trauma-200","globalNumber":2220,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":101,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with left-lower-quadrant pain progresses to diffuse peritonism, sepsis and free intraperitoneal air. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Perforated diverticulitis with generalised peritonitis","Stercoral colonic perforation","Perforated appendicitis with abscess","Emphysematous gastritis","Meckel diverticulitis with perforation"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Perforated diverticulitis with generalised peritonitis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Perforated diverticulitis with generalised peritonitis. The key discriminator is free perforation from diseased sigmoid colon with diffuse contamination. Immediate priorities include: resuscitate, give broad antibiotics and obtain emergency operative source control.","learningPoint":"Perforated diverticulitis with generalised peritonitis: free perforation from diseased sigmoid colon with diffuse contamination. Avoid treating with oral antibiotics or percutaneous drainage despite generalised peritonitis.","id":"general-surgery-101","globalNumber":2221,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":102,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has perforated diverticulitis with generalised peritonitis: left-lower-quadrant pain progresses to diffuse peritonism, sepsis and free intraperitoneal air. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["resuscitate, give acid suppression and antibiotics, and arrange emergency surgery","resuscitate, give broad antibiotics and obtain emergency operative source control","give antibiotics and obtain surgical or interventional radiology input for drainage and interval strategy","resuscitate, give broad antibiotics and arrange emergency surgery","resuscitate, give broad antibiotics and obtain urgent surgical assessment"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"resuscitate, give broad antibiotics and obtain emergency operative source control","explanation":"The priority is to resuscitate, give broad antibiotics and obtain emergency operative source control. The decisive feature is free perforation from diseased sigmoid colon with diffuse contamination. A major error is treating with oral antibiotics or percutaneous drainage despite generalised peritonitis.","learningPoint":"Perforated diverticulitis with generalised peritonitis: free perforation from diseased sigmoid colon with diffuse contamination. Avoid treating with oral antibiotics or percutaneous drainage despite generalised peritonitis.","id":"general-surgery-102","globalNumber":2222,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":103,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected perforated diverticulitis with generalised peritonitis, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["appendiceal collection with contained perforation","intramural gastric gas with systemic toxicity and infectious features","free perforation from diseased sigmoid colon with diffuse contamination","perforation adjacent to the gastrojejunal anastomosis","an inflamed blind-ending ileal diverticulum, sometimes with ectopic tissue"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"free perforation from diseased sigmoid colon with diffuse contamination","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is free perforation from diseased sigmoid colon with diffuse contamination. In context, this supports Perforated diverticulitis with generalised peritonitis and should prompt clinicians to resuscitate, give broad antibiotics and obtain emergency operative source control.","learningPoint":"Perforated diverticulitis with generalised peritonitis: free perforation from diseased sigmoid colon with diffuse contamination. Avoid treating with oral antibiotics or percutaneous drainage despite generalised peritonitis.","id":"general-surgery-103","globalNumber":2223,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":104,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of perforated diverticulitis with generalised peritonitis, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["ending exploration after finding a normal appendix despite ongoing peritonitis","sending the patient home after transient pain improvement","immediate unplanned dissection through a mature inflammatory mass in a stable patient","treating with oral antibiotics or percutaneous drainage despite generalised peritonitis","assuming altered anatomy makes peptic perforation unlikely"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"treating with oral antibiotics or percutaneous drainage despite generalised peritonitis","explanation":"The hazardous action is treating with oral antibiotics or percutaneous drainage despite generalised peritonitis. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is septic shock, abscess and fistula.","learningPoint":"Perforated diverticulitis with generalised peritonitis: free perforation from diseased sigmoid colon with diffuse contamination. Avoid treating with oral antibiotics or percutaneous drainage despite generalised peritonitis.","id":"general-surgery-104","globalNumber":2224,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":105,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with perforated diverticulitis with generalised peritonitis initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["peritonitis, sepsis and recurrent ulceration","faecal peritonitis and septic shock","gastric necrosis, perforation and septic shock","septic shock and multiorgan failure","septic shock, abscess and fistula"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"septic shock, abscess and fistula","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is septic shock, abscess and fistula. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of perforated diverticulitis with generalised peritonitis.","learningPoint":"Perforated diverticulitis with generalised peritonitis: free perforation from diseased sigmoid colon with diffuse contamination. Avoid treating with oral antibiotics or percutaneous drainage despite generalised peritonitis.","id":"general-surgery-105","globalNumber":2225,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":106,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with fever and focal left-lower-quadrant pain with a pericolic or pelvic collection?","options":["Diverticular abscess","Splenic abscess","Deep anorectal abscess","Postoperative anastomotic leak","Psoas abscess"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Diverticular abscess","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Diverticular abscess. The key discriminator is a contained diverticular perforation with a drainable abscess. Immediate priorities include: give antibiotics and arrange percutaneous drainage or surgery according to size and physiology.","learningPoint":"Diverticular abscess: a contained diverticular perforation with a drainable abscess. Avoid performing immediate colonoscopy through acute inflamed diverticular disease.","id":"general-surgery-106","globalNumber":2226,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":107,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with diverticular abscess presenting with fever and focal left-lower-quadrant pain with a pericolic or pelvic collection, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["give antibiotics and obtain surgical or interventional radiology input for drainage and interval strategy","give antibiotics and arrange percutaneous drainage or surgery according to size and physiology","obtain imaging when needed and arrange surgical drainage with antibiotics for systemic illness","resuscitate, give antibiotics and obtain urgent CT and source control","give antibiotics and arrange drainage or splenectomy"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give antibiotics and arrange percutaneous drainage or surgery according to size and physiology","explanation":"The priority is to give antibiotics and arrange percutaneous drainage or surgery according to size and physiology. The decisive feature is a contained diverticular perforation with a drainable abscess. A major error is performing immediate colonoscopy through acute inflamed diverticular disease.","learningPoint":"Diverticular abscess: a contained diverticular perforation with a drainable abscess. Avoid performing immediate colonoscopy through acute inflamed diverticular disease.","id":"general-surgery-107","globalNumber":2227,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":108,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for diverticular abscess?","options":["a psoas collection associated with bacteraemia, spinal infection or bowel disease","extraluminal contrast, gas or collection near an anastomosis","a contained diverticular perforation with a drainable abscess","appendiceal collection with contained perforation","deep ischiorectal or supralevator collection despite a normal skin examination"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a contained diverticular perforation with a drainable abscess","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a contained diverticular perforation with a drainable abscess. In context, this supports Diverticular abscess and should prompt clinicians to give antibiotics and arrange percutaneous drainage or surgery according to size and physiology.","learningPoint":"Diverticular abscess: a contained diverticular perforation with a drainable abscess. Avoid performing immediate colonoscopy through acute inflamed diverticular disease.","id":"general-surgery-108","globalNumber":2228,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":109,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in diverticular abscess?","options":["immediate unplanned dissection through a mature inflammatory mass in a stable patient","attributing persistent tachycardia to pain without investigating","treating as musculoskeletal back pain without evaluating sepsis","performing immediate colonoscopy through acute inflamed diverticular disease","repeated bedside incision where no superficial collection exists"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"performing immediate colonoscopy through acute inflamed diverticular disease","explanation":"The hazardous action is performing immediate colonoscopy through acute inflamed diverticular disease. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is free perforation, fistula and recurrent sepsis.","learningPoint":"Diverticular abscess: a contained diverticular perforation with a drainable abscess. Avoid performing immediate colonoscopy through acute inflamed diverticular disease.","id":"general-surgery-109","globalNumber":2229,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":110,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of diverticular abscess, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["peritonitis, fistula and septic shock","vertebral infection, septic arthritis and persistent bacteraemia","sepsis, fistula and necrotising infection","rupture, peritonitis and septic shock","free perforation, fistula and recurrent sepsis"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"free perforation, fistula and recurrent sepsis","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is free perforation, fistula and recurrent sepsis. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of diverticular abscess.","learningPoint":"Diverticular abscess: a contained diverticular perforation with a drainable abscess. Avoid performing immediate colonoscopy through acute inflamed diverticular disease.","id":"general-surgery-110","globalNumber":2230,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":111,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by progressive distension, obstipation and vomiting with a transition at a colonic tumour. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Malignant large-bowel obstruction","Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction","Sigmoid volvulus","Stercoral colonic perforation","Caecal volvulus"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Malignant large-bowel obstruction","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Malignant large-bowel obstruction. The key discriminator is proximal colonic dilatation with an obstructing mass and threatened caecal viability. Immediate priorities include: resuscitate, decompress and obtain urgent colorectal stenting or surgery based on location and stability.","learningPoint":"Malignant large-bowel obstruction: proximal colonic dilatation with an obstructing mass and threatened caecal viability. Avoid giving cathartics or delaying decompression while the caecum enlarges.","id":"general-surgery-111","globalNumber":2231,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":112,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is malignant large-bowel obstruction. The patient has progressive distension, obstipation and vomiting with a transition at a colonic tumour. What is the best immediate management?","options":["resuscitate, give broad antibiotics and arrange emergency surgery","resuscitate, decompress and obtain urgent colorectal stenting or surgery based on location and stability","correct precipitants, decompress and use neostigmine or colonoscopy when indicated","resuscitate, decompress and arrange emergency operative management","obtain urgent operative management rather than endoscopic decompression"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"resuscitate, decompress and obtain urgent colorectal stenting or surgery based on location and stability","explanation":"The priority is to resuscitate, decompress and obtain urgent colorectal stenting or surgery based on location and stability. The decisive feature is proximal colonic dilatation with an obstructing mass and threatened caecal viability. A major error is giving cathartics or delaying decompression while the caecum enlarges.","learningPoint":"Malignant large-bowel obstruction: proximal colonic dilatation with an obstructing mass and threatened caecal viability. Avoid giving cathartics or delaying decompression while the caecum enlarges.","id":"general-surgery-112","globalNumber":2232,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":113,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have malignant large-bowel obstruction. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["C-shaped obstructed loop with converging mesenteric vessels","ectopic kidney-shaped caecum with mesenteric whirl","proximal colonic dilatation with an obstructing mass and threatened caecal viability","diffuse colonic dilatation without a transition point","pressure necrosis adjacent to a faecaloma, often in sigmoid or rectum"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"proximal colonic dilatation with an obstructing mass and threatened caecal viability","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is proximal colonic dilatation with an obstructing mass and threatened caecal viability. In context, this supports Malignant large-bowel obstruction and should prompt clinicians to resuscitate, decompress and obtain urgent colorectal stenting or surgery based on location and stability.","learningPoint":"Malignant large-bowel obstruction: proximal colonic dilatation with an obstructing mass and threatened caecal viability. Avoid giving cathartics or delaying decompression while the caecum enlarges.","id":"general-surgery-113","globalNumber":2233,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":114,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with malignant large-bowel obstruction is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["giving neostigmine without monitoring or despite contraindications","attempting endoscopic decompression despite perforation or gangrene","treating like sigmoid volvulus with colonoscopic decompression","giving cathartics or delaying decompression while the caecum enlarges","treating as uncomplicated constipation with enemas"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"giving cathartics or delaying decompression while the caecum enlarges","explanation":"The hazardous action is giving cathartics or delaying decompression while the caecum enlarges. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is caecal perforation, ischaemia and sepsis.","learningPoint":"Malignant large-bowel obstruction: proximal colonic dilatation with an obstructing mass and threatened caecal viability. Avoid giving cathartics or delaying decompression while the caecum enlarges.","id":"general-surgery-114","globalNumber":2234,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":115,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with malignant large-bowel obstruction?","options":["bowel infarction, perforation and septic shock","ischaemia, perforation and recurrence","caecal gangrene and perforation","faecal peritonitis and septic shock","caecal perforation, ischaemia and sepsis"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"caecal perforation, ischaemia and sepsis","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is caecal perforation, ischaemia and sepsis. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of malignant large-bowel obstruction.","learningPoint":"Malignant large-bowel obstruction: proximal colonic dilatation with an obstructing mass and threatened caecal viability. Avoid giving cathartics or delaying decompression while the caecum enlarges.","id":"general-surgery-115","globalNumber":2235,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":116,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has sudden severe colicky pain and vomiting with two transition points and twisted mesenteric vessels. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Closed-loop small-bowel volvulus","Gallstone ileus","Internal hernia after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass","Obturator hernia","Strangulated incarcerated hernia"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Closed-loop small-bowel volvulus","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Closed-loop small-bowel volvulus. The key discriminator is a whirl sign and C- or U-shaped closed loop with impaired enhancement. Immediate priorities include: give immediate resuscitation and obtain emergency operative detorsion.","learningPoint":"Closed-loop small-bowel volvulus: a whirl sign and C- or U-shaped closed loop with impaired enhancement. Avoid managing as simple adhesive obstruction despite CT signs of vascular compromise.","id":"general-surgery-116","globalNumber":2236,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":117,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for closed-loop small-bowel volvulus when the presentation is sudden severe colicky pain and vomiting with two transition points and twisted mesenteric vessels?","options":["resuscitate, decompress and arrange emergency operative management","give immediate resuscitation and obtain emergency operative detorsion","resuscitate and arrange surgical enterolithotomy assessment","obtain urgent bariatric surgical exploration when suspicion is high","provide fluid and electrolyte replacement, nasogastric decompression and serial surgical review"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give immediate resuscitation and obtain emergency operative detorsion","explanation":"The priority is to give immediate resuscitation and obtain emergency operative detorsion. The decisive feature is a whirl sign and C- or U-shaped closed loop with impaired enhancement. A major error is managing as simple adhesive obstruction despite CT signs of vascular compromise.","learningPoint":"Closed-loop small-bowel volvulus: a whirl sign and C- or U-shaped closed loop with impaired enhancement. Avoid managing as simple adhesive obstruction despite CT signs of vascular compromise.","id":"general-surgery-117","globalNumber":2237,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":118,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for closed-loop small-bowel volvulus in this presentation?","options":["small bowel entering the obturator canal with the Howship-Romberg pattern","Rigler triad of obstruction, pneumobilia and ectopic gallstone","a whirl sign and C- or U-shaped closed loop with impaired enhancement","target-shaped mass containing mesenteric fat in an adult","local tenderness and systemic signs suggesting compromised bowel"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a whirl sign and C- or U-shaped closed loop with impaired enhancement","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a whirl sign and C- or U-shaped closed loop with impaired enhancement. In context, this supports Closed-loop small-bowel volvulus and should prompt clinicians to give immediate resuscitation and obtain emergency operative detorsion.","learningPoint":"Closed-loop small-bowel volvulus: a whirl sign and C- or U-shaped closed loop with impaired enhancement. Avoid managing as simple adhesive obstruction despite CT signs of vascular compromise.","id":"general-surgery-118","globalNumber":2238,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":119,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in closed-loop small-bowel volvulus?","options":["prolonged conservative management despite ischaemic CT signs","assuming idiopathic disease and discharging after transient symptom resolution","forceful bedside reduction when strangulation is suspected","managing as simple adhesive obstruction despite CT signs of vascular compromise","giving oral cathartics or delaying reassessment after worsening pain"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"managing as simple adhesive obstruction despite CT signs of vascular compromise","explanation":"The hazardous action is managing as simple adhesive obstruction despite CT signs of vascular compromise. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is bowel necrosis, perforation and short-gut syndrome.","learningPoint":"Closed-loop small-bowel volvulus: a whirl sign and C- or U-shaped closed loop with impaired enhancement. Avoid managing as simple adhesive obstruction despite CT signs of vascular compromise.","id":"general-surgery-119","globalNumber":2239,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":120,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in closed-loop small-bowel volvulus, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["bowel infarction, perforation and septic shock","closed-loop ischaemia, perforation and extensive bowel loss","strangulation, aspiration and renal injury","bowel necrosis, perforation and sepsis","bowel necrosis, perforation and short-gut syndrome"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"bowel necrosis, perforation and short-gut syndrome","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is bowel necrosis, perforation and short-gut syndrome. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of closed-loop small-bowel volvulus.","learningPoint":"Closed-loop small-bowel volvulus: a whirl sign and C- or U-shaped closed loop with impaired enhancement. Avoid managing as simple adhesive obstruction despite CT signs of vascular compromise.","id":"general-surgery-120","globalNumber":2240,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":121,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient p