suscitate, give acid suppression and antibiotics, and arrange emergency surgery. The decisive feature is perforation adjacent to the gastrojejunal anastomosis. A major error is assuming altered anatomy makes peptic perforation unlikely.","learningPoint":"Perforated marginal ulcer after gastric bypass: perforation adjacent to the gastrojejunal anastomosis. Avoid assuming altered anatomy makes peptic perforation unlikely.","id":"general-surgery-132","globalNumber":2252,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":133,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have perforated marginal ulcer after gastric bypass. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["appendiceal collection with contained perforation","pneumoperitoneum with acute generalised peritonitis","perforation adjacent to the gastrojejunal anastomosis","ongoing blood loss with no external source in the postoperative period","an inflamed blind-ending ileal diverticulum, sometimes with ectopic tissue"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"perforation adjacent to the gastrojejunal anastomosis","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is perforation adjacent to the gastrojejunal anastomosis. In context, this supports Perforated marginal ulcer after gastric bypass and should prompt clinicians to resuscitate, give acid suppression and antibiotics, and arrange emergency surgery.","learningPoint":"Perforated marginal ulcer after gastric bypass: perforation adjacent to the gastrojejunal anastomosis. Avoid assuming altered anatomy makes peptic perforation unlikely.","id":"general-surgery-133","globalNumber":2253,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":134,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with perforated marginal ulcer after gastric bypass is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["repeated crystalloid boluses while delaying 