"globalNumber":2275,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":156,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has painless significant lower gastrointestinal bleeding in a child or young adult. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Meckel diverticulum haemorrhage","Perforated marginal ulcer after gastric bypass","Ischaemic stoma","Postoperative intra-abdominal haemorrhage","Perforated peptic ulcer"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Meckel diverticulum haemorrhage","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Meckel diverticulum haemorrhage. The key discriminator is ectopic gastric mucosa in a Meckel diverticulum causing ulceration. Immediate priorities include: resuscitate and obtain nuclear imaging or operative management based on bleeding severity.","learningPoint":"Meckel diverticulum haemorrhage: ectopic gastric mucosa in a Meckel diverticulum causing ulceration. Avoid assuming all painless bleeding in a young patient is haemorrhoidal.","id":"general-surgery-156","globalNumber":2276,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":157,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for meckel diverticulum haemorrhage when the presentation is painless significant lower gastrointestinal bleeding in a child or young adult?","options":["resuscitate, give acid suppression and antibiotics, and arrange emergency surgery","resuscitate and obtain nuclear imaging or operative management based on bleeding severity","give antibiotics and arrange drainage or splenectomy","obtain urgent surgical revision when deep ischaemia or necrosis is present","activate haemorrhage resuscitation and obtain immediate operative or interventional control"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"resuscitate and obtain nuclear imaging or operative management based on bleeding severity","explanation":"The priority is to resuscitate and obtain nuclear imaging 