te a large persistent abscess.","id":"general-surgery-190","globalNumber":2310,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":191,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by a newly created stoma becomes dusky, black or non-bleeding with systemic deterioration. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Ischaemic stoma","Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction","Abdominal compartment syndrome","Splenic abscess","Postoperative intra-abdominal haemorrhage"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Ischaemic stoma","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Ischaemic stoma. The key discriminator is non-viable mucosa extending below the fascial level. Immediate priorities include: obtain urgent surgical revision when deep ischaemia or necrosis is present.","learningPoint":"Ischaemic stoma: non-viable mucosa extending below the fascial level. Avoid reassuring from superficial discoloration without assessing depth and perfusion.","id":"general-surgery-191","globalNumber":2311,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":192,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is ischaemic stoma. The patient has a newly created stoma becomes dusky, black or non-bleeding with systemic deterioration. What is the best immediate management?","options":["resuscitate, give antibiotics and obtain urgent CT and source control","obtain urgent surgical revision when deep ischaemia or necrosis is present","resuscitate, give acid suppression and antibiotics, and arrange emergency surgery","activate haemorrhage resuscitation and obtain immediate operative or interventional control","resuscitate and obtain nuclear imaging or operative management based on bleeding severity"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"obtain urgent surgical revision when deep ischaemia or necrosis is present","explanation":"The priority is to obtain urgent surgical revision when deep ischaemia or necrosis is present. The decisive feature is non-viable mucosa extending below the fascial level. A major error is reassuring from superficial discoloration without assessing depth and perfusion.","learningPoint":"Ischaemic stoma: non-viable mucosa extending below the fascial level. Avoid reassuring from superficial discoloration without assessing depth and perfusion.","id":"general-surgery-192","globalNumber":2312,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":193,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have ischaemic stoma. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["unexplained persistent tachycardia with extraluminal contrast or perigastric collection","incarcerated bowel within a parastomal defect with compromised enhancement","non-viable mucosa extending below the fascial level","diffuse colonic dilatation without a transition point","ongoing blood loss with no external source in the postoperative period"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"non-viable mucosa extending below the fascial level","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is non-viable mucosa extending below the fascial level. In context, this supports Ischaemic stoma and should prompt clinicians to obtain urgent surgical revision when deep ischaemia or necrosis is present.","learningPoint":"Ischaemic stoma: non-viable mucosa extending below the fascial level. Avoid reassuring from superficial discoloration without assessing depth and perfusion.","id":"general-surgery-193","globalNumber":2313,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"General surgery","categoryNumber":194,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with ischaemic stoma is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["sending the patient home after transient pain improvement","repeated crystalloid boluses while delaying definitive haemostasis","attributing isolated tachycardia to pain without inves