","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in paget-schroetter effort thrombosis, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["limb loss, compartment syndrome and reperfusion injury","irreversible ischaemia, amputation and reperfusion injury","venous gangrene, pulmonary embolism and shock","muscle necrosis, hyperkalaemia and limb loss","pulmonary embolism and chronic post-thrombotic disability"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"pulmonary embolism and chronic post-thrombotic disability","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is pulmonary embolism and chronic post-thrombotic disability. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of paget-schroetter effort thrombosis.","learningPoint":"Paget-Schroetter effort thrombosis: axillosubclavian venous thrombosis from thoracic-outlet compression. Avoid treating as cellulitis without venous imaging.","id":"vascular-surgery-090","globalNumber":2360,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Vascular surgery","categoryNumber":91,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with abdominal or back pain with aneurysm sac expansion after EVAR. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Type I endoleak after endovascular aneurysm repair","Mycotic aortic aneurysm","Ruptured splenic artery aneurysm","Thrombosed popliteal artery aneurysm","Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Type I endoleak after endovascular aneurysm repair","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Type I endoleak after endovascular aneurysm repair. The key discriminator is persistent pressurisation at the proximal or distal graft seal zone. Immediate priorities include: obtain urgent endovascular repair because rupture risk remains high.","learningPoint":"Type I endoleak after endovascular aneurysm repair: persistent pressurisation at the proximal or distal graft seal zone. Avoid observing a symptomatic type I endoleak as if it were a low-pressure type II leak.","id":"vascular-surgery-091","globalNumber":2361,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Vascular surgery","categoryNumber":92,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has type i endoleak after endovascular aneurysm repair: abdominal or back pain with aneurysm sac expansion after EVAR. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["activate massive transfusion and obtain immediate endovascular or open aortic control","obtain urgent endovascular repair because rupture risk remains high","stop anticoagulation when appropriate and arrange ultrasound compression, thrombin injection or repair","give broad intravenous antibiotics and arrange urgent aortic repair","activate massive transfusion, allow permissive hypotension and obtain immediate vascular control"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"obtain urgent endovascular repair because rupture risk remains high","explanation":"The priority is to obtain urgent endovascular repair because rupture risk remains high. The decisive feature is persistent pressurisation at the proximal or distal graft seal zone. A major error is observing a symptomatic type I endoleak as if it were a low-pressure type II leak.","learningPoint":"Type I endoleak after endovascular aneurysm repair: persistent pressurisation at the proximal or distal graft seal zone. Avoid observing a symptomatic type I endoleak as if it were a low-pressure type II leak.","id":"vascular-surgery-092","globalNumber":2362,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Vascular surgery","categoryNumber":93,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected type i endoleak after endovascular aneurysm repair, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["perigraft gas, fluid or fistulation beyond the expected postoperative period","perivascular inflammation or gas around an irregular aneurysm in an infected patient","persistent pressurisation at the proximal or distal graft seal zone","retroperitoneal haemorrhage surrounding an aneurysmal aorta","arterial bleeding from a splenic artery aneurysm, sometimes with a temporary double-rupture phenomenon"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"persistent pressurisation at the proximal or distal graft seal zone","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is persistent pressurisation at the proximal or distal graft seal zone. In context, this supports Type I endoleak after endovascular aneurysm repair and should prompt clinicians to obtain urgent endovascular repair because rupture risk remains high.","learningPoint":"Type I endoleak after endovascular aneurysm repair: persistent pressurisation at the proximal or distal graft seal zone. Avoid observing a symptomatic type I endoleak as if it were a low-pressure type II leak.","id":"vascular-surgery-093","globalNumber":2363,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Vascular surgery","categoryNumber":94,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of type i endoleak after endovascular aneurysm repair, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["placing a large chest drain before aortic control when contained rupture is tamponading the bleed","delaying transfer to theatre for a complete CT in an unstable patient","blindly aspirating a pulsatile groin mass as an abscess","observing a symptomatic type I endoleak as if it were a low-pressure type II leak","treating with antibiotics alone despite structural instability"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"observing a symptomatic type I endoleak as if it were a low-pressure type II leak","explanation":"The hazardous action is observing a symptomatic type I endoleak as if it were a low-pressure type II leak. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is aneurysm rupture and exsanguination.","learningPoint":"Type I endoleak after endovascular aneurysm repair: persistent pressurisation at the proximal or distal graft seal zone. Avoid observing a symptomatic type I endoleak as if it were a low-pressure type II leak.","id":"vascular-surgery-094","globalNumber":2364,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Vascular surgery","categoryNumber":95,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with type i endoleak after endovascular aneurysm repair initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["rupture, sepsis and aortoenteric fistula","aortoenteric fistula, rupture and septic shock","exsanguination and abdominal compartment syndrome","limb loss and recurrent thrombosis","aneurysm rupture and exsanguination"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"aneurysm rupture and exsanguination","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is aneurysm rupture and exsanguination. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of type i endoleak after endovascular aneurysm repair.","learningPoint":"Type I endoleak after endovascular aneurysm repair: persistent pressurisation at the proximal or distal graft seal zone. Avoid observing a symptomatic type I endoleak as if it were a low-pressure type II leak.","id":"vascular-surgery-095","globalNumber":2365,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Vascular surgery","categoryNumber":96,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with sentinel oral or neck bleeding in a patient with head-and-neck cancer, surgery or radiation?","options":["Carotid blowout syndrome","Infected aortic vascular graft","Acute mesenteric arterial occlusion","Femoral pseudoaneurysm after arterial access","Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Carotid blowout syndrome","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Carotid blowout syndrome. The key discriminator is exposed or pseudoaneurysmal carotid artery adjacent to tumour or infected wound. Immediate priorities include: protect the airway, activate massive transfusion and obtain immediate endovascular or surgical control.","learningPoint":"Carotid blowout syndrome: exposed or pseudoaneurysmal carotid artery adjacent to tumour or infected wound. Avoid packing blindly and discharging after a self-limited sentinel bleed.","id":"vascular-surgery-096","globalNumber":2366,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Vascular surgery","categoryNumber":97,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with carotid blowout syndrome presenting with sentinel oral or neck bleeding in a patient with head-and-neck cancer, surgery or radiation, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["activate massive transfusion and obtain immediate vascular and gastrointestinal surgical control","protect the airway, activate massive transfusion and obtain immediate endovascular or surgical control","give heparin and arrange immediate aortic reperfusion","give anti-impulse therapy and obtain urgent aortic specialist review","stop anticoagulation when appropriate and arrange ultrasound compression, thrombin injection or repair"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"protect the airway, activate massive transfusion and obtain immediate endovascular or surgical control","explanation":"The priority is to protect the airway, activate massive transfusion and obtain immediate endovascular or surgical control. The decisive feature is exposed or pseudoaneurysmal carotid artery adjacent to tumour or infected wound. A major error is packing blindly and discharging after a self-limited sentinel bleed.","learningPoint":"Carotid blowout syndrome: exposed or pseudoaneurysmal carotid artery adjacent to tumour or infected wound. Avoid packing blindly and discharging after a self-limited sentinel bleed.","id":"vascular-surgery-097","globalNumber":2367,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Vascular surgery","categoryNumber":98,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for carotid blowout syndrome?","options":["bilateral lower-limb ischaemia with absent femoral pulses","painful Horner syndrome without anhidrosis or mural haematoma","exposed or pseudoaneurysmal carotid artery adjacent to tumour or infected wound","a contrast-filled aortic-wall outpouching with intramural haematoma and no classic flap","perigraft gas, fluid or fistulation beyond the expected postoperative period"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"exposed or pseudoaneurysmal carotid artery adjacent to tumour or infected wound","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is exposed or pseudoaneurysmal carotid artery adjacent to tumour or infected wound. In context, this supports Carotid blowout syndrome and should prompt clinicians to protect the airway, activate massive transfusion and obtain immediate endovascular or surgical control.","learningPoint":"Carotid blowout syndrome: exposed or pseudoaneurysmal carotid artery adjacent to tumour or infected wound. Avoid packing blindly and discharging after a self-limited sentinel bleed.","id":"vascular-surgery-098","globalNumber":2368,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Vascular surgery","categoryNumber":99,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in carotid blowout syndrome?","options":["treating recurrent bacteraemia without graft imaging","diagnosing cauda equina without checking pulses and vascular imaging","forceful neck manipulation or missing the diagnosis after minor trauma","packing blindly and discharging after a self-limited sentinel bleed","reassuring after transient haemodynamic improvement before free rupture"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"packing blindly and discharging after a self-limited sentinel bleed","explanation":"The hazardous action is packing blindly and discharging after a self-limited sentinel bleed. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is catastrophic haemorrhage, stroke and death.","learningPoint":"C