 for chance flexion-distraction fracture in this presentation?","options":["a type II odontoid fracture with high non-union risk","a burst fracture of the atlas with possible transverse-ligament disruption","a flexion-distraction spinal injury associated with a seat-belt mechanism","traumatic spondylolisthesis of C2 after a compatible mechanism","posterior vertebral-body wall disruption with canal compromise"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a flexion-distraction spinal injury associated with a seat-belt mechanism","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a flexion-distraction spinal injury associated with a seat-belt mechanism. In context, this supports Chance flexion-distraction fracture and should prompt clinicians to maintain spinal precautions and actively evaluate for intra-abdominal injury with specialist fixation planning.","learningPoint":"Chance flexion-distraction fracture: a flexion-distraction spinal injury associated with a seat-belt mechanism. Avoid treating it as a simple compression fracture and missing bowel injury.","id":"orthopaedic-118","globalNumber":2388,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":119,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in chance flexion-distraction fracture?","options":["clearing the cervical spine after normal plain films in an older high-risk patient","assuming preserved neurology means the fracture is stable","allowing mobilisation based solely on preserved initial motor power","treating it as a simple compression fracture and missing bowel injury","applying traction before defining transverse-ligament stability"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"treating it as a simple compression fracture and missing bowel injury","explanation":"The hazardous action is treating it as a simple compression fracture and missing bowel injury. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is unstable kyp