. A major error is discharging a non-weight-bearing patient because plain films are normal.","learningPoint":"Occult femoral neck fracture: persistent focal hip pain with MRI or CT evidence of an undisplaced femoral-neck fracture. Avoid discharging a non-weight-bearing patient because plain films are normal.","id":"orthopaedic-127","globalNumber":2397,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":128,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for occult femoral neck fracture?","options":["effusion plus systemic inflammatory features and inability to bear weight","a femoral shaft fracture capable of substantial concealed blood loss","persistent focal hip pain with MRI or CT evidence of an undisplaced femoral-neck fracture","sacral or pubic insufficiency fracture on CT or MRI after minimal trauma","Klein line fails to intersect the epiphysis"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"persistent focal hip pain with MRI or CT evidence of an undisplaced femoral-neck fracture","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is persistent focal hip pain with MRI or CT evidence of an undisplaced femoral-neck fracture. In context, this supports Occult femoral neck fracture and should prompt clinicians to maintain non-weight-bearing status and obtain urgent advanced imaging and orthopaedic management.","learningPoint":"Occult femoral neck fracture: persistent focal hip pain with MRI or CT evidence of an undisplaced femoral-neck fracture. Avoid discharging a non-weight-bearing patient because plain films are normal.","id":"orthopaedic-128","globalNumber":2398,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":129,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in occult femoral neck fracture?","options":["delaying reduction for nonessential imaging in an otherwise stabilised patient","assuming transient synovitis