ication to anticipate is compartment syndrome, vascular injury and post-traumatic arthritis. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of tibial plateau fracture with vascular risk.","learningPoint":"Tibial plateau fracture with vascular risk: a plateau fracture with high-energy pattern or neurovascular abnormality. Avoid allowing weight bearing because the initial radiograph appears minimally displaced.","id":"orthopaedic-140","globalNumber":2410,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":141,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with ankle pain after external rotation injury is accompanied by proximal fibular tenderness and syndesmotic instability. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Maisonneuve fracture","Talar neck fracture with threatened skin","Talar neck fracture","Lisfranc injury","Syndesmotic ankle injury"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Maisonneuve fracture","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Maisonneuve fracture. The key discriminator is proximal fibular fracture with distal tibiofibular syndesmosis disruption. Immediate priorities include: immobilise, keep non-weight-bearing and obtain orthopaedic fixation assessment.","learningPoint":"Maisonneuve fracture: proximal fibular fracture with distal tibiofibular syndesmosis disruption. Avoid examining only the ankle and missing the proximal fibula.","id":"orthopaedic-141","globalNumber":2411,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":142,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has maisonneuve fracture: ankle pain after external rotation injury is accompanied by proximal fibular tenderness and syndesmotic instability. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["reduce urgently when displaced and obtain orthopaedic fixation","immobilise, keep non-weight-bearing and obtain orthopaedic fix