":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":151,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by wrist trauma causes dorsal pain and widening between scaphoid and lunate, sometimes only on stress views. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Scapholunate ligament dissociation","Perilunate dislocation","Bennett fracture-dislocation","Rolando comminuted thumb-base fracture","Extensor pollicis longus rupture after distal radius fracture"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Scapholunate ligament dissociation","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Scapholunate ligament dissociation. The key discriminator is a widened scapholunate interval or abnormal scapholunate angle. Immediate priorities include: immobilise and arrange early hand-surgical assessment.","learningPoint":"Scapholunate ligament dissociation: a widened scapholunate interval or abnormal scapholunate angle. Avoid diagnosing a simple wrist sprain despite persistent focal instability.","id":"orthopaedic-151","globalNumber":2421,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":152,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is scapholunate ligament dissociation. The patient has wrist trauma causes dorsal pain and widening between scaphoid and lunate, sometimes only on stress views. What is the best immediate management?","options":["obtain urgent reduction and hand-surgical fixation","immobilise and arrange early hand-surgical assessment","immobilise and obtain hand-surgical tendon reconstruction assessment","immobilise the thumb and arrange repeat imaging or advanced imaging","reduce, immobilise in thumb spica and obtain hand-surgical fixation assessment"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"immobilise and arrange early hand-surgical assessment","explanation":"The priority is to immobilise and arrange early hand-surgical assessment. The decisive feature is a widened scapholunate interval or abnormal scapholunate angle. A major error is diagnosing a simple wrist sprain despite persistent focal instability.","learningPoint":"Scapholunate ligament dissociation: a widened scapholunate interval or abnormal scapholunate angle. Avoid diagnosing a simple wrist sprain despite persistent focal instability.","id":"orthopaedic-152","globalNumber":2422,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":153,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have scapholunate ligament dissociation. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["snuffbox or scaphoid-tubercle tenderness after axial thumb load","capitate displaced dorsally relative to the lunate on the lateral view","a widened scapholunate interval or abnormal scapholunate angle","loss of retropulsion with preserved thumb abduction after attritional tendon rupture","a small volar-ulnar fragment with displacement of the metacarpal shaft"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a widened scapholunate interval or abnormal scapholunate angle","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a widened scapholunate interval or abnormal scapholunate angle. In context, this supports Scapholunate ligament dissociation and should prompt clinicians to immobilise and arrange early hand-surgical assessment.","learningPoint":"Scapholunate ligament dissociation: a widened scapholunate interval or abnormal scapholunate angle. Avoid diagnosing a simple wrist sprain despite persistent focal instability.","id":"orthopaedic-153","globalNumber":2423,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":154,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with scapholunate ligament dissociation is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["accepting poor reduction because the fracture appears small","discharging without immobilisation because x-rays are normal","treating as a simple extra-articular thum