rtant consequence is post-traumatic arthritis and loss of pinch strength.","learningPoint":"Rolando comminuted thumb-base fracture: a Y- or T-shaped comminuted fracture with unstable carpometacarpal joint. Avoid accepting poor reduction because the fracture appears small.","id":"orthopaedic-164","globalNumber":2434,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":165,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with rolando comminuted thumb-base fracture initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["avascular necrosis and non-union","carpal collapse and degenerative wrist arthritis","chronic instability, weakness and carpometacarpal arthritis","median neuropathy, avascular necrosis and chronic instability","post-traumatic arthritis and loss of pinch strength"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"post-traumatic arthritis and loss of pinch strength","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is post-traumatic arthritis and loss of pinch strength. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of rolando comminuted thumb-base fracture.","learningPoint":"Rolando comminuted thumb-base fracture: a Y- or T-shaped comminuted fracture with unstable carpometacarpal joint. Avoid accepting poor reduction because the fracture appears small.","id":"orthopaedic-165","globalNumber":2435,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":166,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with forced extension of a flexed distal interphalangeal joint causes inability to flex the fingertip?","options":["Jersey finger","Pyogenic flexor tenosynovitis","Rolando comminuted thumb-base fracture","Bennett fracture-dislocation","Clenched-fist human-bite injury"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Jersey finger","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Jersey finger. The key discriminator is avulsion of the flexor digitorum profundus, often from the ring finger. Immediate priorities include: splint and arrange urgent hand-surgical repair.","learningPoint":"Jersey finger: avulsion of the flexor digitorum profundus, often from the ring finger. Avoid reassuring because passive motion is preserved or radiographs are normal.","id":"orthopaedic-166","globalNumber":2436,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":167,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with jersey finger presenting with forced extension of a flexed distal interphalangeal joint causes inability to flex the fingertip, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["splint the PIP in extension and arrange hand follow-up","splint and arrange urgent hand-surgical repair","give intravenous antibiotics and obtain urgent hand-surgical washout","explore with the joint flexed, irrigate, give antibiotics and obtain hand-surgical review","reduce, immobilise in thumb spica and obtain hand-surgical fixation assessment"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"splint and arrange urgent hand-surgical repair","explanation":"The priority is to splint and arrange urgent hand-surgical repair. The decisive feature is avulsion of the flexor digitorum profundus, often from the ring finger. A major error is reassuring because passive motion is preserved or radiographs are normal.","learningPoint":"Jersey finger: avulsion of the flexor digitorum profundus, often from the ring finger. Avoid reassuring because passive motion is preserved or radiographs are normal.","id":"orthopaedic-167","globalNumber":2437,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":168,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for jersey finger?","options":["a Y- or T-shaped comminuted fracture with unstable carpometacarpal joint","positive Elson testing indicating central-slip disruption","avulsion of the flexor digito