umber":172,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is mallet finger with volar subluxation. The patient has forced flexion of an extended fingertip causes extensor avulsion with a large articular fragment. What is the best immediate management?","options":["splint and arrange urgent hand-surgical repair","splint the DIP in extension and obtain hand-surgical assessment for unstable injury","splint the PIP in extension and arrange hand follow-up","immobilise and obtain urgent hand-surgical reconstruction assessment","explore with the joint flexed, irrigate, give antibiotics and obtain hand-surgical review"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"splint the DIP in extension and obtain hand-surgical assessment for unstable injury","explanation":"The priority is to splint the DIP in extension and obtain hand-surgical assessment for unstable injury. The decisive feature is distal phalanx avulsion involving substantial joint surface or volar subluxation. A major error is allowing the DIP to flex repeatedly during early treatment.","learningPoint":"Mallet finger with volar subluxation: distal phalanx avulsion involving substantial joint surface or volar subluxation. Avoid allowing the DIP to flex repeatedly during early treatment.","id":"orthopaedic-172","globalNumber":2442,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":173,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have mallet finger with volar subluxation. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["the four Kanavel signs","a small volar-ulnar fragment with displacement of the metacarpal shaft","distal phalanx avulsion involving substantial joint surface or volar subluxation","a Y- or T-shaped comminuted fracture with unstable carpometacarpal joint","deceptively small dorsal MCP wound communicating with tendon or joint"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"distal phalanx avulsion involving substantial joint surface or volar subluxation","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is distal phalanx avulsion involving substantial joint surface or volar subluxation. In context, this supports Mallet finger with volar subluxation and should prompt clinicians to splint the DIP in extension and obtain hand-surgical assessment for unstable injury.","learningPoint":"Mallet finger with volar subluxation: distal phalanx avulsion involving substantial joint surface or volar subluxation. Avoid allowing the DIP to flex repeatedly during early treatment.","id":"orthopaedic-173","globalNumber":2443,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":174,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with mallet finger with volar subluxation is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["reassuring because passive motion is preserved or radiographs are normal","splinting only the DIP or dismissing the injury as a simple sprain","treating with oral antibiotics and delayed clinic review","allowing the DIP to flex repeatedly during early treatment","treating as a simple extra-articular thumb fracture"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"allowing the DIP to flex repeatedly during early treatment","explanation":"The hazardous action is allowing the DIP to flex repeatedly during early treatment. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is fixed extensor lag and swan-neck deformity.","learningPoint":"Mallet finger with volar subluxation: distal phalanx avulsion involving substantial joint surface or volar subluxation. Avoid allowing the DIP to flex repeatedly during early treatment.","id":"orthopaedic-174","globalNumber":2444,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":175,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with mallet finger with volar subluxation?","options":["post-traumatic arthritis and loss of pinch strength","tendon necrosis, adhesions and deep-space infection","boutonnière deformity and stiffness","septic arthritis, tendon rupture and osteomyelitis","fixed extensor lag and swan-neck deformity"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"fixed extensor lag and swan-neck deformity","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is fixed extensor lag and swan-neck deformity. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of mallet finger with volar subluxation.","learningPoint":"Mallet finger with volar subluxation: distal phalanx avulsion involving substantial joint surface or volar subluxation. Avoid allowing the DIP to flex repeatedly during early treatment.","id":"orthopaedic-175","globalNumber":2445,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":176,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has a jammed or lacerated PIP joint develops inability to actively extend despite normal radiographs. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Central slip extensor injury","Bennett fracture-dislocation","Mallet finger with volar subluxation","Clenched-fist human-bite injury","Jersey finger"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Central slip extensor injury","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Central slip extensor injury. The key discriminator is positive Elson testing indicating central-slip disruption. Immediate priorities include: splint the PIP in extension and arrange hand follow-up.","learningPoint":"Central slip extensor injury: positive Elson testing indicating central-slip disruption. Avoid splinting only the DIP or dismissing the injury as a simple sprain.","id":"orthopaedic-176","globalNumber":2446,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":177,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for central slip extensor injury when the presentation is a jammed or lacerated PIP joint develops inability to actively extend despite normal radiographs?","options":["splint the DIP in extension and obtain hand-surgical assessment for unstable injury","splint the PIP in extension and arrange hand follow-up","immobilise and obtain urgent hand-surgical reconstruction assessment","give intravenous antibiotics and obtain urgent hand-surgical washout","reduce, immobilise in thumb spica and obtain hand-surgical fixation assessment"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"splint the PIP in extension and arrange hand follow-up","explanation":"The priority is to splint the PIP in extension and arrange hand follow-up. The decisive feature is positive Elson testing indicating central-slip disruption. A major error is splinting only the DIP or dismissing the injury as a simple sprain.","learningPoint":"Central slip extensor injury: positive Elson testing indicating central-slip disruption. Avoid splinting only the DIP or dismissing the injury as a simple sprain.","id":"orthopaedic-177","globalNumber":2447,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":178,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for central slip extensor injury in this presentation?","options":["avulsion of the flexor digitorum profundus, often from the ring finger","the four Kanavel signs","positive Elson testing indicating central-slip disruption","a small volar-ulnar fragment with displacement of the metacarpal shaft","deceptively small dorsal MCP wound communicating with tendon or joint"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"positive Elson testing indicating central-slip disruption","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is positive Elson testing indicating central-slip disruption. In context, this supports Central slip extensor injury and should prompt clinicians to splint the PIP in extension and arrange hand follow-up.","learningPoint":"Central slip extensor injury: positive Elson testing indicating central-slip disruption. Avoid splinting only the DIP or dismissing the injury as a simple sprain.","id":"orthopaedic-178","globalNumber":2448,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":179,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in central slip extensor injury?","options":["closing the wound primarily without exploration","accepting poor reduction because the fracture appears small","treating as a simple extra-articular thumb fracture","splinting only the DIP or dismissing the injury as a simple sprain","treating with oral antibiotics and delayed clinic review"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"splinting only the DIP or dismissing the injury as a simple sprain","explanation":"The hazardous action is splinting only the DIP or dismissing the injury as a simple sprain. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is boutonnière deformity and stiffness.","learningPoint":"Central slip extensor injury: positive Elson testing indicating central-slip disruption. Avoid splinting only the DIP or dismissing the injury as a simple sprain.","id":"orthopaedic-179","globalNumber":2449,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":180,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in central slip extensor injury, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["fixed extensor lag and swan-neck deformity","tendon retraction and permanent loss of distal flexion","septic arthritis, tendon rupture and osteomyelitis","post-traumatic arthritis and loss of pinch strength","boutonnière deformity and stiffness"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"boutonnière deformity and stiffness","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is boutonnière deformity and stiffness. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of central slip extensor injury.","learningPoint":"Central slip extensor injury: positive Elson testing indicating central-slip disruption. Avoid splinting only the DIP or dismissing the injury as a simple sprain.","id":"orthopaedic-180","globalNumber":2450,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":181,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with delayed inability to extend the thumb occurs weeks after a seemingly minor distal radius fracture. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Extensor pollicis longus rupture after distal radius fracture","Perilunate dislocation","Rolando comminuted thumb-base fracture","Scapholunate ligament dissociation","Occult scaphoid fracture"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Extensor pollicis longus rupture after distal radius fracture","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Extensor pollicis longus rupture after distal radius fracture. The key discriminator is loss of retropulsion with preserved thumb abduction after attritional tendon rupture. Immediate priorities include: immobilise and obtain hand-surgical tendon reconstruction assessment.","learningPoint":"Extensor pollicis longus rupture after distal radius fracture: loss of retropulsion with preserved thumb abduction after attritional tendon rupture. Avoid attributing weakness to pain without testing isolated thumb extension.","id":"orthopaedic-181","globalNumber":2451,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":182,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has extensor pollicis longus rupture after distal radius fracture: delayed inability to extend the thumb occurs weeks after a seemingly minor distal radius fracture. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["immobilise and arrange early hand-surgical assessment","immobilise and obtain hand-surgical tendon reconstruction assessment","reduce, immobilise in thumb spica and obtain hand-surgical fixation assessment","obtain urgent reduction and hand-surgical fixation","immobilise the thumb and arrange repeat imaging or advanced imaging"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"immobilise and obtain hand-surgical tendon reconstruction assessment","explanation":"The priority is to immobilise and obtain hand-surgical tendon reconstruction assessment. The decisive feature is loss of retropulsion with preserved thumb abduction after attritional tendon rupture. A major error is attributing weakness to pain without testing isolated thumb extension.","learningPoint":"Extensor pollicis longus rupture after distal radius fracture: loss of retropulsion with preserved thumb abduction after attritional tendon rupture. Avoid attributing weakness to pain without testing isolated thumb extension.","id":"orthopaedic-182","globalNumber":2452,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":183,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected extensor pollicis longus rupture after distal radius fracture, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["a Y- or T-shaped comminuted fracture with unstable carpometacarpal joint","a small volar-ulnar fragment with displacement of the metacarpal shaft","loss of retropulsion with preserved thumb abduction after attritional tendon rupture","capitate displaced dorsally relative to the lunate on the lateral view","a widened scapholunate interval or abnormal scapholunate angle"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"loss of retropulsion with preserved thumb abduction after attritional tendon rupture","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is loss of retropulsion with preserved thumb abduction after attritional tendon rupture. In context, this supports Extensor pollicis longus rupture after distal radius fracture and should prompt clinicians to immobilise and obtain hand-surgical tendon reconstruction assessment.","learningPoint":"Extensor pollicis longus rupture after distal radius fracture: loss of retropulsion with preserved thumb abduction after attritional tendon rupture. Avoid attributing weakness to pain without testing isolated thumb extension.","id":"orthopaedic-183","globalNumber":2453,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":184,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of extensor pollicis longus rupture after distal radius fracture, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["labelling the radiograph as a simple wrist sprain","discharging without immobilisation because x-rays are normal","treating as a simple extra-articular thumb fracture","attributing weakness to pain without testing isolated thumb extension","diagnosing a simple wrist sprain despite persistent focal instability"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"attributing weakness to pain without testing isolated thumb extension","explanation":"The hazardous action is attributing weakness to pain without testing isolated thumb extension. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is permanent loss of thumb extension and functional impairment.","learningPoint":"Extensor pollicis longus rupture after distal radius fracture: loss of retropulsion with preserved thumb abduction after attritional tendon rupture. Avoid attributing weakness to pain without testing isolated thumb extension.","id":"orthopaedic-184","globalNumber":2454,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":185,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with extensor pollicis longus rupture after distal radius fracture initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["avascular necrosis and non-union","carpal collapse and degenerative wrist arthritis","post-traumatic arthritis and loss of pinch strength","chronic instability, weakness and carpometacarpal arthritis","permanent loss of thumb extension and functional impairment"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"permanent loss of thumb extension and functional impairment","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is permanent loss of thumb extension and functional impairment. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of extensor pollicis longus rupture after distal radius fracture.","learningPoint":"Extensor pollicis longus rupture after distal radius fracture: loss of retropulsion with preserved thumb abduction after attritional tendon rupture. Avoid attributing weakness to pain without testing isolated thumb extension.","id":"orthopaedic-185","globalNumber":2455,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":186,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with fever and focal bone pain persist with raised inflammatory markers and a deep collection?","options":["Osteomyelitis with subperiosteal abscess","Septic hip in a child","Pyogenic flexor tenosynovitis","Open fracture","Clenched-fist human-bite injury"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Osteomyelitis with subperiosteal abscess","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Osteomyelitis with subperiosteal abscess. The key discriminator is MRI evidence of marrow infection with a drainable subperiosteal abscess. Immediate priorities include: give antibiotics and obtain urgent orthopaedic drainage.","learningPoint":"Osteomyelitis with subperiosteal abscess: MRI evidence of marrow infection with a drainable subperiosteal abscess. Avoid treating with oral antibiotics alone despite a drainable abscess.","id":"orthopaedic-186","globalNumber":2456,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":187,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a patient with osteomyelitis with subperiosteal abscess presenting with fever and focal bone pain persist with raised inflammatory markers and a deep collection, which intervention should be prioritised?","options":["give early intravenous antibiotics, tetanus prophylaxis, sterile coverage and urgent debridement","give antibiotics and obtain urgent orthopaedic drainage","make non-weight-bearing and arrange urgent in-situ fixation","give intravenous antibiotics and obtain urgent hand-surgical washout","obtain urgent ultrasound-guided aspiration and surgical washout with antibiotics"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give antibiotics and obtain urgent orthopaedic drainage","explanation":"The priority is to give antibiotics and obtain urgent orthopaedic drainage. The decisive feature is MRI evidence of marrow infection with a drainable subperiosteal abscess. A major error is treating with oral antibiotics alone despite a drainable abscess.","learningPoint":"Osteomyelitis with subperiosteal abscess: MRI evidence of marrow infection with a drainable subperiosteal abscess. Avoid treating with oral antibiotics alone despite a drainable abscess.","id":"orthopaedic-187","globalNumber":2457,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":188,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for osteomyelitis with subperiosteal abscess?","options":["the four Kanavel signs","effusion plus systemic inflammatory features and inability to bear weight","MRI evidence of marrow infection with a drainable subperiosteal abscess","purulent fluid with high synovial neutrophils, noting crystals do not exclude infection","absent pulse or poor perfusion after an extension-type fracture"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"MRI evidence of marrow infection with a drainable subperiosteal abscess","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is MRI evidence of marrow infection with a drainable subperiosteal abscess. In context, this supports Osteomyelitis with subperiosteal abscess and should prompt clinicians to give antibiotics and obtain urgent orthopaedic drainage.","learningPoint":"Osteomyelitis with subperiosteal abscess: MRI evidence of marrow infection with a drainable subperiosteal abscess. Avoid treating with oral antibiotics alone despite a drainable abscess.","id":"orthopaedic-188","globalNumber":2458,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":189,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in osteomyelitis with subperiosteal abscess?","options":["delaying aspiration because radiographs are normal or crystals are present","assuming transient synovitis in a toxic child","treating with oral antibiotics and delayed clinic review","treating with oral antibiotics alone despite a drainable abscess","forcing range of motion or allowing weight bearing"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"treating with oral antibiotics alone despite a drainable abscess","explanation":"The hazardous action is treating with oral antibiotics alone despite a drainable abscess. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is sepsis, growth-plate injury and chronic osteomyelitis.","learningPoint":"Osteomyelitis with subperiosteal abscess: MRI evidence of marrow infection with a drainable subperiosteal abscess. Avoid treating with oral antibiotics alone despite a drainable abscess.","id":"orthopaedic-189","globalNumber":2459,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":190,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of osteomyelitis with subperiosteal abscess, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["cartilage destruction, osteomyelitis and sepsis","femoral-head damage, sepsis and growth disturbance","sepsis, implant failure and chronic biofilm infection","avascular necrosis and chondrolysis","sepsis, growth-plate injury and chronic osteomyelitis"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"sepsis, growth-plate injury and chronic osteomyelitis","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is sepsis, growth-plate injury and chronic osteomyelitis. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of osteomyelitis with subperiosteal abscess.","learningPoint":"Osteomyelitis with subperiosteal abscess: MRI evidence of marrow infection with a drainable subperiosteal abscess. Avoid treating with oral antibiotics alone despite a drainable abscess.","id":"orthopaedic-190","globalNumber":2460,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":191,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by a patient with joint arthroplasty develops acute pain, fever or loosening with an inflammatory effusion. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Prosthetic joint infection","Septic hip in a child","Pyogenic flexor tenosynovitis","Septic arthritis","Osteomyelitis with subperiosteal abscess"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Prosthetic joint infection","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Prosthetic joint infection. The key discriminator is purulent aspirate or elevated synovial inflammatory markers around a prosthesis. Immediate priorities include: aspirate before antibiotics when stable, then give appropriate therapy and obtain urgent orthopaedic washout planning.","learningPoint":"Prosthetic joint infection: purulent aspirate or elevated synovial inflammatory markers around a prosthesis. Avoid assuming normal radiographs or low peripheral white count exclude infection.","id":"orthopaedic-191","globalNumber":2461,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":192,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is prosthetic joint infection. The patient has a patient with joint arthroplasty develops acute pain, fever or loosening with an inflammatory effusion. What is the best immediate management?","options":["aspirate urgently, send synovial studies, start antibiotics after cultures and arrange washout","aspirate before antibiotics when stable, then give appropriate therapy and obtain urgent orthopaedic washout planning","explore with the joint flexed, irrigate, give antibiotics and obtain hand-surgical review","obtain urgent ultrasound-guided aspiration and surgical washout with antibiotics","give early intravenous antibiotics, tetanus prophylaxis, sterile coverage and urgent debridement"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"aspirate before antibiotics when stable, then give appropriate therapy and obtain urgent orthopaedic washout planning","explanation":"The priority is to aspirate before antibiotics when stable, then give appropriate therapy and obtain urgent orthopaedic washout planning. The decisive feature is purulent aspirate or elevated synovial inflammatory markers around a prosthesis. A major error is assuming normal radiographs or low peripheral white count exclude infection.","learningPoint":"Prosthetic joint infection: purulent aspirate or elevated synovial inflammatory markers around a prosthesis. Avoid assuming normal radiographs or low peripheral white count exclude infection.","id":"orthopaedic-192","globalNumber":2462,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":193,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have prosthetic joint infection. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["purulent fluid with high synovial neutrophils, noting crystals do not exclude infection","the four Kanavel signs","purulent aspirate or elevated synovial inflammatory markers around a prosthesis","effusion plus systemic inflammatory features and inability to bear weight","MRI evidence of marrow infection with a drainable subperiosteal abscess"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"purulent aspirate or elevated synovial inflammatory markers around a prosthesis","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is purulent aspirate or elevated synovial inflammatory markers around a prosthesis. In context, this supports Prosthetic joint infection and should prompt clinicians to aspirate before antibiotics when stable, then give appropriate therapy and obtain urgent orthopaedic washout planning.","learningPoint":"Prosthetic joint infection: purulent aspirate or elevated synovial inflammatory markers around a prosthesis. Avoid assuming normal radiographs or low peripheral white count exclude infection.","id":"orthopaedic-193","globalNumber":2463,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":194,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with prosthetic joint infection is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["treating with oral antibiotics and delayed clinic review","assuming transient synovitis in a toxic child","delaying aspiration because radiographs are normal or crystals are present","assuming normal radiographs or low peripheral white count exclude infection","closing the wound primarily without exploration"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"assuming normal radiographs or low peripheral white count exclude infection","explanation":"The hazardous action is assuming normal radiographs or low peripheral white count exclude infection. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is sepsis, implant failure and chronic biofilm infection.","learningPoint":"Prosthetic joint infection: purulent aspirate or elevated synovial inflammatory markers around a prosthesis. Avoid assuming normal radiographs or low peripheral white count exclude infection.","id":"orthopaedic-194","globalNumber":2464,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":195,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with prosthetic joint infection?","options":["tendon necrosis, adhesions and deep-space infection","femoral-head damage, sepsis and growth disturbance","septic arthritis, tendon rupture and osteomyelitis","cartilage destruction, osteomyelitis and sepsis","sepsis, implant failure and chronic biofilm infection"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"sepsis, implant failure and chronic biofilm infection","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is sepsis, implant failure and chronic biofilm infection. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of prosthetic joint infection.","learningPoint":"Prosthetic joint infection: purulent aspirate or elevated synovial inflammatory markers around a prosthesis. Avoid assuming normal radiographs or low peripheral white count exclude infection.","id":"orthopaedic-195","globalNumber":2465,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":196,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has an older osteoporotic patient has severe pelvic or groin pain and inability to mobilise despite normal radiographs. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Pelvic insufficiency fracture","Thoracolumbar burst fracture with canal compromise","Slipped capital femoral epiphysis","Posterior hip dislocation","Jefferson burst fracture of C1"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Pelvic insufficiency fracture","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Pelvic insufficiency fracture. The key discriminator is sacral or pubic insufficiency fracture on CT or MRI after minimal trauma. Immediate priorities include: provide analgesia, protected mobilisation and evaluate bone health and associated fracture patterns.","learningPoint":"Pelvic insufficiency fracture: sacral or pubic insufficiency fracture on CT or MRI after minimal trauma. Avoid discharging a non-ambulant patient because plain films are normal.","id":"orthopaedic-196","globalNumber":2466,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":197,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for pelvic insufficiency fracture when the presentation is an older osteoporotic patient has severe pelvic or groin pain and inability to mobilise despite normal radiographs?","options":["maintain spinal precautions and actively evaluate for intra-abdominal injury with specialist fixation planning","provide analgesia, protected mobilisation and evaluate bone health and associated fracture patterns","perform urgent reduction, ideally within six hours, after excluding associated priorities","maintain rigid immobilisation and obtain urgent CT, MRI and spinal specialist assessment","obtain urgent ultrasound-guided aspiration and surgical washout with antibiotics"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"provide analgesia, protected mobilisation and evaluate bone health and associated fracture patterns","explanation":"The priority is to provide analgesia, protected mobilisation and evaluate bone health and associated fracture patterns. The decisive feature is sacral or pubic insufficiency fracture on CT or MRI after minimal trauma. A major error is discharging a non-ambulant patient because plain films are normal.","learningPoint":"Pelvic insufficiency fracture: sacral or pubic insufficiency fracture on CT or MRI after minimal trauma. Avoid discharging a non-ambulant patient because plain films are normal.","id":"orthopaedic-197","globalNumber":2467,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":198,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for pelvic insufficiency fracture in this presentation?","options":["a femoral shaft fracture capable of substantial concealed blood loss","posterior vertebral-body wall disruption with canal compromise","sacral or pubic insufficiency fracture on CT or MRI after minimal trauma","Klein line fails to intersect the epiphysis","a type II odontoid fracture with high non-union risk"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"sacral or pubic insufficiency fracture on CT or MRI after minimal trauma","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is sacral or pubic insufficiency fracture on CT or MRI after minimal trauma. In context, this supports Pelvic insufficiency fracture and should prompt clinicians to provide analgesia, protected mobilisation and evaluate bone health and associated fracture patterns.","learningPoint":"Pelvic insufficiency fracture: sacral or pubic insufficiency fracture on CT or MRI after minimal trauma. Avoid discharging a non-ambulant patient because plain films are normal.","id":"orthopaedic-198","globalNumber":2468,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":199,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in pelvic insufficiency fracture?","options":["treating it as a simple compression fracture and missing bowel injury","delaying reduction for nonessential imaging in an otherwise stabilised patient","applying traction before defining transverse-ligament stability","discharging a non-ambulant patient because plain films are normal","assuming transient synovitis in a toxic child"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"discharging a non-ambulant patient because plain films are normal","explanation":"The hazardous action is discharging a non-ambulant patient because plain films are normal. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is immobility complications and fracture progression.","learningPoint":"Pelvic insufficiency fracture: sacral or pubic insufficiency fracture on CT or MRI after minimal trauma. Avoid discharging a non-ambulant patient because plain films are normal.","id":"orthopaedic-199","globalNumber":2469,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Orthopaedic","categoryNumber":200,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in pelvic insufficiency fracture, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["delayed neurological deterioration and spinal deformity","progressive displacement, cord injury and vertebral artery injury","avascular necrosis and sciatic nerve injury","haemorrhagic shock, fat embolism and compartment syndrome","immobility complications and fracture progression"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"immobility complications and fracture progression","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is immobility complications and fracture progression. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of pelvic insufficiency fracture.","learningPoint":"Pelvic insufficiency fracture: sacral or pubic insufficiency fracture on CT or MRI after minimal trauma. Avoid discharging a non-ambulant patient because plain films are normal.","id":"orthopaedic-200","globalNumber":2470,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Head and Neck","categoryNumber":101,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with fever, painful restricted eye movements, proptosis and reduced vision after sinusitis. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Orbital cellulitis","Lemierre syndrome","Acute invasive fungal rhinosinusitis","Acute mastoiditis","Cavernous sinus thrombosis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Orbital cellulitis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Orbital cellulitis. The key discriminator is post-septal inflammation with ophthalmoplegia or orbital abscess. Immediate priorities include: give intravenous antibiotics and obtain urgent ENT and ophthalmology assessment for drainage.","learningPoint":"Orbital cellulitis: post-septal inflammation with ophthalmoplegia or orbital abscess. Avoid treating as preseptal cellulitis when eye movements are painful or vision is affected.","id":"head-and-neck-101","globalNumber":2471,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Head and Neck","categoryNumber":102,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has orbital cellulitis: fever, painful restricted eye movements, proptosis and reduced vision after sinusitis. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["give antipseudomonal therapy and obtain urgent ENT and skull-base imaging","give intravenous antibiotics and obtain urgent ENT and ophthalmology assessment for drainage","start urgent antifungal therapy and obtain immediate surgical debridement","give broad intravenous antibiotics and obtain urgent ENT, neurology and anticoagulation advice","give prolonged intravenous antibiotics and obtain ENT and critical-care source-control advice"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give intravenous antibiotics and obtain urgent ENT and ophthalmology assessment for drainage","explanation":"The priority is to give intravenous antibiotics and obtain urgent ENT and ophthalmology assessment for drainage. The decisive feature is post-septal inflammation with ophthalmoplegia or orbital abscess. A major error is treating as preseptal cellulitis when eye movements are painful or vision is affected.","learningPoint":"Orbital cellulitis: post-septal inflammation with ophthalmoplegia or orbital abscess. Avoid treating as preseptal cellulitis when eye movements are painful or vision is affected.","id":"head-and-neck-102","globalNumber":2472,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Head and Neck","categoryNumber":103,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected orbital cellulitis, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["internal jugular vein thrombophlebitis with Fusobacterium infection","rapid tissue necrosis crossing sinus boundaries with fungal invasion","post-septal inflammation with ophthalmoplegia or orbital abscess","afferent pupillary defect, reduced acuity and markedly raised intraocular pressure","bilateral or progressive cranial nerve III, IV, V1, V2 or VI dysfunction with cavernous sinus thrombosis"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"post-septal inflammation with ophthalmoplegia or orbital abscess","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is post-septal inflammation with ophthalmoplegia or orbital abscess. In context, this supports Orbital cellulitis and should prompt clinicians to give intravenous anti