t another ocular explanation. Avoid assuming a normal fundus excludes serious optic-nerve injury.","id":"head-and-neck-192","globalNumber":2562,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Head and Neck","categoryNumber":193,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have traumatic optic neuropathy. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["arterialised conjunctival vessels and abnormal cavernous sinus flow","mobile vitreous echoes or haemorrhage with symptoms concerning for retinal tear","optic-nerve dysfunction after trauma without another ocular explanation","rapid tissue necrosis crossing sinus boundaries with fungal invasion","afferent pupillary defect, reduced acuity and markedly raised intraocular pressure"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"optic-nerve dysfunction after trauma without another ocular explanation","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is optic-nerve dysfunction after trauma without another ocular explanation. In context, this supports Traumatic optic neuropathy and should prompt clinicians to protect associated injuries and obtain urgent neuro-ophthalmic and orbital imaging assessment.","learningPoint":"Traumatic optic neuropathy: optic-nerve dysfunction after trauma without another ocular explanation. Avoid assuming a normal fundus excludes serious optic-nerve injury.","id":"head-and-neck-193","globalNumber":2563,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Head and Neck","categoryNumber":194,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with traumatic optic neuropathy is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["delaying irrigation to identify the chemical or measure visual acuity","diagnosing posterior vitreous detachment without checking the peripheral retina","reassuring because visual acuity is partly preserved","assuming a normal fundus excludes serious optic-nerve injury","waiting for CT or specialist arrival before decompression"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"assuming a normal fundus excludes serious optic-nerve injury","explanation":"The hazardous action is assuming a normal fundus excludes serious optic-nerve injury. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is permanent monocular blindness.","learningPoint":"Traumatic optic neuropathy: optic-nerve dysfunction after trauma without another ocular explanation. Avoid assuming a normal fundus excludes serious optic-nerve injury.","id":"head-and-neck-194","globalNumber":2564,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Head and Neck","categoryNumber":195,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with traumatic optic neuropathy?","options":["retinal detachment and permanent visual loss","meningitis, stroke and blindness","permanent central visual loss","irreversible optic nerve ischaemia and blindness","permanent monocular blindness"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"permanent monocular blindness","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is permanent monocular blindness. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of traumatic optic neuropathy.","learningPoint":"Traumatic optic neuropathy: optic-nerve dysfunction after trauma without another ocular explanation. Avoid assuming a normal fundus excludes serious optic-nerve injury.","id":"head-and-neck-195","globalNumber":2565,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Head and Neck","categoryNumber":196,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has an immunocompromised patient has facial pain, fever, black nasal tissue or cranial neuropathy. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Acute invasive fungal rhinosinusitis","Acute mastoiditis","Cavernous sinus thrombosis","Orbital compartment syndrome","Orbital cellulitis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Acute invasive fungal rhinosinusitis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Acute invasive fungal rhinosinusitis. The key discriminator is rapid tissue necrosis crossing sinus boundaries with fungal invasion. Immediate priorities include: start urgent antifungal therapy and obtain immediate surgical debridement.","learningPoint":"Acute invasive fungal rhinosinusitis: rapid tissue necrosis crossing sinus boundaries with fungal invasion. Avoid waiting for culture confirmation or treating as routine bacterial sinusitis.","id":"head-and-neck-196","globalNumber":2566,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Head and Neck","categoryNumber":197,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for acute invasive fungal rhinosinusitis when the presentation is an immunocompromised patient has facial pain, fever, black nasal tissue or cranial neuropathy?","options":["perform immediate lateral canthotomy and inferior cantholysis","start urgent antifungal therapy and obtain immediate surgical debridement","protect vision and obtain urgent neurointerventional closure","give intravenous antibiotics and obtain urgent ENT and ophthalmology assessment for drainage","give broad intravenous antibiotics and obtain urgent ENT, neurology and anticoagulation advice"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"start urgent antifungal therapy and obtain immediate surgical debridement","explanation":"The priority is to start urgent antifungal therapy and obtain immediate surgical debridement. The decisive feature is rapid tissue necrosis crossing sinus boundaries with fungal invasion. A major error is waiting for culture confirmation or treating as routine bacterial sinusitis.","learningPoint":"Acute invasive fungal rhinosinusitis: rapid tissue necrosis crossing sinus boundaries with fungal invasion. Avoid waiting for culture confirmation or treating as routine bacterial sinusitis.","id":"head-and-neck-197","globalNumber":2567,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Head and Neck","categoryNumber":198,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for acute invasive fungal rhinosinusitis in this presentation?","options":["afferent pupillary defect, reduced acuity and markedly raised intraocular pressure","bilateral or progressive cranial nerve III, IV, V1, V2 or VI dysfunction with cavernous sinus thrombosis","rapid tissue necrosis crossing sinus boundaries with fungal invasion","post-septal inflammation with ophthalmoplegia or orbital abscess","arterialised conjunctival vessels and abnormal cavernous sinus flow"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"rapid tissue necrosis crossing sinus boundaries with fungal invasion","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is rapid tissue necrosis crossing sinus boundaries with fungal invasion. In context, this supports Acute invasive fungal rhinosinusitis and should prompt clinicians to start urgent antifungal therapy and obtain immediate surgical debridement.","learningPoint":"Acute invasive fungal rhinosinusitis: rapid tissue necrosis crossing sinus boundaries with fungal invasion. Avoid waiting for culture confirmation or treating as routine bacterial sinusitis.","id":"head-and-neck-198","globalNumber":2568,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Head and Neck","categoryNumber":199,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in acute invasive fungal rhinosinusitis?","options":["treating isolated sixth-nerve palsy without investigating septic cavernous disease","waiting for CT or specialist arrival before decompression","treating persistent red eye after trauma as conjunctivitis","waiting for culture confirmation or treating as routine bacterial sinusitis","treating as preseptal cellulitis when eye movements are painful or vision is affected"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"waiting for culture confirmation or treating as routine bacterial sinusitis","explanation":"The hazardous action is waiting for culture confirmation or treating as routine bacterial sinusitis. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is cavernous sinus invasion, stroke and death.","learningPoint":"Acute invasive fungal rhinosinusitis: rapid tissue necrosis crossing sinus boundaries with fungal invasion. Avoid waiting for culture confirmation or treating as routine bacterial sinusitis.","id":"head-and-neck-199","globalNumber":2569,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Head and Neck","categoryNumber":200,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in acute invasive fungal rhinosinusitis, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["permanent monocular blindness","meningitis, stroke and blindness","skull-base osteomyelitis and cranial nerve palsies","subperiosteal abscess, cavernous sinus thrombosis and blindness","cavernous sinus invasion, stroke and death"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"cavernous sinus invasion, stroke and death","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is cavernous sinus invasion, stroke and death. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of acute invasive fungal rhinosinusitis.","learningPoint":"Acute invasive fungal rhinosinusitis: rapid tissue necrosis crossing sinus boundaries with fungal invasion. Avoid waiting for culture confirmation or treating as routine bacterial sinusitis.","id":"head-and-neck-200","globalNumber":2570,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":101,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with a pregnant patient has an intrauterine pregnancy but develops shock, pain and haemoperitoneum after assisted reproduction. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Heterotopic pregnancy","Interstitial ectopic pregnancy","Ovarian cyst rupture with haemoperitoneum","Septic abortion","Ruptured ectopic pregnancy"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Heterotopic pregnancy","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Heterotopic pregnancy. The key discriminator is simultaneous intrauterine and extrauterine gestations. Immediate priorities include: activate haemorrhage resuscitation and obtain urgent operative management of the ectopic pregnancy.","learningPoint":"Heterotopic pregnancy: simultaneous intrauterine and extrauterine gestations. Avoid excluding ectopic pregnancy solely because an intrauterine pregnancy is visible.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-101","globalNumber":2571,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":102,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has heterotopic pregnancy: a pregnant patient has an intrauterine pregnancy but develops shock, pain and haemoperitoneum after assisted reproduction. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["obtain urgent specialist treatment with haemorrhage planning","activate haemorrhage resuscitation and obtain urgent operative management of the ectopic pregnancy","obtain urgent specialist medical or surgical treatment before rupture","give broad intravenous antibiotics and arrange urgent uterine evacuation and source control","activate haemorrhage resuscitation and obtain immediate operative gynaecologic control"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"activate haemorrhage resuscitation and obtain urgent operative management of the ectopic pregnancy","explanation":"The priority is to activate haemorrhage resuscitation and obtain urgent operative management of the ectopic pregnancy. The decisive feature is simultaneous intrauterine and extrauterine gestations. A major error is excluding ectopic pregnancy solely because an intrauterine pregnancy is visible.","learningPoint":"Heterotopic pregnancy: simultaneous intrauterine and extrauterine gestations. Avoid excluding ectopic pregnancy solely because an intrauterine pregnancy is visible.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-102","globalNumber":2572,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":103,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In suspected heterotopic pregnancy, which finding is the most discriminating?","options":["empty endometrial cavity with gestational tissue in the caesarean scar and thin anterior myometrium","haemoperitoneum from a ruptured haemorrhagic ovarian cyst without ectopic pregnancy","simultaneous intrauterine and extrauterine gestations","infected retained products with systemic toxicity","positive pregnancy test with no intrauterine pregnancy and haemoperitoneum"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"simultaneous intrauterine and extrauterine gestations","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is simultaneous intrauterine and extrauterine gestations. In context, this supports Heterotopic pregnancy and should prompt clinicians to activate haemorrhage resuscitation and obtain urgent operative management of the ectopic pregnancy.","learningPoint":"Heterotopic pregnancy: simultaneous intrauterine and extrauterine gestations. Avoid excluding ectopic pregnancy solely because an intrauterine pregnancy is visible.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-103","globalNumber":2573,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":104,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"During emergency management of heterotopic pregnancy, which action is most hazardous or most likely to delay definitive care?","options":["giving antibiotics without evacuating infected tissue","assuming all cyst rupture is self-limiting despite ongoing shock","misclassifying the pregnancy as a normal eccentric intrauterine implantation","excluding ectopic pregnancy solely because an intrauterine pregnancy is visible","performing blind curettage without recognising scar implantation"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"excluding ectopic pregnancy solely because an intrauterine pregnancy is visible","explanation":"The hazardous action is excluding ectopic pregnancy solely because an intrauterine pregnancy is visible. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is maternal exsanguination and loss of one or both pregnancies.","learningPoint":"Heterotopic pregnancy: simultaneous intrauterine and extrauterine gestations. Avoid excluding ectopic pregnancy solely because an intrauterine pregnancy is visible.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-104","globalNumber":2574,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":105,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with heterotopic pregnancy initially stabilises. Which serious complication should be specifically anticipated?","options":["exsanguination and ovarian loss","haemorrhage, thyrotoxicosis and gestational trophoblastic neoplasia","septic shock, DIC and infertility","exsanguination and disseminated coagulopathy","maternal exsanguination and loss of one or both pregnancies"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"maternal exsanguination and loss of one or both pregnancies","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is maternal exsanguination and loss of one or both pregnancies. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of heterotopic pregnancy.","learningPoint":"Heterotopic pregnancy: simultaneous intrauterine and extrauterine gestations. Avoid excluding ectopic pregnancy solely because an intrauterine pregnancy is visible.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-105","globalNumber":2575,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":106,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which diagnosis is most likely in a patient with early pregnancy pain and bleeding with a gestational sac implanted laterally in the uterine cornua?","options":["Interstitial ectopic pregnancy","Heterotopic pregnancy","Ruptured ectopic pregnancy","Caesarean-scar ectopic pregnancy","Ovarian cyst rupture with haemoperitoneum"],"answ