id misclassifying the pregnancy as a normal eccentric intrauterine implantation.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-107","globalNumber":2577,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":108,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which feature should carry the greatest diagnostic weight when assessing for interstitial ectopic pregnancy?","options":["empty endometrial cavity with gestational tissue in the caesarean scar and thin anterior myometrium","simultaneous intrauterine and extrauterine gestations","a sac separate from the endometrial cavity with a thin myometrial mantle and interstitial-line sign","uterine size greater than dates with markedly elevated hCG","haemoperitoneum from a ruptured haemorrhagic ovarian cyst without ectopic pregnancy"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"a sac separate from the endometrial cavity with a thin myometrial mantle and interstitial-line sign","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is a sac separate from the endometrial cavity with a thin myometrial mantle and interstitial-line sign. In context, this supports Interstitial ectopic pregnancy and should prompt clinicians to obtain urgent specialist medical or surgical treatment before rupture.","learningPoint":"Interstitial ectopic pregnancy: a sac separate from the endometrial cavity with a thin myometrial mantle and interstitial-line sign. Avoid misclassifying the pregnancy as a normal eccentric intrauterine implantation.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-108","globalNumber":2578,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":109,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management error should be specifically avoided in interstitial ectopic pregnancy?","options":["excluding ectopic pregnancy solely because an intrauterine pregnancy is visible","giving antibiotics without evacuating infected tissue","performing blind curettage without recognising scar implantation","misclassifying the pregnancy as a normal eccentric intrauterine implantation","delaying surgery for serial hCG in an unstable patient"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"misclassifying the pregnancy as a normal eccentric intrauterine implantation","explanation":"The hazardous action is misclassifying the pregnancy as a normal eccentric intrauterine implantation. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is catastrophic haemorrhage from late rupture.","learningPoint":"Interstitial ectopic pregnancy: a sac separate from the endometrial cavity with a thin myometrial mantle and interstitial-line sign. Avoid misclassifying the pregnancy as a normal eccentric intrauterine implantation.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-109","globalNumber":2579,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":110,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of interstitial ectopic pregnancy, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["exsanguination and ovarian loss","haemorrhage, thyrotoxicosis and gestational trophoblastic neoplasia","exsanguination and disseminated coagulopathy","maternal exsanguination and loss of one or both pregnancies","catastrophic haemorrhage from late rupture"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"catastrophic haemorrhage from late rupture","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is catastrophic haemorrhage from late rupture. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of interstitial ectopic pregnancy.","learningPoint":"Interstitial ectopic pregnancy: a sac separate from the endometrial cavity with a thin myometrial mantle and interstitial-line sign. Avoid misclassifying the pregnancy as a normal eccentric intrauterine implantation.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-110","globalNumber":2580,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":111,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by a patient with previous caesarean delivery has an early gestation embedded in the anterior lower uterine segment. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Caesarean-scar ectopic pregnancy","Heterotopic pregnancy","Uterine rupture","Placenta accreta spectrum haemorrhage","Hydatidiform mole"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Caesarean-scar ectopic pregnancy","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Caesarean-scar ectopic pregnancy. The key discriminator is empty endometrial cavity with gestational tissue in the caesarean scar and thin anterior myometrium. Immediate priorities include: obtain urgent specialist treatment with haemorrhage planning.","learningPoint":"Caesarean-scar ectopic pregnancy: empty endometrial cavity with gestational tissue in the caesarean scar and thin anterior myometrium. Avoid performing blind curettage without recognising scar implantation.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-111","globalNumber":2581,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":112,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is caesarean-scar ectopic pregnancy. The patient has a patient with previous caesarean delivery has an early gestation embedded in the anterior lower uterine segment. What is the best immediate management?","options":["resuscitate, correct coagulopathy and expedite delivery according to maternal and fetal status","obtain urgent specialist treatment with haemorrhage planning","stabilise complications and arrange suction evacuation with oncology follow-up","activate massive transfusion and obtain immediate senior obstetric and surgical haemorrhage control","activate haemorrhage resuscitation and obtain urgent operative management of the ectopic pregnancy"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"obtain urgent specialist treatment with haemorrhage planning","explanation":"The priority is to obtain urgent specialist treatment with haemorrhage planning. The decisive feature is empty endometrial cavity with gestational tissue in the caesarean scar and thin anterior myometrium. A major error is performing blind curettage without recognising scar implantation.","learningPoint":"Caesarean-scar ectopic pregnancy: empty endometrial cavity with gestational tissue in the caesarean scar and thin anterior myometrium. Avoid performing blind curettage without recognising scar implantation.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-112","globalNumber":2582,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":113,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have caesarean-scar ectopic pregnancy. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["painless bright bleeding with placental tissue over or near the cervix","haemoperitoneum from a ruptured haemorrhagic ovarian cyst without ectopic pregnancy","empty endometrial cavity with gestational tissue in the caesarean scar and thin anterior myometrium","uterine size greater than dates with markedly elevated hCG","infected retained products with systemic toxicity"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"empty endometrial cavity with gestational tissue in the caesarean scar and thin anterior myometrium","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is empty endometrial cavity with gestational tissue in the caesarean scar and thin anterior myometrium. In context, this supports Caesarean-scar ectopic pregnancy and should prompt clinicians to obtain urgent specialist treatment with haemorrhage planning.","learningPoint":"Caesarean-scar ectopic pregnancy: empty endometrial cavity with gestational tissue in the caesarean scar and thin anterior myometrium. Avoid performing blind curettage without recognising scar implantation.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-113","globalNumber":2583,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":114,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with caesarean-scar ectopic pregnancy is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["assuming the bleeding is maternal and delaying fetal rescue","excluding ectopic pregnancy solely because an intrauterine pregnancy is visible","misclassifying the pregnancy as a normal eccentric intrauterine implantation","performing blind curettage without recognising scar implantation","assuming all cyst rupture is self-limiting despite ongoing shock"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"performing blind curettage without recognising scar implantation","explanation":"The hazardous action is performing blind curettage without recognising scar implantation. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is massive haemorrhage, uterine rupture and placenta accreta spectrum.","learningPoint":"Caesarean-scar ectopic pregnancy: empty endometrial cavity with gestational tissue in the caesarean scar and thin anterior myometrium. Avoid performing blind curettage without recognising scar implantation.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-114","globalNumber":2584,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":115,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with caesarean-scar ectopic pregnancy?","options":["maternal exsanguination and fetal death","catastrophic haemorrhage, hysterectomy and coagulopathy","catastrophic haemorrhage from late rupture","exsanguination and disseminated coagulopathy","massive haemorrhage, uterine rupture and placenta accreta spectrum"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"massive haemorrhage, uterine rupture and placenta accreta spectrum","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is massive haemorrhage, uterine rupture and placenta accreta spectrum. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of caesarean-scar ectopic pregnancy.","learningPoint":"Caesarean-scar ectopic pregnancy: e