rts Acute fatty liver of pregnancy and should prompt clinicians to stabilise maternal organ failure and expedite delivery with obstetric and critical-care teams.","learningPoint":"Acute fatty liver of pregnancy: acute hepatic dysfunction with hypoglycaemia and microvesicular-fatty-liver pattern rather than isolated hypertension. Avoid delaying delivery while treating presumed viral hepatitis alone.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-118","globalNumber":2588,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":119,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in acute fatty liver of pregnancy?","options":["continuing antibiotics alone despite documented venous thrombosis","assuming normal blood pressure excludes the syndrome","using diazepam alone or delivering before maternal stabilisation","delaying delivery while treating presumed viral hepatitis alone","attributing orthopnoea and oedema to normal pregnancy"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"delaying delivery while treating presumed viral hepatitis alone","explanation":"The hazardous action is delaying delivery while treating presumed viral hepatitis alone. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is liver failure, disseminated coagulopathy and fetal death.","learningPoint":"Acute fatty liver of pregnancy: acute hepatic dysfunction with hypoglycaemia and microvesicular-fatty-liver pattern rather than isolated hypertension. Avoid delaying delivery while treating presumed viral hepatitis alone.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-119","globalNumber":2589,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":120,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in acute fatty liver of pregnancy, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["pulmonary embolism and sepsis","cardiac arrest, massive haemorrhag