e neck","explanation":"The hazardous action is pulling forcefully on the fetal body or hyperextending the neck. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is fetal asphyxia and cervical spinal injury.","learningPoint":"Breech delivery with entrapped after-coming head: incomplete breech delivery with persistent head entrapment and fetal compromise. Avoid pulling forcefully on the fetal body or hyperextending the neck.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-149","globalNumber":2619,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":150,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Following initial treatment of breech delivery with entrapped after-coming head, which complication requires focused monitoring?","options":["massive haemorrhage and refractory shock","fetal hypoxic-ischaemic injury","fetal hypoxia, uterine rupture and emergency operative delivery","maternal exsanguination and fetal death","fetal asphyxia and cervical spinal injury"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"fetal asphyxia and cervical spinal injury","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is fetal asphyxia and cervical spinal injury. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of breech delivery with entrapped after-coming head.","learningPoint":"Breech delivery with entrapped after-coming head: incomplete breech delivery with persistent head entrapment and fetal compromise. Avoid pulling forcefully on the fetal body or hyperextending the neck.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-150","globalNumber":2620,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":151,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The emergency presentation is characterised by sudden unilateral pelvic pain and shock occur with free fluid and an adnexal cyst. What is the single best diagnosis?","options":["Ovarian cyst rupture with haemoperitoneum","Bartholin gland abscess with sepsis","Hydatidiform mole","Ruptured ectopic pregnancy","Septic abortion"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Ovarian cyst rupture with haemoperitoneum","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Ovarian cyst rupture with haemoperitoneum. The key discriminator is haemoperitoneum from a ruptured haemorrhagic ovarian cyst without ectopic pregnancy. Immediate priorities include: resuscitate and obtain urgent gynaecologic haemostasis when unstable.","learningPoint":"Ovarian cyst rupture with haemoperitoneum: haemoperitoneum from a ruptured haemorrhagic ovarian cyst without ectopic pregnancy. Avoid assuming all cyst rupture is self-limiting despite ongoing shock.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-151","globalNumber":2621,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":152,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"The diagnosis is ovarian cyst rupture with haemoperitoneum. The patient has sudden unilateral pelvic pain and shock occur with free fluid and an adnexal cyst. What is the best immediate management?","options":["stabilise complications and arrange suction evacuation with oncology follow-up","resuscitate and obtain urgent gynaecologic haemostasis when unstable","give broad intravenous antibiotics and arrange drainage or surgery if large, ruptured or unresponsive","obtain urgent specialist medical or surgical treatment before rupture","activate haemorrhage resuscitation and obtain immediate operative gynaecologic control"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"resuscitate and obtain urgent gynaecologic haemostasis when unstable","explanation":"The priority is to resuscitate and obtain urgent gynaecologic haemostasis when unstable. The decisive feature is haemoperitoneum from a ruptured haemorrhagic ovarian cyst without ectopic pregnancy. A major error is assuming all cyst rupture is self-limiting despite ongoing shock.","learningPoint":"Ovarian cyst rupture with haemoperitoneum: haemoperitoneum from a ruptured haemorrhagic ovarian cyst without ectopic pregnancy. Avoid assuming all cyst rupture is self-limiting despite ongoing shock.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-152","globalNumber":2622,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":153,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient may have ovarian cyst rupture with haemoperitoneum. Which finding most strongly supports this diagnosis over its mimics?","options":["a posterolateral vestibular abscess rather than diffuse cellulitis or malignancy","uterine size greater than dates with markedly elevated hCG","haemoperitoneum from a ruptured haemorrhagic ovarian cyst without ectopic pregnancy","positive pregnancy test with no intrauterine pregnancy and haemoperitoneum","empty endometrial cavity with gestational tissue in the caesarean scar and thin anterior myometrium"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"haemoperitoneum from a ruptured haemorrhagic ovarian cyst without ectopic pregnancy","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is haemoperitoneum from a ruptured haemorrhagic ovarian cyst without ectopic pregnancy. In context, this supports Ovarian cyst rupture with haemoperitoneum and should prompt clinicians to resuscitate and obtain urgent gynaecologic haemostasis when unstable.","learningPoint":"Ovarian cyst rupture with haemoperitoneum: haemoperitoneum from a ruptured haemorrhagic ovarian cyst without ectopic pregnancy. Avoid assuming all cyst rupture is self-limiting despite ongoing shock.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-153","globalNumber":2623,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":154,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient with ovarian cyst rupture with haemoperitoneum is deteriorating. Which proposed action represents the most important pitfall?","options":["inducing labour or failing to arrange serial hCG surveillance","aggressive diuresis despite low effective circulating volume","delaying surgery for serial hCG in an unstable patient","assuming all cyst rupture is self-limiting despite ongoing shock","excluding torsion because arterial flow is present"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"assuming all cyst rupture is self-limiting despite ongoing shock","explanation":"The hazardous action is assuming all cyst rupture is self-limiting despite ongoing shock. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is exsanguination and ovarian loss.","learningPoint":"Ovarian cyst rupture with haemoperitoneum: haemoperitoneum from a ruptured haemorrhagic ovarian cyst without ectopic pregnancy. Avoid assuming all cyst rupture is self-limiting despite ongoing shock.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-154","globalNumber":2624,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":155,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which life-threatening complication is most closely associated with ovarian cyst rupture with haemoperitoneum?","options":["exsanguination and disseminated coagulopathy","haemorrhage, thyrotoxicosis and gestational trophoblastic neoplasia","haemorrhagic shock and severe anaemia","massive concealed blood loss and tissue necrosis","exsanguination and ovarian loss"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"exsanguination and ovarian loss","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is exsanguination and ovarian loss. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of ovarian cyst rupture with haemoperitoneum.","learningPoint":"Ovarian cyst rupture with haemoperitoneum: haemoperitoneum from a ruptured haemorrhagic ovarian cyst without ectopic pregnancy. Avoid assuming all cyst rupture is self-limiting despite ongoing shock.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-155","globalNumber":2625,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":156,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has postpartum fever and lower abdominal or flank pain persist despite antibiotics. Which underlying diagnosis should be prioritised?","options":["Postpartum ovarian vein thrombosis","Postpartum endometritis","Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome","Hyperemesis gravidarum with Wernicke encephalopathy","Septic pelvic thrombophlebitis"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Postpartum ovarian vein thrombosis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Postpartum ovarian vein thrombosis. The key discriminator is enlarged thrombosed ovarian vein, usually right-sided, with no better source. Immediate priorities include: give anticoagulation and appropriate antibiotics when septic thrombophlebitis is suspected.","learningPoint":"Postpartum ovarian vein thrombosis: enlarged thrombosed ovarian vein, usually right-sided, with no better source. Avoid continuing antibiotics alone despite documented venous thrombosis.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-156","globalNumber":2626,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":157,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which management option is most appropriate for postpartum ovarian vein thrombosis when the presentation is postpartum fever and lower abdominal or flank pain persist despite antibiotics?","options":["provide immediate advanced cardiopulmonary support, massive transfusion and expedite delivery if undelivered","give anticoagulation and appropriate antibiotics when septic thrombophlebitis is suspected","continue antimicrobial therapy and add anticoagulation with specialist input","manage intravascular depletion carefully, prevent thrombosis and drain tense ascites when indicated","stabilise, give magnesium when indicated and arrange urgent delivery planning"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"give anticoagulation and appropriate antibiotics when septic thrombophlebitis is suspected","explanation":"The priority is to give anticoagulation and appropriate antibiotics when septic thrombophlebitis is suspected. The decisive feature is enlarged thrombosed ovarian vein, usually right-sided, with no better source. A major error is continuing antibiotics alone despite documented venous thrombosis.","learningPoint":"Postpartum ovarian vein thrombosis: enlarged thrombosed ovarian vein, usually right-sided, with no better source. Avoid continuing antibiotics alone despite documented venous thrombosis.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-157","globalNumber":2627,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":158,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for postpartum ovarian vein thrombosis in this presentation?","options":["third spacing with haemoconcentration after ovulation induction","microangiopathic haemolysis with hepatic injury and low platelets","enlarged thrombosed ovarian vein, usually right-sided, with no better source","postpartum fever with uterine tenderness and no better source","acute hepatic dysfunction with hypoglycaemia and microvesicular-fatty-liver pattern rather than isolated hypertension"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"enlarged thrombosed ovarian vein, usually right-sided, with no better source","explanation":"The most discriminating finding is enlarged thrombosed ovarian vein, usually right-sided, with no better source. In context, this supports Postpartum ovarian vein thrombosis and should prompt clinicians to give anticoagulation and appropriate antibiotics when septic thrombophlebitis is suspected.","learningPoint":"Postpartum ovarian vein thrombosis: enlarged thrombosed ovarian vein, usually right-sided, with no better source. Avoid continuing antibiotics alone despite documented venous thrombosis.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-158","globalNumber":2628,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":159,"type":"pitfall","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which action could directly worsen physiology or delay definitive treatment in postpartum ovarian vein thrombosis?","options":["aggressive diuresis despite low effective circulating volume","attributing orthopnoea and oedema to normal pregnancy","repeatedly broadening antibiotics without considering pelvic thrombosis","continuing antibiotics alone despite documented venous thrombosis","delaying delivery while treating presumed viral hepatitis alone"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"continuing antibiotics alone despite documented venous thrombosis","explanation":"The hazardous action is continuing antibiotics alone despite documented venous thrombosis. It can worsen physiology or delay definitive care; an important consequence is pulmonary embolism and sepsis.","learningPoint":"Postpartum ovarian vein thrombosis: enlarged thrombosed ovarian vein, usually right-sided, with no better source. Avoid continuing antibiotics alone despite documented venous thrombosis.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-159","globalNumber":2629,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":160,"type":"complication","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"After apparent early improvement in postpartum ovarian vein thrombosis, which complication remains a major concern?","options":["permanent amnesia, aspiration and fetal growth restriction","thrombosis, renal failure and respiratory compromise","liver failure, disseminated coagulopathy and fetal death","pelvic abscess, septic pelvic thrombophlebitis and sepsis","pulmonary embolism and sepsis"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"pulmonary embolism and sepsis","explanation":"The complication to anticipate is pulmonary embolism and sepsis. Continued reassessment is required even after initial stabilisation of postpartum ovarian vein thrombosis.","learningPoint":"Postpartum ovarian vein thrombosis: enlarged thrombosed ovarian vein, usually right-sided, with no better source. Avoid continuing antibiotics alone despite documented venous thrombosis.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-160","globalNumber":2630,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":161,"type":"diagnosis","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient presents with persistent postpartum fever continues despite broad antibiotics with little localising examination. Which diagnosis best explains the overall clinical pattern?","options":["Septic pelvic thrombophlebitis","Postpartum endometritis","Disseminated gonococcal infection","Tubo-ovarian abscess","Septic abortion"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"Septic pelvic thrombophlebitis","explanation":"The presentation is most consistent with Septic pelvic thrombophlebitis. The key discriminator is diagnosis of exclusion with pelvic venous thrombosis or rapid response to anticoagulation. Immediate priorities include: continue antimicrobial therapy and add anticoagulation with specialist input.","learningPoint":"Septic pelvic thrombophlebitis: diagnosis of exclusion with pelvic venous thrombosis or rapid response to anticoagulation. Avoid repeatedly broadening antibiotics without considering pelvic thrombosis.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-161","globalNumber":2631,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":162,"type":"management","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has septic pelvic thrombophlebitis: persistent postpartum fever continues despite broad antibiotics with little localising examination. What is the most appropriate immediate emergency-department management?","options":["give broad intravenous antibiotics covering polymicrobial genital flora","continue antimicrobial therapy and add anticoagulation with specialist input","give broad intravenous antibiotics and arrange drainage or surgery if large, ruptured or unresponsive","provide immediate advanced cardiopulmonary support, massive transfusion and expedite delivery if undelivered","give appropriate parenteral antibiotics and evaluate for septic joints and partner treatment"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"continue antimicrobial therapy and add anticoagulation with specialis