st appropriate for pregnancy-associated spontaneous coronary artery dissection when the presentation is a pregnant or postpartum patient develops acute coronary syndrome without typical atherosclerotic risk?","options":["resuscitate, correct coagulopathy and expedite delivery according to maternal and fetal status","obtain urgent coronary and obstetric management, favouring conservative treatment when stable","treat acute heart failure with pregnancy-compatible therapy and obtain urgent cardiology and obstetric input","perform immediate uterine evacuation at the arrest site while continuing high-quality resuscitation","provide immediate advanced cardiopulmonary support, massive transfusion and expedite delivery if undelivered"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"obtain urgent coronary and obstetric management, favouring conservative treatment when stable","explanation":"The priority is to obtain urgent coronary and obstetric management, favouring conservative treatment when stable. The decisive feature is coronary intramural haematoma or dissection in the pregnancy-associated period. A major error is giving fibrinolysis without considering coronary dissection.","learningPoint":"Pregnancy-associated spontaneous coronary artery dissection: coronary intramural haematoma or dissection in the pregnancy-associated period. Avoid giving fibrinolysis without considering coronary dissection.","id":"obstetrics-and-gynaecology-197","globalNumber":2667,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","categoryNumber":198,"type":"discriminator","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Which investigation or clinical feature is most specific for pregnancy-associated spontaneous coronary artery dissection in this presentation?","options":["abrupt fetal compromise with cessation of contractions or palpable fetal parts","reduced ejection fraction without another cause near the end of pregnancy","coronary intramural haematoma or dissection in the pregnancy-associated period","abrupt 