19. What is the specificity?","options":["85.0%","91.5%","81.7%","84.0%","70.0%"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"85.0%","explanation":"Specificity = TN/(TN+FP) = 119/140 = 85.0%.","learningPoint":"Specificity is the proportion of people without disease who test negative.","id":"medical-administration-036","globalNumber":2676,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":37,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"For TP=96, FN=24, FP=24, and TN=156, what is the positive predictive value?","options":["32.0%","80.0%","83.2%","40.0%","86.7%"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"80.0%","explanation":"PPV = TP/(TP+FP) = 96/120 = 80.0%.","learningPoint":"Predictive values vary with disease prevalence.","id":"medical-administration-037","globalNumber":2677,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":38,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"For TP=135, FN=15, FP=45, and TN=205, what is the positive predictive value?","options":["37.5%","82.0%","75.0%","90.0%","93.2%"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"75.0%","explanation":"PPV = TP/(TP+FP) = 135/180 = 75.0%.","learningPoint":"Predictive values vary with disease prevalence.","id":"medical-administration-038","globalNumber":2678,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":39,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"For TP=84, FN=16, FP=34, and TN=166, what is the negative predictive value?","options":["83.0%","71.2%","83.3%","91.2%","84.0%"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"91.2%","explanation":"NPV = TN/(TN+FN) = 166/182 = 91.2%.","learningPoint":"Negative predictive value is the probability that a negative result is truly disease-free.","id":"medical-administration-039","globalNumber":2679,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":40,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"For TP=172, FN=28, FP=20, and TN=280, what is the negative predictive value?","options":["93.3%","89.6%","90.4%","86.0%","90.9%"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"90.9%","explanation":"NPV = TN/(TN+FN) = 280/308 = 90.9%.","learningPoint":"Negative predictive value is the probability that a negative result is truly disease-free.","id":"medical-administration-040","globalNumber":2680,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":41,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A test has sensitivity 92.0% and specificity 78.0%. What is the positive likelihood ratio?","options":["4.18","9.75","1.18","0.10","0.24"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"4.18","explanation":"LR+ = sensitivity/(1-specificity) = 0.92/0.22 = 4.18.","learningPoint":"LR+ indicates how much a positive result increases disease odds.","id":"medical-administration-041","globalNumber":2681,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":42,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A test has sensitivity 81.0% and specificity 94.0%. What is the positive likelihood ratio?","options":["0.20","13.50","0.86","0.07","4.95"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"13.50","explanation":"LR+ = sensitivity/(1-specificity) = 0.81/0.06 = 13.50.","learningPoint":"LR+ indicates how much a positive result increases disease odds.","id":"medical-administration-042","globalNumber":2682,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":43,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A test has sensitivity 88.0% and specificity 84.0%. What is the negative likelihood ratio?","options":["5.50","7.00","0.14","1.05","0.75"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"0.14","explanation":"LR− = (1-sensitivity)/specificity = 0.12/0.84 = 0.14.","learningPoint":"Smaller LR− values are better for ruling disease out.","id":"medical-administration-043","globalNumber":2683,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":44,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A test has sensitivity 73.0% and specificity 91.0%. What is the negative likelihood ratio?","options":["3.00","0.80","3.37","0.30","8.11"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"0.30","explanation":"LR− = (1-sensitivity)/specificity = 0.27/0.91 = 0.30.","learningPoint":"Smaller LR− values are better for ruling disease out.","id":"medical-administration-044","globalNumber":2684,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":45,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"An adverse outcome occurs in 31.0% of controls and 19.0% of treated patients. What is the number needed to treat, rounded up?","options":["12","2","3","5","9"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"9","explanation":"ARR = 0.31−0.19 = 0.12; NNT = 1/ARR = 8.33, rounded up to 9.","learningPoint":"NNT is the reciprocal of absolute risk reduction and is rounded up.","id":"medical-administration-045","globalNumber":2685,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":46,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"An adverse outcome occurs in 16.0% of controls and 9.0% of treated patients. What is the number needed to treat, rounded up?","options":["15","7","2","6","11"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"15","explanation":"ARR = 0.16−0.09 = 0.07; NNT = 1/ARR = 14.29, rounded up to 15.","learningPoint":"NNT is the reciprocal of absolute risk reduction and is rounded up.","id":"medical-administration-046","globalNumber":2686,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":47,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"An adverse outcome occurs in 55.0% of controls and 39.0% of treated patients. What is the number needed to treat, rounded up?","options":["3","7","16","2","5"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"7","explanation":"ARR = 0.55−0.39 = 0.16; NNT = 1/ARR = 6.25, rounded up to 7.","learningPoint":"NNT is the reciprocal of absolute risk reduction and is rounded up.","id":"medical-administration-047","globalNumber":2687,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":48,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A treatment-related harm occurs in 4.0% of controls and 10.0% of treated patients. What is the number needed to harm, rounded up?","options":["10","19","17","25","6"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"17","explanation":"Absolute risk increase = 0.10−0.04 = 0.06; NNH = 1/ARI = 16.67, rounded up to 17.","learningPoint":"NNH is the reciprocal of absolute risk increase and is rounded up.","id":"medical-administration-048","globalNumber":2688,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":49,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A treatment-related harm occurs in 8.0% of controls and 13.0% of treated patients. What is the number needed to harm, rounded up?","options":["5","8","12","20","22"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"20","explanation":"Absolute risk increase = 0.13−0.08 = 0.05; NNH = 1/ARI = 20.00, rounded up to 20.","learningPoint":"NNH is the reciprocal of absolute risk increase and is rounded up.","id":"medical-administration-049","globalNumber":2689,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":50,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Event risk is 26.0% with control and 18.0% with treatment. What is the absolute risk reduction?","options":["30.8%","44.4%","18.0%","26.0%","8.0%"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"8.0%","explanation":"ARR = control risk − treatment risk = 26.0% − 18.0% = 8.0%.","learningPoint":"Absolute risk reduction is the arithmetic difference between event rates.","id":"medical-administration-050","globalNumber":2690,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":51,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Event risk is 40.0% with control and 29.0% with treatment. What is the absolute risk reduction?","options":["11.0%","40.0%","37.9%","27.5%","29.0%"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"11.0%","explanation":"ARR = control risk − treatment risk = 40.0% − 29.0% = 11.0%.","learningPoint":"Absolute risk reduction is the arithmetic difference between event rates.","id":"medical-administration-051","globalNumber":2691,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":52,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Event risk is 14.0% with control and 8.0% with treatment. What is the absolute risk reduction?","options":["14.0%","6.0%","75.0%","8.0%","42.9%"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"6.0%","explanation":"ARR = control risk − treatment risk = 14.0% − 8.0% = 6.0%.","learningPoint":"Absolute risk reduction is the arithmetic difference between event rates.","id":"medical-administration-052","globalNumber":2692,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":53,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Event risk falls from 25.0% to 15.0%. What is the relative risk reduction?","options":["25.0%","66.7%","40.0%","10.0%","60.0%"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"40.0%","explanation":"RRR = (control−treatment)/control = (0.25−0.15)/0.25 = 40.0%.","learningPoint":"Relative risk reduction can appear large even when absolute benefit is modest.","id":"medical-administration-053","globalNumber":2693,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":54,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"Event risk falls from 32.0% to 20.0%. What is the relative risk reduction?","options":["62.5%","12.0%","23.1%","37.5%","60.0%"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"37.5%","explanation":"RRR = (control−treatment)/control = (0.32−0.20)/0.32 = 37.5%.","learningPoint":"Relative risk reduction can appear large even when absolute benefit is modest.","id":"medical-administration-054","globalNumber":2694,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":55,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a cohort, exposed cases=54, exposed non-cases=146, unexposed cases=30, and unexposed non-cases=170. What is the relative risk?","options":["2.10","0.27","0.56","0.12","1.80"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"1.80","explanation":"Risk exposed=54/200=0.270; risk unexposed=30/200=0.150; RR=1.80.","learningPoint":"Relative risk compares event probabilities between exposed and unexposed groups.","id":"medical-administration-055","globalNumber":2695,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":56,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a cohort, exposed cases=84, exposed non-cases=116, unexposed cases=48, and unexposed non-cases=152. What is the relative risk?","options":["1.75","0.18","2.29","0.42","0.57"],"answerIndex":0,"answer":"1.75","explanation":"Risk exposed=84/200=0.420; risk unexposed=48/200=0.240; RR=1.75.","learningPoint":"Relative risk compares event probabilities between exposed and unexposed groups.","id":"medical-administration-056","globalNumber":2696,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":57,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a case-control table, exposed cases=44, exposed controls=156, unexposed cases=22, and unexposed controls=178. What is the odds ratio?","options":["0.11","2.28","2.00","0.44","0.22"],"answerIndex":1,"answer":"2.28","explanation":"OR = (a×d)/(b×c) = (44×178)/(156×22) = 2.28.","learningPoint":"The odds ratio is the cross-product ratio in a 2×2 table.","id":"medical-administration-057","globalNumber":2697,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":58,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"In a case-control table, exposed cases=63, exposed controls=137, unexposed cases=35, and unexposed controls=165. What is the odds ratio?","options":["0.32","0.46","2.17","1.80","0.14"],"answerIndex":2,"answer":"2.17","explanation":"OR = (a×d)/(b×c) = (63×165)/(137×35) = 2.17.","learningPoint":"The odds ratio is the cross-product ratio in a 2×2 table.","id":"medical-administration-058","globalNumber":2698,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":59,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A diagnostic cohort has TP=72, FN=28, FP=36, and TN=164. What is the disease prevalence?","options":["72.0%","85.4%","66.7%","33.3%","78.7%"],"answerIndex":3,"answer":"33.3%","explanation":"Prevalence = (TP+FN)/total = 100/300 = 33.3%.","learningPoint":"Prevalence is the proportion of the tested population that truly has disease.","id":"medical-administration-059","globalNumber":2699,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"},{"category":"Medical Administration","categoryNumber":60,"type":"calculation","difficulty":"5/5","stem":"A patient has a pre-test probability of 30% and a positive test with LR+ 5.0. What is the approximate post-test probability?","options":["31.8%","76.4%","44.0%","83.3%","68.2%"],"answerIndex":4,"answer":"68.2%","explanation":"Pre-test odds=0.30/0.70=0.429; post-test odds=0.429×5.0=2.143; probability=68.2%.","learningPoint":"Convert probability to odds, multiply by the likelihood ratio, then convert back to probability.","id":"medical-administration-060","globalNumber":2700,"questionSet":"Expansion 2026"}]</script>
